= Astrophysics
{wiki}

Astrophysics applies physical laws to stars, planets, galaxies, radiation, and the universe.

= Parsec
{parent=Astrophysics}
{title2=pc}
{wiki}

A parsec is the distance corresponding to one arcsecond of annual parallax for a baseline of one <astronomical unit>. It is approximately $3.086\times10^{16}$ metres. A megaparsec is one million parsecs.

= Intergalactic medium
{parent=Astrophysics}
{title2=IGM}

The intergalactic medium is the gas between <galaxies>. Its diffuse, ionized component contains much of the ordinary matter outside stars. <Lyman-alpha absorption> measures its small neutral-hydrogen fraction, while hotter phases require other tracers.

= Cosmic ionizing background
{parent=Intergalactic medium}
{title2=$J_\nu$}

= Ionizing background
{synonym}

The cosmic ionizing background is the diffuse radiation field capable of <photoionization> of intergalactic gas. Its <specific intensity> depends on source <luminosity functions>, <ionizing photon escape fractions>, cosmological redshifting, and <radiative transfer> through absorbers. <Quasars> and <star formation> supply major contributions with different spectral shapes and evolution.

= Lyman-alpha forest
{c}
{parent=Intergalactic medium}
{wiki}

The Lyman-alpha forest is the collection of foreground <Lyman-alpha absorption> features in a distant <quasar> spectrum. Each observed wavelength identifies a <cosmological redshift>. Weak forest lines usually sample diffuse <intergalactic medium> at modest <density contrast>; strong, self-shielding absorbers need a different density interpretation.

= Proximity effect
{parent=Lyman-alpha forest}
{disambiguate=astrophysics}

= Quasar proximity effect
{synonym}

The quasar proximity effect is a reduction of nearby <Lyman-alpha forest> absorption caused by extra <photoionization> from the <quasar>. At fixed density and temperature in <photoionization equilibrium>, an added rate $\Gamma_Q$ changes the neutral fraction by the factor $\Gamma_{\rm bg}/(\Gamma_{\rm bg}+\Gamma_Q)$. A host environment with positive <density contrast> can mask this reduction, and a variable or anisotropic source complicates its interpretation.

= Photoevaporation
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

Radiation heats gas sufficiently to drive an escaping thermal outflow. It can remove material from <protoplanetary disks>, planetary atmospheres and irradiated clouds. In a heated <Keplerian disk>, the <photoevaporative gravitational radius> compares the <sound speed> with the orbital speed. The mass-loss rate depends on the heating, density and wind geometry rather than radiation pressure alone.

= Photoevaporative gap opening
{parent=Photoevaporation}

When a <protoplanetary disk>'s inward viscous supply approaches its wind-loss rate, <photoevaporation> can prevent replenishment of the inner disk. That region drains on a <viscous timescale>, leaving a gap or hole. A negative density in a formal <steady state> calculation signals the breakdown of that approximation, not a physical continuation. An exposed outer edge can subsequently disperse rapidly.

= Steady photoevaporating-disk mass flux
{parent=Photoevaporation}
{title2=$F_{\mathrm{acc}}=\dot M_d-\dot M_w\sqrt{r_g/r}$}

For a sink $W=W_0(r/r_g)^{-5/2}$ outside $r_g$, <mass conservation> gives $dF_{\mathrm{acc}}/dr=2\pi rW$ in <steady state>. Remote feeding gives $F_{\mathrm{acc}}=\dot M_d-\dot M_w$ inside $r_g$, and $\dot M_d-\dot M_w\sqrt{r_g/r}$ outside, where $\dot M_w=4\pi W_0r_g^2$. The inward flux decreases towards the star because mass is diverted into the wind. A finite feeding radius or outer truncation supplies an additional boundary condition.

= Surface density of a steady photoevaporating disk
{parent=Steady photoevaporating-disk mass flux}
{title2=$3\pi\nu\Sigma=\dot M_d-\dot M_w\sqrt{r_g/r}[1+\tfrac12\log(r/r_g)]$}

With constant <kinematic viscosity> and zero inner <viscous torque in an accretion disk>, $\nu\Sigma\sqrt r=(6\pi)^{-1}\int_0^rF_{\mathrm{acc}}(r')/\sqrt{r'}\,dr'$. For the <steady photoevaporating-disk mass flux>, the density is $(\dot M_d-\dot M_w)/(3\pi\nu)$ inside $r_g$ and the displayed logarithmic profile outside. At equal feeding and wind rates the inner density vanishes, and the outer normalized profile rises from zero with zero slope and tends to one. Its near-edge expansion is $(r/r_g-1)^2/8$.

= Photoevaporative gravitational radius
{parent=Photoevaporation}
{title2=$r_g=GM/c_s^2$}

The thermal binding scale defined by equating heated gas <sound speed> to circular <Keplerian orbit> speed. It is $r_g=GM/c_s^2$. The circular-orbit binding energy is $GM/(2r)$ per unit mass, while the gas <specific enthalpy> is of order $c_s^2$. A heated disk surface at radii comparable to or larger than $r_g$ can therefore drive <photoevaporation>. The actual launching radius can differ by a factor of order unity; $c_s$ is not the ballistic escape speed.

= Gravitational collapse
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

<Gravitational collapse> is contraction driven by a system's own gravitational attraction or an enclosing potential. Pressure, angular momentum and other stresses can oppose it. A free-fall timescale measures the dynamical contraction rate when pressure support is removed; gas cooling determines whether thermal support can be removed fast enough.

= Free-fall time of a uniform sphere
{parent=Gravitational collapse}
{title2=$t_{\rm ff}=\sqrt{3\pi/(32G\rho)}$}

= Uniform-sphere free-fall time
{synonym}

A pressureless uniform sphere initially at rest collapses under Newtonian gravity in a time depending only on its initial mass density. Solving the radial shell equation gives $\sqrt{3\pi/(32G\rho)}$. It is commonly used as a dynamical-time benchmark for cooling and <star formation>, with the gravitating density rather than gas density alone when other matter supplies gravity.

= Solar system
{c}
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

= Solar wind
{parent=Solar system}
{wiki}

The solar wind is the outward plasma flow from the Sun, carrying an interplanetary <magnetic field>. Its pressure and field compress intrinsic planetary <magnetospheres>, form magnetotails, and can drive an <induced magnetosphere> around a planet with no appreciable intrinsic field.

= Mars
{parent=Solar system}
{c}
{wiki}

A <terrestrial planet> with a thin present atmosphere and evidence of a different past volatile and climate history.

= Uranus
{parent=Solar system}
{c}
{wiki}

An <ice giant> with substantial heavy material beneath its H/He envelope. Its low intrinsic luminosity makes efficient, fully mixed cooling an imperfect universal assumption.

= Saturn
{parent=Solar system}
{c}
{wiki}

A <gas giant> with a substantial H/He envelope. Intrinsic cooling, contraction and helium differentiation contribute to its thermal evolution.

= Sidereal time
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

Local <sidereal time> is the <right ascension> on the upper <celestial meridian>. A sidereal day is approximately $1436.07$ minutes; its angular rate is $2\pi/T_{\rm sid}$. Mean and apparent <sidereal time> must be paired with corresponding <equatorial coordinate systems>.

= Horizontal coordinate system
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

The horizontal coordinate system describes a direction by its <altitude> above the <astronomical horizon> and its <azimuth>. Always specify the origin and orientation of <azimuth>.

= Geographical latitude
{parent=Horizontal coordinate system}
{title2=$\theta$}

= Latitude
{synonym}

For a spherical Earth, geographical latitude is the <angle> of the local vertical above the equatorial plane. It equals the <altitude> of the north <celestial pole> in the northern hemisphere. Precision astrometry distinguishes geodetic and astronomical latitude.

= Azimuth
{parent=Horizontal coordinate system}
{title2=$\eta$}
{wiki}

<Azimuth> is the <angle> about the local vertical measured in the <astronomical horizon>. A common convention increases eastward from north; some astronomical formulae use westward from north. The convention changes the sign in the <hour angle> transformation.

= Altitude
{parent=Horizontal coordinate system}
{title2=$\gamma$}

= Elevation angle
{synonym}

<Altitude> is the <angle> of a direction above the <astronomical horizon>, ranging from $-90^\circ$ to $+90^\circ$.

= Zenith
{parent=Horizontal coordinate system}
{wiki}

The <zenith> is the direction of the local upward vertical on the <celestial sphere>. <Azimuth> is undefined there.

= Astronomical horizon
{parent=Horizontal coordinate system}

The astronomical horizon is the <great circle> perpendicular to the local vertical. It is a geometric horizon, distinct from a terrain boundary or an apparent refracted horizon.

= Celestial sphere
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

The <celestial sphere> is the <sphere> of viewing directions around an observer; distances to objects are suppressed. <Great circles> define coordinate reference planes.

= Celestial meridian
{parent=Celestial sphere}

An observer’s <celestial meridian> is the <great circle> containing the <zenith> and the <celestial poles>. Its upper half passes through the local north and south directions.

= Celestial pole
{parent=Celestial sphere}
{wiki}

The <celestial poles> are the two intersections of the rotation axis of Earth with the <celestial sphere>. The northern pole has <declination> $+90^\circ$.

= Celestial equator
{parent=Celestial sphere}
{wiki}

The <celestial equator> is the <great circle> perpendicular to the rotation axis of Earth; <declination> measures angular displacement from it.

= Accretion
{parent=Astrophysics}

Growth of an astronomical object's <mass> by capture of surrounding matter. During <planetary scattering>, a collision can lead to <accretion> if material is retained by the <planet>; not every collision retains all material. An <accretion disk> is one particular geometry supplying material.

= Equatorial coordinate system
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

An equatorial coordinate system locates directions on the celestial sphere using <right ascension> and <declination>, referred to a specified equator, equinox and epoch.

= Hour angle
{parent=Equatorial coordinate system}
{title2=$h$}
{wiki}

The <hour angle> of a direction is the westward <angle> from the upper <celestial meridian> to its hour circle. In a consistent equinox convention, $h=\mathrm{LST}-\alpha$ modulo one turn, where $\alpha$ is <right ascension> and LST is local <sidereal time>.

= Parallactic angle
{parent=Equatorial coordinate system}
{wiki}

The parallactic angle is the angle between the great circles from an observed direction toward the celestial pole and toward the zenith. Its change gives sky rotation in pupil-stabilized observations.

= Declination
{parent=Equatorial coordinate system}
{title2=$\delta$}
{wiki}

Declination is angular latitude north of the celestial equator, ranging from minus to plus 90 degrees.

= Right ascension
{parent=Equatorial coordinate system}
{title2=$\alpha$}
{wiki}

Right ascension is the eastward angular coordinate measured around the celestial equator from the reference equinox. It is often quoted in hours, with 24 hours corresponding to a full turn.

= Light curve
{parent=Astrophysics}

A light curve is the brightness of an astronomical source as a function of time, expressed as flux or <apparent magnitude>. A periodic light curve repeats after a characteristic time; <Gaussian process> models can reconstruct irregularly sampled light curves and propagate observational uncertainty.

= Luminosity
{title2=$L$}
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

Luminosity is the total radiative energy emitted by an astronomical source per unit time. It differs from the received <radiative flux>, which also depends on distance and geometry.

= Spectral luminosity
{parent=Luminosity}
{title2=$L_\nu$}

Spectral luminosity is energy radiated per unit time per unit frequency. Its frequency integral is the total <luminosity>. For an isotropic source at small distance $r$, neglecting attenuation and cosmological effects, the <spectral flux density> is $L_\nu/(4\pi r^2)$.

= Luminosity function
{disambiguate=astronomy}
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

= Luminosity function
{synonym}

An astronomical luminosity function gives the number density of objects per luminosity interval, often also per comoving volume. Integrating luminosity times the function gives their luminosity density.

= Astronomical magnitude
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

Astronomical magnitude is a logarithmic measure of the brightness of an astronomical source; a smaller magnitude denotes a brighter source.

= Apparent magnitude
{title2=$m$}
{parent=Astronomical magnitude}
{wiki}

Apparent magnitude measures how bright an astronomical source appears to an observer.

= Magnitude zero point
{parent=Apparent magnitude}

A magnitude zero point fixes the reference measurement $I_0$ in a specified <photometric passband>. Relative to a zero-magnitude reference, the <apparent magnitude> is $m=-2.5\log_{10}(I/I_0)$. Both measurements must use the same passband and calibration convention.

= Euclidean magnitude-limited source count
{parent=Apparent magnitude}

For identical <luminosities>, uniform spatial density, and no extinction, the <inverse-square law> gives limiting distance $r\propto F_{\min}^{-1/2}$. The number in the enclosed volume is therefore $N\propto F_{\min}^{-3/2}\propto10^{0.6m_{\lim}}$. Increasing the sample by a factor $b$ needs a magnitude-limit increase $(5/3)\log_{10}b$. This relation need not hold with evolving source populations, dust, or cosmological distance effects.

= Photometric system
{parent=Astronomical magnitude}
{wiki}

A photometric system specifies <photometric passbands> and <magnitude zero points>, allowing calibrated brightness comparisons.

= UBV photometric system
{c}
{parent=Photometric system}
{wiki}

= Photometric passband
{parent=Photometric system}

A photometric passband is the wavelength-dependent response used for a brightness measurement. An energy-weighted measurement is $I=\int\eta_\lambda F_\lambda\,d\lambda$, with <spectral flux density> $F_\lambda$. Photon-counting conventions can instead include a factor $\lambda/(hc)$; the calibration must use the same convention as the measurement.

= Infrared photometric band
{parent=Photometric passband}

The traditional near- and thermal-infrared photometric bands in the 1–5 micrometre region are $J,H,K,L,M$, with approximate centers $1.25,1.65,2.2,3.5,4.8$ micrometres. Variants such as $K_s$, $L'$ and $M'$ have different detailed responses. They largely follow <atmospheric windows>; transmission, <sky brightness>, and detector response determine practical band edges. The https://www.eso.org/instruments/isaac/doc/VLT-MAN-ESO-14100-0841_v76.pdf[ISAAC filter documentation] records instrument-specific variants, including an $L$ filter closer to $L'$ near 3.8 micrometres.

= Absolute magnitude
{title2=$M$}
{parent=Astronomical magnitude}
{wiki}

Absolute magnitude is the <apparent magnitude> that an astronomical source would have at a conventional reference distance. It separates intrinsic luminosity from distance-dependent dimming.

= Standard candle
{parent=Absolute magnitude}

A standard candle is an astronomical source whose calibrated <absolute magnitude> or <luminosity> allows its <apparent magnitude> or flux to determine a distance. A population with unknown common absolute magnitude only determines relative distances without an external luminosity calibration. <Type Ia supernova cosmology> models this distinction explicitly.

= Distance modulus
{title2=$\mu$}
{parent=Astronomical magnitude}
{wiki}

The distance modulus is the difference between <apparent magnitude> and <absolute magnitude>. For distance $d$ in parsecs,
$$
\mu=m-M=5\log_{10}(d/10\,\mathrm{pc}).
$$

= Exoplanet
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

An exoplanet is a planet orbiting a star other than the Sun.

= Super-Earth
{parent=Exoplanet}
{wiki}

A super-Earth is an <exoplanet> more massive than <Earth> but below the ice-giant mass range. The label does not guarantee a rocky <exoplanet interior>, a specific <radius>, or habitability. Radius-based usage can overlap the sub-Neptune population.

= HD 149026 b
{c}
{parent=Exoplanet}
{wiki}

= Hot Neptune
{parent=Exoplanet}
{wiki}

= Hot Jupiter
{parent=Exoplanet}
{wiki}

= HD 209458 b
{c}
{parent=Hot Jupiter}
{wiki}

= HD 189733 b
{c}
{parent=Hot Jupiter}
{wiki}

= Circumbinary planet
{parent=Exoplanet}
{wiki}

A circumbinary planet orbits both members of a <binary star> rather than just one. If its orbital radius greatly exceeds the binary separation, the stellar contributions to irradiation add approximately as luminosities at a common distance.

= Equilibrium temperature of a circumbinary planet
{title2=$T_{\rm eq}$}
{parent=Circumbinary planet}

For <blackbody> stars of radii $R_A,R_B$ and <temperatures> $T_A,T_B$, a distant <circumbinary planet> with <Bond albedo> $A_B$ and uniform global reradiation has <planetary equilibrium temperature>
$$
T_{\rm eq}^4=\frac{1-A_B}{4a^2}(R_A^2T_A^4+R_B^2T_B^4).
$$
This neglects internal cooling, eccentricity, and corrections from finite binary separation. The stars heat the planet through the sum of their absorbed bolometric fluxes, not an average of their <temperatures>.

= Exoplanet atmosphere
{parent=Exoplanet}
{wiki}

An exoplanet atmosphere controls the planet's spectrum, heat redistribution, chemistry, clouds, and atmospheric escape.

= Secondary planetary atmosphere
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}

A secondary planetary atmosphere is supplied or replenished after the original nebular-envelope stage, through outgassing, volatile delivery and processing. The present atmospheres of Earth, Venus and Mars are predominantly secondary. The distinction from a <primary planetary atmosphere> concerns origin, not merely current molecular weight.

= Primary planetary atmosphere
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}

A primary planetary atmosphere is a retained gas envelope acquired from the protoplanetary nebula, commonly rich in <molecular hydrogen> and <helium>. The solar-system giant planets retain such envelopes, while escape and subsequent processing can substantially change their composition.

= Atmospheric pressure-temperature profile
{title2=$T(P)$}
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}

= Planetary troposphere
{parent=Atmospheric pressure-temperature profile}

The lower part of a planetary atmosphere where temperature generally decreases outward and <convection> can carry intrinsic or surface heat. Its upper boundary is a <tropopause>. Stellar irradiation, composition gradients and clouds affect the extent of this region; pressure alone does not universally identify it.

= Tropopause
{parent=Planetary troposphere}

The transition above a <planetary troposphere>, usually near a temperature minimum or the change to a weakly varying or outward-increasing temperature profile. The overlying <planetary stratosphere> is stable against ordinary thermal <convection>. Its pressure is planet- and location-dependent.

= Square-root exponential atmospheric profile
{parent=Atmospheric pressure-temperature profile}
{title2=$P=P_0e^{\alpha\sqrt{T-T_0}}$}

For $P_0>0$, $\alpha\ne0$, $T\ge T_0$ and physical $T>0$, the <atmospheric pressure-temperature profile> $P=P_0\exp(\alpha\sqrt{T-T_0})$ is equivalent to
$$
T=T_0+\frac{\log^2(P/P_0)}{\alpha^2},\qquad \frac{\log(P/P_0)}{\alpha}\ge0.
$$
The branch restriction is essential: squaring does not create a second physical branch. Its <temperature gradient> is $dT/d\log P=2\sqrt{T-T_0}/\alpha$. Thus, in <hydrostatic equilibrium>, $\alpha<0$ gives an <atmospheric thermal inversion>, while $\alpha>0$ gives outward cooling. At $T=T_0$ the inverse branch has zero <derivative>; $\alpha=0$ gives constant <pressure> and cannot describe a nontrivial hydrostatic vertical interval.

= Convective instability of a square-root exponential atmospheric profile
{parent=Square-root exponential atmospheric profile}

For a homogeneous <ideal gas> of constant <specific-heat ratio> $\gamma>1$, the <Schwarzschild criterion> applied to the <square-root exponential atmospheric profile> gives
$$
\nabla=\frac{d\log T}{d\log P}=\frac{2\sqrt{T-T_0}}{\alpha T}>\nabla_{\rm ad}=\frac{\gamma-1}{\gamma}.
$$
For $T_0>0$ and $\alpha>0$, put $s=\sqrt{T-T_0}$ and $A=\nabla_{\rm ad}$. The <quadratic inequality> $A\alpha s^2-2s+A\alpha T_0<0$ has solutions precisely when $A\alpha\sqrt{T_0}<1$, between $s_\pm=[1\pm\sqrt{1-(A\alpha)^2T_0}]/(A\alpha)$. This is a local instability of the imposed <temperature gradient>; actual <convection> adjusts that gradient and the prescribed profile need not survive.

= Lapse rate
{title2=$\Gamma$}
{disambiguate=atmosphere}
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}
{wiki}

= Atmospheric lapse rate
{synonym}

The atmospheric lapse rate is the decrease of temperature with altitude, $\Gamma=-dT/dz$. In an ideal gas with constant gravity, a constant lapse rate produces a power-law relation between temperature and pressure.

= Irradiated planetary atmosphere
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}

An irradiated planetary atmosphere receives stellar energy from above while internal heat emerges from below. Their competition creates radiative, convective, and sometimes thermally inverted regions.

= Greenhouse effect
{parent=Irradiated planetary atmosphere}
{wiki}

An atmosphere that absorbs and reemits thermal radiation can reduce outgoing energy at a given surface temperature, requiring a warmer surface to balance absorbed stellar energy. The <planetary equilibrium temperature> refers to the radiation budget, not the resulting surface temperature. <Water>, <carbon dioxide> and clouds can alter this balance.

= Greenhouse gas
{parent=Greenhouse effect}
{wiki}

A greenhouse gas absorbs and emits planetary thermal radiation and therefore contributes to the <greenhouse effect>. Its climatic impact depends on absorption spectrum, concentration, lifetime and chemical effects. <Carbon dioxide>, <methane> and water vapour are important examples.

= Global warming potential
{parent=Greenhouse gas}
{title2=$\operatorname{GWP}_H$}
{wiki}

Global warming potential compares the time-integrated radiative forcing of equal-mass emission pulses of a <greenhouse gas> and <carbon dioxide>, over a stated time horizon $H$. It is not an instantaneous temperature ratio, and its numerical value depends on the horizon and included chemical and climatic responses.

= Single-layer greenhouse model
{parent=Irradiated planetary atmosphere}

A single-layer greenhouse model represents an atmosphere by one uniform layer that absorbs and emits a fraction of thermal radiation. With infrared emissivity $\epsilon$, the layer emits $\epsilon\sigma T_a^4$ both upward and downward.

= Planetary equilibrium temperature
{title2=$T_{\rm eq}$}
{parent=Irradiated planetary atmosphere}

For a planet of <Bond albedo> $A_B$ orbiting a <blackbody> star of radius $R_*$ and temperature $T_*$, complete global redistribution and negligible internal heat give
$$
T_{\rm eq}^4=(1-A_B)T_*^4\frac{R_*^2}{4a^2}.
$$
Uniform dayside-only reradiation replaces $4a^2$ by $2a^2$. The <planetary equilibrium temperature> describes absorbed stellar power; including an intrinsic cooling flux gives a total <effective temperature> $T_{\rm eff}^4=T_{\rm int}^4+T_{\rm eq}^4$, distinct from $T_{\rm eq}$. Neither quantity is necessarily the greenhouse-warmed surface temperature.

= Semi-grey irradiated atmosphere
{parent=Irradiated planetary atmosphere}

A semi-grey atmosphere uses separate mean visible and infrared opacities. Their ratio $\gamma=\kappa_{\rm vis}/\kappa_{\rm IR}$ controls where stellar energy is deposited.

= Irradiation temperature
{title2=$T_{\rm irr}$}
{parent=Semi-grey irradiated atmosphere}

The irradiation temperature is defined from the incident stellar flux, commonly by $T_{\rm irr}=T_*\sqrt{R_*/a}$ before redistribution and albedo factors are applied.

= Internal effective temperature of a planet
{title2=$T_{\rm int}$}
{parent=Semi-grey irradiated atmosphere}

The internal effective temperature parametrizes the intrinsic cooling luminosity of a planet through $F_{\rm int}=\sigma T_{\rm int}^4$.

= Atmospheric thermal inversion
{parent=Semi-grey irradiated atmosphere}
{wiki=Inversion_(meteorology)}

= Thermal inversion
{synonym}

= Temperature inversion
{synonym}

An atmospheric thermal inversion is a region where temperature increases outward. In a strongly irradiated atmosphere it can arise when shortwave opacity exceeds thermal infrared opacity and stellar energy is absorbed high in the atmosphere.

= Planetary stratosphere
{parent=Atmospheric thermal inversion}

An upper atmospheric region above the <tropopause> in which shortwave heating can produce an <atmospheric thermal inversion>. <Ozone> on <Earth>, and <methane> and aerosols on giant planets, are examples of absorbers that help heat it. Its positive altitude <temperature gradient> makes it stable against homogeneous-gas thermal <convection>.

= Inversion criterion with intrinsic planetary flux
{parent=Atmospheric thermal inversion}

For the usual <semi-grey irradiated atmosphere> with downward thermal <optical depth> $\tau$, an inversion occurs where $f_rT_{\rm irr}^4(\gamma^2-1)e^{-\sqrt3\gamma\tau}>T_{\rm int}^4$, with $\gamma$ the visible-to-thermal <opacity> ratio. At negligible intrinsic flux the threshold reduces to $\gamma>1$.

= Spectral-line emission from an atmospheric thermal inversion
{parent=Atmospheric thermal inversion}

A strong-opacity line forms higher than the nearby continuum. Under the Eddington-Barbier approximation, an outward-decreasing temperature therefore makes an absorption line, while an <atmospheric thermal inversion> makes the line-forming layer hotter and reverses it into emission.

= Radiative-convective boundary
{title2=RCB}
{parent=Irradiated planetary atmosphere}
{wiki}

The radiative-convective boundary is where radiative transport first requires a temperature gradient steeper than the adiabatic gradient, causing convection below.

= Radiative-convective equilibrium model
{parent=Radiative-convective boundary}

This model solves a vertical <atmospheric pressure-temperature profile> consistent with heating, <radiative transfer> and a convective adjustment where the radiative gradient exceeds the <adiabatic temperature gradient>. Irradiation, intrinsic flux, gravity and <opacity> determine the <radiative-convective boundary>. A one-dimensional model describes a column or average rather than resolving all atmospheric winds.

= Convective stability of a constant-opacity irradiated atmosphere
{parent=Radiative-convective boundary}

A constant radiative-diffusion coefficient and constant gravity give $T^4=T_0^4+4AP/g$. Thus $\nabla_{\rm rad}=AP/(gT^4)<1/4$ for $T_0>0$. A diatomic <ideal gas> has <adiabatic temperature gradient> $2/7$, so this idealized profile never becomes convective. A physical <radiative-convective boundary> requires additional <opacity>, flux, or thermodynamic variation.

= Radiative temperature gradient
{title2=$\nabla_{\rm rad}$}
{parent=Radiative-convective boundary}

The radiative temperature gradient is the logarithmic pressure gradient $\nabla_{\rm rad}=d\log T/d\log P$ required to carry the flux by radiation. Convection begins when it exceeds the relevant adiabatic gradient.

= Adiabatic temperature gradient
{title2=$\nabla_{\rm ad}$}
{parent=Radiative-convective boundary}

The adiabatic temperature gradient is $\nabla_{\rm ad}=(\partial\log T/\partial\log P)_s$. For a perfect gas with constant adiabatic index, $\nabla_{\rm ad}=(\gamma-1)/\gamma$.

= Dry adiabatic lapse rate
{parent=Adiabatic temperature gradient}
{title2=$\Gamma_{\rm dry}=g/C_p$}

For a homogeneous dry <ideal gas> in a hydrostatic adiabat, $dT/dz=-g/C_p$, where $z$ increases upwards and $C_p$ is the <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> per unit mass. It follows from $dT/dP=1/(\rho C_p)$ and <hydrostatic equilibrium>. The altitude version of the <Schwarzschild criterion> is $dT/dz<-g/C_p$ for instability; equality is marginal. Moisture, composition gradients and nonideal equations of state modify the criterion.

= Adiabatic parcel gradient in a hydrostatic environment
{parent=Dry adiabatic lapse rate}
{title2=$dT_g/dz=-(g/C_p)T_g/T_{\rm env}$}

A dry parcel in rapid pressure balance with an ambient <ideal gas> has $dT_g/dP=T_g\mathcal R/(PC_p)$. Ambient <hydrostatic equilibrium> gives $dP/dz=-Pg/(\mathcal RT_{\rm env})$, hence $dT_g/dz=-(g/C_p)T_g/T_{\rm env}$. At a temperature-matched launch point this equals the <dry adiabatic lapse rate>, supplying the local linear stability test. A substantially hotter parcel does not exactly follow the ambient density's hydrostatic adiabat.

= Exoplanet secondary eclipse
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}
{wiki=Methods_of_detecting_exoplanets#Secondary_eclipse}

During secondary eclipse the planet passes behind its star. The lost system light measures the planet-star flux ratio from reflected starlight and planetary thermal emission.

= Geometric albedo
{title2=$A_g$}
{parent=Exoplanet secondary eclipse}
{wiki}

Geometric albedo compares a body's full-phase brightness with that of a flat, perfectly diffusing reference disc of the same cross-section.

= Reflected-light planetary phase curve
{parent=Geometric albedo}
{title2=$F_p/F_*=A_g(R_p/a)^2\Phi(\alpha)$}

The orbital variation of a planet's reflected stellar light as the visible illuminated area changes. In a simple spherical model, $F_p/F_*=A_g(R_p/a)^2\Phi(\alpha)$, where $\Phi(0)=1$ and $\alpha$ is the star-planet-observer angle. <Exoplanet clouds> can alter the <geometric albedo> and the angular scattering law; this observable differs from an <exoplanet thermal phase curve>.

= Planetary phase integral
{title2=$q=2\int_0^\pi\Phi(\alpha)\sin\alpha\,d\alpha$}
{parent=Geometric albedo}

The phase integral converts a monochromatic <geometric albedo> into the corresponding reflected power fraction. The bolometric <Bond albedo> is the incident-spectrum-weighted average of $q_\lambda A_{g,\lambda}$.

= Bond albedo
{title2=$A_B$}
{c}
{parent=Geometric albedo}
{wiki}

Bond albedo is the fraction of total incident power reflected in all directions and over all wavelengths.

= Lambertian surface
{c}
{parent=Geometric albedo}
{wiki=Lambert's_cosine_law}

A Lambertian surface has direction-independent radiance. A perfectly reflecting Lambertian patch receiving normal flux $F$ emits specific intensity $I=F/\pi$ into its outward hemisphere.

= Thermal eclipse depth
{parent=Exoplanet secondary eclipse}

For <blackbody> star and planet, define the planet-star contrast
$$
r_\lambda=\frac{F_{p,\lambda}}{F_{*,\lambda}}=\left(\frac{R_p}{R_*}\right)^2\frac{B_\lambda(T_p)}{B_\lambda(T_*)}.
$$
A complete <exoplanet secondary eclipse> removes the planet's light from the combined out-of-eclipse light. Its exact normalized depth is $d_\lambda=r_\lambda/(1+r_\lambda)$. The common approximation $d_\lambda\simeq r_\lambda$ requires $r_\lambda\ll1$. This thermal model omits reflected light and spatial variations.

= Monotonicity of blackbody planet-star contrast
{parent=Thermal eclipse depth}
{title2=$dr_\lambda/d\lambda>0\quad(T_p<T_s)$}

For <blackbodies> with $T_p<T_s$, the contrast $r_\lambda=(R_p/R_s)^2(e^{x_s}-1)/(e^{x_p}-1)$ increases with wavelength, where $x_i=hc/(\lambda k_BT_i)$. Indeed $d\log r/d\log\lambda=G(x_p)-G(x_s)>0$, with $G(x)=x/(1-e^{-x})$ increasing for $x>0$ because $1-(1+x)e^{-x}>0$. The contrast approaches $(R_p/R_s)^2T_p/T_s$ in the <Rayleigh-Jeans law> limit. This behavior differs from the peaked absolute <blackbody> flux.

= Planet-star radius estimate in the Rayleigh-Jeans limit
{parent=Thermal eclipse depth}

For <blackbody> star and planet observed at one common <wavelength> sufficiently long for the <Rayleigh-Jeans law> to hold for both,
$$
f_\lambda=\frac{F_{p,\lambda}}{F_{*,\lambda}}=\left(\frac{R_p}{R_*}\right)^2\frac{T_p}{T_*},\qquad R_p=R_*\sqrt{f_\lambda T_*/T_p}.
$$
This follows by substituting $B_\lambda=2ck_BT/\lambda^4$ in the <thermal eclipse depth>. A <radiative flux> ratio alone does not determine the <radius> without a planetary <brightness temperature> and a stellar <temperature>. Spectral peaks interpreted with <Wien's displacement law> must refer to $F_\lambda$, not $F_\nu$ or $\lambda F_\lambda$.

= Transit spectroscopy
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}

= Exoplanet transmission spectroscopy
{synonym}

<Transit spectroscopy> infers the properties of an <exoplanet atmosphere> from the wavelength dependence of an <exoplanet transit>. Its measured product is an <exoplanet transmission spectrum>, while an <annulus model for transmission spectroscopy> connects the spectrum to atmospheric <optical depth>. The observing technique is described by https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/how-we-find-and-characterize/[NASA].

= Exoplanet transmission spectrum
{parent=Transit spectroscopy}

= Exoplanet transmission spectra
{synonym}

An exoplanet transmission spectrum measures the wavelength-dependent area blocked as starlight passes tangentially through the atmosphere during transit.

= Annulus model for transmission spectroscopy
{parent=Exoplanet transmission spectrum}

Take a planet with opaque radius $R_p$ and an atmosphere of thickness $z\ll R_p$, projected against a uniform stellar disc of radius $R_\star$. If the atmospheric annulus has one representative slant <optical depth> $\tau_\lambda$, its additional transit depth is
$$
\delta_\lambda=A(1-e^{-\tau_\lambda}),
\qquad
A=\frac{(R_p+z)^2-R_p^2}{R_\star^2}\simeq\frac{2R_pz}{R_\star^2}.
$$
The general <exoplanet transmission spectrum> instead uses an integral over impact parameter $b$,
$$
\delta_\lambda=\frac2{R_\star^2}\int_{R_p}^{\infty}b[1-e^{-\tau_\lambda(b)}]\,db.
$$
The annulus model replaces this varying slant opacity by an effective constant. The atmospheric signal scales with optical depth when <optically thin> and saturates at the annular area when opaque.

= Two-zone cloudy annulus transmission model
{parent=Annulus model for transmission spectroscopy}

An <exoplanet cloud deck> divides the projected atmospheric annulus into cloudy and clear radial zones. Their contributions to the <exoplanet transmission spectrum> add as projected areas multiplied by $1-e^{-\tau_\lambda}$ for each zone's slant <optical depth>.

= Atmospheric scale height
{title2=$H$}
{parent=Exoplanet transmission spectrum}
{wiki=Scale_height}

For an isothermal ideal-gas atmosphere, $H=k_BT/(\mu m_Hg)$. A strong transmission feature spanning $N_H$ scale heights has approximate transit-depth amplitude $2R_pN_HH/R_*^2$.

= Slant optical depth of an isothermal atmosphere
{parent=Atmospheric scale height}

For an isothermal thin atmosphere with <number density> $n(z)=n_0e^{-z/H}$ and extinction cross-section $\sigma_\lambda$, a tangent ray near radius $R_p+z$ has height $z+\ell^2/(2R_p)$ at distance $\ell$ along the ray. The resulting <Gaussian integral> gives slant <optical depth>
$$
\tau_\lambda(z)\simeq\sigma_\lambda n(z)\sqrt{2\pi R_pH}.
$$
The effective transit height is near $\tau_\lambda\sim1$, so $z(\lambda)=H\log\sigma_\lambda+\mathrm{constant}$ if composition is constant. This explains the <scattering slope of a transmission spectrum>.

= Isothermal pressure-level transit radius
{parent=Atmospheric scale height}

If an isothermal thin atmosphere has surface radius $R_p$, surface pressure $P_0$, and nearly constant gravity, the radius of a pressure level $P$ is
$$
R(P)=R_p+H\log\frac{P_0}{P},
\qquad
H=\frac{k_BT}{\mu m_Hg}.
$$

= Mass of a thin hydrostatic atmosphere
{parent=Atmospheric scale height}

The column mass between pressures $P_0$ and $P_{\rm top}$ is $(P_0-P_{\rm top})/g$. A thin atmosphere over a spherical planet therefore has
$$
M_{\rm atm}\simeq\frac{4\pi R_p^2(P_0-P_{\rm top})}{g}
\simeq\frac{4\pi R_p^4P_0}{GM_p}
$$
when its top pressure is negligible.

= Scattering slope of a transmission spectrum
{parent=Exoplanet transmission spectrum}

If extinction scales as $\sigma\propto\lambda^\gamma$, an isothermal hydrostatic atmosphere has $dR_p/d\log\lambda=\gamma H$. The measured continuum slope can therefore estimate atmospheric temperature when gravity and mean molecular mass are known.

= Logarithmic slope of total transit depth
{parent=Scattering slope of a transmission spectrum}

For $\delta=A+B(1-e^{-\tau})$ and $\tau\propto\lambda^{-p}$, the logarithmic slope is $-pB\tau e^{-\tau}/\delta$. The opaque planetary baseline generally dilutes the slope. In the <optically thin> limit, the atmospheric excess $\delta-A$ has logarithmic slope $-p$.

= Day-night terminator
{parent=Exoplanet transmission spectrum}

The day-night terminator is the boundary between the illuminated and dark hemispheres of a planet. During transit, stellar rays probe morning and evening limbs that can have different temperatures, clouds, and compositions.

= Two-limb transmission spectrum
{parent=Day-night terminator}

If the morning and evening limbs have effective radii $R_m(\lambda)$ and $R_e(\lambda)$ and each occupies half the annulus, the transit area is $\pi[R_m^2(\lambda)+R_e^2(\lambda)]/2$.

= Exoplanet emission spectrum
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}
{wiki}

An exoplanet emission spectrum is the wavelength-dependent thermal radiation emerging from the planet. Vertical temperature gradients turn opacity differences into absorption or emission features.

= Brightness temperature
{title2=$T_b$}
{parent=Exoplanet emission spectrum}
{wiki}

The brightness temperature at a frequency or wavelength is the temperature for which a blackbody has the measured specific intensity there: $I_\nu=B_\nu(T_b)$. It need not equal a physical temperature shared by the entire emitting object.

= Emission-pressure degeneracy of a brightness temperature
{parent=Brightness temperature}

A <brightness temperature> equates a measured intensity with a <Planck function>; it does not determine its contribution pressure. In <hydrostatic equilibrium>, $\tau_\lambda(P)=\int_0^P\kappa_\lambda\,dP'/g$. Estimating $P\sim g/\kappa_\lambda$ therefore needs gravity and <opacity>, as well as a model of the contribution function. Different pressures can produce the same band brightness.

= Exoplanet cloud
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}

An exoplanet cloud is a suspended condensate aerosol that scatters or absorbs radiation. Its particle sizes, composition, vertical extent, and horizontal coverage shape transmission, reflection, and thermal-emission spectra.

= Atmospheric condensate rainout
{parent=Exoplanet cloud}

Condensation followed by gravitational settling removes chemical elements from an atmospheric layer. The remaining gas may be in local <chemical equilibrium> with a depleted elemental inventory, while the column is not a closed equilibrium system.

= Atmospheric cold trap
{parent=Atmospheric condensate rainout}

A sufficiently cold layer condenses a volatile or refractory carrier and limits its transport to higher layers. <Water> depletion above the terrestrial tropopause and titanium-bearing condensates in some <hot Jupiter> models illustrate this process.

= Exoplanet cloud deck
{parent=Exoplanet cloud}

An exoplanet cloud deck is an optically thick cloud layer whose top pressure fixes the deepest level visible over a wavelength interval. A high deck can truncate a transmission spectrum and mute molecular features.

= Cloud-top pressure in an isothermal atmosphere
{title2=$P_c=P_s e^{-H_c/h}$}
{parent=Exoplanet cloud deck}

With constant gravity and <mean molecular weight>, <hydrostatic equilibrium> gives $P_c=P_s\exp(-H_c/h)$, where $h$ is the <atmospheric scale height>. A flat <exoplanet transmission spectrum> alone does not determine $H_c$ or a unique cloud-top <pressure>.

= Cloud pressure degeneracy of a transmission feature
{parent=Cloud-top pressure in an isothermal atmosphere}

With constant <atmospheric scale height> $H$, a molecular band at height $\Delta z$ above the measured continuum satisfies $P_{\rm band}=P_{\rm cont}e^{-\Delta z/H}$. If an opaque <exoplanet cloud deck> sets that continuum, then $P_c\simeq P_{\rm cont}$; the band <pressure> is not the cloud <pressure>. If the clear band height would be $NH$ above a clear continuum at $P_0$ and the cloud reduces the visible feature to $mH$, then
$$
P_c=P_0e^{-(N-m)}.
$$
Without the clear-atmosphere band opacity or $N$, the measured feature amplitude alone cannot locate the cloud top. This follows directly by subtracting the two hydrostatic height relations.

= Atmospheric haze
{parent=Exoplanet cloud}
{wiki=Haze}

Atmospheric haze consists of small suspended particles, often produced photochemically. A haze can flatten molecular features or produce a wavelength-dependent scattering slope.

= Exoplanet atmospheric dynamics
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}

Exoplanet atmospheric dynamics includes day-night circulation, zonal jets, waves, vortices, storms, and heat redistribution under irradiation, rotation, drag, and magnetic effects.

= Day-night heat redistribution
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric dynamics}

Day-night heat redistribution transports absorbed stellar energy from an irradiated hemisphere to a dark hemisphere through atmospheric or oceanic motion. Its efficiency controls the thermal phase-curve amplitude and nightside temperature of a synchronously rotating planet.

= Stellar flux redistribution factor
{title2=$f_r$}
{parent=Day-night heat redistribution}

This factor converts substellar incident flux into the representative flux used in an atmospheric model. Uniform global averaging gives $f_r=1/4$; dayside averaging gives $1/2$ if albedo is treated separately. The convention must be stated when defining an <irradiation temperature>.

= Nightside equilibrium temperature
{title2=$T_n$}
{parent=Day-night heat redistribution}

If a fraction $\epsilon$ of total absorbed stellar power reaches the night side, blackbody balance gives
$$
T_n=T_*\sqrt{\frac{R_*}{a}}
\left[\frac{\epsilon(1-A_B)}2\right]^{1/4}.
$$

= Radiative relaxation time in a planetary atmosphere
{title2=$\tau_{\rm rad}$}
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric dynamics}

A column near <pressure> $p$ contains mass per area of order $p/g$. Linearizing its <blackbody> cooling around <temperature> $T$ gives the estimate
$$
\tau_{\rm rad}\sim\frac{c_pp}{4g\sigma_{\rm SB}T^3},
$$
where $c_p$ is <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>. This estimate omits details of wavelength-dependent <radiative transfer>, but captures faster relaxation in hotter and lower-pressure atmospheric layers.

= Atmospheric advection time
{title2=$\tau_{\rm adv}$}
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric dynamics}

The atmospheric advection time over length $L$ at wind speed $U$ is $\tau_{\rm adv}\sim L/U$. <Day-night heat redistribution> tends to be efficient when this time and the relevant wave-adjustment time are shorter than the <radiative relaxation time in a planetary atmosphere>.

= Exoplanet thermal phase curve
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric dynamics}

An exoplanet thermal phase curve measures infrared brightness throughout the orbit. Its amplitude constrains <day-night heat redistribution>, while its maximum's displacement from secondary eclipse constrains the offset of the hottest atmospheric region. Wavelength-dependent <opacity> makes different channels probe different pressures.

= Atmospheric superrotation
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric dynamics}
{wiki=Super-rotation}

Atmospheric superrotation is an eastward equatorial jet whose angular momentum per unit mass exceeds that of the underlying solid body at the equator. Hot-Jupiter waves can transport angular momentum into the equatorial region and maintain such a jet.

= Disequilibrium chemistry in an exoplanet atmosphere
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}

Atmospheric chemistry is out of equilibrium when reaction times exceed transport or photochemical times. Vertical quenching, ultraviolet photochemistry, and atmospheric escape can then maintain abundances far from local thermochemical equilibrium.

= Atmospheric photochemistry
{parent=Disequilibrium chemistry in an exoplanet atmosphere}

Atmospheric photochemistry begins when a molecule absorbs a photon and dissociates or ionizes. The products can drive reaction networks whose steady abundances differ strongly from thermochemical equilibrium.

= Photochemical steady state
{parent=Atmospheric photochemistry}

A photochemical steady state has production and loss rates balanced for each short-lived species even though individual photochemical reactions continue.

= Chapman ozone equilibrium
{c}
{parent=Atmospheric photochemistry}

The Chapman ozone mechanism combines oxygen photodissociation, three-body ozone formation, ozone photodissociation, and the reaction between atomic oxygen and ozone. Under local steady state and dominant photolytic ozone loss,
$$
[{\rm O}_3]\simeq[{\rm O}_2]
\left(\frac{K_2J_1[{\rm M}]}{K_4J_3}\right)^{1/2}.
$$

= Ozone layer
{parent=Chapman ozone equilibrium}
{wiki}

An ozone layer is an atmospheric altitude range with enhanced ozone abundance. Oxygen-dissociating ultraviolet radiation is strongest aloft while three-body formation is fastest at greater density, so their competition can concentrate ozone at intermediate altitude.

= Eddy mixing time
{title2=$\tau_{\rm mix}\simeq H^2/K_{zz}$}
{parent=Disequilibrium chemistry in an exoplanet atmosphere}

The eddy mixing time over one atmospheric scale height is $\tau_{\rm mix}\simeq H^2/K_{zz}$, where $K_{zz}$ is the vertical eddy diffusion coefficient.

= Vertical eddy diffusion coefficient
{title2=$K_{zz}$}
{parent=Eddy mixing time}

The vertical eddy diffusion coefficient parametrizes turbulent transport in an atmosphere by a diffusive flux proportional to an abundance gradient. Over distance $L$, the corresponding <eddy mixing time> is $L^2/K_{zz}$. Its units are area per time, so $1\,\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}=10^4\,\mathrm{cm^2\,s^{-1}}$.

= Chemical quench level
{parent=Disequilibrium chemistry in an exoplanet atmosphere}

A chemical quench level is where a reaction time equals the atmospheric transport time. Above it, transport is faster and carries an approximately fixed deeper abundance faster than local chemistry can restore equilibrium.

= Dependence of a quench diffusivity on mixing length
{title2=$K_{zz}\gtrsim\ell^2/\tau_{\rm chem}$}
{parent=Chemical quench level}

For a vertical <eddy diffusivity> $K_{zz}$, mixing over a length $\ell$ takes $\tau_{\rm mix}\sim\ell^2/K_{zz}$. The condition that transport outrun a <chemical relaxation time> therefore gives $K_{zz}\gtrsim\ell^2/\tau_{\rm chem}$. Taking $\ell=0.1H$ instead of one <atmospheric scale height> $H$ changes this estimate by a factor of one hundred. The <atmospheric scale height> itself depends on both temperature and gravity.

= Horizontal chemical quenching
{parent=Chemical quench level}

<Atmospheric advection time> shorter than the <chemical relaxation time> allows winds to carry a composition into regions where it differs from local <thermochemical equilibrium>. In a <hot Jupiter>, this can transport dayside carbon chemistry into the cooler nightside.

= Quenched atmospheric mixing ratio
{title2=$f_A=n_A/n$}
{parent=Chemical quench level}

Above a <chemical quench level>, rapid eddy mixing can keep a species' number mixing ratio $f_A$ approximately fixed at its deeper value, provided condensation, photochemical loss, and molecular diffusion remain negligible. The species' <number density> is then $n_A=f_Ap/(k_BT)$; constant mixing ratio does not imply constant <number density>. In an isothermal atmosphere, $n_A$ decreases exponentially with altitude on the <atmospheric scale height>.

= Carbon monoxide–methane quenching
{title2=$\mathrm{CO}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{CH}_4$ quenching}
{parent=Chemical quench level}

Carbon monoxide–methane quenching freezes the CO-to-CH4 abundance ratio near the level where their interconversion time first exceeds the vertical mixing time.

= Nitrogen–ammonia quenching
{title2=$\mathrm{N}_2\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{NH}_3$ quenching}
{parent=Chemical quench level}

Nitrogen–ammonia quenching similarly preserves a deeper N2-to-NH3 ratio above the level where chemical conversion becomes slower than atmospheric mixing.

= Atmospheric metallicity of a giant planet
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}

Atmospheric metallicity measures enrichment in elements heavier than hydrogen and helium relative to the host star. Solar-System giant planets suggest decreasing enrichment with increasing planetary mass, although clouds, condensation, and formation history complicate comparisons.

= Atmospheric carbon-to-oxygen ratio
{title2=$\mathrm{C/O}$}
{parent=Atmospheric metallicity of a giant planet}

The atmospheric carbon-to-oxygen ratio compares the elemental abundances of carbon and oxygen in the atmosphere. It influences molecular composition under <chemical equilibrium>, but condensation, <disequilibrium chemistry in an exoplanet atmosphere>, and incomplete spectral coverage complicate its inference from molecular bands.

= Carbon partition and atmospheric water abundance
{parent=Atmospheric carbon-to-oxygen ratio}

In hot hydrogen-rich <thermochemical equilibrium>, much carbon can be bound in <carbon monoxide>, consuming oxygen that is unavailable for <water>. At a C/O ratio near or above one, water can be strongly depleted. Cooler <methane>-dominated chemistry can release more oxygen for water at the same elemental inventory. Pressure, quenching, dissociation and <atmospheric photochemistry> can modify this temperature-dependent partition.

= Exoplanet atmospheric observatory
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}

An exoplanet atmospheric observatory characterizes planetary spectra through transit, eclipse, phase-curve, high-dispersion, or direct-imaging observations.

= Atmospheric retrieval
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric observatory}

An atmospheric retrieval fits a parametrized <radiative transfer> model to an <exoplanet transmission spectrum> or <exoplanet emission spectrum>. Temperature, abundances, clouds and reference radius/pressure can trade off against one another. Posterior precision within one model is not proof that its structural assumptions are unique.

= Spitzer Space Telescope
{c}
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric observatory}
{wiki}

The Spitzer Space Telescope provided infrared observations of <exoplanet secondary eclipses> and <exoplanet thermal phase curves>. Its original mission included infrared spectroscopy; its warm mission retained the 3.6 and 4.5 micrometre imaging bands.

= Hubble Space Telescope
{title2=HST}
{c}
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric observatory}
{wiki}

The Hubble Space Telescope provides ultraviolet, visible, and near-infrared transit and eclipse spectroscopy and established many early detections of atoms, aerosols, and atmospheric escape.

= James Webb Space Telescope
{title2=JWST}
{c}
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric observatory}
{wiki}

The James Webb Space Telescope combines a 6.5-metre aperture with stable near- and mid-infrared spectroscopy. Its instruments measure transit, eclipse, and phase-curve spectra across multiple molecular bands.

= Near Infrared Imager and Slitless Spectrograph
{c}
{parent=James Webb Space Telescope}

= NIRISS
{c}
{synonym}

The NIRISS single-object slitless spectroscopy mode measures time-series spectra from $0.6$ to $2.8\,\mu\mathrm m$. It supports <exoplanet transmission spectra> and eclipse observations, as described in https://jwst-docs.stsci.edu/jwst-near-infrared-imager-and-slitless-spectrograph/niriss-observing-modes/niriss-single-object-slitless-spectroscopy[the instrument guide].

= Near Infrared Spectrograph
{c}
{parent=James Webb Space Telescope}
{wiki=NIRSpec}

= NIRSpec
{c}
{synonym}

NIRSpec provides multiple spectroscopic modes within $0.6$–$5.3\,\mu\mathrm m$. Its time-series modes can measure <exoplanet transmission spectra> and <exoplanet secondary eclipses>; individual settings have narrower coverage. See https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/nirspec/[NASA's instrument overview].

= Mid-Infrared Instrument
{c}
{parent=James Webb Space Telescope}
{wiki}

= MIRI
{c}
{synonym}

MIRI's low-resolution spectroscopy includes a slitless time-series mode for <exoplanet> observations. Its useful coverage includes the $5$–$12\,\mu\mathrm m$ region, with longer-wavelength limits dependent on calibration and sensitivity. See https://jwst-docs.stsci.edu/jwst-mid-infrared-instrument/miri-observing-modes/miri-low-resolution-spectroscopy[the instrument guide].

= Ariel space telescope
{title2=Ariel}
{c}
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric observatory}

Ariel is an ESA mission designed to survey the visible and infrared atmospheres of about one thousand planets through transit and eclipse spectroscopy.

= Extremely Large Telescope
{title2=ELT}
{c}
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric observatory}
{wiki}

The Extremely Large Telescope combines a 39-metre-class aperture, adaptive optics, direct imaging, and high-dispersion spectroscopy to characterize giant and terrestrial exoplanets.

= Very Large Telescope
{c}
{title2=VLT}
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric observatory}
{wiki}

= CRIRES
{c}
{parent=Very Large Telescope}
{wiki}

= CRIRES+
{c}
{synonym}

CRIRES is a high-resolution infrared spectrograph. Its upgraded form supports molecular spectroscopy of <exoplanet atmospheres> and measurements based on the <Doppler effect>. See https://www.eso.org/public/teles-instr/paranal-observatory/vlt/vlt-instr/crires%2B/[ESO's instrument overview].

= Exoplanet direct imaging
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric observatory}

= Directly imaged exoplanet
{synonym}

Exoplanet direct imaging spatially separates planetary light from host-star light. It is most sensitive to young, hot, self-luminous giant planets on wide orbits around nearby stars.

= Exoplanet transit photometry
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric observatory}

Exoplanet transit photometry measures the stellar flux decrement as an <exoplanet> crosses the visible stellar disc. After accounting for stellar intensity variation and transit geometry, its depth is approximately $(R_p/R_\star)^2$, determining a radius ratio rather than an absolute planetary radius.

= Transit-timing variation
{title2=TTV}
{wiki}
{parent=Exoplanet transit photometry}

A departure of transit arrival times from a constant-period ephemeris can reveal gravitational interactions with other bodies. Dynamical fits may constrain an <exoplanet> mass independently of transit depth, providing a mass to combine with radius for <exoplanet interior> models.

= Near-resonant transit-timing superperiod
{parent=Transit-timing variation}
{title2=$P_t=2\pi/|(j+1)n_2-jn_1|$}

For two nearly circular <Kepler orbits> near a first-order $(j+1):j$ period ratio, the slow <resonant argument> circulates on the displayed superperiod when <longitude of periapsis> precession is negligible. If $P_2/P_1=((j+1)/j)(1+\Delta)$, then $P_t=P_1(1+\Delta)/(j|\Delta|)$. Within <resonant-argument libration>, this circulation formula is replaced by an amplitude-dependent libration period.

= Eccentricity-enhanced near-resonant transit-timing variation
{parent=Near-resonant transit-timing superperiod}
{title2=$|A_t|/P_t\simeq3\mu_2\alpha|f|e/(2\pi|\Delta|)$}

A first-order <resonant orbital perturbation> changes the <semi-major axis> and therefore the <mean motion>. Integrating the sinusoidal mean-motion variation over the <near-resonant transit-timing superperiod> gives a mean-longitude amplitude proportional to $\mu_2e/\Delta^2$. Converting phase to transit time gives the displayed amplitude ratio. It assumes small perturbations, circulating <resonant argument>, effectively fixed free <orbital eccentricity>, and dominance of this enhanced term over direct phase and forced-eccentricity contributions. The formal divergence as $\Delta\to0$ signals failure of this expansion, not infinitely large transit times.

= Simultaneous transit in a resonant chain
{parent=Exoplanet transit photometry}

= Simultaneous transits in a resonant chain
{synonym}

In leading mean-conjunction geometry a common observer phase $x$ for two <resonant arguments> requires $qx=\phi_1$ and $nx=\phi_2$ modulo $2\pi$. With $d=\gcd(q,n)$, the exact modular criterion is
$$
\frac{n\phi_1-q\phi_2}{d}\in2\pi\mathbb Z.
$$
Necessity follows by subtracting the two congruences after multiplication; sufficiency follows from the integer linear combinations of $q$ and $n$, whose set is $d\mathbb Z$. For exterior centres $\pi$ and interior centres $0,\pi,0$ at orders one to three, only $q=n=2$ satisfies it. This is phase compatibility, not a guarantee of an observed <exoplanet transit>: timing, observer orientation, <resonant-argument libration> widths and true-versus-mean longitude must also be considered.

= Transit dimming by an optically thin orbital wire
{parent=Exoplanet transit photometry}
{title2=$\Delta F/F_\star$}

For an <edge-on orbit> crossing the centre of a uniformly bright stellar disk at distance $r\gg R_\star$, projected speed is $h/r$ and chord time is $2R_\star r/h$. A steady <cross-sectional-area current> $J_\sigma=2fh$ therefore places area $4fR_\star r$ in front of the <star>, giving
$$
\frac{\Delta F}{F_\star}=\frac4\pi\frac r{R_\star}f.
$$
This requires a geometrically narrow, <optically thin> wire with negligible overlap. A noncentral chord, <limb darkening>, finite angular extent and large <optical depth> change the estimate; it cannot predict dimming greater than one.

= Exoplanet transit
{parent=Exoplanet transit photometry}

An <exoplanet> passes in front of its host <star> as seen by an observer and blocks part of the stellar flux. <Exoplanet transit photometry> infers geometry and radii from the resulting light curve. A true alignment involves the <true anomaly>, not generally the <mean anomaly>.

= Transit impact parameter
{parent=Exoplanet transit}
{title2=$b$}

The minimum projected planet-star separation during an <exoplanet transit>, divided by the stellar radius. It determines the stellar chord length and hence the <transit duration of a small planet>.

= Transit duration of a small planet
{parent=Exoplanet transit}
{title2=$D\simeq PR_\star\sqrt{1-b^2}/(\pi a)$}

For a small <planet> on a nearly circular <Kepler orbit>, a stellar chord at normalized <transit impact parameter> $b$ has length $2R_\star\sqrt{1-b^2}$. Dividing by transverse orbital speed $2\pi a/P$ gives the duration. A central transit has the longest duration within this approximation. Eliminating $a$ with <Kepler's third law> yields $P/D^3\ge\pi^2G\rho_\star/3$ for known mean stellar <mass density>. Finite planet radius, appreciable <orbital eccentricity>, and $R_\star/a$ corrections change the bound.

= Hill-cloud transit mass estimate
{c}
{parent=Transit duration of a small planet}
{title2=$M_p/M_\star=3(\pi D/(\eta P))^3$}

For an opaque or detectably extinguishing spherical debris cloud of radius $R_c=\eta R_H$ much larger than the stellar radius, a central <exoplanet transit> lasts approximately $2R_c/(na)$. Combining this with the <Hill radius> gives the displayed mass estimate. For cloud impact distance $s$, replace $R_c$ by $\sqrt{R_c^2-s^2}$: the central formula then gives a lower mass estimate unless the chord geometry is known.

= Doppler spectroscopy
{c}
{parent=Exoplanet atmospheric observatory}
{wiki}

= Radial-velocity method
{synonym}

The radial-velocity method detects an <exoplanet> through the star's orbital <Doppler shift>. It measures a projected planetary mass $M_p\sin i$, not the planetary radius. Combining it with <exoplanet transit photometry> determines mass and radius when the stellar properties are known.

= Atmospheric escape
{parent=Exoplanet atmosphere}
{wiki}

Atmospheric escape removes gas from a planet through thermal motion, hydrodynamic outflow, photochemistry, and interactions with stellar radiation and plasma.

= Nonthermal atmospheric escape
{parent=Atmospheric escape}

Nonthermal atmospheric escape supplies particle energy through processes other than the local thermal distribution. Examples include dissociation producing fast atoms, ion pickup by a stellar-wind electric field, and sputtering caused by impacting energetic particles. <Radiation pressure> can also accelerate material in an extended <exosphere>.

= Jeans escape
{c}
{parent=Atmospheric escape}

Jeans escape is collisionless <atmospheric escape> by particles in the high-speed tail of a thermal distribution at the <exobase>. The <Jeans escape parameter> $\lambda=GMm/(k_BTr_e)$ controls the exponential suppression, and the <Jeans escape flux> is proportional to $(1+\lambda)e^{-\lambda}$. There is no exact positive-temperature threshold below which the tail vanishes. For $\lambda$ of order a few or smaller, a purely hydrostatic tail model can fail and <hydrodynamic atmospheric escape> becomes important.

= Exosphere
{parent=Atmospheric escape}
{wiki}

The exosphere is the dilute outer atmospheric region in which a particle can travel a substantial distance without a <collision>. Its lower boundary, the <exobase>, is near equality of the <mean free path> and the local <atmospheric scale height>. Escaping and gravitationally bound trajectories can coexist.

= Exobase
{parent=Exosphere}
{title2=$\ell\sim H$}

The exobase marks the transition between a collisional atmosphere and its <exosphere>. For a neutral, approximately hydrostatic <ideal gas>, take <mean free path> $\ell\simeq1/(n\sigma_c)$ and <atmospheric scale height> $H=k_BT/(mg)$. The criterion $\ell\sim H$ gives
$$
n_e\sim\frac1{\sigma_c H},\qquad P_e\sim\frac{mg}{\sigma_c}.
$$
An order-one collision convention can change this coefficient. The <pressure> is not universal: the <collision cross-section>, composition, local gravity, and ionization must be specified. A strongly escaping, non-hydrostatic atmosphere requires an appropriate density-gradient length instead of a hydrostatic $H$.

= Jeans escape parameter
{title2=$\lambda$}
{c}
{parent=Atmospheric escape}

For particles of mass $m$ at radius $r$ and temperature $T$, the Jeans escape parameter is
$$
\lambda=\frac{GM_pm}{k_BTr}.
$$
Large $\lambda$ gives weak tail escape, while order-unity $\lambda$ indicates hydrodynamic blow-off.

= Jeans escape flux
{c}
{parent=Jeans escape parameter}

For exobase number density $n_e$ and $v_{\rm th}=\sqrt{2k_BT/m}$, the collisionless Jeans number flux is
$$
\Phi_J=\frac{n_ev_{\rm th}}{2\sqrt\pi}(1+\lambda)e^{-\lambda}.
$$

= Hydrodynamic atmospheric escape
{parent=Atmospheric escape}
{wiki=Hydrodynamic_escape}

= Hydrodynamic escape
{synonym}

Hydrodynamic atmospheric escape is a collisional transonic outflow in which bulk gas carries multiple species away. It replaces the hydrostatic Jeans-tail description when gravity is weak compared with thermal energy.

= Energy-limited atmospheric escape
{title2=$\dot M=\eta\pi R_{\rm EUV}^2F_{\rm EUV}R_p/(GM_p)$}
{parent=Hydrodynamic atmospheric escape}

Energy-limited atmospheric escape equates a fraction $\eta$ of absorbed high-energy stellar power to the gravitational work needed to remove gas. Ignoring tidal and cooling corrections gives $\dot M=\eta\pi R_{\rm EUV}^2F_{\rm EUV}R_p/(GM_p)$.

= Exoplanet habitability
{parent=Exoplanet}
{wiki}

Exoplanet habitability concerns whether planetary conditions could sustain liquid water and life, while recognizing that observability and habitability are distinct questions.

= Circumstellar habitable zone
{title2=HZ}
{parent=Exoplanet habitability}
{wiki}

= Habitable zone
{synonym}

The circumstellar habitable zone is the orbital range in which a specified planet and atmosphere can maintain surface liquid water. Its boundaries depend on stellar spectrum, albedo, greenhouse physics, clouds, planetary mass, and atmospheric inventory.

= Runaway greenhouse
{parent=Circumstellar habitable zone}
{wiki}

A runaway greenhouse occurs when outgoing longwave radiation reaches a limiting value while absorbed stellar energy is larger, causing ocean evaporation and rapid surface warming.

= Maximum greenhouse outer habitable-zone limit
{parent=Circumstellar habitable zone}

At the maximum-greenhouse limit, adding carbon dioxide no longer warms efficiently because condensation and Rayleigh scattering offset its greenhouse effect. This defines a common conservative outer habitable-zone boundary.

= Hycean planet
{c}
{parent=Exoplanet habitability}
{wiki}

A Hycean planet is a proposed sub-Neptune with a hydrogen-rich atmosphere above a liquid-water ocean. The category is a model hypothesis rather than evidence of habitation.

= Exoplanet biosignature
{parent=Exoplanet habitability}
{wiki=Biosignature}

An exoplanet biosignature is an observable gas, surface feature, temporal variation, or chemical disequilibrium whose biological explanation survives plausible abiotic alternatives and environmental context.

= Atmospheric biosignature gas
{parent=Exoplanet biosignature}

An atmospheric gas or gas combination whose abundance and environmental context can support a biological interpretation. Detectability, a plausible production flux and exclusion of significant abiotic alternatives matter together. Modern-Earth <molecular oxygen> and <nitrous oxide> have predominantly biological sources, but an <oxygen biosignature false positive> illustrates why extrapolation to another planet requires context.

= Oxygen biosignature false positive
{parent=Atmospheric biosignature gas}

Nonbiological <molecular oxygen> can accumulate when photochemistry and planetary redox sinks allow it, for example after water photolysis and hydrogen loss. <Ozone> produced from that oxygen is not independent proof of a biological source. The stellar spectrum, water inventory, atmospheric escape and other gases constrain these interpretations.

= Atmospheric spectral-feature amplitude
{parent=Exoplanet habitability}

For transmission through $N_H$ scale heights, a useful estimate is
$$
\Delta D\simeq\frac{2R_pN_HH}{R_*^2}.
$$
Small host stars, low gravity, high temperature, and low mean molecular mass make atmospheric features larger.

= Temperature from a transmission-band amplitude
{parent=Atmospheric spectral-feature amplitude}
{title2=$T\simeq\frac{\mu m_ug\,\Delta D R_\star^2}{2k_BR_pN_H}$}

For a thin hydrostatic <isothermal atmosphere>, $\Delta D\simeq2R_pN_HH/R_\star^2$ and $H=k_BT/(\mu m_ug)$. Hence $T\simeq\mu m_ug\Delta D R_\star^2/(2k_BR_pN_H)$. A measured <exoplanet transmission spectrum> amplitude constrains $N_HH$, so gravity, mean molecular mass and the number $N_H$ of sampled <atmospheric scale heights> are additional inputs. In a clear fixed-composition model, $N_H\simeq\log(\kappa_{\rm band}/\kappa_{\rm cont})$; clouds or abundance gradients can change it.

= Exoplanet interior
{parent=Exoplanet}

An exoplanet interior is inferred from mass, radius, age, irradiation, and atmospheric composition. Distinct mixtures of metal, rock, water, and hydrogen-helium can share the same bulk mass and radius.

= Incompressible planetary interior
{parent=Exoplanet interior}
{title2=$\rho(r)=\rho_0$}

An idealized self-gravitating interior with constant <mass density>. Negligible surface pressure and spherical <hydrostatic equilibrium> give $M=4\pi\rho R^3/3$ and the <uniform-density planetary pressure profile>. It is the $n=0$ <polytrope> interpretation, rather than a literal substitution into $P=K\rho^{1+1/n}$. It approximates weak compression and misses real compositional stratification.

= Uniform-density planetary pressure profile
{parent=Incompressible planetary interior}
{title2=$P(r)=P_c(1-r^2/R^2),\quad P_c=3g_s^2/(8\pi G)$}

For a spherical <incompressible planetary interior>, $M(r)=4\pi\rho r^3/3$ gives $dP/dr=-4\pi G\rho^2r/3$. With $P(R)=0$, $P(r)=2\pi G\rho^2(R^2-r^2)/3$. The surface gravity $g_s=4\pi G\rho R/3$ then yields $P_c=3g_s^2/(8\pi G)$. Add an imposed surface pressure to the entire profile if needed.

= Hydrostatic lower bound on planetary central pressure
{title2=$P_c\ge GM^2/(8\pi R^4)$}
{parent=Exoplanet interior}

The <hydrostatic pressure support equation> in enclosed-mass coordinate is $dP/dm=-Gm/(4\pi r^4)$. Since $r(m)\le R$, integration from surface pressure zero to the centre gives
$$
P_c=\int_0^M\frac{Gm}{4\pi r(m)^4}\,dm\ge\frac{GM^2}{8\pi R^4}.
$$
If <mass density> decreases outward, the mean density within $r$ is at least the global mean, so $r(m)\le R(m/M)^{1/3}$. This improves the bound to
$$
P_c\ge\frac{3GM^2}{8\pi R^4},
$$
with equality for uniform <mass density>. Centrally concentrated planets need larger central <pressure> to support their inner mass.

= Two-layer constant-density planet
{parent=Exoplanet interior}

A two-layer constant-density planet has a uniform core density $\rho_1$ and uniform mantle density $\rho_2$. Hydrostatic pressure follows by integrating $dP/dr=-G\rho(r)M(r)/r^2$ separately in each layer and matching pressure at their boundary.

= Exoplanet interior-composition degeneracy
{parent=Exoplanet interior}

The interior-composition degeneracy is the failure of mass and radius alone to determine unique layer mass fractions. Atmospheric mean molecular mass and composition, stellar abundances, tidal response, and thermal evolution add independent constraints.

= Mass-radius relation
{parent=Exoplanet interior}
{wiki}

A mass-radius relation compares the characteristic radius of a class of self-gravitating objects with mass. Its form records the equation of state and composition.

= Mass-radius curve of solar-composition substellar objects
{parent=Mass-radius relation}

For a specified age and irradiation, a <hydrogen>–<helium> sequence rises at low mass, has radii near Jupiter's across giant planets and <brown dwarfs>, and declines as <electron degeneracy pressure> becomes important. Above the <hydrogen-burning minimum mass>, sustained fusion supports a rising low-mass stellar branch.

= Planetary mass-radius relation
{parent=Mass-radius relation}

= Planetary mass-radius curve
{synonym}

A planetary mass-radius relation compares the sizes of bodies at a given mass. Rock compression, volatile layers, gaseous envelopes, thermal entropy, irradiation, and electron degeneracy produce distinct branches and substantial composition degeneracy.

= Transit-radius contribution of a gaseous envelope
{parent=Planetary mass-radius relation}

A low-<mean molecular weight> hydrogen-helium envelope can add a large <radius> while adding a small fraction of the <mass>. The <exoplanet transit photometry> radius is the altitude where tangential <optical depth> becomes order unity, so <temperature>, <opacity>, and <exoplanet clouds> can move the measured radius above the deep material radius. Atmospheric loss and cooling change this contribution over time.

= Hot-Jupiter radius inflation
{parent=Planetary mass-radius relation}

Hot-Jupiter radius inflation is the anomalously large radius of many strongly irradiated gas giants relative to isolated cooling models. Delayed cooling and energy deposition by tides, atmospheric circulation, or electrical currents are leading contributions.

= Delayed cooling of an inflated giant planet
{parent=Hot-Jupiter radius inflation}

Delayed cooling preserves a young giant planet's large radius by reducing the escape of internal <energy>. Enhanced atmospheric <opacity>, an irradiation-maintained outer radiative blanket, and composition gradients that inhibit <convection> can slow <Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction>. These mechanisms retain existing heat rather than supplying a new deep heat source.

= Layered convection in a giant planet
{parent=Delayed cooling of an inflated giant planet}

A stabilizing composition gradient can suppress large overturning motions while permitting <double-diffusive convection> in small layers separated by interfaces. If heat loss is less efficient than in a single well-mixed adiabat, the planet retains more entropy and contracts more slowly. This is a proposed delayed-cooling contribution to <hot-Jupiter radius inflation>, with an effectiveness that depends on the gradient and layer structure.

= Heating of an inflated giant planet
{parent=Hot-Jupiter radius inflation}

A giant planet can also remain inflated when power is deposited sufficiently deep to alter its internal energy budget. Candidate sources include <tidal heating>, <Joule heating> by wind-driven currents, and dissipation of circulation-driven mechanical energy. The depth and efficiency of deposition matter, as discussed in https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.02087[models of hot-Jupiter heating].

= Ohmic heating of a giant planet
{c}
{title2=$q=J^2/\sigma$}
{parent=Heating of an inflated giant planet}

Atmospheric winds across a magnetic field can induce electric currents. Their <Ohmic heating> converts mechanical energy into heat, at local rate $q=\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E=J^2/\sigma$ in the simplest resistive description. To maintain <hot-Jupiter radius inflation>, current paths and deposited heat must influence the deep interior; dissipation restricted to optically thin upper layers need not maintain the planet's entropy.

= Exoplanet bulk-composition ternary diagram
{parent=Exoplanet}

A bulk-composition ternary diagram represents the mass fractions of three planetary components at a point whose barycentric coordinates sum to one.

= Radiative transfer
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

Radiative transfer describes emission, absorption, and scattering of radiation through matter.

= Constant flux in a plane-parallel radiative-equilibrium atmosphere
{parent=Radiative transfer}

Taking the first angular moment of the radiative-transfer equation gives $dF_\nu/dz=4\pi\alpha_\nu(S_\nu-J_\nu)$. Radiative equilibrium sets the frequency integral of the right-hand side to zero, so the bolometric flux is constant with depth.

= Planck law
{c}
{parent=Radiative transfer}
{wiki=Planck's_law}

The Planck spectral radiance of a blackbody is
$$
B_\lambda(T)=\frac{2hc^2}{\lambda^5}
\frac1{e^{hc/(\lambda k_BT)}-1}.
$$

= Planck function
{title2=$B_\nu(T)$}
{c}
{parent=Planck law}

The Planck function is the equilibrium spectral specific intensity of a blackbody. It can be written per unit frequency or per unit wavelength, with $B_\nu\,d\nu=-B_\lambda\,d\lambda$ under the corresponding change of variable.

= Radiation constant
{title2=$a_{\rm rad}$}
{parent=Planck law}
{wiki}

The radiation constant relates equilibrium radiation energy density to temperature by $u=a_{\rm rad}T^4$. It satisfies $a_{\rm rad}=4\sigma/c$ in terms of the Stefan-Boltzmann constant.

= Rayleigh-Jeans law
{c}
{parent=Planck law}
{wiki}

The Rayleigh-Jeans law is the long-wavelength, low-frequency limit of <Planck law>. When $hc/(\lambda k_BT)\ll1$,
$$
B_\lambda(T)\simeq\frac{2ck_BT}{\lambda^4}.
$$

= Wien approximation
{c}
{parent=Planck law}
{wiki=Wien_approximation}

= Wien limit
{c}
{synonym}

The Wien approximation is the short-wavelength limit of the Planck law:
$$
B_\lambda(T)\simeq\frac{2hc^2}{\lambda^5}
e^{-hc/(\lambda k_BT)}.
$$

= Wien's displacement law
{c}
{parent=Planck law}
{wiki}

= Wien displacement law
{c}
{synonym}

For a <blackbody>, the wavelength at which $B_\lambda(T)$ is largest obeys
$$
\lambda_{\max}T=b,
$$
where $b$ is <Wien displacement constant>.

= Wien displacement constant
{c}
{title2=$b$}
{parent=Wien's displacement law}
{wiki}

The <Wien displacement constant> is the <constant of proportionality> $b$ in $\lambda_{\max}T=b$.

= Blackbody
{parent=Radiative transfer}
{wiki}

A <blackbody> absorbs all incident <electromagnetic radiation>. In <thermal equilibrium> it emits the universal <Planck law> spectrum determined solely by its <temperature>.

= Luminosity of a spherical blackbody
{parent=Blackbody}

An isotropic blackbody surface has outward flux $F=\pi\int B_\nu\,d\nu=\sigma T^4$. A sphere of radius $R$ therefore has luminosity
$$
L=4\pi R^2\sigma T^4.
$$

= Thermal bremsstrahlung
{parent=Radiative transfer}

= Thermal free-free emission
{synonym}

Thermal bremsstrahlung is free-free electromagnetic radiation from the acceleration of charged particles during Coulomb encounters in a thermal plasma. Its frequency-integrated emissivity in a fully ionized, nonrelativistic plasma scales approximately as $n_en_iT^{1/2}$.

= Gaunt factor
{parent=Thermal bremsstrahlung}
{c}
{title2=$g_{\rm ff}$}
{wiki}

The <Gaunt factor> is the dimensionless quantum correction to a classical free-free radiation calculation. Its weak temperature and frequency dependence modifies the approximate <thermal bremsstrahlung> scaling; a frequency-averaged value appears in the bolometric cooling coefficient.

= Specific intensity
{title2=$I_\nu$}
{parent=Radiative transfer}
{wiki}

Specific intensity is power per projected area, solid angle, and frequency interval. In vacuum it is conserved along a ray.

= Vacuum conservation of specific intensity
{parent=Specific intensity}

In a transparent, stationary Euclidean vacuum, the <radiative transfer equation> has zero absorption and emission, so $dI_\nu/ds=0$. Equivalently, two surface elements exchange beam power $I_\nu\cos\theta_1\cos\theta_2\,dA_1dA_2d\nu/s^2$; reversing the description of the same beam cancels its geometric factors. The observed <radiative flux> of an unresolved source still falls as its apparent <solid angle>, proportional to $s^{-2}$. Across gravitational or cosmological frequency shifts the invariant is $I_\nu/\nu^3$, not generally $I_\nu$.

= Limb darkening
{parent=Specific intensity}
{wiki}

Limb darkening is the decrease of emergent <specific intensity> toward the apparent edge of a stellar disc. A transit depth then depends on the intensity sampled by the planet as well as its projected area; the uniform-disc area ratio is only an approximation.

= Optical depth
{title2=$\tau_\nu$}
{parent=Radiative transfer}
{wiki}

Optical depth satisfies $d\tau_\nu=\alpha_\nu ds$ along a path and measures attenuation through $I_\nu\propto e^{-\tau_\nu}$ in the absence of emission.

= Optically thick medium
{parent=Optical depth}

= Optically thick
{synonym}

An optically thick medium has <optical depth> $\tau\gg1$ along the relevant path. Photons undergo many interactions before escaping. <Radiative diffusion> applies under suitable near-isotropy conditions, but a large scattering depth alone does not guarantee thermalization when true absorption is weak.

= Optically thin medium
{parent=Optical depth}

= Optically thin
{synonym}

An optically thin medium has $\tau\ll1$, so attenuation and emission may be expanded to first order in optical depth.

= Absorption coefficient
{title2=$\alpha_\nu$}
{parent=Optical depth}
{wiki}

The absorption coefficient is the fractional reduction of beam intensity per unit path length: $dI_\nu/ds=-\alpha_\nu I_\nu$ when emission and scattering are absent.

= Collision-induced absorption and emission
{wiki}
{parent=Absorption coefficient}

= Collision-induced absorption
{synonym}

A collision or transient molecular pair can possess a dipole even when the isolated molecules do not. The resulting <opacity> can be important in a dense <molecular hydrogen> atmosphere. Pair absorption per unit length scales approximately as the product of the two partner number densities, so its <mass absorption coefficient> is generally density-dependent. It supplies continuum absorption between ordinary spectral lines.

= Dust optical depth
{title2=$\tau_d$}
{parent=Optical depth}

For dust-grain number density $n_d$ and absorption cross-section $\sigma_d$, the dust optical depth along a path is $\tau_d=\int n_d\sigma_d\,ds$.

= Mass absorption coefficient
{title2=$\kappa_\nu$}
{parent=Dust optical depth}
{wiki}

The mass absorption coefficient is absorbing cross-section per unit mass. For identical spherical dust grains of radius $a$, mass $m_d$, and absorption efficiency $Q_\nu$, it is $\kappa_\nu=\pi a^2Q_\nu/m_d$.

= Optically thin dust-mass estimator
{title2=$M_d=F_\nu D^2/[\kappa_\nu B_\nu(T)]$}
{parent=Mass absorption coefficient}

For isothermal <optically thin> dust at distance $D$, its measured spectral flux density determines its mass through $M_d=F_\nu D^2/[\kappa_\nu B_\nu(T)]$. Temperature and opacity uncertainty commonly dominate the inferred mass uncertainty.

= Radiative transfer equation
{parent=Radiative transfer}
{wiki}

In plane-parallel optical-depth coordinates without scattering,
$$
\mu\frac{dI_\nu}{d\tau_\nu}=I_\nu-S_\nu,
$$
with source function $S_\nu$.

= Radiative transfer source function
{parent=Radiative transfer equation}
{title2=$S_\nu$}

= Source function
{synonym}

The <radiative transfer> <source function> is emission per unit extinction. If $j_\nu$ is emission per unit path length and $\rho\kappa_\nu$ is extinction per unit path length, $S_\nu=j_\nu/(\rho\kappa_\nu)$ and the transfer equation is $\mu\partial_{\tau_\nu}I_\nu=I_\nu-S_\nu$. Under thermal absorption in <local thermodynamic equilibrium> it is the <Planck function>; for conservative <coherent isotropic scattering> it is the <mean intensity>.

= Radiative diffusion
{parent=Radiative transfer equation}

In an optically thick medium whose radiation field is nearly isotropic and thermal, the first angular correction to the Planck function produces the diffusive flux
$$
F=-\frac{16\sigma T^3}{3\kappa_R\rho}\nabla T,
$$
where $\kappa_R$ is the Rosseland mean opacity.

= Rosseland mean opacity
{title2=$\kappa_R$}
{c}
{parent=Radiative diffusion}
{wiki=Rosseland_mean_opacity}

The Rosseland mean opacity is the harmonic frequency average weighted by $\partial B_\nu/\partial T$. It controls radiative diffusion because flux preferentially travels through low-opacity frequencies.

= Local thermodynamic equilibrium
{title2=LTE}
{parent=Radiative transfer equation}
{wiki}

= LTE
{c}
{synonym}

In local thermodynamic equilibrium, matter populations are thermal at the local temperature and the source function is the Planck function, $S_\nu=B_\nu(T)$.

= Formal solution of the radiative transfer equation
{parent=Radiative transfer equation}

For an outward ray through a nonscattering atmosphere,
$$
I_\nu(0,\mu)=I_\nu(\tau_b,\mu)e^{-\tau_b/\mu}
+\int_0^{\tau_b}B_\nu[T(t)]e^{-t/\mu}\frac{dt}{\mu}.
$$

= Constant source transfer through a thin layer
{parent=Formal solution of the radiative transfer equation}
{title2=$I_{\rm out}=I_{\rm in}e^{-\tau}+S(1-e^{-\tau})$}

Along a radial ray through a layer with constant <source function>, the incident <specific intensity> is attenuated by $e^{-\tau}$ and the layer adds $S(1-e^{-\tau})$. At small <optical depth>, $I_{\rm out}=I_{\rm in}+(S-I_{\rm in})\tau+O(\tau^2)$. Whether the source exceeds the incident <specific intensity> determines the first-order emission or absorption contrast.

= Polynomial source function moments for emergent intensity
{parent=Formal solution of the radiative transfer equation}
{title2=$A_j=j!a_j$}

= Emergent-intensity polynomial coefficients
{synonym}

For a semi-infinite plane-parallel atmosphere with polynomial <source function> $S(\tau)=\sum a_j\tau^j$, the outward <specific intensity> is $\int_0^\infty S(\tau)e^{-\tau/\mu}\,d\tau/\mu$. Substitution $u=\tau/\mu$ gives coefficients $A_j=j!a_j$ in the surface polynomial in $\mu$. The deep boundary term must vanish. A local truncated Taylor series instead gives this contribution plus a remainder, so its surface polynomial is not automatically exact.

= Blackbody limit of an isothermal atmosphere
{parent=Formal solution of the radiative transfer equation}
{title2=$I_\lambda\longrightarrow B_\lambda(T)$}

For a thermalized <radiative transfer source function> $S_\lambda=B_\lambda(T)$ constant along a ray, the <specific intensity> after optical-depth interval $\Delta\tau$ is $I_\lambda=B_\lambda+(I_{\rm in}-B_\lambda)e^{-\Delta\tau/\mu}$ for $\mu>0$. As $\Delta\tau\to\infty$, it tends to the <Planck function>, independently of a bounded boundary input. A temperature gradient is unnecessary for <blackbody> emission. In a scattering medium, <local thermodynamic equilibrium> alone does not guarantee $S_\lambda=B_\lambda$; thermalization must be justified.

= One-layer spectral contrast
{parent=Formal solution of the radiative transfer equation}

A non-scattering isothermal atmospheric layer in <local thermodynamic equilibrium>, of <temperature> $T_a$ and slant <optical depth> $t_\lambda$, lies above a <blackbody> background at $T_b$. The <specific intensity> is
$$
I_\lambda=B_\lambda(T_b)e^{-t_\lambda}+B_\lambda(T_a)(1-e^{-t_\lambda}).
$$
Thus an increase in <opacity> shifts the <specific intensity> toward the layer's <Planck function>. If a line is more opaque than its continuum, it appears in absorption for $T_a<T_b$, in emission for $T_a>T_b$, and has no contrast for $T_a=T_b$. This is a local explanation, not a claim that all features diagnose one uniform layer.

= Eddington-Barbier relation
{c}
{parent=Formal solution of the radiative transfer equation}
{wiki=Eddington–Barbier_relation}

When the source function varies approximately linearly with optical depth, the emergent intensity obeys $I_\nu(0,\mu)\simeq S_\nu(\tau_\nu=\mu)$.

= Coherent isotropic scattering
{parent=Radiative transfer equation}

Coherent isotropic scattering preserves photon frequency and redistributes direction uniformly. Its source function is the mean intensity $J_\nu$, so conservative scattering removes light from a pencil beam while preserving luminosity integrated over all escaping directions.

= Single-scattering albedo
{title2=$\omega_\nu$}
{parent=Coherent isotropic scattering}
{wiki}

The single-scattering albedo is the fraction of total extinction due to scattering, $\omega_\nu=\alpha_{\nu,\rm sca}/\alpha_{\nu,\rm ext}$. Its complement is the absorptive fraction.

= Kirchhoff's law of thermal radiation
{c}
{parent=Radiative transfer equation}
{wiki}

Kirchhoff's law of thermal radiation states that a body in thermal equilibrium has equal absorptivity and emissivity at each frequency and direction. A layer with infrared absorptivity $\epsilon$ therefore emits $\epsilon B_\nu(T)$.

= Radiation-field moment
{parent=Radiative transfer}

For plane-parallel intensity, the standard moments are
$$
J_\nu=\frac12\int_{-1}^1I_\nu d\mu,
\quad
H_\nu=\frac12\int_{-1}^1\mu I_\nu d\mu,
\quad
K_\nu=\frac12\int_{-1}^1\mu^2I_\nu d\mu,
$$
and the radiative flux is $F_\nu=4\pi H_\nu$.

= Mean intensity
{title2=$J_\nu$}
{parent=Radiation-field moment}

The mean intensity is the angular average $J_\nu=(4\pi)^{-1}\int I_\nu d\Omega$ of the <specific intensity>.

= Radiative flux
{title2=$F_\nu$}
{parent=Radiation-field moment}

Radiative flux is the first angular moment $F_\nu=\int I_\nu\cos\theta,d\Omega$ and measures net radiant power crossing unit area.

= Observed flux of an unresolved spherical emitter
{parent=Radiative flux}

For a uniformly emitting spherical body of <radius> $R$ at <Euclidean distance> $d\gg R$, integration of its <specific intensity> over the apparent <solid angle> gives $f_\lambda=\pi I_\lambda(R/d)^2$. For a <blackbody>, $I_\lambda=B_\lambda(T)$ and the bolometric result is $f=\sigma_{\rm SB}T^4(R/d)^2=L/(4\pi d^2)$. A grey <emissivity> factor multiplies this expression; isothermality alone does not imply blackbody emission.

= Spectral flux density
{parent=Radiative flux}
{title2=$F_\lambda,\ F_\nu$}

Spectral flux density is radiant power received per unit area and per unit wavelength or frequency. The two conventions satisfy $F_\lambda\,d\lambda=-F_\nu\,d\nu$ under $\nu=c/\lambda$, hence $F_\lambda=(c/\lambda^2)F_\nu$ when the arguments refer to the same photons. Integrating over a <photometric passband> gives its measured flux.

= Eddington closure approximation
{c}
{parent=Radiation-field moment}

The Eddington closure approximation sets the second angular moment to one third of the zeroth, so the radiation pressure and mean intensity satisfy $cP_r=4\pi J/3$.

= Positivity limitation of a linear Eddington intensity
{parent=Eddington closure approximation}
{title2=$I(0,-1)=-F/(4\pi)$}

For the affine angular ansatz $I=A+C\cos\theta$, the <radiative flux> is $F=4\pi C/3$. Setting the inward hemispheric flux to zero gives $A(0)=2C/3$, but then the formal intensity at $\cos\theta=-1$ is negative when $F>0$. Exact no-incoming radiation and nonnegative intensity cannot both be represented by a nonzero affine polynomial on all directions. The <Eddington surface boundary condition> therefore specifies approximate moments, rather than an exact emergent angular radiation field.

= Grey atmosphere
{parent=Radiative transfer}
{wiki}

A grey atmosphere has frequency-independent opacity. In plane-parallel radiative equilibrium under the Eddington closure, its temperature obeys
$$
T^4=\frac34T_{\rm eff}^4\left(\tau+\frac23\right).
$$

= Obstruction to a single smooth exponential mode in conservative grey transfer
{parent=Grey atmosphere}

Consider $I=\alpha[\beta+\tau+\mu+\gamma e^{-\delta\tau}/(1+\epsilon\mu)]$ with real $|\epsilon|<1$, $\alpha\gamma\ne0$, and $\delta>0$. Its angular mean includes $A_0=\operatorname{atanh}\epsilon/\epsilon$. Substitution into $\mu I_\tau=I-J$ requires $1-A_0+(\delta-A_0\epsilon)\mu=0$. Thus $A_0=1$ and $\delta=\epsilon$. For real $\epsilon$, $A_0=1$ only at zero, giving $\delta=0$, a contradiction. Independently, its flux contains a varying term proportional to $A_1=(1-A_0)/\epsilon$, which is nonzero for nonzero real $\epsilon$. Exact conservative transfer therefore needs a more general angular/depth structure than this one smooth exponential correction.

= Hopf function of a grey atmosphere
{parent=Grey atmosphere}
{c}
{title2=$q(\tau)$}

The Hopf function encodes the departure of a plane-parallel <grey atmosphere> from a purely linear source profile. With physical bolometric <radiative flux> $F$ and standard <mean intensity> $J$, it is defined by $J=3F[\tau+q(\tau)]/(4\pi)$. In thermal <radiative equilibrium>, $T^4=3T_{\rm eff}^4[\tau+q(\tau)]/4$. The <Eddington closure approximation> with its approximate surface condition gives constant $q=2/3$; exact angular transfer generally gives a depth-dependent function.

= Eddington surface boundary condition
{c}
{parent=Grey atmosphere}
{title2=$P_{\rm rad}(0)=2F/(3c)$}

For no incident radiation and outward intensity approximated as direction independent, the surface angular moments are $F=\pi I_0$ and $P_{\rm rad}(0)=2\pi I_0/(3c)$. Integrating the radiation momentum equation with the <Eddington closure approximation> yields the <grey atmosphere> law $T^4=3T_{\rm eff}^4(\tau+2/3)/4$. This is an approximate angular boundary condition, not isotropy across both hemispheres.

= Plane-parallel atmosphere
{parent=Grey atmosphere}

A plane-parallel atmosphere neglects curvature over its vertical thickness, so thermodynamic variables depend on one Cartesian height coordinate.

= Plane-parallel validity from pressure scale height
{parent=Plane-parallel atmosphere}
{title2=$H/R\ll1$}

A <plane-parallel atmosphere> is geometrically accurate only if the relevant line-forming region is thin relative to the stellar radius. An isothermal estimate gives $H=k_BT/(\mu m_ug_{\rm eff})$. Radiative acceleration reduces the effective gravity and increases extension. A small photospheric $H/R$ does not justify a plane-parallel description of wind lines formed far above that photosphere.

= Line of sight
{parent=Astrophysics}

= Lines of sight
{synonym}

The line of sight is the ray from an observer toward an astronomical source.

= Plane of the sky
{parent=Line of sight}

The plane of the sky is the plane through a source perpendicular to the observer's line of sight.

= Position angle
{title2=PA}
{parent=Plane of the sky}
{wiki}

Position angle specifies a projected direction on the sky, conventionally measured from North toward East.

= Projected separation
{title2=$R_{\rm sky}$}
{parent=Plane of the sky}

Projected separation is the distance between two objects after their relative displacement is projected onto the plane of the sky.

= Planetary science
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

Planetary science studies planets, moons, small bodies, planetary systems, and the processes that form and evolve them.

= Magnetosphere
{parent=Planetary science}
{wiki}

A magnetosphere is the region around a body in which the body's magnetic interaction controls charged-particle motion and its response to the surrounding plasma. A planet with an intrinsic <magnetic field> can support a compressed dayside and an extended magnetotail in the <solar wind>. Magnetospheric currents must be distinguished from the internal field when inferring a <planetary dynamo>.

= Induced magnetosphere
{parent=Magnetosphere}

An induced magnetosphere is maintained by the interaction of an external magnetized plasma flow with a conducting ionosphere or atmosphere. Its currents and draped <magnetic field> do not establish an internal <planetary dynamo>. <Venus> is a central example; <Mars> combines this interaction with localized crustal fields.

= Planetary magnetic field
{parent=Planetary science}

A planetary magnetic field may include an intrinsic field maintained by an internal <planetary dynamo>, local crustal <remanent magnetization>, and externally driven fields associated with the <solar wind>. Separating these components requires spatial and temporal observations. A global internally sourced field, a localized crustal anomaly, and an <induced magnetosphere> are different evidence about the planet's present and past interior.

= Planetary dynamo
{parent=Planetary magnetic field}

A planetary dynamo maintains an intrinsic <magnetic field> by motion of an electrically conducting fluid. The <resistive induction equation> balances stretching and transport against <magnetic diffusion>. A sufficiently large <magnetic Reynolds number> is necessary but does not ensure that the flow regenerates every required field component. Thermal or compositional <convection> supplies mechanical power; rotation organizes the flow through the <Coriolis force>, and the <Lorentz force density> eventually modifies the flow and limits growth. A fluid core can fail to operate a dynamo if it is stably stratified or inadequately driven.

= Geomagnetic reversal
{parent=Planetary dynamo}
{wiki}

A geomagnetic reversal changes the large-scale polarity of Earth's intrinsic <magnetic field>. Crustal <remanent magnetization> records past reversals. The reversal times are not a regular clock; their occurrence reflects time-dependent <dynamo action> rather than rigid rotation of a permanent magnet.

= Asteroid
{parent=Planetary science}
{wiki}

An <asteroid> is a small rocky or metallic body orbiting a <star>. <Asteroid> dynamics can involve <mean-motion resonances>, <planetary scattering> and <secular perturbations>. Solar-system <asteroids> illustrate how phase protection can prevent close encounters even when orbital paths approach a <planet>.

= Astronomical unit
{parent=Planetary science}
{title2=$\mathrm{AU}$}

The astronomical unit is a length scale used for Solar System distances, approximately $1.5\times10^{13}\,\mathrm{cm}$. Coarse estimates sometimes round it to $10^{13}\,\mathrm{cm}$. Numerical coefficients in <planet formation> estimates depend on which approximation is used.

= Planet formation
{parent=Planetary science}

= Core accretion
{parent=Planet formation}

Core accretion forms a planet by building a solid core through growth and accretion of solid material. A sufficiently massive core can then bind a gaseous envelope. It is distinct from direct fragmentation through <gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk>.

= Minimum-mass solar nebula
{parent=Planet formation}

The minimum-mass solar nebula estimates a primordial gas-and-solid <protoplanetary disk> by spreading planetary material into annuli and restoring a solar mixture. A common idealized <surface density> is proportional to $r^{-3/2}$. This reconstruction is a model of minimum material requirements, not a unique history of the Solar System. Extrapolating it far beyond the planetary region need not give a physically consistent disk.

= Hot and cold starts of a giant planet
{title2=$s_0$}
{parent=Planet formation}

Accretion releases gravitational energy. Efficient radiation at an accretion shock leaves a lower-entropy cold start, while greater retained energy gives a higher-entropy hot start; intermediate outcomes are possible. Initial entropy changes a young planet's radius, luminosity and spectrum. Consequently an <exoplanet direct imaging> luminosity and age need not uniquely fix mass without a formation/evolution model.

= Comet
{parent=Planetary science}
{wiki}

= Phase-mixed comet surface density
{parent=Comet}
{title2=$\Sigma_c(r)$}

With uniformly distributed apsidal orientations, the <phase-mixed radial probability of a Kepler orbit> gives the surface probability per <comet>
$$
\Sigma_c(r)=\frac{1}{\pi^2(Q+q)\sqrt{(r-q)(Q-r)}}.
$$
For $Q\gg r>q$, this is $\pi^{-2}Q^{-3/2}r^{-1/2}(1-q/r)^{-1/2}$. Multiply by the population size for a number <surface density of a disk>.

= Planet
{parent=Planetary science}
{wiki}

= Mercury
{c}
{disambiguate=planet}
{parent=Planet}
{wiki=Mercury_(planet)}

= Mercury
{c}
{synonym}

The innermost <planet> has a large metallic core and a weak but global intrinsic <magnetic field>. Its observed field supports an active <planetary dynamo> in a fluid conducting part of that core. Slow rotation does not by itself exclude <dynamo action>.

= Terrestrial planet
{parent=Planet}
{wiki}

= Rocky planet
{synonym}

A planet dominated by rocky and metallic material rather than a massive H/He envelope. <Earth> and <Mars> are examples. A <super-Earth> is a mass category and need not be a terrestrial planet.

= Ice giant
{parent=Planet}
{wiki}

A giant planet with a much larger heavy-element fraction than a typical <gas giant>, exemplified by <Uranus> and <Neptune>. Water, ammonia and other volatile-rich materials are called “ices” in formation terminology; they need not remain ordinary frozen solids in the hot deep interior.

= Gas giant
{parent=Planet}
{wiki}

A large planet whose bulk mass includes a substantial <hydrogen>–<helium> envelope, as in <Jupiter> and <Saturn>. The deep envelope is fluid rather than a dilute atmosphere with a sharp solid surface. Its cooling, compression and possible <electron degeneracy pressure> influence the <mass-radius relation>.

= Venus
{c}
{parent=Planet}
{wiki}

= Neptune
{c}
{parent=Planet}
{wiki}

= Earth
{c}
{parent=Planet}
{wiki}

= Terrestrial atmosphere
{parent=Earth}

The terrestrial atmosphere has regions with opposite signs of the <atmospheric lapse rate>: the <troposphere> usually cools upward, the <stratosphere> warms upward through ultraviolet heating, the <mesosphere> cools, and the <thermosphere> warms. A monotone <atmospheric pressure-temperature profile> can approximate one such interval, but cannot describe all their transitions with one fixed branch.

= Thermosphere
{parent=Terrestrial atmosphere}
{wiki}

The thermosphere is a high atmospheric region where absorption of energetic stellar radiation can produce an upward-rising kinetic <temperature>. Its very low density distinguishes kinetic temperature from a large thermal energy content.

= Mesosphere
{parent=Terrestrial atmosphere}
{wiki}

The mesosphere lies above the <stratosphere> and below the <thermosphere>. Its typical terrestrial <temperature> decreases upward.

= Stratosphere
{parent=Terrestrial atmosphere}
{wiki}

The terrestrial stratosphere lies above the <troposphere> and has an upward-warming region associated with ultraviolet heating by the <ozone layer>.

= Troposphere
{parent=Terrestrial atmosphere}
{wiki}

The troposphere is the lowest main layer of the <terrestrial atmosphere>, with weather and generally decreasing <temperature> with altitude. Local inversions and moist convection complicate a single lapse-rate description.

= Jupiter
{c}
{parent=Planet}
{wiki}

= Rogue planet
{parent=Planet}
{wiki}

A rogue planet has no bound host star. Without stellar irradiation, its luminosity can still be supplied by internal cooling and <Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction>; its emitting <temperature> need not equal the negligible irradiation-based <planetary equilibrium temperature>.

= Planetary system dynamics
{parent=Planetary science}

Planetary system dynamics studies the orbital evolution of planets, planetesimals, and dust under gravity, radiation, collisions, and dissipative forces.

= Orbit crossing
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}

<Orbit crossing> occurs when two geometrical orbital paths intersect. It permits <collisions> or close encounters when bodies also reach the intersection at compatible times; crossing alone does not imply a <collision>. For nearly circular coplanar nested orbits, a useful local test follows by differentiating their radial positions at fixed longitude.

= Differential secular orbit-crossing criterion
{parent=Orbit crossing}
{title2=$|z+a\partial_a z|=1$}

For a cold coplanar disk with <complex eccentricity> $z(a,t)$, the first-order radial shape is $r=a[1-\operatorname{Re}(ze^{-i\theta})]$. Infinitesimally neighboring orbits first cross when $|z+a\partial_a z|=1$. Under <secular forcing by a distant outer planet>, $z=Z_f(1-e^{iAt})$, $Z_f\propto a$ and $A\propto a^{3/2}$, so the secular phase gradient gives $t_{\rm cross}\simeq2/(3Z_fA)$. The finite-neighbor version requires the accumulated phase difference to remain small when replacing a difference by a derivative.

= Isotropic stellar mass loss
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}
{title2=$\ddot{\mathbf r}=-\mu(t)\mathbf r/r^3$}

<Isotropic stellar mass loss> changes the central gravitational parameter $\mu(t)$ while preserving the central direction of the force, provided the escaping material causes no recoil or drag on the orbiting body. The relative equation is $\ddot{\mathbf r}=-\mu(t)\mathbf r/r^3$. <Specific angular momentum> remains exactly constant; <specific orbital energy> obeys $\dot C=-\dot\mu/r$. Orbital expansion is adiabatic only when the <mass>-loss law varies slowly on the <orbital period>.

= Adiabatic orbital expansion under isotropic mass loss
{parent=Isotropic stellar mass loss}
{title2=$\mu a=\mathrm{constant}$}

Time averaging over a <Kepler orbit> gives $\langle\cos f\rangle=-e$, as follows from $dt=(1-e\cos E)dE/n$ and $\cos f=(\cos E-e)/(1-e\cos E)$. Consequently slow <isotropic stellar mass loss> preserves the secular <orbital eccentricity>. Conserved <specific angular momentum> then gives $\mu a=\mathrm{constant}$, so <pericentre>, <apocentre> and <semimajor axis> expand inversely with the remaining gravitational parameter.

= Adiabatic apsidal condition
{parent=Adiabatic orbital expansion under isotropic mass loss}
{title2=$|\dot\varpi/\dot\theta|\ll1$}

The signed instantaneous contribution of <apsidal precession> to azimuthal motion during <isotropic stellar mass loss> is $\dot\varpi/\dot\theta=-(\dot\mu/\mu n)(1-e^2)^{3/2}\sin f/[e(1+e\cos f)^2]$. Its maximum absolute magnitude is small when $n|\mu/\dot\mu|\gg F(e)$, where $F(e)\sim1/e$ near a circular orbit and $F(e)\to3\sqrt3/4$ as $e\to1$. The low-<orbital eccentricity> coordinate singularity can be avoided by evolving the <eccentricity vector>.

= Osculating-element evolution under isotropic mass loss
{parent=Isotropic stellar mass loss}
{title2=$\dot{\mathbf e}=-(\dot\mu/\mu)(\mathbf e+\widehat{\mathbf r})$}

For <isotropic stellar mass loss> the <eccentricity vector> obeys $\dot{\mathbf e}=-(\dot\mu/\mu)(\mathbf e+\widehat{\mathbf r})$. With <true anomaly> $f$ and $e>0$, this gives $\dot e=-(\dot\mu/\mu)(e+\cos f)$ and $\dot\varpi=-(\dot\mu/\mu)\sin f/e$. The osculating <pericentre> satisfies $\dot q/q=-(\dot\mu/\mu)(1-\cos f)/(1+e)$, hence cannot decrease during <mass> loss and is instantaneously stationary at <pericentre>. The vector form is nonsingular at a circular orbit.

= Yarkovsky effect
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Yarkovsky force
{synonym}
{c}

Anisotropic <thermal radiation> produces recoil that changes the <semi-major axis> of a rotating solid body. Finite <thermal inertia> provides the temperature lag. Large bodies have small acceleration per unit mass, while very small bodies become nearly isothermal. The <Yarkovsky effect> can supply bodies to dynamical escape regions.

= Yarkovsky removal
{parent=Yarkovsky effect}
{title2=$t_y=BD+C/D$}
{c}

Drift driven by the <Yarkovsky effect> can carry a body out of a belt or into an unstable orbital region. A useful two-limit removal law is $t_y=BD+C/D$, with slow escape at both large and small diameters.

= External population supplied by belt removal
{parent=Yarkovsky removal}
{title2=$n_{\rm out}=T n_b/t_y$}

A population supplied at differential number rate $n_b(D)/t_y(D)$ and surviving externally for a size-independent time $T$ has $n_{\rm out}(D)=Tn_b(D)/t_y(D)$. Its differential number slope changes by the negative of the removal-time exponent, unless the belt is already removal-dominated, in which case it reflects the fragment injection spectrum.

= Critical belt mass for Yarkovsky removal
{parent=Yarkovsky removal}
{title2=$M_y=\frac{3^{3/4}}4 AB^{-3/4}C^{-1/4}$}

For $t_c=(A/M)D^{1/2}$, the ratio $t_y/t_c$ has its minimum at $D_*=\sqrt{3C/B}$. Removal beats collisions somewhere exactly when $M<M_y=(3^{3/4}/4)AB^{-3/4}C^{-1/4}$, provided the relevant diameters are present. This criterion uses the collision-only cascade as a reference.

= Minimum Yarkovsky removal time
{parent=Yarkovsky removal}
{title2=$D_0=\sqrt{C/B}$}

The law $t_y=BD+C/D$ for positive $B,C$ has a minimum at $D_0=\sqrt{C/B}$ with value $2\sqrt{BC}$. This size differs from the minimum of the ratio of removal and collision times.

= Stellar flyby
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}

A passing <star> perturbs a planetary or cometary system through its differential <Newtonian gravitational field>. A distant encounter is predominantly tidal; a sufficiently close one can substantially change <specific orbital energy> and <angular momentum>, eject a <comet>, or shift its <periapsis> into or out of the planetary region.

= Planetary migration
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}
{wiki}

Planetary migration changes a <planet>'s orbital semimajor axis through torques from a disc or exchanges of energy and angular momentum with other bodies, often followed by <tidal dissipation>. Short-period giant planets, eccentric progenitors, resonant architectures, and <stellar obliquity> provide complementary constraints rather than one unique migration diagnostic.

= Circular-orbit migration rate from disk torque
{parent=Planetary migration}
{title2=$\dot r_0=-2\Gamma/(M_sr_0\Omega)$}

For a fixed mass on a Keplerian <circular orbit>, $L_s=M_s\sqrt{GM_*r_0}$ has derivative $M_sr_0\Omega/2$. The <torque> on the satellite is minus the <torque> $\Gamma$ on the disk. This gives the displayed drift law. In the <density-slope satellite torque> model, $\dot r_0=-(8\chi/9)\beta q(\Sigma_0r_0^2/M_*)r_0\Omega(H/r_0)^{-2}$.

= Stellar obliquity
{parent=Planetary migration}
{title2=$\psi$}

The stellar obliquity is the angle between a star's spin and a planet's orbital angular momentum. The <Rossiter-McLaughlin effect> constrains the angle $\lambda$ between the sky projections of those two axes, rather than a component of $\psi$. If the orbital and stellar-spin inclinations to the line of sight are $i_{\rm orb}$ and $i_*$, then $\cos\psi=\cos i_*\cos i_{\rm orb}+\sin i_*\sin i_{\rm orb}\cos\lambda$; $\lambda$ alone generally does not determine $\psi$. Large misalignments constrain formation and <planetary migration>, but can also reflect a tilted birth disc.

= Rossiter-McLaughlin effect
{c}
{parent=Stellar obliquity}

A transiting body hides different velocity regions of a rotating stellar disc and causes an anomalous measured <Doppler shift>. Its time dependence constrains the sky-projected spin-orbit angle, which is not generally the full <stellar obliquity>.

= Tidal locking
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}
{wiki}

Tidal locking arises when tidal torques drive a body's spin toward an orbital resonance. In the circular synchronous case, its rotation period equals the orbital period, so the same hemisphere continually faces its companion.

= Synchronous rotation
{parent=Tidal locking}

= Tidal synchronization
{synonym}

Synchronous rotation has spin period equal to orbital period. A synchronously rotating <exoplanet> on a circular orbit has a persistent illuminated hemisphere and a persistent dark hemisphere, making <day-night heat redistribution> crucial.

= Tidal heating
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}
{wiki}

Tidal heating converts mechanical energy of tidal deformation into internal heat through dissipation. Sustained eccentricity or obliquity can maintain the deformation and heat an <exoplanet> interior.

= Tidal dissipation
{parent=Tidal heating}

Tidal dissipation converts mechanical <energy> associated with a time-varying tidal deformation into <heat>. It can circularize an eccentric orbit, synchronize spin, and contribute deep planetary heating. A perfectly synchronized circular orbit with no additional forcing need not sustain substantial <tidal heating>.

= Astronomical conjunction
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}
{wiki=Conjunction_(astronomy)}

= Conjunction
{synonym}

An astronomical conjunction occurs when two orbiting bodies have the same apparent longitude as viewed from their primary or from an observer.

= Conjunction longitude from a transit time lag
{parent=Astronomical conjunction}
{title2=$\Lambda$}

For two uniform angular motions with $P_b<P_c$, an inner <exoplanet transit> at zero and next outer <exoplanet transit> at $\tau$, the next <conjunction> solves $(n_b-n_c)t+n_c\tau=2\pi$. Hence its longitude modulo $2\pi$ is
$$
\Lambda=2\pi\frac{1-\tau/P_b}{P_c/P_b-1}.
$$
For an eccentric outer body this is only an approximation. Let $f_L,M_L$ be its line-of-sight anomalies and $\Delta_e(t)=f(t)-M(t)-(f_L-M_L)$. Exact true alignment instead requires $(n_b-n_c)t+n_c\tau-\Delta_e(t)=2\pi$. The correction follows directly from uniform <mean anomaly> and nonuniform <true anomaly>.

= Keplerian shear
{c}
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}

Keplerian shear is differential orbital motion caused by $n\propto a^{-3/2}$: neighboring material at smaller semi-major axis advances in longitude relative to material farther out.

= Phase mixing
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}

Phase mixing disperses an initially localized ensemble because its members have slightly different orbital frequencies, causing their phases to spread with time.

= Planetesimal
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}
{wiki}

A planetesimal is a solid body in a young or evolved planetary system whose collisions and gravitational interactions help build or erode larger bodies.

= Planetesimal collision rate
{parent=Planetesimal}

For an impactor number density per size $n(D_{\rm im})$, relative speed $v_{\rm rel}$, and collision cross-section $\sigma(D,D_{\rm im})$, the differential collision rate of one target is $n(D_{\rm im})v_{\rm rel}\sigma(D,D_{\rm im})dD_{\rm im}$.

= Specific impact energy
{title2=$Q$}
{parent=Planetesimal}

The specific impact energy is an impactor's kinetic energy divided by the target mass. For equal-density spherical bodies,
$$
Q=\frac12\left(\frac{D_{\rm im}}D\right)^3v_{\rm rel}^2.
$$

= Binary planetesimal
{parent=Planetesimal}

A binary planetesimal is a gravitationally bound pair of <planetesimals>. Its internal <two-body problem> and its <centre of mass> orbit respond differently to external forces, including <gas drag>.

= Gas drag on a planetesimal binary
{parent=Binary planetesimal}

For linear <gas drag> with <aerodynamic stopping times> $t_1,t_2$, masses $m_1,m_2$, total mass $m=m_1+m_2$, and relative velocity $\mathbf v$, define
$$
\kappa=\frac{m_1/t_2+m_2/t_1}{m},\qquad
\kappa_b=\frac{m_1/t_1+m_2/t_2}{m},\qquad
\Delta=\frac1{t_2}-\frac1{t_1}.
$$
If $\mathbf w$ is the gas velocity relative to the <centre of mass>, the relative drag acceleration is $-\kappa\mathbf v+\Delta\mathbf w$. The first term dissipates internal <specific orbital energy>; the second is a differential headwind that vanishes for equal <aerodynamic stopping times>.

= Gas-driven contraction of a planetesimal binary
{parent=Gas drag on a planetesimal binary}

For a weakly perturbed circular <binary planetesimal>, the constant differential headwind does zero work averaged over the unperturbed orbit. <Orbit-averaged drag work> gives
$$
\langle\dot a\rangle=-2\kappa a,\qquad
\tau=\frac1{2\kappa}=\frac{m_1+m_2}{2(m_1/t_2+m_2/t_1)}.
$$
With constant <aerodynamic stopping times>, $a(t)=a_0e^{-t/\tau}$. This averaging assumes drag is slow compared with the <orbital period> and does not appreciably distort the circular orbit within one period.

= Gas-driven migration of a binary centre of mass
{parent=Gas drag on a planetesimal binary}

For a nearly circular stellar orbit of radius $a_b$ in gas moving at $(1-\eta)$ times the local circular speed, the averaged tangential <gas drag> on the <centre of mass> is $-\eta\kappa_bv_K$. <Orbit-averaged drag work> gives
$$
\dot a_b=-2\eta\kappa_ba_b,\qquad \tau_b=\frac1{2\eta\kappa_b}.
$$
If $m_2/m_1=\epsilon\ll1$ and <aerodynamic stopping time> scales as $m^k$, $0<k<1$, then $\tau/\tau_b\simeq\eta\epsilon^k\ll1$. Internal contraction can outpace stellar migration. Tightly gas-coupled drift requires solving radial and azimuthal motion together instead of this weak-drag approximation.

= Tisserand parameter
{title2=$T_p$}
{c}
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}
{wiki}

For a small body with orbital elements $a,e,I$ relative to a planet on a circular orbit of semi-major axis $a_p$, the Tisserand parameter is approximately
$$
T_p=\frac{a_p}{a}+2\sqrt{\frac a{a_p}(1-e^2)}\cos I.
$$
It is derived from the Jacobi constant and is approximately conserved across separated weak encounters in the circular restricted three-body problem.

= Tisserand periapsis bound
{parent=Tisserand parameter}
{c}
{title2=$q\ge q_{\min}(T)$}

For bound <planet>-crossing <Kepler orbits>, $T=2/(q+Q)+2\sqrt{2qQ/(q+Q)}\cos I$, with the <planet> radius taken as one. At fixed $q<1$, $T$ is maximized by $I=0$ and $Q=1$. For $2<T<3$ this gives $q\ge(1-\sqrt{3-T})^2/[1+2\sqrt{3-T}-(3-T)]$. This form remains regular at $T=\sqrt8$. It is an invariant-based accessibility bound, not a guarantee that a scattering history attains it; for $0<T\le2$ it gives no positive universal <pericentre> floor.

= Planet-encounter relative velocity
{parent=Tisserand parameter}
{title2=$u^2=3-T$}

In units where a circular <planet> has orbital radius and speed one, the particle's relative speed $u$ outside a close encounter satisfies $u^2=3-T$, to leading order in the <planet>-to-<star> <mass> ratio. This follows from the <vis-viva equation> and $u^2=v^2+1-2h_z$ at the encounter radius. A short conservative encounter changes the direction of this relative velocity while preserving its magnitude.

= Disturbing function
{title2=$\mathcal R$}
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}
{wiki}

The disturbing function is the perturbing part of the gravitational potential used to derive the evolution of orbital elements in celestial mechanics.

= Stellar quadrupole disturbing function
{parent=Disturbing function}
{title2=$\mathcal R_\star=GM_\star r^2P_2(\cos\psi)/a_p^3$}

For a particle far inside a planet's stellar orbit, the planet-centred <disturbing function> is $GM_\star/|\boldsymbol r_\star-\boldsymbol r|-GM_\star\boldsymbol r\cdot\boldsymbol r_\star/r_\star^3$. Expanding the first term cancels its dipole against the <indirect disturbing function>. Dropping the spatially constant monopole leaves $GM_\star r^2(3\cos^2\psi-1)/(2r_\star^3)$, where $\psi$ is the angle between the two offset vectors. The omitted instantaneous terms are $O(GM_\star r^3/r_\star^4)$. The gradient of the retained expression is the leading differential stellar tidal acceleration.

= Double-averaged circular tidal potential
{parent=Stellar quadrupole disturbing function}
{title2=$\overline{\mathcal R}_\star=\frac{GM_\star a^2}{8a_p^3}(3\cos^2I_m-1)$}

For two circular <Kepler orbits> with <mutual inclination> $I_m$, independently average the <stellar quadrupole disturbing function> over both <mean anomalies>. A circular ring with unit normal $\boldsymbol k$ has $\langle\widehat r_i\widehat r_j\rangle=(\delta_{ij}-k_i k_j)/2$. Contracting the two ring tensors gives $\langle\cos^2\psi\rangle=[1+(\boldsymbol k\cdot\boldsymbol k_p)^2]/4$, proving the displayed exact angular factor. For small <orbital inclinations> relative to a common plane, $I_m^2=I^2+I_p^2-2II_p\cos(\Omega_p-\Omega)+O(I^4)$. Thus the secular angular factor is $1/4-3(I^2+I_p^2)/8+3II_p\cos(\Omega_p-\Omega)/4$. This potential generates the slow stellar contribution to satellite <nodal precession>.

= Lagrange planetary equations
{c}
{parent=Disturbing function}

The Lagrange planetary equations express changes of osculating <orbital elements> in terms of derivatives of a <disturbing function>. Their small-eccentricity secular limit becomes a linear equation for <complex eccentricity>.

= Eccentricity and semimajor-axis invariant of a truncated resonance
{parent=Lagrange planetary equations}
{title2=$e^2-\frac{q}{p+q}\log a=\mathrm{constant}$}

If retained <Lagrange planetary equations> have $\dot a=-2(p+q)ae^q C_r\sin\phi$ and $\dot e=-qe^{q-1}C_r\sin\phi$, then $d(e^2)/d\log a=q/(p+q)$. Thus $e^2-[q/(p+q)]\log(a/a_{\rm ref})$ is constant. A resonantly oscillating <orbital eccentricity>–<semi-major axis> trajectory retraces one curve rather than enclosing area in that projection.

= Mean-longitude equation for a first-order resonant term
{parent=Lagrange planetary equations}
{title2=$g(\alpha)=\alpha f(\alpha)/2-2\alpha^2f'(\alpha)$}

For <disturbing function> $\mathcal R=(GM_2/a_2)f(\alpha)e\cos\phi$, $\alpha=a/a_2$, the leading planar <Lagrange planetary equations> give $\dot\lambda=n+n(M_2/M_\star)e\,g(\alpha)\cos\phi$. The derivative with respect to $a$ holds the other <osculating orbital elements> fixed. If one writes $\lambda=n(t)t+\epsilon(t)$, its literal derivative includes $t\dot n$; the physical mean-longitude equation cannot be obtained by silently dropping that term. A consistent accumulated-phase parametrization uses $\lambda=\int_0^t n(s)\,ds+\epsilon_{\rm int}$.

= Gauss planetary equation for the semi-major axis
{c}
{parent=Lagrange planetary equations}
{title2=$\dot a$}

For perturbing <acceleration> $R\widehat{\boldsymbol r}+T\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}$ on an <osculating orbital element> system with fixed central parameter $\mu$,
$$
\dot a=2\sqrt{\frac{a^3}{\mu(1-e^2)}}[Re\sin f+T(1+e\cos f)].
$$
To prove it, differentiate the <specific orbital energy> $-\mu/(2a)$, obtaining $\dot a=(2a^2/\mu)(R\dot r+Th/r)$. Substitute $\dot r=\mu e\sin f/h$, $h/r=\mu(1+e\cos f)/h$ and $h^2=\mu a(1-e^2)$. Here $f$ is the <true anomaly>; a normal perturbing <acceleration> does no instantaneous <work>.

= Direct disturbing function
{parent=Disturbing function}

The direct disturbing function is the gravitational potential of the perturbing body evaluated at the disturbed body. In heliocentric coordinates it is proportional to the inverse separation of the two bodies.

= Indirect disturbing function
{parent=Disturbing function}

The indirect disturbing function accounts for the acceleration of a non-inertial origin, such as a star-centered coordinate frame accelerated by a perturbing planet.

= D'Alembert characteristic
{c}
{parent=Disturbing function}

The D'Alembert characteristic constrains each disturbing-function harmonic so that the integer coefficients of its angular arguments sum to zero, with corresponding parity and eccentricity-inclination order restrictions imposed by rotational symmetry.

= Laplace coefficient
{title2=$b_s^{(j)}(\alpha)$}
{c}
{parent=Disturbing function}

A Laplace coefficient is the Fourier coefficient
$$
b_s^{(j)}(\alpha)=\frac1\pi\int_0^{2\pi}
\frac{\cos(j\psi)\,d\psi}{(1-2\alpha\cos\psi+\alpha^2)^s}.
$$
It packages the radial dependence of a disturbing-function expansion.

= Short-period orbital perturbation
{parent=Disturbing function}

A short-period orbital perturbation is driven by rapidly varying orbital-angle combinations and usually averages to zero over many conjunctions away from resonance.

= Resonant orbital perturbation
{parent=Disturbing function}

A resonant orbital perturbation has a slowly varying angular argument because a nearly integer relation between orbital frequencies prevents successive conjunction effects from averaging away.

= Secular perturbation
{parent=Disturbing function}
{wiki}

A secular perturbation survives averaging over fast orbital phases and drives slow precession and exchange of eccentricity or inclination.

= Orbit averaging
{parent=Secular perturbation}
{title2=$\langle x\rangle=P^{-1}\int_0^P x\,dt$}

= Orbital averaging
{synonym}

For a quantity on a periodic <Kepler orbit>, its orbit average is $\langle x\rangle=P^{-1}\int_0^P x(t)\,dt$. Equal increments of <true anomaly> are not equal increments of time. <Eccentric anomaly> gives the weight $dt=(1-e\cos E)dE/n$, which yields $\langle\cos f\rangle=-e$. Slow orbital perturbations can be averaged using elements held fixed over a single <orbital period>.

= Angular momentum deficit
{parent=Secular perturbation}
{title2=$\mathrm{AMD}=\sum_j L_j(1-\sqrt{1-e_j^2}\cos I_j)$}

= AMD
{c}
{synonym}

The difference between the <angular momentum> of circular coplanar orbits at fixed <semi-major axes> and the system's actual angular momentum along the reference normal. With circular momenta $L_j=m_j\sqrt{GM_\star a_j}$ it is $\sum_jL_j(1-\sqrt{1-e_j^2}\cos I_j)$. For small planar <orbital eccentricities> its quadratic part is $\frac12\sum_jL_j|z_j|^2$. Its conservation in $\dot z=iAz$ requires $L_jA_{jk}=L_kA_{kj}$.

= Proper eccentricity
{parent=Secular perturbation}
{title2=$e_p$}

= Free eccentricity
{synonym}

The proper eccentricity is the magnitude of the homogeneous part of the <complex eccentricity> in linear <secular perturbation> theory. It remains constant in conservative theory, while its <proper longitude of periapsis> advances at the free precession frequency.

= Proper longitude of periapsis
{parent=Proper eccentricity}
{title2=$\varpi_p$}

= Proper longitudes of periapsis
{synonym}

= Laplace-Lagrange secular theory
{c}
{parent=Secular perturbation}

Laplace-Lagrange secular theory linearizes the orbit-averaged disturbing function to second order in eccentricities and inclinations. It represents planetary eccentricity evolution by a constant matrix.

= Secular forcing by a distant outer planet
{parent=Laplace-Lagrange secular theory}
{title2=$Z_f=5\alpha e_p/4,\quad A\propto\alpha^{3/2}$}

For a test particle interior to a distant eccentric <planet>, write $\alpha=a/a_p\ll1$. The leading <Laplace coefficients> are $b_{3/2}^{1}=3\alpha$ and $b_{3/2}^{2}=15\alpha^2/4$. Thus its <forced eccentricity> is $Z_f=5\alpha e_p/4$ and its secular frequency is $A=(3/4)n_p(m_p/M)\alpha^{3/2}$. More distant inner particles have larger forced <orbital eccentricities> and faster secular precession, producing a growing gradient of <complex eccentricity>.

= Secular forcing of a test particle
{parent=Laplace-Lagrange secular theory}

= Secular phase integral for proportional mass growth
{parent=Secular forcing of a test particle}
{title2=$\Phi(t)=\int_0^t g(t\prime)\,dt\prime$}

If both precession and forcing in $\dot z=ig(t)(z-z_f)$ share a common mass factor while $z_f$ stays fixed, then $z=z_f+(z(0)-z_f)\exp(i\int_0^t g(t\prime)\,dt\prime)$. The <Argand diagram> circle is unchanged; only its traversal speed varies. With $g(t)=g_f t/t_p$, the phase is $g_ft^2/(2t_p)$.

= Laplace-Lagrange secular matrix
{title2=$\mathbf A$}
{c}
{parent=Laplace-Lagrange secular theory}

The eigenvalues of the Laplace-Lagrange secular matrix are apsidal precession frequencies, and its eigenvectors give the relative eccentricities and apsidal phases of the corresponding normal modes.

= Complex eccentricity
{title2=$z$}
{parent=Laplace-Lagrange secular theory}

The complex eccentricity packages eccentricity and longitude of periapsis as $z=e e^{i\varpi}$, turning linear secular dynamics into a complex linear system.

= Forced eccentricity circle
{parent=Complex eccentricity}
{title2=$z=Z_f(1-e^{iAt})$}

With a fixed planetary <pericentre>, the linear secular equation $\dot z=iA(z-Z_f)$ has solution $z=Z_f+(z(0)-Z_f)e^{iAt}$. An initially circular orbit follows $z=Z_f(1-e^{iAt})$, a counterclockwise circle in the complex plane centered at the <forced eccentricity> $Z_f$, with <free eccentricity> amplitude $Z_f$. Its maximum <orbital eccentricity> is $2Z_f$ and its phase-averaged mean <orbital eccentricity> is $4Z_f/\pi$.

= Secular eigenmode
{parent=Laplace-Lagrange secular theory}

A secular eigenmode is a normal mode in which all complex eccentricities precess at one eigenfrequency with fixed amplitude ratios and relative apsidal phases.

= Secular eigenmode amplitude product
{parent=Secular eigenmode}
{title2=$r_pr_m=A_{12}/A_{21}$}

For a real two-planet <Laplace-Lagrange secular matrix> with negative off-diagonal entries, the upper <secular eigenmode> has anti-aligned apsides and the lower has aligned apsides. Their positive planet-1 to planet-2 amplitude ratios obey $r_pr_m=A_{12}/A_{21}$. Under the conventional <angular momentum deficit> normalization this is $L_2/L_1$.

= Complex inclination
{title2=$y=I e^{i\Omega}$}
{parent=Laplace-Lagrange secular theory}

Complex inclination packages small <orbital inclination> $I$ and <longitude of ascending node> $\Omega$. It differs from <complex eccentricity>. Linear <Laplace-Lagrange secular theory> gives $\dot{\mathbf y}=iB\mathbf y$, whose <matrix exponential> solution decomposes into nodal <secular eigenmodes>.

= Forced inclination
{parent=Complex inclination}
{title2=$w_f$}

The particular, forced part of a <complex inclination> solution. For a fixed, circular, inclined inner <planet> and an exterior test ring, linear <Laplace-Lagrange secular theory> gives $\dot w=-i\nu(w-w_p)$, so $w_f=w_p$ and $w=w_p+(w(0)-w_p)e^{-i\nu t}$. The ring describes a circle about the planetary tilt in an <Argand diagram>; relative to the planet, its <mutual inclination> stays constant while its <ascending node> precesses.

= Laplace-Lagrange inclination matrix
{title2=$B$}
{c}
{parent=Complex inclination}

For circular planetary orbits with <angular momenta> $L_j=M_j\sqrt{GM_\star a_j}$, the inclination matrix has positive off-diagonal entries, zero row sums, and $L_jB_{jk}=L_kB_{kj}$. <Positive diagonal symmetrization of a matrix> with $W=\operatorname{diag}(L_j)$ makes $W^{1/2}BW^{-1/2}$ a <symmetric matrix>. Moreover,
$$
\mathbf z^\dagger WB\mathbf z=-\sum_{j<k}L_jB_{jk}|z_j-z_k|^2\le0.
$$
All <eigenvalues> are real and nonpositive. The <zero eigenvalue> is a common tilt of every orbital plane; nonzero modes describe relative nodal precession.

= Rigid inner pair in hierarchical secular inclination dynamics
{parent=Laplace-Lagrange inclination matrix}

If two nearby inner planets couple much more strongly to each other than to a distant third planet, their slow <secular eigenmode> has a common <complex inclination>. With $L_{12}=L_1+L_2$, the effective inner-to-outer coupling is
$$
c=\frac{L_1B_{13}+L_2B_{23}}{L_{12}}.
$$
<Angular momentum> balance makes the reverse coupling $cL_{12}/L_3$. The reduced matrix has <eigenvalues> $0$ and $-c(1+L_{12}/L_3)$. If $L_3\gg L_{12}$, the nonzero frequency is approximately $-c$, while the fast inner differential mode has frequency $-(B_{12}+B_{21})$.

= Forced eccentricity
{parent=Secular perturbation}

The forced eccentricity is the particular secular response of a small body to planetary eigenmodes, as distinct from its freely precessing homogeneous eccentricity.

= Secular resonance
{parent=Forced eccentricity}
{wiki}

A secular resonance occurs when a small body's free precession frequency equals a planetary secular eigenfrequency. Undamped linear theory then predicts resonant eccentricity growth.

= Sweeping secular resonance
{parent=Secular resonance}

Changing planetary masses or other system parameters move the frequencies of <secular eigenmodes>. A particle can cross a <secular resonance> when its free precession frequency meets a forcing frequency. Passage can change its <free eccentricity> and mix modes; a single scalar phase replacement generally cannot describe the response.

= Eccentricity damping
{parent=Secular perturbation}

Linear eccentricity damping contributes $\dot z=-z/\tau$ to the complex-eccentricity equation, exponentially suppressing the free mode and regularizing a secular resonance.

= Damping of coupled secular eigenmodes
{parent=Eccentricity damping}
{title2=$A'=A+i\operatorname{diag}(\tau^{-1},0)$}

Linear <eccentricity damping> on one planet changes $\dot z=iAz$ to $\dot z=i[A+i\operatorname{diag}(\tau^{-1},0)]z$. For a nondegenerate, weakly damped two-planet matrix, let $D=\sqrt{(A_{11}-A_{22})^2+4A_{12}A_{21}}$. The modal damping rates are $g_{p,m}=[1\pm(A_{11}-A_{22})/D]/(2\tau)>0$. Both <secular eigenmodes> damp even though only one planet is directly affected; the coupling shares the dissipation.

= Secular torque
{parent=Secular perturbation}

A secular torque is the orbit-averaged gravitational torque that changes orbital orientation or shape after fast orbital phases have been averaged out.

= Quadrupole approximation
{parent=Secular perturbation}

In a hierarchical orbital system, the quadrupole approximation keeps the first nontrivial term beyond the monopole in the orbit-averaged expansion in the ratio of inner to outer separation.

= Quadrupole potential of a circular binary
{parent=Quadrupole approximation}

A <binary star> of total mass $M$, <reduced mass> $\mu$ and separation vector $\mathbf a$ has far-field <potential function>
$$
\Phi=-\frac{GM}{x}-\frac{G\mu a^2}{2x^3}(3\cos^2\theta-1)+O(GMa^3/x^4),
$$
where $\theta$ is the angle between $\mathbf x$ and $\mathbf a$. The dipole term vanishes in <centre-of-mass> coordinates. Averaging a circular coplanar binary over its fast phase gives $\overline\Phi=-GM/x-G\mu a^2/(4x^3)$.

= Circumbinary orbital-period correction
{parent=Quadrupole potential of a circular binary}

For a distant coplanar <circular orbit> about a <binary star>, the averaged <quadrupole potential of a circular binary> gives
$$
P=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{x^3}{GM}}\left(1+\frac{3\mu a^2}{4Mx^2}\right)^{-1/2}.
$$
At fixed outer radius $x$, the quadrupole shortens the period relative to the monopole result. If the inner binary evolves slowly, the outer <specific angular momentum> stays constant in the axisymmetric averaged potential, but the outer energy can change. The outer radius must then be allowed to adjust when computing a period derivative.

= Nodal precession
{parent=Secular perturbation}
{wiki}

Nodal precession is the gradual rotation of an orbit's line of nodes, usually driven by a nonspherical or external gravitational torque.

= Differential nodal precession
{parent=Nodal precession}

Differential nodal precession occurs when neighboring orbits precess at different rates, winding a flat collection of rings into a warp or a phase-mixed thick structure.

= Planet-induced debris-disk warp
{parent=Differential nodal precession}

An inclined inner <planet> causes exterior <debris disk> rings to undergo <nodal precession> at different rates. In the <quadrupole approximation>, $\nu\simeq(3/4)n(M_p/M_\star)(a_p/a)^2\propto a^{-7/2}$. Rings that have precessed appreciably tilt away from their original <orbital plane>, whereas distant rings remain near it, making an evolving warp. Conservative <secular perturbations> alone do not damp every ring into the planet's plane; unresolved <phase mixing> can instead produce a thick inner disk whose mean normal is near the forced plane.

= Laplace plane
{c}
{parent=Nodal precession}
{wiki}

The Laplace plane is the local equilibrium orbital plane about which a satellite or disk annulus precesses under competing secular torques.

= Laplace radius
{c}
{parent=Laplace plane}
{title2=$a_L=[2J_2(M_p/M_\star)R_p^2a_p^3]^{1/5}$}

The Laplace radius is the satellite distance where the small-tilt <nodal precession> contributions from a planet's axisymmetric quadrupole and the stellar tide have equal magnitudes. In the circular hierarchical limit those rates are $3GM_pJ_2R_p^2/(2na^5)$ and $3GM_\star/(4na_p^3)$. Equating them proves the displayed fifth-root expression. Inside this radius the <planetary quadrupole coefficient> controls the preferred plane; outside it the <double-averaged circular tidal potential> dominates. When equator and planetary orbit are inclined to one another, their balance determines the local <Laplace plane>.

= Apsidal precession
{parent=Secular perturbation}
{wiki}

Apsidal precession is the gradual rotation of an orbit's line of apsides, equivalently a change in its <longitude of periapsis>.

= Kozai–Lidov mechanism
{c}
{parent=Secular perturbation}
{wiki}

The Kozai–Lidov mechanism exchanges eccentricity and inclination in a hierarchical gravitational system while preserving a component of angular momentum in the quadrupole limit.

= Planetary scattering
{parent=Planetary system dynamics}

Planetary scattering is the alteration of a smaller body's orbital energy and angular momentum by close encounters with a planet. Repeated encounters can eject, accrete, or detach the body.

= Angular-momentum kicks to nearly radial orbits
{parent=Planetary scattering}
{title2=$q\simeq h^2/(2GM)$}

For a nearly radial <Kepler orbit>, $q\simeq h^2/(2GM)$, so modest changes in <specific angular momentum> can strongly change <pericentre>. A planetary <torque> acting for a reference orbital time gives $\delta h\sim(m_p/M)\sqrt{GMa_p}$. Coherent kicks change $q$ by order itself after $N\sim(M/m_p)\sqrt{q/a_p}$ orbital-scale encounters, whereas uncorrelated kicks require an order-$N^2$ random-walk count. Both estimates are conditional on encounter geometry: a close encounter with a smaller <impact parameter> can give a much larger kick.

= Comet energy diffusion
{parent=Planetary scattering}
{title2=$x=1/a$}

Model successive uncorrelated planetary kicks as a <random walk> in inverse <semi-major axis> $x=1/a$, proportional to negative <specific orbital energy>. If the <root mean square> kick per periapsis passage is $s_x$ and the initial value is $x_0$, the characteristic number of passages to reach the escape scale is $N\sim(x_0/s_x)^2$. For $s_x=10M_p/(a_pM_\star)$ and passage interval $P(a_0)$,
$$
t_{\rm ej}\sim\frac{P(a_0)}{100}\left(\frac{a_p}{a_0}\right)^2
\left(\frac{M_\star}{M_p}\right)^2.
$$
The quantity $s_x$ has inverse-length units, not the units of a variance-per-time <diffusion coefficient>. With $\langle(\Delta x)^2\rangle=2D_xt$, that coefficient is $D_x=s_x^2/(2P)$. This estimate assumes many independent encounters; changing <orbital periods> and resonant correlations can invalidate the clock. It is not an exact mean <first-passage time>.

= Finite-age planetary scattering regimes
{parent=Comet energy diffusion}

At fixed stellar <mass> $M_\star$, planetary <mass density> $\rho_p$, age $t_\star$ and initial ratio $\kappa=a_0/a_p$, the order-unity <Safronov number> comparison and <comet energy diffusion> give boundaries
$$
M_{\rm esc}=\sqrt{\frac{3}{32\pi}}M_\star^{3/2}\rho_p^{-1/2}a_p^{-3/2},
\qquad
M_{\rm age}=M_\star\sqrt{\frac{P_p}{100t_\star}}\kappa^{-1/4}.
$$
Their logarithmic slopes in planetary <mass> versus radius are $-3/2$ and $3/4$. Above both, strong kicks and a short estimated diffusion time favour ejection; below the escape boundary, repeated weak encounters favour collision statistically. Below the age boundary, the stated diffusion mechanism has not completed on its characteristic clock. Collision lifetimes require additional cross-section and encounter-rate information, so this map alone cannot establish retention or <accretion> within the age.

= Safronov number
{c}
{parent=Planetary scattering}
{title2=$\Theta$}

For a <planet> on a circular stellar <orbit>, a common convention is $\Theta=\tfrac12(v_{\rm esc}/v_k)^2$. The escape-to-orbital-speed ratio measures the size of a strong <gravitational assist> relative to the stellar binding <energy> of a nearby <comet>. Large values tend to favour ejection over collision; small values favour repeated weak encounters with more collision opportunities. This is a statistical comparison, dependent on the initial <orbit> and encounter geometry, rather than a universal outcome threshold.

= Planetary ejection of a comet
{parent=Planetary scattering}

A <gravitational assist> ejects a weakly bound <comet> when the increase in its <specific orbital energy> exceeds its binding energy. For near-parabolic coplanar encounters, the weak-deflection ejection <impact parameter> scales as $(M_p/M_\star)Q$. Combining this with the <phase-mixed comet surface density> gives a one-encounter ejection time proportional to $Q^{1/2}$, within the local two-body encounter approximation.

= Single-encounter escape velocity bound
{parent=Planetary ejection of a comet}
{title2=$u>(\sqrt2-1)/2$}

For a particle initially having <semimajor axis> one and encountering a circular <planet> at radius one, the incoming stellar-frame speed is one. A conservative encounter at <planet>-relative speed $u$ changes the stellar-frame velocity by at most $2u$. Since stellar escape requires speed $\sqrt2$, a necessary bound is $u>(\sqrt2-1)/2$. Retaining the outgoing relative-velocity geometry sharpens the necessary bound to $u>\sqrt2-1$. Neither inequality by itself guarantees a realizable deflection at a specified <impact parameter>.

= Collision-ejection lifetime of a comet
{parent=Planetary ejection of a comet}

= Coplanar comet collision time
{parent=Planetary scattering}

The <geometric collision cross-section> in a coplanar calculation is a length $2R_p$, rather than an area. For one <comet> with local surface probability $\Sigma_c$ and <relative velocity> $v_{\rm rel}$, the rare-event rate is $2R_p\Sigma_c v_{\rm rel}$. Without <gravitational focusing>, the resulting mean collision time scales as $Q^{3/2}M_p^{-1/3}a_p$ for deep planet-crossing orbits at fixed stellar mass and planetary <mass density>.

= Inclined comet collision time
{parent=Coplanar comet collision time}

For a population with randomized vertical phases, <orbital inclination> $I$, and $R_p\ll a_p I$, the effective volume probability near the planetary plane is proportional to $\Sigma_c/(a_p I)$. The collision time then scales as $I Q^{3/2}M_p^{-2/3}a_p^2$, at fixed stellar mass and planetary <mass density>. An individual inclined <Kepler orbit> whose <orbital nodes> do not intersect the <planet>'s orbit need not collide at all.

= Debris disk
{parent=Planetary science}
{wiki}

= Debris belt
{synonym}

A debris disk is an optically thin circumstellar population of dust and larger solid bodies maintained mainly by collisions.

= Eccentric debris ring
{parent=Debris disk}

= Surface density contrast in an eccentric ring
{parent=Eccentric debris ring}

A higher orbital line density at <apoapsis> does not always give a higher <surface density of a disk>: the local ring width also changes. In a narrow <aligned eccentric ring>, neglecting disc twist and keeping first-order <orbital eccentricity>, $\Sigma_{\rm peri}\propto1+a\,de/da$ and $\Sigma_{\rm apo}\propto1-a\,de/da$. Constant eccentricity therefore gives constant surface density to this order.

= Aligned eccentric ring
{parent=Eccentric debris ring}

An aligned eccentric ring consists of nested <Kepler orbits> with nearly common <longitude of periapsis>. Its width and <surface density of a disk> depend on both the distribution of <semi-major axis> values and the radial gradient of <forced eccentricity>.

= Proper-eccentricity annulus
{parent=Eccentric debris ring}

For a common <semi-major axis> $a$ and <proper eccentricity> $e_p$, randomly distributed <proper longitudes of periapsis> give an <annulus> of radii $a(1-e_p)$ and $a(1+e_p)$ about the point displaced from the <star> by minus $a$ times the <forced eccentricity> vector, to first order in <orbital eccentricity>.

= Dust halo
{parent=Debris disk}

= Dust birth ring
{parent=Debris disk}

A dust birth ring is a belt of parent <planetesimals> whose collisions supply dust. <Radiation pressure> gives the fragments different orbits even when their release velocities match those of their parents.

= Fractional luminosity of a debris disk
{title2=$f$}
{parent=Debris disk}

The fractional luminosity is $f=L_{\rm disk}/L_*$; for blackbody grains in an optically thin belt at radius $r$, it equals their total geometric cross-section divided by $4\pi r^2$.

= Fractional luminosity of a phase-mixed eccentric wire
{parent=Fractional luminosity of a debris disk}
{title2=$f$}

On a steady <phase-mixed orbit>, <line density on a Kepler orbit> is proportional to $1/v$, so cross-sectional area is uniform in time. For an <optically thin> wire of <blackbodies> in <radiative equilibrium>,
$$
f=\frac{\sigma_{\rm tot}}{4\pi P}\int_0^P\frac{dt}{r^2}
=\frac{\sigma_{\rm tot}}{4\pi a^2\sqrt{1-e^2}}.
$$
The proof uses <specific angular momentum>: $dt/r^2=df/h$, and $Ph=2\pi a^2\sqrt{1-e^2}$. If one instead imposes a line density proportional to $v$, area is weighted by $v^2dt$, yielding $f=\sigma_{\rm tot}(1+e^2)/[4\pi a^2(1-e^2)^{3/2}]$. The two distributions agree only for a <circular Kepler orbit>.

= Dust clump
{parent=Debris disk}

A dust clump is a localized concentration of collision fragments or trapped grains whose orbital phases have not yet become uniformly distributed around their parent belt.

= Collisional cascade
{parent=Debris disk}
{wiki}

A collisional cascade transfers mass from large bodies to progressively smaller fragments. A steady cascade has a size distribution whose mass flux through logarithmic size bins is independent of size.

= Two-size fragmentation cascade
{parent=Collisional cascade}
{title2=$\dot F=\alpha SF-\beta F^2$}

A simplified <collisional cascade> tracks large objects $S$ and small fragments $F$. A fragment can disrupt a large object and multiply, while fragment–fragment collisions remove material. With $\dot S=-\kappa SF$ and $\dot F=\alpha SF-\beta F^2$, elimination of time gives a <linear differential equation>, $dF/dS-(\beta/\kappa)F/S=-\alpha/\kappa$. It has solution $F=C S^k-(\alpha/\kappa)S/(1-k)$ for $k=\beta/\kappa\ne1$. Its maximum occurs at $F=(\alpha/\beta)S$; early exponential growth does not imply permanent exponential growth after the large-object reservoir is depleted.

= Collisional age after uniform dynamical depletion
{parent=Collisional cascade}
{title2=$t_{\rm inferred}=fT+(t-T)$}

If a collision operator is homogeneous of degree two in its bin masses, reducing every mass by $f$ slows the subsequent evolution by $f$. If $m(t)$ is the undepleted trajectory and depletion occurs at $T$, the post-depletion trajectory is $m_d(t)=m(T+(t-T)/f)/f$. Compared with a collision-only model starting with normalization $m(0)/f$, the same observed distribution has inferred age $fT+(t-T)$. The distribution alone cannot distinguish the depletion factor from unknown initial normalization and age.

= Collisional-cascade relaxation time
{parent=Collisional cascade}
{title2=$t_c\sim1/R_{\rm cc}$}

A bin with mass $m$ loses mass at approximately $mR_{\rm cc}$, so changing an order-one fraction takes $m/(mR_{\rm cc})=1/R_{\rm cc}$. The <catastrophic planetesimal collision rate> must be evaluated using the contemporaneous projectile population. It supplies a relaxation scale, not an exact equilibration time for an arbitrary fragment kernel.

= Strength-dependent steady collisional-cascade slope
{parent=Collisional cascade}
{title2=$\alpha=(21+b)/(6+b)$}

With size-independent collision speed and <catastrophic disruption threshold> $Q_D^*\propto D^b$, the <catastrophic planetesimal collision rate> scales as $R\propto D^{3-\alpha+b(1-\alpha)/3}$. Multiplying by <mass per logarithmic size bin> gives processed mass proportional to $D^{7-2\alpha+b(1-\alpha)/3}$. <Constant mass flux in a collisional cascade> sets the exponent to zero, yielding the displayed differential-number index when $b\ne-6$. This is the zero-velocity-exponent case of a <velocity-dependent collisional cascade>.

= Depletion feedback on a collision timescale
{parent=Collisional cascade}

Once escape depletes a diameter interval, collision rates must be recomputed from the depleted projectile population. For a local power law $n(D)=K D^{-\alpha}$ and self-similar catastrophic-projectile limits, the rate scales as $KD^{3-\alpha}$. Retaining the original rate after changing $K$ or $\alpha$ can give incorrect transition sizes.

= Secondary cascade below a removal band
{parent=Depletion feedback on a collision timescale}
{title2=$D_-\sim D_0^{8/5}D_+^{-3/5}$}

When a loss mechanism becomes slow again at very small diameters, a depleted intermediate band can feed a lower-normalization <collisional cascade>. For size-independent strength the asymptotic collision-dominated slope returns to $-7/2$. In the local two-limit <Yarkovsky removal> model, matching gives $D_-\sim D_0^{8/5}D_+^{-3/5}$ and normalization ratio $K_-/K\sim(D_0/D_+)^{2/5}$. Coefficients depend on the collision kernel; a sharp join is only an asymptotic sketch.

= Fragment redistribution function
{parent=Collisional cascade}

This function specifies the fraction of disrupted mass assigned to each fragment size interval. A self-similar law depends on fragment-to-parent size ratios, rather than an absolute size. In logarithmic bins its translation invariance gives the constant processed <mass flux> of the interior steady <collisional cascade>. In a removal-dominated interval, it instead sets the fragment source spectrum.

= Removal-dominated fragment size distribution
{parent=Fragment redistribution function}
{title2=$n(D)=S_N(D)t_y(D)$}

If fragments are injected with number spectrum $S_N(D)\propto D^{-\alpha_r}$ and escape faster than they collide, steady balance gives $n(D)=S_N(D)t_y(D)$. Thus $t_y\propto D^\gamma$ changes the differential number index to $\alpha_r-\gamma$. A removed population with size-independent subsequent lifetime retains the source index $\alpha_r$.

= Constant mass flux in a collisional cascade
{parent=Collisional cascade}

In a steady <collisional cascade> supplied at large sizes and depleted only at small sizes, <mass conservation> makes the downward mass flux across every intermediate size threshold equal. With self-similar fragmentation and comparable transfer between logarithmic bins, the mass removed per bin per unit time is consequently independent of size. Nonlocal or size-dependent fragmentation, additional sources, and proximity to the cutoffs can invalidate the simplified bin-by-bin relation.

= Power-law size distribution
{parent=Collisional cascade}

A differential power-law size distribution has $n(D)dD=kD^{-\alpha}dD$ objects with sizes between $D$ and $D+dD$.

= Mass per logarithmic size bin
{parent=Power-law size distribution}
{title2=$m_{\log}\propto D^{4-\alpha}$}

For equal-density spherical bodies with differential number distribution $n(D)\propto D^{-\alpha}$, a fixed logarithmic bin has width $dD=D\,d\log D$. Multiplying the body mass proportional to $D^3$ by $n(D)dD$ gives $m_{\log}\propto D^{4-\alpha}$. Distinguishing differential-number and logarithmic-mass slopes is essential in <collisional cascade> plots.

= Cross-section of a power-law fragment population
{parent=Power-law size distribution}
{title2=$\sigma_{\rm tot}$}

= Cross-sections of a power-law fragment population
{synonym}

For spherical fragments of common <mass density> $\rho$, with $n(D)=KD^{-\alpha}$ between specified diameter cutoffs, <mass normalization of a power-law size distribution> and integration of the <geometric cross-section> give
$$
\sigma_{\rm tot}=\frac{3m}{2\rho}\frac{4-\alpha}{\alpha-3}
\frac{D_{\min}^{3-\alpha}-D_{\max}^{3-\alpha}}
{D_{\max}^{4-\alpha}-D_{\min}^{4-\alpha}},\qquad 3<\alpha<4.
$$
Indeed $m=(\pi\rho K/6)\int D^{3-\alpha}\,dD$ and $\sigma_{\rm tot}=(\pi K/4)\int D^{2-\alpha}\,dD$; eliminating $K$ proves the formula. If both cutoff ratios raised to $\alpha-3$ and $4-\alpha$ are small, respectively, this reduces to $3m(4-\alpha)D_{\min}^{3-\alpha}D_{\max}^{\alpha-4}/[2\rho(\alpha-3)]$. Approaching either endpoint requires the corresponding logarithmic integral.

= Parent-body size from a fragment cross-section
{parent=Cross-section of a power-law fragment population}
{title2=$D_c$}

If a spherical parent and its fragments have the same <mass density>, substitute $m=\pi\rho D_c^3/6$ into the endpoint-dominated <cross-section of a power-law fragment population>. The diameter obeys
$$
D_c^3=\frac{4(\alpha-3)}{\pi(4-\alpha)}
\sigma_{\rm tot}D_{\min}^{\alpha-3}D_{\max}^{4-\alpha}.
$$
This inversion requires specified fragment cutoffs. If $D_{\max}=\eta D_c$ instead, solving that dependence changes the power of $D_c$ to $\alpha-1$. It is useful for estimating the parent <comet> needed to supply an observed <debris disk>, subject to the assumed fragment model.

= Mass normalization of a power-law size distribution
{parent=Power-law size distribution}

For spherical <planetesimals> with <mass density> $\rho$ and total-number distribution $n(D)=KD^{-\alpha}$ between $D_{\min}$ and $D_{\max}$,
$$
M_{\rm tot}=\frac{\pi\rho K}{6}\int_{D_{\min}}^{D_{\max}}D^{3-\alpha}\,dD.
$$
Thus $K=6M_{\rm tot}(4-\alpha)/[\pi\rho(D_{\max}^{4-\alpha}-D_{\min}^{4-\alpha})]$ for $\alpha\ne4$. At $\alpha=4$, use $K=6M_{\rm tot}/[\pi\rho\log(D_{\max}/D_{\min})]$. Divide $n(D)$ by belt volume to obtain the number-density distribution used in a <planetesimal collision rate>.

= Velocity-dependent collisional cascade
{parent=Collisional cascade}

For <catastrophic disruption threshold> $Q_D^*=Q_bD^b$, encounter speed $v_{\rm rel}=v_pD^p$, fixed belt volume, and small impactors, the <catastrophic projectile diameter> scales as $D_{\rm cc}\propto D^{1+(b-2p)/3}$. If $\alpha>1$, small destructive impactors dominate the <catastrophic planetesimal collision rate>, giving
$$
R_{\rm cc}(D)\propto D^{2+p}D_{\rm cc}^{1-\alpha}.
$$
Mass per logarithmic bin scales as $D^{4-\alpha}$. <Constant mass flux in a collisional cascade> then gives
$$
\alpha=\frac{21+b+p}{6+b-2p}.
$$
Size-independent speed and strength recover the <Dohnanyi collisional cascade> slope $7/2$. A size-dependent belt thickness changes this derivation. The broader coupled size-and-velocity problem is studied by https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.0667[Pan and Schlichting].

= Dohnanyi collisional cascade
{c}
{parent=Collisional cascade}

The ideal steady Dohnanyi cascade assumes self-similar fragmentation, size-independent collision speeds, and a size-independent catastrophic disruption threshold. Its differential size distribution is $n(D)\propto D^{-7/2}$.

= Catastrophic disruption threshold
{title2=$Q_D^*$}
{parent=Collisional cascade}

The catastrophic disruption threshold is the impact energy per unit target mass required both to shatter a body and to disperse enough fragments against self-gravity.

= Strength-gravity disruption transition
{parent=Catastrophic disruption threshold}
{title2=$D_w=(aQ_a/(bQ_b))^{1/(a+b)}$}

For $Q_D^*=Q_aD^{-a}+Q_bD^b$, $a,b>0$, <differentiation> gives the minimum at the displayed diameter. Equal strength and gravity contributions occur instead at $D_{\rm eq}=(Q_a/Q_b)^{1/(a+b)}=(b/a)^{1/(a+b)}D_w$. These two transition conventions coincide only for $a=b$.

= Catastrophic projectile diameter
{title2=$D_{\rm cc}$}
{parent=Catastrophic disruption threshold}

For equal-density spherical <planetesimals>, an impactor much smaller than target diameter $D$ causes a <catastrophic planetesimal collision> when its <specific impact energy> reaches $Q_D^*$. Neglecting <gravitational focusing>,
$$
D_{\rm cc}=D\left(\frac{2Q_D^*}{v_{\rm rel}^2}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
When encounter speed is much smaller than the target's <escape velocity>, impact speed is approximately $v_{\rm esc}=D\sqrt{2\pi G\rho/3}$. For $Q_D^*=Q_bD^b$, the focusing-dominated threshold is $D_{\rm cc}=(3Q_b/\pi G\rho)^{1/3}D^{(1+b)/3}$. This assumes the disruption threshold uses impact energy per target mass and neglects energy-coupling efficiency variations.

= Rubblising collision
{parent=Catastrophic disruption threshold}

A rubblising collision shatters a body but does not supply enough energy to disperse its fragments against self-gravity, leaving a gravitationally bound rubble pile.

= Rubblising-to-dispersal collision-rate ratio
{parent=Rubblising collision}
{title2=$R_{\rm rub}/R_{\rm cc}=(Q_D^*/Q_s)^{(\alpha-1)/3}$}

For a common projectile <power-law size distribution>, the rate of impacts exceeding a threshold $Q$ is proportional to $Q^{(1-\alpha)/3}$. Taking the strength-only threshold $Q_s=Q_aD^{-a}$ rather than the full <catastrophic disruption threshold> gives the displayed ratio. Here the strength-shattering rate counts all impacts above $Q_s$, including dispersive ones; subtracting $R_{
m cc}$ gives the bound-remnant-only <rubblising collision> rate. In a gravity-dominated cascade with $\alpha=3$, $a=1/2$, and $b=3/2$, it scales as $D^{4/3}$. Using the actual <strength-gravity disruption transition> minimum diameter gives coefficient $3^{-2/3}$ in the asymptotic rate ratio; treating that diameter as the equal-contribution crossover silently loses this factor.

= Catastrophic planetesimal collision
{parent=Catastrophic disruption threshold}

A catastrophic planetesimal collision has $Q\geq Q_D^*$ and disperses a substantial fraction of the target mass.

= Collision disruption radius under secular stirring
{parent=Catastrophic planetesimal collision}
{title2=$a/a_p\gtrsim Q_D^*a_p/(GM e_p^2)$}

A phase-mixed population secularly forced by a distant eccentric <planet> has typical <orbital eccentricity> proportional to $e_p a/a_p$. Consequently a relative speed of order <orbital eccentricity> times Keplerian speed gives $v_{\rm col}^2\sim e_p^2(a/a_p)GM/a_p$. Comparing <specific impact energy> with a <catastrophic disruption threshold> gives a lower disruption radius $a/a_p\gtrsim Q_D^*a_p/(GM e_p^2)$, up to an order-one coefficient depending on velocity statistics and impact-energy convention. The <planet> <mass> sets the secular stirring time, but cancels from this energy scaling.

= Catastrophic planetesimal collision rate
{title2=$R_{\rm cc}$}
{parent=Catastrophic planetesimal collision}

The catastrophic planetesimal collision rate is the collision rate integrated over impactors large enough to satisfy $Q\geq Q_D^*$.

= Catastrophic collision mass-loss timescale
{parent=Catastrophic planetesimal collision}

The catastrophic collision mass-loss timescale is the target mass divided by the rate at which catastrophic impacts remove mass from its largest remnant.

= Cratering collision
{parent=Collisional cascade}

A cratering collision has $Q<Q_D^*$ and removes part of a target without catastrophically dispersing it.

= Interplanetary dust
{parent=Planetary science}
{wiki}

Interplanetary dust consists of small solid particles orbiting within a planetary system and responding to gravity, radiation, plasma, and collisions.

= Dust tail
{parent=Interplanetary dust}

A dust tail is an extended distribution of grains released from a localized moving source. <Radiation pressure> changes the grains\' orbits; their release times, grain properties and survival probabilities determine the spatial profile.

= Steady dust-tail continuity equation
{parent=Dust tail}
{title2=$n(s)\dot s=\dot N P(t(s))$}

For a single-stream <dust tail> with injection rate $\dot N$, monotone angular distance $s(t)$, and survival probability $P(t)$, the number per unit angle is $n(s)=\dot N P(t(s))/\dot s$. A vanishing angular drift creates an idealized density enhancement; velocity dispersion rounds it.

= Dust-tail caustic
{parent=Steady dust-tail continuity equation}

A zero of the angular drift in a cold single-grain-size <dust tail> produces a formally divergent number density per unit angle. The divergence is integrable when the exact local angle-to-age map is retained. A spread of launch velocities or <radiation-pressure coefficients> removes the singularity.

= Dust-tail angular lag
{parent=Dust tail}
{title2=$\Delta\theta=\theta_d-\theta_p$}

For unwrapped grain and parent azimuths, define a signed lag $\Delta\theta=\theta_d-\theta_p$ and a positive trailing distance $s=-\Delta\theta$. A grain released without a velocity kick from a circular parent orbit has $\Delta\theta=2\beta(\sin\theta_d-\theta_d)+O(\beta^2\theta_d)$. Keeping track of this sign avoids mistaking trailing motion for leading motion.

= Secular phase approximation for a dust tail
{parent=Dust-tail angular lag}

The replacement $s=2\beta(\theta-\sin\theta)\mapsto2\beta\theta$ neglects a bounded orbital ripple in accumulated lag. Substituting the resulting angle into the instantaneous periodic drift gives a convenient $\csc^2(s/(4\beta))$ profile, but is not a uniform small-$\beta$ approximation. The consistent first-order profile retains the implicit angle-to-lag map; near release it gives $n(s)\propto s^{-2/3}$.

= Radiation-pressure coefficient
{title2=$\beta$}
{parent=Interplanetary dust}

The radiation-pressure coefficient is the ratio of outward stellar radiation pressure to inward stellar gravity on a dust grain. The grain therefore feels effective gravitational parameter $(1-\beta)GM_*$.

= Dust orbit released from a circular parent ring
{parent=Radiation-pressure coefficient}

If release imparts no velocity kick, a grain's initial <Kepler orbit> under reduced stellar attraction has $q=r_b$, $Q=r_b/(1-2\beta)$, and $e=\beta/(1-\beta)$ for $0\leq\beta<1/2$. Its <pericentre distance> remains at the <dust birth ring>, while its <apocentre distance> grows with the <radiation-pressure coefficient>.

= Radiation-pressure efficiency
{title2=$Q_{\rm pr}$}
{parent=Radiation-pressure coefficient}

The radiation-pressure efficiency is the radiation-force cross-section of a grain divided by its geometric cross-section. It is of order unity for grains larger than the illuminating wavelength and depends strongly on size and composition in the small-particle regime.

= Radiation-pressure blowout orbit
{parent=Radiation-pressure coefficient}

= Radiation-pressure blowout
{synonym}

A grain released from a circular parent orbit without a relative velocity impulse is unbound when radiation pressure reduces the effective stellar attraction sufficiently. For $\beta>1$, the effective inverse-square force is repulsive.

= Radiation-pressure blowout threshold
{parent=Radiation-pressure blowout orbit}
{title2=$\beta=1/2$}

The zero-kick circular-parent threshold is $\beta=1/2$: below it grains are initially bound, at it their conservative release orbit is parabolic, and above it they are initially unbound. <Poynting–Robertson drag> can modify a marginal orbit, so the threshold is a statement about the instantaneous release orbit, not an exact capture criterion with dissipation included.

= Finson–Probstein model
{c}
{parent=Interplanetary dust}

The Finson–Probstein model follows cometary dust after release with negligible ejection speed under stellar gravity reduced by <radiation pressure>. A grid of <synchrones> and <syndynes> organizes the resulting dust-tail morphology.

= Synchrone
{parent=Finson–Probstein model}

A synchrone is the locus, at one observation time, of grains released at the same time with different <radiation-pressure coefficients>.

= Syndyne
{parent=Finson–Probstein model}

A syndyne is the locus, at one observation time, of grains with the same <radiation-pressure coefficient> released at different times.

= Cometary dust tail
{parent=Finson–Probstein model}
{wiki=Comet_tail}

A cometary dust tail is the extended distribution of solid grains released by a comet. <Radiation pressure> sorts its grains by size: high-$\beta$ grains are displaced rapidly in the anti-stellar direction, while larger low-$\beta$ grains remain closer to the projected orbit and form a more curved trail.

= Steady radial dust outflow
{parent=Interplanetary dust}

For an axisymmetric steady dust outflow with surface density $\Sigma$ and radial speed $v_r$, number or mass conservation gives $2\pi r\Sigma v_r=\text{constant}$.

= Poynting–Robertson drag
{c}
{parent=Interplanetary dust}
{wiki}

Poynting–Robertson drag is the tangential component of stellar radiation force caused by aberration in an orbiting dust grain's frame. It removes orbital energy and angular momentum and drives the grain inward.

= Burst transport under inverse-size radial drag
{parent=Poynting–Robertson drag}
{title2=$r^2(D,t)=r_0^2-2\gamma t/D$}

For grains of fixed diameter $D$ released instantaneously at $r_0$, the drift law $\dot r=-\gamma/(rD)$ integrates to the displayed size-radius map. The time to radius $r<r_0$ is $t_r(D)=D(r_0^2-r^2)/(2\gamma)$. Grains therefore arrive in increasing diameter order. This model of <Poynting–Robertson drag> assumes nearly circular <Kepler orbits>, fixed positive $\gamma$, and no grain loss or diameter changes before arrival. At a positive time, the map is monotone in $D$ and allows the initial <power-law size distribution> to be transformed into a spatial distribution or an arrival-time distribution.

= Surface density of a size-sorted dust burst
{parent=Burst transport under inverse-size radial drag}
{title2=$\Sigma=\frac{m_d(4-\alpha)}{\pi(r_0^2-r^2)[1-\eta^{4-\alpha}]}(t/T_r)^{4-\alpha}$}

For initial dust mass $m_d$ in a ring at $r_0$ with a fixed-diameter <power-law size distribution> of index $\alpha<4$, <burst transport under inverse-size radial drag> maps radius to diameter $D_r=2\gamma t/(r_0^2-r^2)$. Set $T_r=t_r(D_{\max})$ and $\eta=D_{\min}/D_{\max}$. The <surface density> is the azimuthal average: $2\pi r\Sigma\,dr=(\pi\rho K/6)D_r^{3-\alpha}dD_r$. Since $dD_r/dr=2rD_r/(r_0^2-r^2)$, <mass normalization of a power-law size distribution> gives the displayed expression. It is supported where $\eta\le t/T_r\le1$. It applies at $t>0$ before removal at the radius in question; the initial state is a delta-function ring. A local angular density needs additional phase information.

= Dust-burst accretion pulse
{parent=Burst transport under inverse-size radial drag}
{title2=$\dot M_p=\frac{m_d(4-\alpha)}{T[1-\eta^{4-\alpha}]}(t/T)^{3-\alpha}$}

Let the initial dust mass be $m_d$, let its differential number distribution be $KD^{-\alpha}$ with $\alpha<4$ between $D_{\min}$ and $D_{\max}$, and put $\eta=D_{\min}/D_{\max}$. Under <burst transport under inverse-size radial drag>, complete absorption at a fixed radius occurs at $t=TD/D_{\max}$, with $T=t_r(D_{\max})$. The arriving <mass> is proportional to $D^{3-\alpha}dD$. Substitute $D=D_{\max}t/T$ and $dD/dt=D_{\max}/T$ and use <mass normalization of a power-law size distribution> to prove the displayed rate for $\eta T\le t\le T$, with zero rate elsewhere. Its integral is exactly $m_d$. Resonant delays, scattering losses, a finite release duration or grain destruction change this ideal pulse.

= Poynting–Robertson decay of a circumplanetary orbit
{c}
{parent=Poynting–Robertson drag}
{title2=$\langle\dot a\rangle_{\rm cp}$}

Consider a circular <circumplanetary orbit> well inside the <Hill sphere>, with dust speed $u^2=GM_p/a$ and unit plane normal $\widehat{\boldsymbol k}$. During one dust <orbital period>, take the stellar direction $\widehat{\boldsymbol R}$ and flux at the <planet>'s stellar radius $a_p$ as constant. The velocity-dependent <Poynting–Robertson drag> has relative <work>
$$
\langle\dot\varepsilon_p\rangle=-\frac A{a_p^2}
\langle u^2+(\boldsymbol u\cdot\widehat{\boldsymbol R})^2\rangle,\qquad
A=\frac{\beta GM_\star}c.
$$
The constant terms have zero <work> over the closed circular <orbit>. Since $\langle u_i u_j\rangle=(u^2/2)(\delta_{ij}-k_i k_j)$,
$$
\langle\dot a\rangle=-\frac A{a_p^2}a
[3-(\widehat{\boldsymbol k}\cdot\widehat{\boldsymbol R})^2].
$$
The coefficient is three for coplanar stellar and dust <orbits>; it is two for a dust plane normal to the instantaneous stellar direction. Averaging over a circular planetary year at fixed dust <orbital inclination> $I$ gives $3-\sin^2I/2$. Conservative perturbations must remain small enough for this circular-orbit average.

= Inspiral time of circumplanetary dust
{parent=Poynting–Robertson decay of a circumplanetary orbit}
{title2=$t_{\rm cp}$}

For coplanar circular dust, constant $A=\beta GM_\star/c$, fixed stellar radius $a_p$ and initial planetocentric radius $a_0>R_p$, integrating $\dot a=-3Aa/a_p^2$ gives
$$
a(t)=a_0e^{-3At/a_p^2},\qquad
t_{\rm cp}=\frac{a_p^2}{3A}\log\frac{a_0}{R_p}.
$$
Compare the <inspiral time under Poynting–Robertson drag> for a circular <circumstellar orbit>, $t_{\rm cs}=(a_p^2-R_\star^2)/(4A)$. Planetary arrival is not automatically faster; in the point-star limit it is faster only for $a_0/R_p<e^{3/4}$. A different constant orientation coefficient replaces three.

= Poynting–Robertson decay of a circumstellar orbit
{c}
{parent=Poynting–Robertson drag}
{title2=$\langle\dot a\rangle_{\rm cs}$}

With $A=\beta GM_\star/c$ constant, average the drag <work> $\dot\varepsilon=-A(2\dot r^2+v_\theta^2)/r^2$ over a bound <elliptic orbit>. Using $dt=r^2df/h$ gives the integral $\pi(2+3e^2)\mu^2/h^2$, where $\mu$ is the conservative central parameter. Combining with $\dot a=2a^2\dot\varepsilon/\mu$ gives
$$
\langle\dot a\rangle=-\frac A a\frac{2+3e^2}{(1-e^2)^{3/2}}.
$$
This is a weak-drag secular average. It also holds when the conservative parameter is $(1-\beta)GM_\star$, provided a bound <orbit> exists; the conservative parameter cancels but the drag amplitude remains $A$. For a <circular Kepler orbit>, $a^2(t)=a_0^2-4At$.

= First-order stellar radiation force on a blackbody grain
{parent=Poynting–Robertson drag}
{title2=$\boldsymbol F_{\rm rad}$}

For a <blackbody> grain with <geometric cross-section> $\sigma$, <mass> $m$ and stellar-frame <velocity> $\boldsymbol v$, define the <radiation-pressure coefficient> $\beta=L_\star\sigma/(4\pi cGM_\star m)$. To first order in $v/c$, steady absorption and isotropic rest-frame reradiation give
$$
\boldsymbol F_{\rm rad}=\frac{m\beta GM_\star}{r^2}
\left[\left(1-\frac{\dot r}c\right)\widehat{\boldsymbol r}-\frac{\boldsymbol v}c\right].
$$
The relative incoming flux provides $1-\dot r/c$; <photon> reradiation carries stellar-frame <momentum> proportional to $\boldsymbol v/c$. Subtracting static <radiation pressure> gives radial drag $-2m\beta GM_\star\dot r/(cr^2)$ and transverse drag $-m\beta GM_\star v_\theta/(cr^2)$. Non-blackbody grains require their actual <radiation-pressure efficiency>.

= Inspiral time under Poynting–Robertson drag
{parent=Poynting–Robertson drag}
{title2=$t_{\rm PR}$}

For the <Poynting–Robertson drag invariant> $C$ and $A=\beta GM_\star/c$, the orbit-averaged time to the point-star limit is
$$
t_{\rm PR}=\frac{2C^2}{5A}\int_0^{e_0}\frac{u^{3/5}}{(1-u^2)^{3/2}}\,du.
$$
This tends to $a_0^2/(4A)$ for a circular orbit and to $4Q_0^{1/2}q_0^{3/2}/(5A)$ for a highly eccentric orbit. Stellar radius, sublimation, collisions, and failure of orbital averaging can terminate the evolution earlier.

= Apsidal evolution under Poynting–Robertson drag
{parent=Poynting–Robertson drag}

= Poynting–Robertson drag invariant
{c}
{parent=Poynting–Robertson drag}
{title2=$C$}

The orbit-averaged <Poynting–Robertson drag> equations preserve $C=a(1-e^2)e^{-4/5}$ for $0<e<1$. This relates shrinking <semi-major axis> to decreasing <orbital eccentricity>; a circular orbit is treated by its separate limiting solution.

= Stellar-wind drag
{parent=Interplanetary dust}

Stellar-wind drag is the tangential force exerted by a star's outflow on an orbiting grain. Like Poynting–Robertson drag, it removes orbital energy and angular momentum.

= Resonant trapping of dust
{parent=Interplanetary dust}

During inward migration, dust can be captured into an exterior mean-motion resonance with a planet. A phase-shifted conjunction then lets planetary torque replace angular momentum lost to Poynting–Robertson drag.

= Damped first-order resonant complex eccentricity
{parent=Resonant trapping of dust}
{title2=$\dot z=-iC-(A+iBx)z$}

For an exterior $(p+1):p$ <mean-motion resonance>, use <mean longitude> in $\phi=(p+1)\lambda-p\lambda_p-\varpi$, $z=e\exp(i\phi)$, $x=(a-a_r)/a_r$ and $B=3pn_p/2$. Given $\dot e=-C\sin\phi-Ae$ and $\dot\varpi=(C/e)\cos\phi$, the leading Keplerian detuning gives $\dot\phi=-Bx-(C/e)\cos\phi$. Differentiating $z$ and using $e^{i\phi}(\sin\phi+i\cos\phi)=i$ proves the displayed equation. It retains resonant forcing and <eccentricity damping> without the coordinate singularity at $e=0$. True longitude adds a fast orbital ripple and does not yield this autonomous averaged equation directly.

= Fixed points of a damped first-order exterior resonance
{parent=Damped first-order resonant complex eccentricity}
{title2=$e_f^2=2/[5(p+1)]$}

For positive locally constant $A,C$, supplement <damped first-order resonant complex eccentricity> by $\dot a/a=-2(p+1)Ce\sin\phi-4A/5$. Balancing the two dissipative rates gives $e_f^2=2/[5(p+1)]$, $\sin\phi_f=-Ae_f/C$ and $Bx_f=-(C/e_f)\cos\phi_f$. Two fixed points exist when $A<C\sqrt{5(p+1)/2}$. The negative-cosine branch has $x_f>0$ and is stable in this leading model: a phase increase raises the positive resonant <torque>, raises <semi-major axis>, lowers <mean motion>, and reverses the phase drift. The positive-cosine branch has the opposite feedback.

For a coupled verification put $z=u+iv$. The <characteristic polynomial> at either fixed point is $\lambda^3+2A\lambda^2+[A^2+B^2x_f^2-2B(p+1)Cu_f]\lambda-4AB(p+1)Cu_f$. For $u_f<0$ its positive coefficients satisfy the cubic <Routh-Hurwitz stability criterion>, since the remaining inequality reduces to $2A(A^2+B^2x_f^2)>0$. For $u_f>0$ its negative constant term forces a positive real root. At the equality threshold the equilibria merge and an <eigenvalue> is zero. This is local stability for the frozen smooth-coefficient approximation, not a conclusion for arbitrary rapidly varying dissipation laws.

= Dissipative resonant eccentricity equilibrium
{title2=$e_{\rm eq}^2$}
{parent=Resonant trapping of dust}

For <resonant trapping of dust> in an exterior $(p+q):p$ <mean-motion resonance>, let <Poynting–Robertson drag> have parameter $A$. Holding <semi-major axis> $a$ fixed and retaining secular rates through second order in <orbital eccentricity> gives
$$
\frac{d(e^2)}{dt}=\frac A{a^2}\left[\frac{2q}{p+q}-\frac{5p-q}{p+q}e^2\right],
\qquad e_{\rm eq}^2=\frac{2q}{5p-q},\qquad
\tau=\frac{a^2(p+q)}{A(5p-q)}.
$$
For $5p>q$, squared <orbital eccentricity> approaches this equilibrium exponentially in the truncated model. If the predicted value is not small, higher-order terms are needed for quantitative dynamics. Finite planetary torque also imposes a minimum eccentricity for balance, which alone does not guarantee capture during migration.

= Pericentre geometry of an exterior resonance
{parent=Resonant trapping of dust}

At pericentre, <mean longitude> equals <longitude of periapsis>, so <resonant argument> $\phi=(p+q)\lambda-p\lambda_p-q\varpi$ becomes $\phi=p(\varpi-\lambda_p)$. The pericentre angle in the planet's rotating frame is $\phi/p$ modulo $2\pi/p$. For coprime $p,q$, there are $p$ distinct pericentre directions before the trajectory closes. <Resonant-argument libration> restricts these directions and supplies <resonance protection>.

= Stellar astrophysics
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

Stellar astrophysics studies the structure, formation, energy production, observable properties, and evolution of stars.

= Stellar gravity
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{title2=$GM_\star/r^2$}

Outside a spherical <star>, its gravitational acceleration is $-GM_\star\mathbf r/r^3$. It provides the dominant central force for <Kepler orbits> in a star-dominated planetary system.

= Stellar population
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}

A stellar population is an ensemble of <stars> described by its formation times, initial <masses>, composition and present <stellar evolution> states. Number fractions must be distinguished from mass-weighted fractions. Shared formation times can correlate the states of components of a <binary star> even when their initial masses have <independence>.

= Age-metallicity degeneracy
{parent=Stellar population}

Integrated spectra can confuse older <stellar populations> with more metal-rich ones because both can redden the light and change <absorption lines>. Line strengths depend on <stellar metallicity>, temperatures, ages, and abundance ratios. Weak metal lines in a young population dominated by hot bright <stars> need not indicate low <stellar metallicity>.

= Evolutionary-state selection by stellar lifetimes
{parent=Stellar population}

Given a birth-mass distribution and age distribution, an evolutionary state occupies the mass-age set where its starting lifetime has elapsed and its ending lifetime has not. The state number fraction is the <probability> measure of this set. <Initial mass function> tail coordinates and the <probability integral transform> can turn these weighted regions into simple geometric areas.

= Stellar lifetime regions in a mass-age diagram
{parent=Evolutionary-state selection by stellar lifetimes}
{title2=$\tau_a(M)<t<\tau_b(M)$}

A phase bounded by mass-dependent lifetimes $\tau_a(M)$ and $\tau_b(M)$ occupies $\tau_a(M)<t<\tau_b(M)$, clipped to the population age range. Its boundaries can be mapped into uniform <probability> coordinates to evaluate evolutionary-state fractions.

= Birth-age distribution under constant star formation
{parent=Stellar population}
{title2=$Y(t)=t/T\quad(0\le t\le T)$}

If a population forms at a constant number rate for duration $T$ and every birth object is retained in the census, its present ages are uniform on $[0,T]$. A fixed <initial mass function> with finite mean birth mass also makes constant mass-based <star formation rate> equivalent to constant number rate.

= Stellar atmosphere
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

A <stellar atmosphere> is the outer region that determines the escaping spectrum and matches the interior <stellar structure> to its radiation boundary conditions. A <photosphere> is the layer near unit <optical depth>, not a zero-temperature boundary. The inward intensity at the outer surface vanishes when there is no external illumination.

= Stellar chemical abundance
{parent=Stellar atmosphere}
{title2=$X_i,\quad n_i/n_{\rm H}$}

A stellar abundance can be expressed as an elemental or isotopic mass fraction, or as a number ratio to <hydrogen>. Inferring it from a <spectral line> requires a model for level populations, <opacity>, radiation and velocity fields. Agreement between suitable ionization stages and weak photospheric lines tests whether the adopted <stellar atmosphere> is adequate.

= Non-LTE stellar atmosphere
{parent=Stellar atmosphere}
{title2=$\sum_jn_jP_{ji}=n_i\sum_jP_{ij}$}

= Non-LTE atmosphere
{synonym}

When radiative rates compete with or dominate collisions, level populations need statistical equilibrium coupled to <radiative transfer>, rather than thermal population laws at the local <temperature>. Radiation from other depths can make the local radiation field differ from the <Planck function>. This changes ionization and spectral-line strengths, so a geometrically thin atmosphere may still require non-LTE abundance modelling.

= Chromosphere
{parent=Stellar atmosphere}
{title2=$dT/dh>0\text{ in a heated layer}$}
{wiki}

A heated layer above a photosphere can have an outward <temperature> increase. Strong lines formed there may show emission where an outward-cooling photosphere would produce absorption. The energy maintaining the <temperature> inversion is additional atmospheric heating; the exact height and transfer regime vary between stars.

= Spectral line
{parent=Stellar atmosphere}
{title2=$\nu\simeq\nu_0$}
{wiki}

A narrow spectral feature records enhanced emission or absorption near a transition frequency. In a <stellar atmosphere>, its strength depends on atomic populations, <opacity>, velocity fields and the <source function>. Lines therefore diagnose composition as well as the physical atmosphere in which they form.

= Emission line
{parent=Spectral line}
{title2=$I_{\rm line}>I_{\rm continuum}$}

An <emission line> is a localized excess of <specific intensity> above the continuum. Heated chromospheric material can give a line source above the photospheric continuum source. Optically thin circumstellar gas can also add line photons through recombination or collisional excitation. Such line formation need not obey a thermal photospheric source prescription.

= Absorption line
{parent=Spectral line}
{title2=$I_{\rm line}<I_{\rm continuum}$}

An <absorption line> is a deficit of observed spectral <specific intensity> relative to the surrounding continuum. In a photosphere with <temperature> falling outward, increased line <opacity> makes photons emerge from cooler layers than the continuum. For an absorption-dominated <LTE> source, the <Planck function> then supplies a smaller line <specific intensity>. A foreground layer with source below the incident continuum gives another direct transfer description.

= Star
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

= Low-mass main-sequence star
{parent=Star}

A low-mass star maintained by sustained <hydrogen burning>, rather than the continuing cooling of a <brown dwarf>. Near the <hydrogen-burning minimum mass> its radius is of order a Jovian radius. Stars below roughly $0.35M_\odot$ are approximately fully convective, with a radiative photosphere; mass, composition and age affect the precise transition.

= Solar radius
{parent=Star}
{title2=$R_\odot$}
{wiki}

The solar radius is the conventional unit of length based on the radius of the Sun. The nominal value is $R_\odot=6.957\times10^8\,\mathrm m$.

= Sun
{c}
{parent=Star}
{wiki}

= Stellar pulsation
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}

= Kappa mechanism
{parent=Stellar pulsation}
{title2=$\kappa$ mechanism}

= Opacity mechanism
{synonym}

The opacity mechanism drives <stellar pulsation> when compression increases <opacity> in a layer, trapping heat at a phase that feeds mechanical oscillation. Helium <ionization> zones provide this driving in <Cepheid variables>.

= Solar mass
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{title2=$M_\odot$}
{wiki}

= Cepheid variable
{c}
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

A Cepheid variable is a pulsating star whose period is tightly related to its luminosity, allowing its distance to be inferred from its measured period and <apparent magnitude>.

= Supernova
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

= Supernovae
{synonym}

A supernova is a luminous stellar explosion produced by thermonuclear disruption or the collapse of a stellar core.

= Core-collapse supernova
{parent=Supernova}

= Core-collapse supernovae
{synonym}

Collapse of a massive stellar core can launch an explosion that ejects material processed by hydrostatic and explosive burning. Whether an explosion succeeds, and how much material escapes instead of falling back, depend on the core and envelope structure. A <failed supernova> can retain much of the nucleosynthetic core; initial stellar mass alone is not a complete prediction.

= Failed supernova
{parent=Core-collapse supernova}
{wiki}

Core collapse can fail to produce an ordinary successful ejecting <supernova>, leaving a <black hole> and retaining much of the processed core. Some envelope material can still escape, depending on the event; failure is not equivalent to a universal zero-yield prescription.

= Pair-instability supernova
{parent=Supernova}

In a hot radiation-supported stellar core, <electron-positron thermal pair abundance> can grow as radiation energy is converted into pair rest energy. The <stellar adiabatic exponent> can fall below $4/3$, permitting contraction and rapid heating. If the resulting explosive <nuclear fusion> releases enough energy, it disrupts the star in a pair-instability supernova. Weaker events can produce pulses rather than full disruption, and still heavier cores can collapse without an explosion. Thus the thermal relativity scale alone neither establishes the instability nor predicts its outcome.

= Type Ia supernova
{c}
{parent=Supernova}
{wiki}

= Type Ia supernovae
{c}
{synonym}

A Type Ia supernova is a thermonuclear supernova whose standardized luminosity makes it a useful astronomical distance indicator.

= Radioactively powered Type Ia supernova light curve
{parent=Type Ia supernova}
{title2=$L_{\mathrm{peak}}\propto M_{56}$}

The nickel-56 to cobalt-56 to iron-56 decay chain supplies gamma rays and charged particles that heat supernova ejecta. Early expansion and <photon> diffusion delay the escaping optical light; after a peak on a scale of weeks, the <luminosity> falls as decay power declines and gamma rays escape more readily. With comparable diffusion times and deposition efficiencies, peak <luminosity> scales approximately with the nickel-56 <mass>. Using one fixed absolute <luminosity> without accounting for composition-dependent yields can therefore bias standard-candle distances.

= Metallicity dependence of nickel-56 yield
{parent=Type Ia supernova}
{title2=$X_{56}=1-\frac{27}{11}X_{22}$}

Approximate a fixed <mass> of iron-group freeze-out ashes by nickel-56 and iron-54, with no further weak neutronization and negligible residual light particles. Their electron fractions are $1/2$ and $26/54$. Conservation of the initial <electron fraction in stellar matter>, $1/2-X_{22}/22$, then gives the displayed <nickel> <mass> fraction. Its argument is the initial neon-22 <mass> fraction, which need not equal the earlier <nitrogen> metallicity. The two-isotope mass-budget approximation is not an exact full nuclear equilibrium with all free-particle densities set to zero.

= Brown dwarf
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

A brown dwarf is a self-gravitating object too light to sustain ordinary hydrogen fusion. Electron degeneracy limits its contraction, leaving radii near that of Jupiter across a broad mass range.

= CoRoT-3b
{c}
{parent=Brown dwarf}
{wiki}

= Deuterium-burning mass
{parent=Brown dwarf}

The conventional deuterium-burning boundary is near $13$ Jupiter masses, with composition-dependent variation. It is a useful classification scale rather than a sharp formation-based distinction between planets and brown dwarfs.

= Hydrogen-burning minimum mass
{parent=Brown dwarf}

The hydrogen-burning minimum mass is the lowest mass that can sustain core hydrogen fusion, about $0.075$--$0.08$ solar masses for near-solar composition. Less massive brown dwarfs cool continually after any deuterium burning ends.

= Contraction-limited central temperature
{parent=Hydrogen-burning minimum mass}
{title2=$T_{\max}\propto\mu_iM^{4/3}$}

A fixed <stellar homology> profile with $P=\rho\mathcal RT/\mu_i+K_{\rm nr}\rho^{5/3}$ gives
$$
T_c=\frac{\mu_i}{\mathcal R}\left(AGM/R-BK_{\rm nr}M^{2/3}/R^2\right),
$$
where $A,B>0$. As a function of $1/R$ this is a concave quadratic; its maximum is $A^2G^2\mu_iM^{4/3}/(4BK_{\rm nr}\mathcal R)$, at $R=2BK_{\rm nr}M^{-1/3}/(AG)$. At the maximum, the central <electron degeneracy pressure> and thermal pressure are equal. If this temperature is insufficient for sustained <hydrogen burning>, the object becomes a <brown dwarf>. Only the positive-temperature segment represents a physical sequence.

= Star formation
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

Star formation is the gravitational collapse and fragmentation of cold interstellar gas into protostars and eventually stars. Cooling, turbulence, magnetic fields, rotation, and feedback determine its efficiency and characteristic masses.

= Gravitational fragmentation
{parent=Star formation}

<Gravitational fragmentation> divides a self-gravitating gas distribution into condensations that can undergo separate collapse. Pressure support, cooling and rotation influence the scales and outcome; it is not equivalent to an instantaneous equal-mass fission of a rigidly rotating sphere. <Isothermal fragmentation> is a useful limiting case in which thermal adjustment keeps the gas temperature approximately fixed.

= Kennicutt–Schmidt law
{c}
{parent=Star formation}
{wiki}

The Kennicutt–Schmidt law relates star-formation-rate surface density to gas surface density, commonly as $\Sigma_{\rm SFR}=A\Sigma_g^N$. Disk-averaged total-gas measurements give $N$ near $1.4$, while resolved molecular-gas relations are often closer to linear.

= Gas-depletion time
{title2=$t_{\rm dep}$}
{parent=Kennicutt–Schmidt law}

The gas-depletion time is the time required to consume a gas reservoir at its current star-formation rate, $t_{\rm dep}=M_g/\dot M_*$. It ignores replenishment, returned stellar mass, and changes in the rate.

= Star-formation efficiency per free-fall time
{title2=$\epsilon_{\rm ff}$}
{parent=Gas-depletion time}

The star-formation efficiency per free-fall time is the fraction of gas converted into stars during one gravitational free-fall time, $\epsilon_{\rm ff}=t_{\rm ff}/t_{\rm dep}$. Molecular clouds commonly have values of order one per cent.

= Jeans mass
{title2=$M_J$}
{c}
{parent=Star formation}
{wiki}

The Jeans mass is the characteristic gas mass above which self-gravity overcomes pressure support. Up to the convention used to define the collapsing region,
$$
M_J\propto\frac{c_s^3}{G^{3/2}\rho^{1/2}}
\propto T^{3/2}\rho^{-1/2}
$$
for an ideal gas of fixed composition.

= Isothermal fragmentation
{parent=Jeans mass}

During isothermal collapse, $T$ is constant and the <Jeans mass> decreases as $\rho^{-1/2}$. Successively denser regions can therefore become independently unstable and fragment to smaller masses.

= Adiabatic suppression of fragmentation
{parent=Jeans mass}

For adiabatic monatomic gas, $T\propto\rho^{2/3}$ and the <Jeans mass> increases as $\rho^{1/2}$. Once cooling fails and collapse becomes adiabatic, further fragmentation is suppressed.

= Initial mass function
{title2=IMF}
{parent=Star formation}
{wiki}

The initial mass function is the distribution of stellar birth masses. It records the mass scales selected by cloud fragmentation, accretion, feedback, and stellar multiplicity.

= Population II star
{c}
{parent=Initial mass function}
{wiki=Stellar_population#Population_II_stars}

A Population II star is an old metal-poor star formed from gas enriched by earlier stellar generations. Metal and dust cooling let enriched gas fragment to lower masses than primordial gas under otherwise comparable conditions.

= Population III star
{c}
{parent=Initial mass function}
{wiki=Stellar_population#Population_III_stars}

A Population III star is a first-generation star formed from essentially metal-free primordial gas. Limited cooling generally raises its characteristic fragmentation mass and favors a more top-heavy <initial mass function> than in enriched star formation.

= Stellar structure
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

Stellar structure follows from mass conservation, hydrostatic equilibrium, energy generation, and energy transport together with an equation of state and opacity law.

= Uniformly rotating stellar hydrostatic equilibrium
{parent=Stellar structure}
{title2=$\nabla P=-\rho\nabla\phi,\ \nabla^2\phi=4\pi G\rho-2\Omega^2$}

Uniform <solid-body rotation> adds the <centrifugal potential> $-\Omega^2\varpi^2/2$ to the gravitational potential. In the corotating frame, <hydrostatic equilibrium> becomes $\nabla P=-\rho\nabla\phi$. The <Poisson equation> gives $\nabla^2\phi=4\pi G\rho-2\Omega^2$. On regular connected <equipotential surfaces>, $P$ is constant; taking the curl shows that $\rho$ is constant too. Uniform composition and an invertible equation of state then make $T$ constant on each such surface. These statements do not require $|\nabla\phi|$ to be constant there.

= Roche model of a uniformly rotating star
{parent=Uniformly rotating stellar hydrostatic equilibrium}
{c}
{title2=$\phi=-GM/r-\Omega^2\varpi^2/2$}

This approximation represents the <gravitational potential> by a central point mass while retaining the <centrifugal potential>. A surface through polar radius $R_p$ satisfies $R_p/r+q(r/R_p)^2\sin^2\theta/2=1$, where $q=\Omega^2R_p^3/(GM)$. The inner closed surface is regular below $q=8/27$; at the limiting value its equatorial radius is $3R_p/2$ and equatorial effective gravity vanishes. The approximation is useful for illustrating oblateness and <gravity darkening>, rather than modeling the density distribution of every rotating star.

= Radiative gradient in a uniformly rotating star
{parent=Uniformly rotating stellar hydrostatic equilibrium}
{title2=$\nabla_{\rm rad}=\frac{3\kappa PL}{16\pi acGmT^4}\left(1-\frac{\Omega^2V}{2\pi Gm}\right)^{-1}$}

The <divergence theorem> and <Poisson equation> give $\int_S\nabla\phi\cdot d\mathbf S=4\pi Gm-2\Omega^2V$ on a bounding <equipotential surface>. <Equipotential luminosity conservation with stellar circulation> then fixes $f=L/(4\pi Gm-2\Omega^2V)$. Combining $dT/d\phi=-f/\chi$, $dP/d\phi=-\rho$ and <radiative conductivity> gives the displayed logarithmic temperature-pressure gradient. Both appearances of mass mean the enclosed mass $m$; only on the outer boundary can both be replaced by the total mass.

= Meridional circulation in a star
{parent=Uniformly rotating stellar hydrostatic equilibrium}

= Stellar meridional circulation
{synonym}

Slow circulation in planes containing the rotation axis can transport entropy through a rotating star while the leading mechanical balance remains nearly <hydrostatic equilibrium>. Its steady mass flux satisfies the <continuity equation>. It is distinct from assuming an exactly motionless radiative interior, and from turbulent <convection>.

= Equipotential luminosity conservation with stellar circulation
{parent=Meridional circulation in a star}
{title2=$\int_S\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S=\int_V\rho\epsilon\,dV$}

For steady <stellar meridional circulation>, $T\nabla s=\nabla(h+\phi)$ and $\nabla\cdot(\rho\mathbf v)=0$. Thus $\rho\mathbf v\cdot T\nabla s=\nabla\cdot[\rho\mathbf v(h+\phi)]$. In the leading <uniformly rotating stellar hydrostatic equilibrium>, $h+\phi$ is constant on an <equipotential surface>; its total advected flux vanishes because the net mass flux vanishes. Integrating the energy equation then identifies the outward <luminosity> with enclosed nuclear energy generation, even though local heat advection is nonzero.

= Radiative-equilibrium obstruction in a rotating barotropic star
{parent=Uniformly rotating stellar hydrostatic equilibrium}
{title2=$\nabla\cdot(f\nabla\phi)=f'|\nabla\phi|^2+f\nabla^2\phi$}

= von Zeipel paradox
{synonym}
{c}

On a regular <equipotential surface>, $f,f',\rho$ and $\nabla^2\phi$ are constant, but $|\nabla\phi|^2$ generally varies. Consequently a flux $\mathbf F=f(\phi)\nabla\phi$ cannot ordinarily satisfy local <radiative equilibrium> $\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=\rho\epsilon$ at all latitudes. Special cases such as $f'=0$ or spherical symmetry evade this obstruction. <Stellar meridional circulation> supplies heat advection in the leading hydrostatic treatment.

= Gravity darkening
{parent=Uniformly rotating stellar hydrostatic equilibrium}
{wiki}

<Gravity darkening> is the variation of surface brightness and <effective temperature> with effective gravity over a rotating or tidally distorted star. Lower equatorial gravity generally corresponds to cooler equatorial regions. The <radiative gravity-darkening law> applies to the idealized uniformly rotating, radiatively transporting, composition-uniform case; its exponent is not universal for every stellar envelope.

= Radiative gravity-darkening law
{parent=Gravity darkening}
{title2=$F_n\propto g,\ T_e\propto g^{1/4}$}

= von Zeipel theorem
{synonym}
{c}

In <uniformly rotating stellar hydrostatic equilibrium>, composition-uniform thermodynamic quantities are functions of the total potential. <Radiative conductivity> gives $\mathbf F=f(\phi)\nabla\phi$, with $f=-\chi\,dT/d\phi$. On one surface, $f$ is constant, so the outward flux is $F_n=f g$. The <Stefan–Boltzmann law> therefore gives $T_e\propto g^{1/4}$. This is a surface-flux law: the material temperature constant on an interior equipotential is not the latitude-dependent emergent <effective temperature>.

= Uniform-density stellar model
{parent=Stellar structure}
{title2=$\rho=3M/(4\pi R^3)$}

= Constant-density stellar model
{synonym}

A uniform-density spherical <hydrostatic equilibrium> model with zero surface pressure has $P(r)=2\pi G\rho^2(R^2-r^2)/3$, $M=4\pi\rho R^3/3$ and gravitational energy $-3GM^2/(5R)$. A <perfect gas> can have this mechanical structure with varying <temperature> and entropy; the imposed equilibrium density does not require incompressible perturbations.

= Linear-density stellar model
{parent=Stellar structure}
{title2=$\rho(r)=\rho_c(1-r/R)$}

An imposed spherical profile $\rho=\rho_c(1-r/R)$ gives $M=\pi\rho_cR^3/3$ in <hydrostatic equilibrium>. For an <ideal gas> with zero surface pressure, $P=P_c(1-x)^2(1+2x-9x^2/5)$ and $T=T_c(1-x)(1+2x-9x^2/5)$, where $x=r/R$, $P_c=5GM^2/(4\pi R^4)$ and $T_c=5GM/(12\mathcal RR)$. The density has a central cusp and temperature initially rises outward, so positive outward <radiative diffusion> with regular positive nuclear heating cannot satisfy this imposed profile near the centre.

= Stellar energy balance equation
{parent=Stellar structure}
{title2=$\partial L/\partial m=\epsilon_{\rm nuc}-\epsilon_\nu-Du/Dt-PD(1/\rho)/Dt$}

For a slowly evolving spherical star, the Lagrangian energy equation separates nuclear rest-mass release, <stellar neutrino energy loss>, thermal storage and pressure-volume work. Specific internal energy and nuclear energy conventions must avoid counting nuclear mass changes or escaping reaction-neutrino energy twice. At fixed composition the <gravothermal stellar energy generation> is $-TDS/Dt$. Energy transport processes redistribute heat rather than creating it.

= Stellar neutrino energy loss
{parent=Stellar energy balance equation}
{title2=$\epsilon_\nu\ge0$}

Escaping <neutrinos> remove energy produced by nuclear reactions or drawn from thermal reservoirs. Pair annihilation, plasma excitations, photoneutrino emission and electron-ion bremsstrahlung provide thermal losses. This reduces photon <luminosity> or increases the required nuclear and gravothermal supply. At sufficiently extreme collapse densities, <neutrino> transport must replace a free-escape loss approximation.

= Gravothermal stellar energy generation
{parent=Stellar energy balance equation}
{title2=$\epsilon_{\rm grav}=-Du/Dt-PD(1/\rho)/Dt$}

This term accounts for thermal storage and compression or expansion in the <stellar energy balance equation>. At fixed composition the <first law of thermodynamics> makes it $-TDS/Dt$. <Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction> can supply positive <luminosity>, while thermal adjustment may locally absorb energy. It is not simply the full change in gravitational potential energy: the <stellar virial theorem> also requires changes in internal energy.

= Stellar gas-pressure fraction
{parent=Stellar structure}
{title2=$\beta=P_{\rm gas}/(P_{\rm gas}+P_{\rm rad})$}

The gas-pressure fraction describes the relative support by an <ideal gas> and <radiation pressure>. For monatomic gas plus equilibrium radiation, it determines the <adiabatic exponents of a monatomic gas-radiation mixture>. Its value at the unperturbed state may be specified, but it generally changes during an isentropic parcel displacement.

= Uniform gas-pressure fraction and constant-luminosity radiative-envelope incompatibility
{parent=Stellar gas-pressure fraction}
{title2=$\beta,\kappa\text{ constant}\ \Longrightarrow\ L_r/m=4\pi cG(1-\beta)/\kappa$}

= Constant-beta radiative-envelope obstruction
{synonym}

For a radiative envelope, $dP_{\rm rad}/dP=\kappa L_r/(4\pi cGm)$. Exactly uniform <stellar gas-pressure fraction> instead requires this derivative to be $1-\beta$. Constant opacity therefore requires $L_r$ to increase in proportion to enclosed mass. A positive-density finite-mass envelope with no local energy generation has constant $L_r$ and cannot satisfy all these conditions pointwise. The <Eddington-model convective-core mass fraction> uses constant $\beta$ as a global approximation, rather than an exact detailed envelope constraint.

= Stellar thermal equilibrium
{parent=Stellar structure}
{title2=$L_{\rm surface}=L_{\rm heat}-L_\nu$}

In <stellar structure>, stellar thermal equilibrium means that internal heating balances the surface <luminosity> and any energy loss through neutrinos, with negligible secular storage of thermal <energy> on the timescale considered. A star can satisfy this balance while its interior is hotter than its surface and <heat> flows outward. It is therefore different from equal-temperature <thermal equilibrium> between systems. Neglecting neutrino losses, the balance is $L_{\rm surface}=L_{\rm heat}$. In a slowly mass-losing <donor star>, a <stellar thermal equilibrium> radius sequence applies only when thermal adjustment is fast compared with the mass-loss timescale; rapid loss instead requires the adiabatic <stellar radius response exponent>.

= Stellar equation-of-state regime diagram
{parent=Stellar structure}

A <temperature> versus density diagram separates classical, degenerate, nonrelativistic and relativistic stellar-matter limits. The <electron> degeneracy boundary is $T\sim T_F(\rho)$, with slope $2/3$ at low density and $1/3$ at high density. Thermal relativity is controlled by $k_BT\sim m_ec^2$, while relativity of degenerate <electrons> is controlled by $p_F\sim m_ec$. <Photon> <pressure> and thermal electron-positron pairs add further crossovers; all depend on composition and are gradual, not thermodynamic phase boundaries.

= Ionic Coulomb coupling parameter
{parent=Stellar equation-of-state regime diagram}
{title2=$\Gamma=Z^2e^2/(4\pi\epsilon_0a_i k_BT)$}

= Coulomb coupling parameter of ions
{c}
{synonym}

For one ionic species of <number density> $n_i$, the ion-sphere radius is $a_i=(3/(4\pi n_i))^{1/3}$. The ionic Coulomb coupling parameter compares the interaction energy from <Coulomb's law> at that separation with thermal energy $k_BT$. At fixed composition, $\Gamma\propto\rho^{1/3}/T$. Values near one mark significant corrections to the ideal ion gas; strong coupling can lead to an ordered solid. Screening, mixtures and quantum motion modify detailed transitions, so this estimate is not a universal crystallization boundary. It is separate from the electron degeneracy criterion based on the <electron Fermi temperature>.

= Radiation-to-degeneracy pressure boundary
{parent=Stellar equation-of-state regime diagram}

Equating <radiation pressure> to cold <electron degeneracy pressure> gives $T=[3P_e(\rho)/a_{\rm rad}]^{1/4}$. Because the two <electron> <pressure> limits scale as $\rho^{5/3}$ and $\rho^{4/3}$, this boundary has logarithmic slopes $5/12$ and $1/3$, respectively. Above it, <photon> <pressure> can dominate even when the net <electrons> remain degenerate; degeneracy of one component and dominance of the total <pressure> are different criteria. Thermal pairs modify the high-temperature <pressure> comparison.

= Radiation-to-gas pressure boundary
{parent=Stellar equation-of-state regime diagram}

For a nondegenerate fully ionized gas, equating $P_{\rm gas}=\rho k_BT/(\mu_{\rm mol}m_u)$ with $P_\gamma=a_{\rm rad}T^4/3$ gives $T^3=3k_B\rho/(a_{\rm rad}\mu_{\rm mol}m_u)$. Radiation dominates at higher <temperature> for fixed density. A degenerate <electron> gas requires its degeneracy <pressure> instead of extrapolating this ideal-gas formula.

= Thermal electron relativistic threshold
{parent=Stellar equation-of-state regime diagram}
{title2=$T_*=m_ec^2/k_B$}

In a dilute <electron> gas, thermal kinetic energies become relativistic near $k_BT=m_ec^2$, corresponding to $T_*\simeq5.93\times10^9\,\mathrm K$. This is a crossover in the momentum distribution, not an abrupt <temperature> transition. Degenerate <electrons> can instead be relativistic at much lower <temperature> if their <Fermi momentum> exceeds $m_ec$.

= Stellar composition profile
{parent=Stellar structure}

A stellar composition profile gives chemical mass fractions against radius or <enclosed mass>. Flat portions can indicate mixing in a <convective core> or <convective envelope>, while burning shells and moving convective boundaries leave gradients and discontinuities.

= Stellar structure equations
{parent=Stellar structure}

The stellar structure equations describe spherical hydrostatic stars through radial conservation of mass, force balance, energy generation, and energy transport. An equation of state, opacity, energy-generation law, and boundary conditions close the system.

= Stellar adiabatic exponent
{title2=$\Gamma_i$}
{parent=Stellar structure}

The stellar adiabatic exponents describe pressure and temperature responses to an isentropic density change:
$$
\Gamma_1=\left(\frac{\partial\log P}{\partial\log\rho}\right)_s,
\qquad
\Gamma_3-1=\left(\frac{\partial\log T}{\partial\log\rho}\right)_s,
\qquad
\frac{\Gamma_2}{\Gamma_2-1}
=\left(\frac{\partial\log P}{\partial\log T}\right)_s.
$$

= Adiabatic exponents of a monatomic gas-radiation mixture
{parent=Stellar adiabatic exponent}
{title2=$\Gamma_1=(32-24\beta-3\beta^2)/(24-21\beta)$}

For fixed-composition monatomic <ideal gas> plus equilibrium <radiation pressure>, specific internal energy is $u=3\mathcal RT/2+a_{\rm r}T^4/\rho$. An <isentropic process> satisfies $du=P\,d\rho/\rho^2$, giving $\Gamma_3-1=(8-6\beta)/(24-21\beta)$ and
$$
\Gamma_2=\frac{32-24\beta-3\beta^2}{24-18\beta-3\beta^2},\qquad
\nabla_{\rm ad}=\frac{8-6\beta}{32-24\beta-3\beta^2}.
$$
The <stellar gas-pressure fraction> must be allowed to change along the adiabat. The pure-radiation and pure-gas limits give $\Gamma_1=\Gamma_2=4/3$ and $5/3$, respectively. Partial ionization or different gas heat capacities change these formulas.

= Specific heats of a monatomic gas-radiation mixture
{parent=Adiabatic exponents of a monatomic gas-radiation mixture}
{title2=$c_P=\mathcal R(32-24\beta-3\beta^2)/(2\beta^2)$}

For fixed-composition monatomic <ideal gas> plus equilibrium <blackbody radiation>, let $\mathcal R=k/(\mu H)$ and $\beta=P_g/P$. The mass-specific heats are $c_P=\mathcal R(32-24\beta-3\beta^2)/(2\beta^2)$ and $c_V=\mathcal R(24-21\beta)/(2\beta)$. The <specific-heat ratio> is $\Gamma_1/\beta$, not generally any one of the <stellar adiabatic exponents>. Differentiate at constant total pressure rather than at constant $\beta$.

= Enclosed mass
{title2=$m(r)$}
{parent=Stellar structure}

For a spherically symmetric density $\rho(r)$, the mass enclosed inside radius $r$ is
$$
m(r)=4\pi\int_0^r\rho(s)s^2\,ds,
\qquad
\frac{dm}{dr}=4\pi r^2\rho(r).
$$

= Hydrostatic pressure support equation
{parent=Stellar structure}

= Stellar hydrostatic equation
{synonym}

For a static spherically symmetric Newtonian star, balancing the inward gravitational force against the pressure gradient gives
$$
\frac{dP}{dr}=-\frac{Gm(r)\rho(r)}{r^2},
\qquad
\frac{dm}{dr}=4\pi r^2\rho(r).
$$

= Stellar polytrope
{parent=Stellar structure}

A stellar polytrope obeys $P=K\rho^{1+1/n}$ for constant $K$ and polytropic index $n$. Its dimensionless density profile satisfies the <Lane-Emden equation>.

= Moment of inertia of a polytropic star
{parent=Stellar polytrope}
{title2=$I/(MR^2)$}

For a spherical <stellar polytrope>, the axial <moment of inertia> is $I=(8\pi/3)\int_0^R\rho(r)r^4\,dr$. With <Lane-Emden variables for a stellar polytrope>, first zero $\xi_1$ and <Lane-Emden surface mass constant> $\omega_n$, its dimensionless form is
$$
\frac{I}{MR^2}=\frac{2}{3\omega_n\xi_1^2}\int_0^{\xi_1}\xi^4\theta(\xi)^n\,d\xi.
$$
A <polytrope of index zero> gives $2/5$, while a <polytrope of index one> gives $(2/3)(1-6/\pi^2)$. The latter is smaller because more of the mass lies near the center. The scalar second mass moment $\int r^2dm$ is $3I/2$, not the axial <moment of inertia>.

= Polytrope of index zero
{parent=Stellar polytrope}
{title2=$\theta(\xi)=1-\xi^2/6$}

The regular $n=0$ solution of the <Lane-Emden equation> is $\theta=1-\xi^2/6$, with first zero $\sqrt6$. Its interior density is constant and its pressure varies as $\theta$. This is the structural incompressible limit; $P=K\rho^{1+1/n}$ itself is singular at $n=0$, and does not assign the perturbative <stellar adiabatic exponent> of a thermally stratified ideal-gas model.

= Low-density polytropic mass divergence
{parent=Stellar polytrope}
{title2=$M\propto\rho_c^{(3-n)/(2n)}$}

For $p=K\rho^{1+1/n}$, the <Lane-Emden mass formula> gives $M\propto\rho_c^{(3-n)/(2n)}$ at fixed $K$, while $M/R\propto\rho_c^{1/n}$. For $3<n<5$, the <Lane-Emden equation> has a finite-radius solution, yet $M\to\infty$ as $\rho_c\to0$. The relativistic <Tolman–Oppenheimer–Volkoff equation> approaches this limit since $p_c/\rho_c\to0$. Monotone pressure and density alone therefore do not impose a finite stellar maximum mass.

= Gravitational energy of a stellar polytrope
{parent=Stellar polytrope}
{title2=$\Omega=-3GM^2/[(5-n)R]$}

For a spherical <stellar polytrope> of finite radius and zero surface pressure, the <specific enthalpy> is $(n+1)P/\rho$. <Hydrostatic equilibrium> gives $h+\Phi=-GM/R$, hence $(n+1)\int P\,dV=-GM^2/R-2\Omega$. Eliminating the pressure integral with the <stellar virial theorem> proves the displayed formula. For a gas <adiabatic stellar polytrope>, $n=1/(\gamma-1)$ and $U=GM^2/[(5\gamma-6)R]$; a general <polytropic index> need not have this relation to the <specific-heat ratio>.

= Lane-Emden variables for a stellar polytrope
{c}
{parent=Stellar polytrope}

Writing
$$
\rho=\rho_c\theta^n,\qquad r=\alpha\xi,\qquad
\alpha^2=\frac{(n+1)K}{4\pi G}\rho_c^{(1-n)/n}
$$
reduces Newtonian hydrostatic balance to
$$
\frac1{\xi^2}\frac d{d\xi}\left(\xi^2\theta'\right)=-\theta^n,
\qquad \theta(0)=1,\quad\theta'(0)=0.
$$
The stellar surface is the first zero $\xi_1$ of $\theta$, and $R=\alpha\xi_1$.

= Polytrope of index one
{title2=$P=K\rho^2$}
{parent=Stellar polytrope}

= Index-one polytrope
{synonym}

A <stellar polytrope> with <polytropic index> one has $\nabla\Phi=-2K\nabla\rho$ in <hydrostatic equilibrium>. Combining this with the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> gives the <Helmholtz equation>
$$
\nabla^2\rho+k^2\rho=0,\qquad k^2=\frac{2\pi G}{K}.
$$
The spherical solution regular at the origin is $\rho(r)=\rho_c\sin(kr)/(kr)$. Its first zero gives $R=\pi/k=\sqrt{\pi K/(2G)}$, independent of central <mass density>, and $\bar\rho/\rho_c=3/\pi^2$.

= Cubic polytropic interior
{parent=Polytrope of index one}

The interior <Helmholtz equation> for a <polytrope of index one> also admits a positive solution vanishing on the faces of a cube:
$$
\rho(x,y,z)=\rho_c\sin\frac{\pi x}{L}\sin\frac{\pi y}{L}\sin\frac{\pi z}{L},
\qquad L^2=\frac{3\pi K}{2G}.
$$
Its central value is $\rho_c$ and its average is $\bar\rho=8\rho_c/\pi^3$. Together with $\Phi=C-2K\rho$, it solves the interior <hydrostatic equilibrium> and <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity>. A local interior solution need not match the external field of its own mass.

= Cubic polytrope fails isolated gravitational matching
{parent=Cubic polytropic interior}

For the <cubic polytropic interior>, $\nabla\rho$ vanishes at each vertex, so the locally defined potential $\Phi=C-2K\rho$ predicts zero gravitational acceleration there. But at the vertex $(0,0,0)$, the actual self-gravitational acceleration of the positive mass distribution is
$$
\mathbf g(0)=G\int_{[0,L]^3}\rho(\mathbf r')\frac{\mathbf r'}{|\mathbf r'|^3}\,d^3r',
$$
whose three components are strictly positive. The fields cannot match continuously. Thus the interior <Helmholtz equation> and zero density on the faces do not construct an isolated static cubic star. External stresses or an external gravitational field would be needed to realize such a boundary-value construction.

= Corner-force obstruction for a cubic polytrope
{parent=Cubic polytrope fails isolated gravitational matching}
{title2=$\nabla\Phi_{\rm int}(0)=0\ne\nabla\Phi_N(0)$}

The sine-product <cubic polytropic interior> has zero <mass density> gradient at every corner. Its hydrostatic potential $C-2K\rho$ therefore has zero gradient there. In contrast, at a corner of a cube containing a positive <mass density>, every interior mass element contributes a gravitational-potential gradient pointing into the same coordinate octant. Each component of the gradient is strictly nonzero. This directly proves failure of global isolated gravitational matching, even though the interior hydrostatic and Poisson equations hold.

= Eddington standard model
{c}
{parent=Stellar polytrope}
{wiki}

The Eddington standard model is a chemically homogeneous $n=3$ stellar polytrope with constant gas-pressure fraction $\beta$. It approximates massive radiative stars in which gas and radiation both contribute appreciably to pressure.

= Polytropic index
{title2=$n$}
{parent=Stellar polytrope}

The polytropic index $n$ is defined by $P=K\rho^{1+1/n}$. A monatomic convective ideal gas has $n=3/2$, while a radiation-supported Eddington model has $n=3$.

= Adiabatic stellar polytrope
{parent=Stellar polytrope}

An adiabatic stellar polytrope has $P=K\rho^\gamma$ with constant specific entropy, so $n=1/(\gamma-1)$. For a monatomic perfect gas, $\gamma=5/3$ and $n=3/2$.

= Polytropic mass-radius relation
{parent=Stellar polytrope}

Eliminating central density from the Lane-Emden scalings gives
$$
R\propto K^{n/(3-n)}M^{(1-n)/(3-n)}
$$
for $n\ne3$. In particular, $R\propto KM^{-1/3}$ for $n=3/2$.

With $Y_n=-\xi_1^2\theta'(\xi_1)$, the exact rearranged relation is
$$
M=A_nK^{n/(n-1)}R^{(3-n)/(1-n)},
$$
where
$$
A_n=4\pi Y_n
\left(\frac{n+1}{4\pi G}\right)^{n/(n-1)}
\xi_1^{(3-n)/(n-1)}.
$$
For $n=3$, the radius dependence disappears and the mass is proportional to $K^{3/2}$.

= Entropy dependence of a polytropic mass-radius relation
{parent=Polytropic mass-radius relation}
{title2=$R\propto K^{n/(3-n)}M^{(1-n)/(3-n)}$}

For a finite regular <polytrope> with fixed index, the scale $\alpha^2\propto K\rho_c^{(1-n)/n}$ implies $M^{n-1}R^{3-n}\propto K^n$. Thus, for $n\ne3$, $R\propto K^{n/(3-n)}M^{(1-n)/(3-n)}$. For $n=3/2$, $R\propto KM^{-1/3}$. Fixed composition makes the cold <electron degeneracy pressure> coefficient approximately fixed, but a convective <ideal gas> has $K$ controlled by <entropy>. A stellar sequence with changing entropy therefore need not follow the fixed-$K$ negative slope.

= Lane-Emden mass formula
{c}
{parent=Stellar polytrope}

If $\rho=\rho_c\theta^n$, $r=\alpha\xi$, and $\xi_1$ is the first zero of the Lane-Emden function, then a spherical polytrope has
$$
M=4\pi\alpha^3\rho_c\left[-\xi_1^2\theta'(\xi_1)\right].
$$

= Lane-Emden surface mass constant
{title2=$Y_n$}
{c}
{parent=Lane-Emden mass formula}

For a Lane-Emden function whose first zero is $\xi_1$,
$$
Y_n=-\xi_1^2\theta'(\xi_1)
=\int_0^{\xi_1}\xi^2\theta(\xi)^n\,d\xi.
$$

= Eddington quartic relation
{c}
{parent=Stellar polytrope}

For a chemically uniform $n=3$ gas-plus-radiation polytrope with constant gas-pressure fraction $\beta$, the mass obeys
$$
\frac{1-\beta}{\beta^4}\propto\mu^4M^2.
$$
It follows from the $n=3$ mass scaling $M\propto K^{3/2}$ and the equation of state.

= Stellar homology
{parent=Stellar structure}

Homologous stellar models have the same dimensionless radial profiles after mass, radius, density, pressure, and temperature are scaled by characteristic values.

= Homology scaling of central stellar pressure
{parent=Stellar homology}
{title2=$\rho_c\propto M/R^3,\ P_c\propto GM^2/R^4$}

= Homology scalings of central stellar pressure
{synonym}

In <stellar homology>, a fixed dimensionless density profile gives $\rho_c=C_\rho M/R^3$ through the mass integral. Integrating the <stellar hydrostatic equation> through the same profile gives $P_c=C_PGM^2/R^4$ when surface pressure is negligible. The constants depend on the shape, rather than the mass and radius. For an <ideal gas>, $T_c=(C_P/C_\rho)\mu GM/(\mathcal R R)$.

= Homologous rotating collapse
{parent=Stellar homology}
{title2=$\Omega\propto R^{-2},\quad F_{\rm cent}/F_{\rm grav}\propto R^{-1}$}

During a spherical <stellar homology> contraction at fixed mass and <angular momentum>, a constant inertia coefficient gives $I=\alpha MR^2$ and <solid-body rotation> gives $\Omega=J/I\propto R^{-2}$. A fixed fluid label $s=r/R$ retains its enclosed mass and polar angle, so centrifugal acceleration scales as $R^{-3}$ and gravity as $R^{-2}$. Their ratio increases as $R^{-1}$. This assumes spin conservation, not continuous synchronization to an external orbit.

= Critical rotation of a spherical cloud
{parent=Homologous rotating collapse}
{title2=$\Omega_c^2=GM/R_c^3,\quad R_c=J^2/(\alpha^2GM^3)$}

In the spherical approximation, equatorial centrifugal acceleration balances surface gravity when $\Omega_c^2R_c=GM/R_c^2$. For $I=\alpha MR^2$ and conserved <angular momentum> $J$, this gives the displayed critical radius. Real rotating gas can deform, lose matter and exchange torques before a spherical model remains accurate at this limit.

= Homology relations for radiative stars
{parent=Stellar homology}

For chemically uniform, gas-pressure-supported stars with homologous dimensionless profiles, <mass conservation> and <hydrostatic pressure support equation> give $\rho_c\propto M/R^3$ and $T_c\propto M/R$. Suppose <opacity> scales as $\kappa\propto\rho^aT^b$ and specific nuclear energy generation as $\epsilon\propto\rho^cT^d$. Then
$$
L_{\rm nuc}\propto M^{1+c+d}R^{-3c-d},\qquad
L_{\rm rad}\propto M^{3-a-b}R^{3a+b}.
$$
The second scaling follows from <radiative diffusion in a star>, $L\propto RT_c^4/(\kappa_c\rho_c)$. Equating the two gives
$$
R\propto M^{(a+b+c+d-2)/(3a+b+3c+d)}
$$
when the denominator is nonzero. Fixed composition and self-similar profiles are essential; these are relations between idealized models rather than universal stellar laws.

= Composition-dependent CNO homology
{parent=Homology relations for radiative stars}
{title2=$L\propto\mu^4M^3/\kappa,\quad R\propto(X\kappa\mu^9)^{1/16}M^{3/4}$}

For homologous, chemically uniform, gas-pressure-supported radiative stars with specific energy generation $\epsilon\propto X\rho T^{13}$ and composition-dependent constant <opacity>, <stellar homology> gives $L_{\mathrm{nuc}}\propto X\mu^{13}M^{15}R^{-16}$ and $L_{\mathrm{rad}}\propto\mu^4M^3/\kappa$. Equating them gives the displayed scalings. <Hydrogen> depletion changes both <mean molecular weight> and <electron-scattering opacity>; holding composition fixed gives $R\propto M^{3/4}$ and $L\propto M^3$. The power-law burning approximation applies only while <hydrogen> fuel remains.

= Fully mixed hydrogen-burning evolutionary track
{parent=Composition-dependent CNO homology}
{title2=$\left.d\log L/d\log T_e\right|_{X=1}=256/53$}

With negligible heavy-element contribution to the <mean molecular weight>, $\mu=4/(3+5X)$ and $\kappa\propto1+X$. At fixed <mass>, <composition-dependent CNO homology> gives $L\propto(1+X)^{-1}(3+5X)^{-4}$ and $R\propto[X(1+X)]^{1/16}(3+5X)^{-9/16}$. The <Stefan–Boltzmann law> then gives $T_e\propto X^{-1/32}(1+X)^{-9/32}(3+5X)^{-23/32}$. From the formal $X=1$ reference, declining $X$ initially increases <luminosity> and <effective temperature>, unlike the usual redward main-sequence turn-off. A trace CNO catalyst abundance is implicit in the burning coefficient; an exactly metal-free pure-hydrogen star cannot burn through that cycle.

= Hertzsprung-Russell slope of a stellar homology sequence
{c}
{parent=Homology relations for radiative stars}
{title2=$d\log L/d\log T_{\rm eff}=4Y/(Y-2X)$}

For a <stellar homology> sequence with $R\propto M^X$ and $L\propto M^Y$, the <Stefan–Boltzmann law> gives $T_{\rm eff}\propto M^{(Y-2X)/4}$. Eliminating mass gives the displayed <Hertzsprung-Russell diagram> slope when $Y\ne2X$. The usual horizontal axis decreases in <temperature> toward the right, reversing the visual left-to-right sign. The relation is between logarithmic variables and depends on the specified structural assumptions.

= Radiative homology with proton-proton burning and Kramers opacity
{parent=Homology relations for radiative stars}

With <proton–proton chain> energy generation $\epsilon\propto\rho T^4$ and <Kramers opacity law> $\kappa\propto\rho T^{-7/2}$, <homology relations for radiative stars> give
$$
R\propto M^{1/13},\qquad L\propto M^{71/13},\qquad
T_{\rm eff}\propto L^{69/284}.
$$
The <Hertzsprung-Russell diagram> relation has $d\log L/d\log T_{\rm eff}=284/69$, with <effective temperature> increasing toward the left in the usual plotting convention.

= Radiative homology with CNO burning and electron scattering
{parent=Homology relations for radiative stars}

Using the idealized <CNO cycle> law $\epsilon\propto\rho T^{16}$ and constant <electron-scattering opacity>, <homology relations for radiative stars> give
$$
R\propto M^{15/19},\qquad L\propto M^3,\qquad T_{\rm eff}\propto L^{9/76}.
$$
These retain ideal-gas pressure support; significant <radiation pressure> or convection changes the model.

= Surface gravity of a star
{title2=$g$}
{parent=Stellar structure}

The surface gravity of a spherical star of mass $M$ and radius $R$ is $g=GM/R^2$ in Newtonian gravity.

= Stellar surface boundary condition
{parent=Stellar structure}

A stellar surface boundary condition matches the interior pressure and temperature to an atmospheric solution, commonly near optical depth $\tau=2/3$ where a grey atmosphere has $T=T_{\rm eff}$.

= Photosphere
{parent=Stellar surface boundary condition}
{wiki}

The photosphere is the layer from which most observed stellar radiation escapes; in a grey atmosphere it is represented approximately by optical depth $\tau=2/3$.

= Hydrostatic equilibrium in optical depth
{parent=Photosphere}

In a plane-parallel atmosphere with $d\tau=-\kappa\rho\,dr$ and $dP/dr=-\rho g$, one has $dP/d\tau=g/\kappa$. If gravity and opacity vary slowly, the pressure at optical depth $\tau$ is approximately $P\simeq g\tau/\kappa$.

= Grey-atmosphere pressure with power-law opacity
{parent=Hydrostatic equilibrium in optical depth}
{title2=$P^\alpha=\frac{2\alpha g}{3\beta\kappa_0T_0^4}(T^{4\beta}-T_0^{4\beta})$}

In a <grey atmosphere> with $T^4=T_0^4(1+3\tau/2)$ and $\kappa=\kappa_0P^{\alpha-1}T^{4-4\beta}$, <hydrostatic equilibrium in optical depth> gives $dP/d\tau=g/\kappa$. With $\alpha,\beta>0$ and $P(0)=0$, integration gives the displayed pressure-temperature relation. At the matching surface $T=T_e$, the <Eddington surface boundary condition> gives $T_e^4=2T_0^4$, hence $P\kappa/g=4\alpha(1-2^{-\beta})/(3\beta)$.

= Stellar virial theorem
{parent=Stellar structure}

For a self-gravitating fluid star with surface pressure $P_s$, volume $V$, kinetic energy $T$, gravitational energy $\Omega$, and $I=\int r^2dm$,
$$
\frac12\ddot I=2T+3\int P\,dV-3P_sV+\Omega.
$$

= Stellar convective stability
{parent=Stellar structure}

Stellar convective stability compares the density of an adiabatically displaced fluid element with its new surroundings. Temperature and composition gradients determine whether buoyancy restores the element or amplifies its displacement.

= Stellar convective instability
{parent=Stellar convective stability}

= Convective instability
{synonym}

Stellar convective instability occurs when an <adiabatic process> displaces a fluid element and <buoyancy> amplifies the displacement. For uniform composition the <Schwarzschild criterion> gives instability when the actual <temperature gradient> exceeds the <adiabatic temperature gradient>; composition gradients instead require the <Ledoux criterion>.

= Convective instability of a uniform-density star
{parent=Stellar convective instability}

A uniform-density perfect-gas sphere supported by decreasing pressure has <buoyancy frequency> squared $N^2=-2\omega_d^2r^2/[\gamma(R^2-r^2)]$ in the interior. Its <specific entropy> decreases outward and adiabatic displacements are buoyantly unstable. For the nonradial polynomial family, the product of the two normalized squared mode frequencies is $-l(l+1)$, displaying one growing branch even when radial compression modes are stable.

= Schwarzschild criterion
{c}
{parent=Stellar convective stability}
{wiki}

For uniform composition, a stellar layer is convectively stable when $\nabla<\nabla_{\rm ad}$ and unstable when $\nabla>\nabla_{\rm ad}$, where $\nabla=d\log T/d\log P$.

= Ledoux criterion
{c}
{parent=Stellar convective stability}
{wiki}

With a composition gradient, stability requires
$$
\nabla<\nabla_{\rm ad}+\frac{\phi}{\delta}\nabla_\mu.
$$
A mean molecular weight increasing inward normally provides an additional stabilizing term.

= Convection onset in a logarithmic-pressure grey atmosphere
{parent=Stellar convective stability}

For a <grey atmosphere> with $T^4=\tfrac34T_{\rm eff}^4(\tau+2/3)$ and $P^2=P_0^2\log(1+3\tau/2)$, logarithmic differentiation gives
$$
\nabla_{\rm rad}=\frac{d\log T}{d\log P}=\frac12\log(1+3\tau/2).
$$
A monatomic <perfect gas> of uniform composition has <adiabatic temperature gradient> $2/5$. The <Schwarzschild criterion> therefore predicts the first marginally unstable layer at $\tau_c=\tfrac23(e^{4/5}-1)$, and convection for larger <optical depth>. Beyond that onset, the assumed purely radiative profile is no longer a self-consistent transport solution.

= Convective core
{parent=Stellar convective stability}

A convective core is a central stellar region in which the radiative temperature gradient exceeds the adiabatic threshold. Efficient convection makes its entropy nearly uniform and mixes its composition.

= Eddington-model convective-core mass fraction
{c}
{parent=Convective core}
{title2=$M_c/M=1/(4\nabla_{\rm ad})=\Gamma_2/[4(\Gamma_2-1)]$}

The global Eddington closure $L=(1-\beta)4\pi cGM/\kappa$, combined with a representative <stellar gas-pressure fraction>, makes the exterior <stellar radiative temperature gradient> approximately $M/(4m)$ when the <luminosity> is constant outside the core. Matching it to the <adiabatic temperature gradient> estimates the displayed boundary mass. This is a closure estimate: imposing exactly uniform $\beta$ throughout a finite-mass radiative envelope also conflicts with constant <luminosity>, as described by the <constant-beta radiative-envelope obstruction>.

= Convective envelope
{parent=Stellar convective stability}

A convective envelope is an outer stellar region in which <convection> carries a substantial fraction of the energy and mixes material. Its nearly adiabatic response to rapid mass loss is important for the <dynamical stability of binary mass transfer>.

= Radiative envelope
{parent=Stellar convective stability}

A radiative envelope transports most of its energy by <radiative diffusion>. Its stratified entropy profile gives a different rapid mass-loss response from a <convective envelope>.

= Self-gravitating Kramers power-law envelope
{parent=Radiative envelope}

For a constant-luminosity <radiative envelope> with ideal-gas <pressure> and <Kramers opacity law>, impose <power laws> on <enclosed mass>, density, <pressure> and <temperature> while retaining $M_r'=4\pi r^2\rho$. If their exponents are $m,b,p,d$, respectively, mass conservation gives $m=b+3$, hydrostatic balance gives $d=m-1$, the gas law gives $d=p-b$, and diffusion gives $d-1=2b-2-13d/2$. Solving gives $(m,b,p,d)=(1,-32,-42,-10)/11$. In particular $R/r_c=(T_c/T_R)^{11/10}$. Neglecting envelope self-gravity instead sets $M_r$ approximately constant and changes the exponents to $d=-1,b=-13/4,p=-17/4$; that approximation is not an exact solution of mass conservation with nonzero density.

= Power-law opacity radiative envelope
{parent=Radiative envelope}
{title2=$\nabla_{\rm rad}=P^{n+1}/(CT^{n+s+4})$}

A thin <radiative envelope> with constant enclosed mass, luminosity and <mean molecular weight>, <ideal gas> pressure and opacity $\kappa=\kappa_0\rho^nT^{-s}$ has $a=n+1$, $b=n+s+4$, and $C=16\pi a_{\rm rad}cGM/[3\kappa_0(\mu m_u/k_B)^nL]$. Combining <radiative diffusion in a star> with <hydrostatic equilibrium> gives $\nabla_{\rm rad}=P^a/(CT^b)$, so $P^a-P_0^a=(aC/b)(T^b-T_0^b)$ for nonzero exponents. Zero exponents give logarithmic limits. The model must be stopped or modified if the <Schwarzschild criterion> predicts <convection>.

= Radiative-envelope convection matching
{parent=Power-law opacity radiative envelope}
{title2=$\nabla_{\mathrm{rad}}(T_b)=\nabla_{\mathrm{ad}}$}

For $P=C(T^b+K)^{1/p}$ with $p=n+1$ and $b=4+n+m$, the logarithmic radiative <temperature> gradient is $(p/b)(1+K/T^b)$. At a marginal boundary with an efficient ideal-gas <convective envelope>, the <Schwarzschild criterion> equates it to $(\gamma-1)/\gamma$. Hence $K=T_b^b[(b/p)(\gamma-1)/\gamma-1]$. The notation $K=T_0^b$ with real positive $T_0$ requires a nonnegative bracket. Vanishing $p$ or $b$ needs the corresponding logarithmic integration instead of this power form.

= Fully convective star
{parent=Stellar convective stability}

A fully convective star has <convection> throughout nearly its entire interior. Very low-mass <red dwarfs> are fully convective; the transition to this structure is used in the <disrupted magnetic braking model>.

= Radiative stellar structure
{parent=Stellar structure}

In a radiative stellar region, photons carry luminosity down the temperature gradient. Dimensional scaling of the radiative-diffusion equation gives $L\sim RT_c^4/(\kappa_c\rho_c)$.

= Radiative core
{parent=Radiative stellar structure}

A stellar core in which <radiative diffusion in a star> carries most of the energy, rather than <convection>. Its formation in a <pre-main-sequence star> breaks the fully convective assumption behind an idealized <Hayashi track>.

= Stellar radiative temperature gradient
{parent=Radiative stellar structure}
{title2=$\nabla_{\rm rad}=3\kappa L_rP/(16\pi a_{\rm r}cGmT^4)$}

The <radiative temperature gradient> needed to carry a spherical stellar <luminosity> follows by dividing <radiative diffusion in a star> by the <hydrostatic pressure support equation>. It is a logarithmic <pressure> gradient, not $dT/dr$ itself. Comparing it with the <adiabatic temperature gradient> by the <Schwarzschild criterion> determines whether purely radiative transport is locally unstable at uniform composition.

= Opacity
{title2=$\kappa$}
{parent=Radiative stellar structure}
{wiki}

Opacity is an extinction cross-section per unit mass. It controls the photon mean free path and therefore the radiative temperature gradient in an optically thick star.

= Negative hydrogen ion opacity
{parent=Opacity}
{title2=$\kappa\propto\rho^{1/2}T^9$}

The <negative hydrogen ion> supplies important continuum <opacity> in cool hydrogen-rich stellar atmospheres. A useful local approximate law is $\kappa=\kappa_0\rho^{1/2}T^9$; it is not valid over arbitrary temperatures and compositions. In an <ideal gas> <power-law opacity radiative envelope> matched to $\nabla_{\rm phot}=1/8$, it gives $\nabla_{\rm rad}=-1/3+(11/24)(T/T_{\rm eff})^{9/2}$, rising rapidly toward <convective instability>.

= Negative-hydrogen-opacity alpha-disk thickness scaling
{parent=Negative hydrogen ion opacity}
{title2=$H^{41/3}\sim\sigma\mathcal R^6/(\kappa_0\alpha\Omega^{15}\Sigma^{7/3})$}

Let $\mathcal R=k_B/(\mu_m m_p)$ for constant <mean molecular weight> $\mu_m$ in proton-mass units, and let $\sigma$ be the Stefan–Boltzmann constant in the <Stefan–Boltzmann law>. The gas <sound speed> used here satisfies $c_s^2=\mathcal R T$, and $\Omega$ is the local orbital frequency. For the local <opacity> approximation $\kappa=\kappa_0\rho^{1/3}T^{10}$, a gas-supported <optically thick> <alpha disk> has $\rho\sim\Sigma/H$ and $\mathcal R T\sim\Omega^2H^2$. Integrated viscous heating is $F_+\sim\alpha\Sigma\Omega^3H^2$. <Radiative diffusion> gives $F_-\sim(\sigma/\kappa_0)T^{-6}\Sigma^{-4/3}H^{1/3}\sim(\sigma/\kappa_0)\mathcal R^6\Omega^{-12}\Sigma^{-4/3}H^{-35/3}$. Equating the fluxes proves the thickness scaling. At fixed composition and $\alpha$, it gives $H/r\propto r^{53/82}\Sigma^{-7/41}$ and $c_s\propto r^{6/41}\Sigma^{-7/41}$ in <Keplerian rotation>. Radial powers therefore require a surface-density profile; they are not determined by <opacity> alone. The formula is local to the stated <opacity> regime and small <disk aspect ratio>.

= Kramers opacity law
{c}
{parent=Opacity}
{wiki=Kramers'_opacity_law}

The Kramers opacity law has $\kappa=\kappa_0\rho T^{-7/2}$ for bound-free and free-free absorption under its idealized ionized-gas assumptions.

= Electron-scattering opacity
{title2=$\kappa_{\rm es}$}
{parent=Opacity}

Electron-scattering opacity is approximately independent of density and temperature in a fully ionized nonrelativistic plasma of fixed composition. For hydrogen mass fraction $X$, $\kappa_{\rm es}\simeq0.2(1+X)\,{\rm cm^2\,g^{-1}}$.

= Free-free opacity
{title2=$\kappa_{\rm ff}$}
{parent=Opacity}

Free-free opacity is absorption caused when a photon is absorbed during a Coulomb encounter between an electron and an ion. In the Kramers approximation its Rosseland mean scales approximately as $\kappa_{\rm ff}\propto\rho T^{-7/2}$.

= Radiative diffusion in a star
{parent=Radiative stellar structure}

= Stellar radiative diffusion
{synonym}

In an optically thick stellar region, radiative diffusion carries luminosity according to
$$
\frac{dT}{dr}=-\frac{3\kappa\rho L_r}{16\pi a_{\rm rad}c r^2T^3}.
$$

= Radiative conductivity
{parent=Radiative diffusion in a star}
{title2=$\chi=4acT^3/(3\kappa\rho)$}

In the optically thick <radiative diffusion in a star> approximation, the heat flux is $\mathbf F=-\chi\nabla T$. The conductivity depends on <opacity>, <mass density> and <temperature>. It relates a material temperature gradient to energy transport and must not be confused with the emergent <effective temperature>.

= Effective temperature
{title2=$T_{\rm eff}$}
{parent=Stellar structure}
{wiki}

The effective temperature is the temperature of a blackbody with the same radiative flux as a star: $L=4\pi R^2\sigma T_{\rm eff}^4$.

= Mass-luminosity relation
{parent=Stellar structure}
{wiki}

The mass-luminosity relation connects a star's mass to its luminosity. Its slope depends on opacity, nuclear burning, pressure support, composition, and evolutionary state.

= Eddington luminosity
{c}
{title2=$L_{\rm Edd}$}
{parent=Mass-luminosity relation}
{wiki}

Balancing outward radiative acceleration against gravity gives
$$
L_{\rm Edd}=\frac{4\pi cGM}{\kappa}.
$$
It is an approximate upper luminosity for a hydrostatic star of opacity $\kappa$.

= Stellar electron-scattering Eddington factor
{parent=Eddington luminosity}
{title2=$\Gamma_e=\kappa_eL/(4\pi cGM)$}

This factor is the ratio of acceleration due to <electron-scattering opacity> to gravitational acceleration in a spherical luminous star. The remaining effective gravity is approximately $GM(1-\Gamma_e)/R^2$ when other radiative forces are neglected. Being below the electron-scattering limit does not preclude a line-driven <stellar wind>, because additional <opacity> can provide more acceleration.

= Dust-grain Eddington limit
{title2=$L_{\rm Edd,d}$}
{parent=Eddington luminosity}

An isolated compact dust grain of radius $r_d$ and material density $\rho_d$ has opacity per unit dust mass $\kappa_d=3Q_{\rm pr}/(4\rho_dr_d)$, where $Q_{\rm pr}$ is its <radiation-pressure efficiency>. Its <Eddington luminosity> is $4\pi GMc/\kappa_d$. Matching the <electron-scattering opacity> gives $r_{d,\rm crit}=3Q_{\rm pr}/(4\rho_d\kappa_{\rm es})$, approximately $1.9\,\mathrm{cm}$ for $Q_{\rm pr}=1$, $\rho_d=1\,\mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}$, and $\kappa_{\rm es}=0.4\,\mathrm{cm^2\,g^{-1}}$. At that electron-scattering luminosity, smaller grains feel a net outward force and larger grains remain attracted. This is an isolated-grain force balance, not the opacity of an arbitrary gas-dust mixture.

= Radiative force multiplier
{title2=$\mathcal M$}
{parent=Eddington luminosity}

A radiative force multiplier is the ratio of total radiative acceleration to a reference electron-scattering acceleration. Spectral lines, bound-free absorption, and dust can make $\mathcal M\gg1$, whereas highly ionized gas approaches $\mathcal M=1$.

= Stellar nuclear fusion
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

Stellar nuclear fusion converts light nuclei into more tightly bound nuclei and supplies most main-sequence stellar luminosity.

= Thermonuclear runaway
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}

Temperature-sensitive <nuclear fusion> can heat material faster than cooling and expansion can regulate it, accelerating the burning. In a <classical nova>, <hydrogen> accumulated on a <white dwarf> ignites; initial <electron degeneracy pressure> helps suppress the usual expansion response. The eruption can eject the envelope without destroying the underlying <white dwarf>.

= Thermonuclear reaction rate
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}
{title2=$r_{12}=n_1n_2\langle\sigma v\rangle/(1+\delta_{12})$}

A thermal distribution of relative velocities weights a nuclear cross-section to determine the reaction rate per volume. Identical reactants require the denominator two to avoid counting each pair twice. Specific deposited power is $r_{12}Q_{\rm deposited}/\rho$. Charged-particle <quantum tunnelling> through a <Coulomb barrier> produces a <Gamow peak>, while resonances and plasma screening can alter the rate.

= Local temperature exponent of a thermonuclear reaction
{parent=Thermonuclear reaction rate}
{title2=$d\log\lambda/d\log T=(\eta-2)/3$}

If a charged-particle rate coefficient has $\lambda\propto\eta^2e^{-\eta}$ with $\eta\propto T^{-1/3}$, its logarithmic <temperature> derivative is $(\eta-2)/3$. This is a local slope evaluated at fixed composition. It increases as <temperature> falls, so extrapolating a single power law across a large <temperature> range can be inaccurate.

= Gamow peak
{c}
{parent=Thermonuclear reaction rate}
{title2=$\exp[-E/(kT)-\sqrt{E_G/E}]$}

For a nonresonant charged-particle <thermonuclear reaction rate>, the thermal high-energy tail favors low $E$ while the <Coulomb barrier> penetration factor favors high $E$. Their product peaks in an intermediate energy window. Resonant reactions such as the <triple-alpha process> require an additional resonance treatment.

= Silicon burning
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}
{title2=silicon-group nuclei $\longrightarrow$ iron-group nuclei}

At very high temperatures, <photodisintegration> and proton, neutron and alpha captures rearrange silicon-group material through networks approaching quasi-statistical equilibrium. The result is an iron-group mixture determined by electron fraction and weak-reaction timescales. Direct silicon-plus-silicon fusion is not the usual mechanism. The approach to maximal <nuclear binding energy> per nucleon ends sustained hydrostatic fusion power.

= Oxygen burning
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}
{title2=${}^{16}\mathrm O+{}^{16}\mathrm O\longrightarrow{}^{28}\mathrm{Si}+\alpha$}

Oxygen fusion in sufficiently hot evolved stellar cores has several reaction channels, including the displayed silicon-plus-alpha channel and channels producing phosphorus and protons. Its ashes feed <silicon burning>, while <stellar neutrino energy loss> strongly affects the evolution timescale.

= Neon burning
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}
{title2=$2\,{}^{20}\mathrm{Ne}\longrightarrow{}^{16}\mathrm O+{}^{24}\mathrm{Mg}$}

High-temperature <photodisintegration> of neon-20 releases an alpha particle and oxygen-16. Capture of that alpha particle by another neon-20 nucleus produces magnesium-24. The breakup consumes energy, but the combined reactions can release net energy. Other captures modify the ashes in a real network.

= Helium-burning shell
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}

A helium-burning shell converts <helium> into <carbon> and <oxygen> outside a helium-exhausted stellar core. Thin-shell burning on the <asymptotic giant branch> can develop a <thermal pulse of an asymptotic-giant-branch star>.

= Helium mass fraction
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}
{title2=$Y$}

The helium mass fraction is the fraction of a material's mass carried by <helium> nuclei. <Hydrogen burning> raises it, whereas <core helium burning> lowers it while producing <carbon> and <oxygen>.

= Stellar energy-generation rate
{title2=$\epsilon$}
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}

The stellar energy-generation rate $\epsilon$ is power produced per unit mass. Spherical energy conservation is $dL_r/dr=4\pi r^2\rho\epsilon$.

= Hydrogen mass fraction
{title2=$X$}
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}

The hydrogen mass fraction is the fraction $X$ of a stellar material's mass carried by hydrogen nuclei.

= Hydrogen burning
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}

Hydrogen burning converts hydrogen into helium through the <proton–proton chain> or <CNO cycle>.

= Hydrogen-burning shell
{parent=Hydrogen burning}

A hydrogen-burning shell converts <hydrogen> into <helium> in a layer surrounding a hydrogen-exhausted stellar core. It moves outward in <enclosed mass> as it supplies fresh helium to that core; its movement in radius need not have the same sign.

= Triple-alpha process
{c}
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}
{wiki}

The triple-alpha process fuses three helium-4 nuclei into carbon-12 through an unstable beryllium-8 intermediate. Its steep temperature dependence can make helium-shell burning thermally unstable.

= Hoyle state
{c}
{parent=Triple-alpha process}
{title2=${}^{12}\mathrm C(0_2^+)$}

This excited carbon-12 resonance lies close to the three-alpha threshold and enables the <triple-alpha process> to make carbon efficiently. A transient beryllium-8 nucleus captures an alpha particle into the resonance; electromagnetic decay then stabilizes carbon-12. A small radiative branching probability still matters because the resonance enhances formation.

= CNO mass fraction
{title2=$X_{\rm CNO}$}
{c}
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}

The CNO mass fraction is the combined mass fraction of carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen nuclei available to catalyse the CNO cycle.

= Proton–proton chain
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}
{wiki}

= PP chain
{c}
{synonym}

The proton–proton chain converts hydrogen to helium and dominates hydrogen burning in stars near and below the Sun's mass. Near $2\times10^7\,\mathrm K$, its specific energy generation is roughly proportional to $\rho T^4$.

= Proton-proton chain branches
{parent=Proton–proton chain}
{title2=pp I, pp II, pp III}

The <proton–proton chain> first produces deuterium by a weak reaction and then helium-3 by proton capture. pp I ends with ${}^3\mathrm{He}+{}^3\mathrm{He}\to{}^4\mathrm{He}+2p$. pp II passes through beryllium-7 <electron capture> to lithium-7 and then proton capture. pp III passes through boron-8 <beta-plus decay> and beryllium-8 breakup. Each branch produces one net helium-4 nucleus from four protons, but the <neutrino> spectrum and deposited heat differ. In pp II the net weak reactions involve one emitted positron and one captured electron.

= Solar hydrogen and helium-3 abundance profiles
{parent=Proton-proton chain branches}
{title2=$X_i\simeq i m_un_i/\rho$}

Present solar <hydrogen> is most depleted in the central burning region and rises toward the envelope. <helium-3> is efficiently destroyed in the hot centre, increases outward as its equilibrium abundance rises, and develops an off-centre maximum where equilibration becomes slow. Still farther out its production becomes weak, leaving a smaller envelope abundance. Convective mixing tends to flatten the outer profiles. This qualitative pattern is not itself a quantitative solar model.

= Effective proton-proton reaction network
{parent=Proton-proton chain branches}
{title2=$\dot{\boldsymbol n}=\mathsf S\boldsymbol R$}

An effective <Proton–proton chain> network can track <protons>, <deuterium>, <helium-3>, <helium-4> and lithium-7 after eliminating a rapidly adjusting beryllium intermediate. Identical-reactant event rates include a factor one half. Stoichiometric changes must count both <helium-4> products of lithium-7 <proton> capture. The weighted baryon <mass density> $n_1+2n_2+3n_3+4n_4+7n_7$ is conserved by the nuclear reactions at fixed volume.

= Helium-3 equilibrium abundance
{parent=Effective proton-proton reaction network}
{title2=$n_{3e}=(-B+\sqrt{B^2+4AC})/(2A)$}

With $A=\lambda_{33}$, $B=\lambda_{34}n_4$ and $C=\lambda_{11}n_1^2/2$, <helium-3> evolves as $\dot n_3=C-An_3^2-Bn_3$ after <deuterium> elimination. The positive root is its unique attracting equilibrium for a fixed background. In the pp-I limit it reduces to $n_1\sqrt{\lambda_{11}/(2\lambda_{33})}$, making equilibrium abundance strongly <temperature> dependent.

= Helium-3 relaxation time
{parent=Helium-3 equilibrium abundance}
{title2=$\tau^{-1}=2An_{3e}+B=\sqrt{B^2+4AC}$}

A small abundance displacement from the <helium-3 equilibrium abundance> obeys $\dot x=-x/\tau$ to linear order. Rapid relaxation relative to background evolution allows the abundance to track its equilibrium. If the equilibrium drifts, the displacement equation also contains $-\dot n_{3e}$. In the pp-I limit the rate is $n_1\sqrt{2\lambda_{11}\lambda_{33}}$, so both production and destruction <temperature> dependences matter.

= Temperature for helium-3 freeze-out
{parent=Helium-3 relaxation time}
{title2=$\tau(T)\sim t_\odot$}

Equilibrium fails where the <helium-3 relaxation time> becomes comparable to stellar age. In a fixed-density pp-I scaling with reaction exponents four and sixteen, the equilibrium abundance scales as $T^{-6}$ and the relaxation time as $T^{-10}$. Central normalization then places the transition near six million kelvin; using the full supplied exponential factors gives a somewhat higher estimate. A precise radius or <temperature> requires a stellar <mass density>/composition profile and the second branch contribution.

= Deuterium quasi-equilibrium in proton-proton burning
{parent=Effective proton-proton reaction network}
{title2=$n_2\simeq\lambda_{11}n_1/(2\lambda_{21})$}

<deuterium> <proton> capture is much faster than the weak reaction that produces <deuterium>. Its abundance therefore adjusts so that $\lambda_{21}n_2n_1\simeq\lambda_{11}n_1^2/2$. Substituting this balance eliminates the fast intermediate from the slower <proton> and <helium-3> evolution equations. This is a <quasi-steady approximation>, not an assumption that <hydrogen> itself is in nuclear equilibrium.

= CNO cycle
{c}
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}
{wiki}

The CNO cycle catalyses hydrogen fusion through carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen nuclei. Near $2\times10^7\,\mathrm K$, its specific energy generation is approximately proportional to $\rho T^{17}$ and is therefore strongly concentrated near a star's centre.

= CNO-II cycle
{c}
{parent=CNO cycle}
{title2=${}^{15}\mathrm N\to{}^{16}\mathrm O\to{}^{17}\mathrm F\to{}^{17}\mathrm O\to{}^{14}\mathrm N$}

A branch of the <CNO cycle> proceeds through proton captures on nitrogen-15 and oxygen-16, <beta-plus decay> of fluorine-17, and ${}^{17}\mathrm O(p,\alpha){}^{14}\mathrm N$. Returning through oxygen-15 and nitrogen-15 closes the cycle. It also catalyzes the conversion of four protons into helium-4, with branch-dependent <neutrino> loss.

= CNO-I cycle
{c}
{parent=CNO cycle}
{title2=$4p\longrightarrow{}^4\mathrm{He}+2e^++2\nu_e$}

= CN cycle
{c}
{synonym}

The principal <CNO cycle> follows ${}^{12}\mathrm C(p,\gamma){}^{13}\mathrm N$, <beta-plus decay> to carbon-13, proton capture to nitrogen-14, proton capture to oxygen-15, <beta-plus decay> to nitrogen-15, and ${}^{15}\mathrm N(p,\alpha){}^{12}\mathrm C$. The carbon seed is regenerated. The ordinary bottleneck is ${}^{14}\mathrm N(p,\gamma){}^{15}\mathrm O$, so nitrogen-14 accumulates in cycle equilibrium.

= Stellar thermostat
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}

The stellar thermostat is the negative feedback between temperature-sensitive nuclear burning and hydrostatic expansion: excess burning expands and cools a star, while deficient burning permits contraction and heating.

= Core helium burning
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}

Core helium burning begins when a hydrogen-exhausted stellar core becomes hot enough for the <triple-alpha process>. Subsequent alpha capture on carbon produces oxygen, leaving a carbon-oxygen core after central helium exhaustion.

= Helium flash
{parent=Core helium burning}
{wiki}

A helium flash is a thermally unstable ignition of <helium> in an electron-degenerate stellar core. A nondegenerate intermediate-mass core can expand and regulate ignition, so a usual isolated star of five <solar masses> begins <core helium burning> without this flash.

= Carbon burning
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}
{wiki}

Carbon burning fuses carbon nuclei at temperatures of order $10^9\,\mathrm K$. In intermediate-mass stars it can ignite off-centre under partial electron degeneracy and propagate inward as a burning flame.

= Slow neutron-capture process
{title2=$s$-process}
{parent=Stellar nuclear fusion}
{wiki=S-process}

= s-process
{synonym}
{c}

The slow neutron-capture process builds nuclei heavier than iron when neutron captures occur more slowly than typical beta decays. Thermal pulses in asymptotic-giant-branch stars produce characteristic enhancements of elements such as strontium, zirconium, and barium.

= Main sequence
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

A main-sequence star is in a long-lived phase of core hydrogen burning.

= Terminal-age main sequence
{parent=Main sequence}
{title2=TAMS}

The terminal-age main sequence denotes the end of central <hydrogen burning>. It does not mark complete exhaustion of hydrogen throughout the star; a <hydrogen-burning shell> can subsequently supply its luminosity.

= Lower main sequence
{parent=Main sequence}

The lower main sequence consists of relatively cool, low-mass hydrogen-burning stars whose luminosity is supplied mainly by the proton–proton chain.

= Red dwarf
{parent=Lower main sequence}
{wiki}

A red dwarf is a cool low-mass main-sequence star. The lowest-mass red dwarfs are fully convective.

= Zero-age main sequence
{title2=ZAMS}
{parent=Main sequence}
{wiki}

The zero-age main sequence is the locus where stars begin stable core hydrogen burning with nearly their initial composition.

= Hertzsprung-Russell diagram
{c}
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

A Hertzsprung-Russell diagram plots stellar luminosity against effective temperature or spectral type. By convention, temperature usually decreases from left to right.

= Instability strip
{parent=Hertzsprung-Russell diagram}
{wiki}

= Cepheid instability strip
{parent=Instability strip}
{c}

The Cepheid instability strip is the region of the <Hertzsprung-Russell diagram> where <Cepheid variables> are unstable to pulsation driven by the <opacity mechanism>.

= Stellar evolution
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

Stellar evolution is the change of a star's structure and composition as nuclear reactions, energy transport, mass loss, and interactions proceed.

= Stellar remnant
{parent=Stellar evolution}

A <stellar remnant> is the object left after a <star> finishes its principal nuclear-burning evolution, such as a <white dwarf>, <neutron star> or <black hole>. <Stellar remnants> retain mass while often contributing little optical light, affecting an old <stellar population>'s <mass-to-light ratio>.

= Stellar age
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{title2=$t_{\rm age}$}

The stellar age is the elapsed time since a <star> formed. A constant number formation rate on a finite interval gives a <uniform distribution> of present ages if every formed object remains counted, including stellar remnants. An age distribution inferred only from surviving <main-sequence> stars generally differs because their lifetimes depend on <mass>.

= Planetary nebula
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

= Carbon-oxygen core
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{title2=$\mathrm{C/O}$ core}

A carbon-oxygen core is the product of <core helium burning>. After central helium exhaustion it has little <hydrogen> or <helium>, while helium-rich and hydrogen-rich layers surround it.

= Blue loop
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

A blue loop is an excursion to higher <effective temperature>, followed by a return redward, during <core helium burning> in some evolved stars. Its extent depends on core and envelope structure, composition gradients and mixing. Only loops reaching the <Cepheid instability strip> produce the associated additional <Cepheid variable> crossings.

= Hertzsprung gap
{c}
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

The Hertzsprung gap is a sparsely populated region of the <Hertzsprung-Russell diagram> traversed rapidly by many stars evolving away from the <main sequence> toward the <red giant> region.

= Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction
{c}
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki=Kelvin–Helmholtz_mechanism}

Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction releases gravitational binding energy as a self-gravitating body cools and shrinks. It powers young stars and giant planets before nuclear burning or degeneracy dominates their thermal evolution.

= Intrinsic planetary luminosity
{parent=Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction}
{title2=$L_{\rm int}$}

The intrinsic planetary luminosity $L_{\rm int}$ is the power supplied by interior cooling, contraction, nuclear reactions if present, or internal heating, excluding reflected or passively reradiated stellar light. For fixed <mass> and no accretion or nuclear source, global <conservation of energy> gives $L_{\rm int}=Q_{\rm deep}-d(U+\Omega)/dt$, where $Q_{\rm deep}$ is any additional power deposited in the interior, such as <tidal heating> or <Joule heating>. For $Q_{\rm deep}=0$ and a gas-supported quasi-static sphere of fixed <specific-heat ratio> $\gamma$ and negligible surface <pressure>, the <stellar virial theorem> gives $\Omega=-3(\gamma-1)U$, hence
$$
L_{\rm int}=-\frac{3\gamma-4}{3(\gamma-1)}\frac{d\Omega}{dt}.
$$
For a dynamically stable gas-supported model take $\gamma>4/3$. For $\gamma=5/3$ this is half the gravitational release. Degenerate giants require a different internal energy reservoir; the half-factor is not universal.

= Entropy loss of an isolated convective planet
{title2=$L_{\rm int}=-\int T\dot s\,dm$}
{parent=Intrinsic planetary luminosity}

With negligible nuclear or external heating, the <stellar structure equations> give $L_{\rm int}=-\int_0^M T\,\partial s/\partial t\,dm$. A nearly isentropic convective interior therefore has $\dot s=-L_{\rm int}/\int T\,dm<0$ whenever it radiates. Decreasing <specific entropy> does not require temperature at every depth to decrease immediately: contraction of an <ideal gas> can raise its central temperature while total energy is lost.

= Power-law planetary cooling
{parent=Intrinsic planetary luminosity}
{title2=$L_{\rm int}\propto t^{-\alpha}$}

If otherwise comparable isolated <planets> of one <mass> follow $L_{\rm int}=Ct^{-\alpha}$, their intrinsic <luminosity> ratio is $L_{\rm int}(t_1)/L_{\rm int}(t_2)=(t_2/t_1)^\alpha$. The normalization depends on composition and initial <entropy>. Strong stellar irradiation, deep heating, or accretion can invalidate extrapolating the same cooling law.

= Kelvin-Helmholtz cooling time
{title2=$\tau_{\rm KH}\sim GM^2/(RL_{\rm int})$}
{c}
{parent=Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction}

The Kelvin-Helmholtz cooling time estimates how long a self-gravitating body can radiate a reservoir of energy of order its gravitational binding energy. It uses intrinsic luminosity $L_{\rm int}$, excluding reradiated stellar light. At fixed radius and structure, this reservoir scales as $M^2$; the approximation changes when degeneracy or other internal physics changes the accessible energy.

= Pre-main-sequence star
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

A pre-main-sequence star contracts toward the main sequence before sustained core hydrogen burning supplies its luminosity. Convection, accretion, and deuterium burning shape its track across the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram.

= Henyey track
{parent=Pre-main-sequence star}
{c}
{wiki}

A <pre-main-sequence star> with a developing <radiative core> can contract along a relatively horizontal path toward higher <effective temperature> in the <Hertzsprung-Russell diagram>. This differs from the nearly vertical fully convective <Hayashi track>.

= Hayashi track
{c}
{parent=Pre-main-sequence star}
{wiki}

A Hayashi track is the nearly vertical path of a fully convective pre-main-sequence star on the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram. Photospheric opacity fixes a weak relation between luminosity and effective temperature.

= Convective contraction with power-law opacity
{parent=Hayashi track}
{title2=$T_e^8\propto\mu^4M^2R$}

For a monatomic fully convective <ideal gas> star, uniform <specific entropy> gives $P=KT^{5/2}$ with $K\propto\mu^{-5/2}M^{-1/2}R^{-3/2}$. Combining this with the <stellar surface boundary condition> $\kappa P=2g/3$ and $\kappa=\kappa_0\rho^aT^b$ gives
$$
T_e^{(3a+5)/2+b}\propto
\mu^{(3a+5)/2}M^{(a+3)/2}R^{(3a-1)/2}.
$$
All composition comparisons here hold $\kappa_0$ fixed. For $a=1,b=4$, $T_e^8\propto\mu^4M^2R$, and the <Stefan–Boltzmann law> gives $L\propto\mu^2MR^{5/2}$. The <stellar virial theorem> and <Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction> then give $R^{-7/2}-R_0^{-7/2}\propto\mu^2M^{-1}(t-t_0)$, with a positive coefficient. Once the initial-radius term is negligible, $R\propto\mu^{-4/7}M^{2/7}(t-t_0)^{-2/7}$.

= Composition shift of a convective pre-main-sequence track
{parent=Convective contraction with power-law opacity}
{title2=$L\propto\mu^{-8}T_e^{20}$}

For <convective contraction with power-law opacity> $\kappa\propto\rho T^4$ at fixed stellar mass and opacity normalization, $T_e\propto\mu^{1/2}R^{1/8}$ and $L\propto\mu^2R^{5/2}$. Eliminating $R$ gives $L\propto\mu^{-8}T_e^{20}$. Thus a larger <mean molecular weight> moves the track to larger <effective temperature> at a given <luminosity>, with $T_e|_L\propto\mu^{2/5}$. Contraction decreases both temperature and luminosity for this particular opacity exponent.

= Accretion luminosity
{title2=$L_{\rm acc}$}
{parent=Pre-main-sequence star}

Matter accreting at rate $\dot M$ onto a star of mass $M$ and radius $R$ releases gravitational power of order
$$
L_{\rm acc}=\frac{GM\dot M}{R},
$$
up to an efficiency and an inner-boundary correction.

= Deuterium-burning energy-generation rate
{title2=$\epsilon_D$}
{parent=Pre-main-sequence star}

At the temperatures relevant to pre-main-sequence stars, a useful steep power-law approximation to the specific deuterium-burning rate is $\epsilon_D\propto\rho T^{15}$. Its sensitivity to central temperature makes deuterium burning act as a temporary thermostat.

= Subgiant
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

A subgiant has begun evolving away from the <main sequence> after central hydrogen exhaustion, increasing its radius before the extended <red giant> stage.

= Red giant
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

A red giant is an evolved star with a large radius and low <effective temperature>. On the first giant branch, a low- or intermediate-mass star is powered primarily by shell <hydrogen burning> around a helium-rich core.

= Degenerate-core shell-burning mass-luminosity relation
{parent=Red giant}
{title2=$L\propto M_c^{32/3}$}

In a thin-shell giant model with a nonrelativistic degenerate core, $R_c\propto M_c^{-1/3}$. For a gas-pressure radiative layer with $\kappa\propto\rho T^{-3}$ and specific energy generation $\epsilon\propto\rho T^{10}$, the deep profile has $P\propto T^4$, $\rho\propto T^3$ and $T\propto M_c/r$. Integrating the strongly concentrated shell power gives $L\propto C^2M_c^{16}R_c^{-13}$, while radiative transport gives $C^2\propto M_c/L$. Therefore $L^2\propto M_c^{17}R_c^{-13}$, yielding the displayed exponent. This is a model-specific scaling, not a universal core-mass law; it fails when envelope stripping removes the shell fuel or the confining structure.

= Stellar rejuvenation
{parent=Stellar evolution}

Stellar rejuvenation occurs when mixing brings fresh hydrogen into the core of a mass-gaining or merged <main-sequence> star, making its remaining nuclear lifetime resemble that of a younger star. The amount of rejuvenation depends on the accretor's internal mixing and prior composition.

= Helium star
{parent=Stellar evolution}

A helium star has lost most of its hydrogen-rich envelope and is made predominantly of helium. If massive enough, its core can ignite helium; a lower-mass stripped core instead becomes a <helium white dwarf>.

= Asymptotic giant branch
{c}
{title2=AGB}
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

= AGB star
{c}
{synonym}

An asymptotic-giant-branch star has an inert carbon-oxygen core surrounded by helium- and hydrogen-burning shells and a deep convective envelope. Alternating shell burning produces thermal pulses and dredge-up, while strong mass loss removes the envelope.

= Hot-bottom burning
{parent=Asymptotic giant branch}

When the bottom of an <asymptotic giant branch> star's convective envelope becomes hot enough for <hydrogen burning>, the <CNO cycle> can convert newly dredged-up <carbon> into <nitrogen>. Its onset and strength depend on mass, composition and envelope structure; it is not a universal property of all stars at one initial mass.

= Stellar dredge-up
{parent=Asymptotic giant branch}

Stellar dredge-up occurs when a convective envelope penetrates into layers altered by nuclear burning and transports their material to the surface.

= First dredge-up
{parent=Stellar dredge-up}
{wiki}

First dredge-up occurs as a star ascends the red-giant branch. Its deepening convective envelope exposes material processed by hydrogen burning, especially enhanced helium and nitrogen and depleted carbon.

= Second dredge-up
{parent=Stellar dredge-up}
{wiki}

Second dredge-up occurs after core helium exhaustion in sufficiently massive intermediate-mass stars. The convective envelope moves inward, mixes helium- and hydrogen-burning products to the surface, and reduces the hydrogen-exhausted core mass.

= Third dredge-up
{parent=Stellar dredge-up}
{wiki}

Third dredge-up follows a thermal pulse on the asymptotic giant branch, when the convective envelope reaches into the intershell and can carry freshly made carbon and slow-neutron-capture elements to the surface.

= Thermal pulse of an asymptotic-giant-branch star
{parent=Asymptotic giant branch}

= AGB thermal pulse
{c}
{synonym}

A thermal pulse is a helium-shell flash caused by thermally unstable burning in a thin shell. It briefly drives intershell convection, expands and cools the hydrogen-burning shell, and can be followed by third dredge-up.

= Härm–Schwarzschild instability
{c}
{parent=Thermal pulse of an asymptotic-giant-branch star}

The Härm–Schwarzschild instability is the thin-shell thermal instability of helium burning in an asymptotic-giant-branch star. Heating greatly raises the nuclear rate while the geometrically thin shell initially expands too little to provide stabilizing cooling.

= Super-AGB star
{c}
{parent=Asymptotic giant branch}
{wiki}

A super-AGB star is massive enough to ignite carbon and form a degenerate oxygen-neon core, but initially too light to proceed directly through all advanced burning stages. Its fate depends on competition between core growth and envelope mass loss.

= Oxygen-neon-magnesium core
{title2=$\mathrm{O/Ne/Mg}$ core}
{parent=Super-AGB star}

An oxygen-neon-magnesium core is the degenerate product of carbon burning, composed mainly of oxygen and neon with a smaller magnesium abundance. It can become an oxygen-neon white dwarf or collapse after electron captures if it approaches the <Chandrasekhar mass>.

= Electron-capture supernova
{parent=Super-AGB star}
{wiki}

An electron-capture supernova occurs when electron captures, especially on neon and magnesium nuclei in a degenerate oxygen-neon core, remove pressure support and trigger collapse before ordinary iron-core formation.

= Stellar wind
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

A stellar wind is a continuous outflow of gas from a star. Radiation, gas pressure, waves, or magnetic fields can supply its acceleration, and the resulting mass loss can determine a star's final remnant.

= Polytropic stellar wind
{parent=Stellar wind}
{title2=$\dot M=4\pi r^2\rho u$}

A steady spherical <polytropic flow> escaping a point-mass potential conserves the outward <mass loss rate> and the <Bernoulli function> $C=u^2/2+c_s^2/(\gamma-1)-GM/r$. For positive terminal speed, a finite-radius smooth <sonic point> requires $C>0$ and $1<\gamma<5/3$. Its sound speed obeys $c_{s,c}^2=2C(\gamma-1)/(5-3\gamma)$, and evaluating the conserved flux there selects the transonic <mass loss rate>. Other parameter limits need separate treatment.

= Minimum matching at a polytropic sonic point
{parent=Polytropic stellar wind}
{title2=$\min F=\min G$}

Put $y=\rho r^{4/(\gamma+1)}$ and $\alpha=4(\gamma-1)/(\gamma+1)$. The <polytropic stellar wind> equations become $F(r)=Cr^\alpha+GMr^{\alpha-1}=G(y)=\gamma Ky^{\gamma-1}/(\gamma-1)+\dot M^2/(32\pi^2y^2)$. At a regular <sonic point> both derivatives vanish. Matching their minima allows the two positive-$y$ branches to join with finite slope; unequal minima either leave a forbidden radial interval or separate the subsonic and supersonic branches. This reduces a singular differential-equation crossing to a geometric minimum comparison.

= Gas and radiation pressure stellar wind equation
{parent=Stellar wind}
{title2=$(v-a_g^2/v)v'=2a_g^2/r-a_g^2(\log T)'-g(1-\Gamma)$}

In an optically thick steady spherical <stellar wind> of constant <mean molecular weight> $\mu_{\rm mol}$, let $v$ be the outward speed and $a_g^2=k_BT/(\mu_{\rm mol}m_u)$ the <isothermal sound speed> squared. Mass conservation gives $\rho'/\rho=-2/r-v'/v$, where a prime denotes differentiation with respect to radius $r$. Use $P_{\rm gas}=\rho a_g^2$ and <radiative diffusion> to write $P_{\rm rad}'/\rho=-g\Gamma$, where $g=GM_r/r^2$ and $\Gamma=L_r/L_{\rm Edd}$ uses the local <Eddington luminosity> $L_{\rm Edd}=4\pi cGM_r/\kappa$ and <opacity> $\kappa$. Substituting into the momentum equation yields the displayed wind equation. Smooth <sonic point> crossing requires both its denominator and numerator to vanish. The radiation-pressure gradient already supplies radiative acceleration; adding that force separately would count it twice.

= Sonic-point obstruction at the Eddington luminosity
{parent=Gas and radiation pressure stellar wind equation}

For an optically thick diffusion-controlled wind, put $\beta=P_{\rm gas}/(P_{\rm gas}+P_{\rm rad})$. Diffusion gives $(\log T)'=-g\Gamma\beta/[4a_g^2(1-\beta)]$. At $\Gamma=1$, the numerator of the <gas and radiation pressure stellar wind equation> is $2a_g^2/r+g\beta/[4(1-\beta)]>0$ for finite-temperature gas and $0<\beta<1$. A subsonic branch then decelerates and cannot pass a regular <sonic point>. This statement is specific to subsonically launched winds in this transport model, not to every supersonic solution or every form of stellar-wind driving.

= Neutron star
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

A neutron star is a compact stellar remnant whose gravity compresses roughly a stellar mass into a radius of order ten kilometres. Degenerate matter supplies most of its pressure support, while rapid rotation and intense magnetic fields can power observable emission.

= Pulsar
{parent=Neutron star}
{wiki}

= Radio pulsar
{synonym}

A pulsar is a rotating magnetized neutron star whose directed emission is observed as periodic pulses. Rotation supplies the energy of an ordinary radio pulsar.

= Pulsar magnetosphere
{parent=Pulsar}

A pulsar magnetosphere is the plasma and electromagnetic field surrounding a pulsar. Close to the star, charges tend to screen electric fields parallel to the magnetic field and approximately corotate; near the light cylinder, relativistic motion and field distortion become essential.

= Corotating pulsar magnetosphere
{parent=Pulsar magnetosphere}

In an idealized corotating pulsar magnetosphere, plasma moves with $\mathbf u=\boldsymbol\Omega\mathbin\times\mathbf r$. In Gaussian units, ideal magnetohydrodynamics then gives $\mathbf E=-\mathbf u\mathbin\times\mathbf B/c$.

= Goldreich-Julian density
{c}
{parent=Corotating pulsar magnetosphere}
{wiki}

= Goldreich-Julian charge density
{c}
{synonym}

The Goldreich-Julian density is the charge density needed to support corotation in an idealized pulsar magnetosphere. Where exterior currents vanish,
$$
\rho_{\rm GJ}=-\frac{\boldsymbol\Omega\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B}{2\pi c}
$$
in Gaussian units.

= Light cylinder
{parent=Pulsar magnetosphere}
{wiki}

The light cylinder is the notional cylindrical surface on which rigid corotation with angular speed $\Omega$ would reach the speed of light.

= Light cylinder radius
{title2=$R_{\rm LC}$}
{parent=Light cylinder}

The light cylinder radius is $R_{\rm LC}=c/\Omega$. A nonrelativistic corotation model must fail as cylindrical radius approaches this scale.

= Pulsar polar cap
{parent=Pulsar magnetosphere}

A pulsar polar cap is the region on the stellar surface threaded by magnetic field lines that reach the outer magnetosphere rather than closing close to the star.

= Polar-cap opening angle
{title2=$\theta_*$}
{parent=Pulsar polar cap}

For an aligned dipole whose last relevant field line reaches equatorial radius $R_e$, the polar-cap opening angle obeys $\sin^2\theta_*=R_*/R_e$. It is approximately $\sqrt{R_*/R_e}$ for a small cap.

= White dwarf
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

A white dwarf is a compact stellar remnant supported mainly by electron degeneracy pressure.

= Carbon-oxygen white dwarf
{parent=White dwarf}
{title2=$\mathrm{C/O}$ white dwarf}

A carbon-oxygen white dwarf is a <white dwarf> whose core is predominantly <carbon> and <oxygen>, retained after <core helium burning> when the star loses its envelope without sustained <carbon burning>.

= Helium white dwarf
{parent=White dwarf}

A helium white dwarf is a <white dwarf> whose core is primarily helium. Stripping a low-mass evolved star before helium ignition can produce one on a much shorter timescale than isolated stellar evolution.

= Nonrelativistic white dwarf
{parent=White dwarf}

A cold nonrelativistic white dwarf supported by <electron degeneracy pressure> has a <polytropic equation of state> $P=K_{\rm NR}\rho^{5/3}$, so its <polytropic index> is $3/2$. The <Lane-Emden mass formula> and <polytropic mass-radius relation> give $R\propto K_{\rm NR}M^{-1/3}$ at fixed composition. As density rises, relativistic corrections soften the exponent toward $4/3$ and lead toward the <Chandrasekhar mass> regime.

= Chandrasekhar mass
{c}
{parent=White dwarf}
{wiki=Chandrasekhar_limit}

= Chandrasekhar limit
{c}
{synonym}

The Chandrasekhar mass is the limiting mass of a cold nonrotating white dwarf supported by relativistic electron degeneracy pressure, about $1.4M_\odot$ for ordinary composition.

= Mestel's cooling law
{c}
{parent=White dwarf}

Mestel's cooling law matches a degenerate isothermal white-dwarf core to a nondegenerate radiative envelope. With Kramers opacity it gives $L/M\propto T_c^{7/2}$ in terms of core temperature.

= Radiative-zero white-dwarf envelope
{parent=Mestel's cooling law}
{title2=$P\propto T^{17/4},\quad\rho\propto T^{13/4}$}

In a negligible-mass <white dwarf> envelope, take enclosed mass and <luminosity> constant, neglect <radiation pressure>, and use an <ideal gas> with the <Kramers opacity law>. Dividing <hydrostatic pressure support equation> by <radiative diffusion in a star> gives $P\,dP\propto(M/L)T^{15/2}dT$. Neglecting the surface integration constant produces the displayed <pressure> and <mass density> laws. It is a deep-envelope approximation, not a finite-temperature photospheric boundary condition.

= Geometrical thickness of a white-dwarf radiative envelope
{parent=Radiative-zero white-dwarf envelope}
{title2=$s/R=q/(1+q),\quad q=17\mathcal RT_cR/(4GM)$}

Integrating the <hydrostatic pressure support equation> with the <radiative-zero white-dwarf envelope> law yields $T=4GM(1/r-1/R)/(17\mathcal R)$. Matching $T=T_c$ at $r_b$ gives $s=R-r_b$ as displayed. A strongly degenerate bulk core has thermal energy small compared with its <Fermi energy>, making $q\ll1$ and hence $s\ll R$. The inequality $T_c\gg T_{\rm eff}$ alone does not establish geometrical thinness.

= White-dwarf envelope thickness-luminosity relation
{parent=Geometrical thickness of a white-dwarf radiative envelope}
{title2=$s_1/s_2=(L_1/L_2)^{2/7}$}

For fixed mass, degenerate radius and composition, the <matching of a white-dwarf envelope to a degenerate core> gives $L\propto T_c^{7/2}$, while the <geometrical thickness of a white-dwarf radiative envelope> gives $s\propto T_c$ to leading thin-layer order. Cooling therefore thins this idealized envelope. At finite thickness the ratio additionally contains $(1+q_2)/(1+q_1)$.

= Matching of a white-dwarf envelope to a degenerate core
{parent=Radiative-zero white-dwarf envelope}
{title2=$\rho_b=(\mathcal RT_c/K_{\rm NR})^{3/2},\quad L/M\propto T_c^{7/2}$}

Continuity of <temperature>, <pressure> and <mass density> matches the <ideal gas> envelope <pressure> $\mathcal R\rho_bT_c$ to the nonrelativistic <electron degeneracy pressure> $K_{\rm NR}\rho_b^{5/3}$. Substituting this base <mass density> into the <radiative-zero white-dwarf envelope> law gives the luminosity-temperature scaling behind <Mestel's cooling law>. The matched point is a transition approximation; the cold degeneracy law does not represent finite-temperature corrections there exactly.

= Hydrogen-burning white-dwarf envelope
{parent=White dwarf}

A hydrogen-burning white-dwarf envelope is a thin nondegenerate surface layer in which accreted or residual hydrogen can undergo nuclear fusion above a degenerate core.

= Thin-shell instability
{parent=Hydrogen-burning white-dwarf envelope}
{wiki=Thin_shell_instability}

Thin-shell instability is a thermal runaway of nuclear burning in a geometrically thin layer whose pressure is constrained by overlying weight, weakening expansion-driven cooling.

= Classical nova
{parent=Thin-shell instability}
{wiki}

= Classical novae
{synonym}

A classical nova is a thermonuclear runaway in hydrogen-rich material accreted onto a white dwarf, producing a sudden luminous outburst and ejecting part of the envelope.

= Nova-induced binary detachment
{parent=Classical nova}

An idealized <classical nova> that slowly ejects <mass> $\delta m$ isotropically from the accretor, compared with the <orbital period>, but quickly compared with the mass-transfer timescale, expands a circular <binary star>'s <Roche lobe>. The ejecta carry accretor <specific angular momentum> $j_1/J=M_2/(M_1M)=q/M$. Taking $\delta J/J=-q\delta m/M$, $\delta M_1=-\delta m$ and $\delta M_2=0$ in $J=M_1M_2\sqrt{Ga/M}$ proves $\delta a/a=\delta m/M$. The lobe law $R_L\propto a(M_2/M)^{1/3}$ then gives $\delta R_L/R_L=4\delta m/(3M)$. A donor whose radius is unchanged moves inside its lobe.

For constant external loss $\Gamma=-\dot J>0$ and a donor sequence $R_2\propto M_2^\zeta$, the fractional gap closes at rate $2\Gamma/J$, giving $t_d=2J\delta m/(3M\Gamma)$. The <binary mass-transfer contact equation> gives accumulation time $t_s=J\delta m(\zeta+5/3-2q)/(2M_2\Gamma)$. Therefore
$$
\frac{t_d}{t_s}=\frac{4q}{(1+q)(3\zeta+5-6q)}.
$$
This first-order result requires $\delta m/M\ll1$, a positive contact denominator and a fixed donor radius during the eruption. At $\zeta=1$ it reduces to $2q/[(1+q)(4-3q)]$.

= Schönberg-Chandrasekhar limit
{c}
{parent=Stellar evolution}
{wiki}

The Schönberg-Chandrasekhar limit is the maximum fractional mass of an isothermal inert core that can support a surrounding hydrogen-burning envelope in hydrostatic and thermal equilibrium.

= Binary star
{parent=Stellar astrophysics}
{wiki}

A binary star is a gravitationally bound pair orbiting their common centre of mass.

= Neutron star merger
{parent=Binary star}
{wiki}

Coalescence of a binary containing two <neutron stars> can eject neutron-rich material and power a <kilonova>. <Rapid neutron-capture process> reactions in the ejecta produce heavy elements. This connects compact-binary dynamics to chemical enrichment.

= Kilonova
{parent=Neutron star merger}
{wiki}

An optical and infrared transient powered by radioactive decay in material ejected from a compact-object merger. Its evolving spectrum and light curve probe ejecta composition and <rapid neutron-capture process> production.

= Synchronous fission model for binary formation
{parent=Binary star}
{title2=$a/R_c=16\alpha^2/(1+\alpha)^2$}

In this idealized model, a critically rotating spherical cloud splits into two equal-mass homologous spheres of radius $a/2$ that share the orbital frequency of their touching circular binary. Their combined orbital and spin inertia is $(1+\alpha)Ma^2/4$. Equating post-fission angular momentum to $\alpha M\sqrt{GMR_c}$ gives the displayed separation relation. A compact outcome $a<R_c$ requires $0<\alpha<1/3$. The model is an angular-momentum budget, not a hydrodynamic proof that stellar fission occurs.

= Repeated fission hierarchy
{parent=Synchronous fission model for binary formation}
{title2=$a'/a=2\alpha^2/(1+\alpha)^2$}

If each daughter subsequently conserves its own spin while contracting at fixed outer separation, its next critical radius is $a/8$. A local repetition of the <synchronous fission model for binary formation> gives the displayed inner-to-outer separation ratio. Continuous <tidal locking> throughout the contraction would instead keep the spin frequency fixed and decrease its centrifugal-to-gravity ratio as the cube of its radius; the repeated fission would not follow. Spin-orbit torque assumptions must therefore be explicit.

= Circular-binary orbital angular momentum
{parent=Binary star}
{title2=$J=\mu\sqrt{GMa}$}

For a <circular Kepler orbit>, neglecting stellar spin, the orbital <angular momentum> of a <binary star> is $J=\mu a^2\Omega=\mu\sqrt{GMa}$, where $M$ is total mass, $\mu$ is the <reduced mass>, and $a$ is separation. <Kepler's third law> also gives $J=G^{2/3}P^{1/3}M_1M_2/[(2\pi)^{1/3}M^{1/3}]$. Distances from the <center of mass> are $aM_2/M$ and $aM_1/M$; adding their individual orbital <angular momenta> proves the formula.

= Binary mass ratio
{parent=Binary star}
{title2=$q=M_d/M_a$}

= Donor-to-accretor mass ratio
{synonym}

A binary <binary mass ratio> is the quotient of its component masses. For mass-transfer stability, specify the donor-to-accretor convention $q=M_d/M_a$ explicitly, since the inverse convention also occurs. Mass-ratio reversal changes which component is more massive.

= Detached binary
{parent=Binary star}

A detached binary has both components lying inside their respective <Roche lobes>, so neither currently transfers matter by <Roche-lobe overflow>. A detached system may still exchange matter through a <stellar wind>.

= Coeval binary population
{parent=Binary star}

A coeval binary has components formed at the same time. A population of such <binary stars> can still have a distribution of system ages. If component masses have <independence> and the state at age $t$ is determined separately for each component, the states have <conditional independence> given $t$. If $g(t)$ and $w(t)$ are the conditional <probabilities> of two states, the unconditional unordered pair fractions are $\mathbb E[g(t)^2]$ and $2\mathbb E[g(t)w(t)]$, not generally products of marginal state fractions.

= Giant-pair fraction in an instantaneous stellar burst
{parent=Coeval binary population}
{title2=$\Pr(GG\mid t)=g(t)^2$}

In an instantaneous burst all binary components have a fixed common age. If the initial component masses are independent, the <probability> of two <red giants> is the square of the individual giant <probability> at that age. This need not equal the age-averaged pair fraction in a continuously forming population.

= Conditional evolved-companion fraction in a coeval binary population
{parent=Coeval binary population}
{title2=$\mathbb E[g^2+2gw]/\mathbb E[2g-g^2]$}

= Evolved-companion fraction
{synonym}

If $g(t)$ is the conditional giant fraction and $w(t)$ a second evolved-state fraction at common age $t$, independent component masses give the fraction of giant-containing systems with another evolved component as $\mathbb E[g^2+2gw]/\mathbb E[2g-g^2]$. Averaging must follow conditioning on age, rather than multiplying marginal state <probabilities>.

= Semidetached binary
{parent=Binary star}

= Semi-detached binary
{synonym}

A semidetached binary has one component filling its <Roche lobe> while the other remains inside its own lobe. <Algol binaries> and most ordinary <cataclysmic variables> are examples.

= RT Lac
{c}
{parent=Semidetached binary}

RT Lac is a named interacting binary used in studies of <binary star> evolution. Conservative-progenitor tests and <nonconservative mass transfer> provide ways to interpret a stripped, mass-losing component.

= Eclipsing binary
{parent=Binary star}

An eclipsing binary has an orbital orientation that lets one star pass in front of the other as seen by the observer, producing periodic changes in received <radiative flux>.

= Algol variable
{c}
{parent=Eclipsing binary}
{wiki}

An Algol variable is an <eclipsing binary> with well-separated eclipse minima and relatively little variation between eclipses. This light-curve classification includes detached systems as well as interacting <Algol binaries>; it does not by itself imply <Roche-lobe overflow>.

= Donor star
{parent=Binary star}

A donor star loses matter to its companion through <Roche-lobe overflow> or a <stellar wind>.

= Mass-gaining star
{parent=Binary star}

= Mass gainer
{synonym}

A mass-gaining star accretes material from a companion. Its changing mass and composition can alter its <main-sequence> location and cause <stellar rejuvenation>.

= Binary mass function
{title2=$F$}
{parent=Binary star}
{wiki=Binary_mass_function}

For a single-lined circular spectroscopic binary whose visible component has radial-velocity amplitude $K_1$ and orbital period $P$, the binary mass function is
$$
F=\frac{PK_1^3}{2\pi G}
=\frac{M_2^3\sin^3i}{(M_1+M_2)^2}.
$$

= Isotropic binary inclination distribution
{title2=$p(i)=\sin i$}
{parent=Binary mass function}

Randomly oriented binary angular-momentum vectors are uniform in $\cos i$. After identifying $i$ with $\pi-i$, their normalized inclination density on $0\leq i\leq\pi/2$ is $p(i)=\sin i$.

= Recycled pulsar
{parent=Binary star}
{wiki}

A recycled pulsar is a neutron star spun up by accretion from a binary companion. Accreted angular momentum can reduce its spin period to milliseconds, while donor-envelope removal often leaves a white-dwarf companion.

= Binary millisecond pulsar
{parent=Recycled pulsar}

A binary millisecond pulsar is a rapidly rotating recycled neutron star that remains in a binary, commonly with the white-dwarf remnant of its donor.

= Wind mass transfer in a binary star
{parent=Binary star}

Wind mass transfer occurs when one star accretes part of a companion's stellar wind without the donor necessarily filling its Roche lobe.

= Fast-wind accretion fraction in a circular binary
{parent=Wind mass transfer in a binary star}
{title2=$\beta_{\rm acc}=G^2M_a^2/(a^2v_w^4)$}

For a fast isotropic cold wind, the <mass density> at a companion at separation $a$ is $(-\dot M_d)/(4\pi a^2v_w)$. The ballistic capture model gives $\dot M_a/(-\dot M_d)=G^2M_a^2/(a^2v_w^4)$. Fast relative to orbital motion also makes the capture radius small compared with the separation.

= Wind Roche-lobe overflow
{c}
{parent=Wind mass transfer in a binary star}

Wind Roche-lobe overflow occurs when a slow dense wind nearly fills the donor's Roche lobe and is gravitationally focused through the inner Lagrange region. It can transfer a much larger fraction of the outflow than a uniform fast-wind Bondi--Hoyle estimate.

= Tidally enhanced stellar wind
{parent=Wind mass transfer in a binary star}

A tidally enhanced stellar wind is a model in which a companion increases a giant's wind mass loss as the giant approaches its <Roche lobe>. Removing envelope mass before contact can reduce or reverse the mass ratio and make subsequent <Roche-lobe overflow> more stable. This is one proposed route to wide systems with Algol-like reversed evolutionary ordering. https://www.astro.ru.nl/~sluys/Thesis/pdfs/vdSluys_thesis_screen.pdf[Van der Sluys's compact-binary study, section 5.7.1] discusses the enhanced-wind scenario and its effect on mass-transfer stability.

= Jeans-mode mass loss
{c}
{parent=Wind mass transfer in a binary star}

In Jeans-mode mass loss, an isotropic wind escapes from one binary component carrying that star's specific orbital angular momentum. If the companion accretes negligibly, a circular binary obeys $aM=\text{constant}$ and $PM^2=\text{constant}$.

= Barium star
{parent=Wind mass transfer in a binary star}
{wiki}

A barium star is a main-sequence or giant star enriched in carbon and slow-neutron-capture elements despite being too unevolved to have made them itself. It was polluted by an asymptotic-giant-branch companion that is now a white dwarf.

= S-type star
{c}
{parent=Wind mass transfer in a binary star}
{wiki}

An S-type star has strong zirconium-oxide bands and enhanced slow-neutron-capture elements. Intrinsic S-type stars are thermally pulsing asymptotic giants, while extrinsic S-type stars were polluted by a former asymptotic-giant-branch companion.

= Cataclysmic variable
{parent=Binary star}
{wiki}

= CV
{c}
{synonym}

A cataclysmic variable is a close binary in which a Roche-lobe-filling low-mass star transfers matter to a white dwarf. Angular-momentum loss drives long-term transfer, while unstable burning of accumulated hydrogen produces classical novae.

= Nova-like variable
{parent=Cataclysmic variable}

A <cataclysmic variable> whose persistent luminous disk resembles a <dwarf nova> in outburst. A sufficiently high transfer rate can keep its <accretion disk> on the hot stable branch of the <hydrogen-ionization disk instability>, avoiding ordinary dwarf-nova cycles.

= Dwarf nova
{parent=Cataclysmic variable}
{wiki}

= Dwarf novae
{synonym}

A <cataclysmic variable> with recurrent disk outbursts associated with the <hydrogen-ionization disk instability>. Enhanced <accretion> powers the bright phase; the event does not require <thermonuclear runaway> on the <white dwarf>.

= Nonmagnetic cataclysmic variable
{parent=Cataclysmic variable}

A <cataclysmic variable> whose <white dwarf> <magnetic field> does not dominate the large-scale <accretion> flow. It normally supports an <accretion disk> fed through <Roche-lobe overflow>. The term does not rule out a magnetized <donor star> or <magnetic braking of a binary star>.

= Accretion-disk stream-impact hot spot
{parent=Nonmagnetic cataclysmic variable}

The bright region where the stream from <Roche-lobe overflow> strikes an <accretion disk>. Dissipation of relative <kinetic energy> produces a localized source of light whose visibility changes around the <orbital period>.

= Cataclysmic-variable orbital-period distribution
{title2=$N(P)$}
{parent=Cataclysmic variable}

Ordinary hydrogen-rich <cataclysmic variables> occur on both sides of a <cataclysmic-variable period gap> at approximately two to three hours, with a <cataclysmic-variable period minimum> near 82 minutes. Selection effects change the observed proportions. https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.2440[Knigge, Baraffe and Patterson's donor-based evolutionary study] summarizes these empirical landmarks.

= Cataclysmic-variable period minimum
{title2=$P_{\min}$}
{parent=Cataclysmic-variable orbital-period distribution}

For a <Roche-lobe>-filling donor with local radius response $R_2\propto M_2^\zeta$, the <Roche-lobe-filling period-density relation> gives $P\propto M_2^{(3\zeta-1)/2}$. As mass is lost, the period falls for $\zeta>1/3$ and rises for $\zeta<1/3$, with a minimum at the transition. The reversal is called a period bounce. Slow evolution near the turning point produces an accumulation in the period distribution: for a steady flow of systems along one evolutionary branch, $N(P)\propto1/|\dot P|$.

= Cataclysmic-variable period bounce
{parent=Cataclysmic-variable period minimum}
{title2=$\zeta=1/3$}

The reversal from decreasing to increasing <orbital period> during continuing donor mass loss. The <Roche-lobe-filling period-density relation> gives $d\log P/d\log M_d=(3\zeta-1)/2$, where $\zeta$ is the effective <stellar radius response exponent> along the mass-losing sequence. A crossing from $\zeta>1/3$ to $\zeta<1/3$ gives a local <cataclysmic-variable period minimum>.

= Cataclysmic-variable period gap
{parent=Cataclysmic-variable orbital-period distribution}

The cataclysmic-variable period gap is the observed deficit of accreting, ordinary hydrogen-rich <cataclysmic variables> with periods around two to three hours. The <disrupted magnetic braking model> interprets it as a detached phase after the donor contracts inside its <Roche lobe>.

= Magnetic braking of a binary star
{parent=Cataclysmic variable}

A magnetized wind removes spin angular momentum from a cool donor star. Tidal synchronization replenishes that spin from the orbit, so the wind indirectly removes orbital angular momentum and drives the binary toward shorter separation.

= Disrupted magnetic braking model
{parent=Magnetic braking of a binary star}

In this model, strong <magnetic braking of a binary star> inflates the mass-losing donor above the <cataclysmic-variable period gap>. A reduction in the torque near the <fully convective star> transition allows thermal contraction and detachment. <Gravitational-wave emission from a binary system> continues to shrink the orbit until contact returns below the gap. The model requires a braking decrease and a previously inflated donor, not disappearance of stellar magnetism. https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.4716[Knigge's review] describes this evolutionary picture and observational tests.

= Isotropic re-emission from a binary star
{parent=Cataclysmic variable}

In isotropic re-emission, transferred mass is expelled from the vicinity of the accretor and carries the accretor's specific orbital angular momentum. The total binary mass and orbital angular momentum then decrease even though the accretor retains no net mass.

= Supernova kick in a binary star
{parent=Binary star}

Asymmetric ejecta or neutrino emission can impart a natal kick to a compact remnant. The impulsive change in relative velocity and total mass can alter the binary's semimajor axis and eccentricity or unbind the system.

= Kick-direction binary survival criterion
{parent=Supernova kick in a binary star}
{title2=$\varepsilon'=\frac{v^2}{2}(1+2\alpha\cos\psi+\alpha^2-2\beta)$}

For an impulsive <supernova kick in a binary star> initially in a <circular Kepler orbit>, let $\beta$ be the remaining total mass fraction and $\alpha$ the kick speed divided by the original relative speed. The displayed post-kick <specific orbital energy> is negative for a bound pair and positive for escape. Over all directions, the velocity factor ranges from $(1-\alpha)^2$ to $(1+\alpha)^2$. Some direction leaves a bound pair exactly when $2\beta>(1-\alpha)^2$; some direction permits positive-energy escape exactly when $2\beta<(1+\alpha)^2$. Equalities are marginal <parabolic Kepler orbits>. The circular initial orbit and impulsive approximation are essential.

= Asymptotic relative speed of a disrupted binary
{parent=Kick-direction binary survival criterion}
{title2=$V=\sqrt{2\varepsilon'}$}

A disrupted <binary star> with positive post-impulse <specific orbital energy> $\varepsilon'$ has relative speed $V=\sqrt{2\varepsilon'}$ at infinite separation, because the <Newtonian gravitational potential energy> tends to zero. With the notation of the <kick-direction binary survival criterion>, $V=v\sqrt{1+2\alpha\cos\psi+\alpha^2-2\beta}$. This is not a systemic recoil velocity. A marginal <parabolic Kepler orbit> has $V=0$ at infinity.

= Gravitational-wave emission from a binary system
{parent=Binary star}

An accelerating binary mass quadrupole emits gravitational waves. The resulting energy and angular-momentum loss shrinks and circularizes a detached compact binary, with the evolution accelerating sharply as its separation decreases.

= Circular gravitational-wave inspiral
{parent=Gravitational-wave emission from a binary system}
{title2=$\dot a=-64G^3M^2\mu/(5c^5a^3)$}

For fixed component <masses>, total <mass> $M=M_1+M_2$ and <reduced mass> $\mu=M_1M_2/M$, in the weak-field slow-motion <circular orbits>, $E=-GM\mu/(2a)$ and $J=\mu\sqrt{GMa}$ obey $dE/dJ=\Omega$. The <gravitational-wave energy and angular-momentum balance> makes the secular loss tangent to this circular family. Substituting $v^2=GM/a$ into the <instantaneous quadrupole luminosity of a Kepler binary> and differentiating $E$ gives the displayed inspiral equation. Integration gives
$$
a^4=a_0^4-\frac{256G^3M^2\mu}{5c^5}(t-t_0),\qquad t_{\rm coal}-t_0=\frac{5c^5a_0^4}{256G^3M^2\mu}.
$$
The orbit is quasicircular, with small radial drift. The formal coalescence time is not a valid extension of the weak-field point-mass model into the final strong-field or stellar-contact phase.

= Equipotential stellar-structure approximation
{parent=Binary star}

The equipotential stellar-structure approximation replaces distorted closed equipotentials in a synchronized binary by one-dimensional shells labeled by enclosed volume and mass.

= Volume-equivalent radius
{title2=$r'$}
{parent=Equipotential stellar-structure approximation}

The volume-equivalent radius of a closed surface enclosing volume $V$ is defined by $V=4\pi r'^3/3$.

= Roche lobe
{c}
{parent=Binary star}
{wiki}

A Roche lobe is the region around one component of a circular binary inside the critical effective-potential surface through the inner Lagrange point.

= Roche-lobe-filling period-density relation
{c}
{title2=$P\propto(R_2^3/M_2)^{1/2}$}
{parent=Roche lobe}

If $R_2=R_L\simeq Ca(M_2/M)^{1/3}$, <Kepler's third law> gives
$$
P=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{GC^3}}\left(\frac{R_2^3}{M_2}\right)^{1/2}.
$$
Thus a <Roche-lobe>-filling star's period measures its inverse square-root mean <mass density>, largely independently of the companion mass in this approximation. A local <mass-radius relation> $R_2\propto M_2^\zeta$ gives $P\propto M_2^{(3\zeta-1)/2}$.

= Roche-lobe period-mass relation for a linear donor radius law
{c}
{parent=Roche-lobe-filling period-density relation}
{title2=$P/P_0=M_d/M_\odot$}

For contact with $R_d/R_\odot=M_d/M_\odot$ and $R_L/a=f(M_d/M)^{1/3}$, <Kepler's third law> gives $P_0=2\pi\sqrt{R_\odot^3/(f^3GM_\odot)}$. The total binary mass cancels. The linear period relation depends on that particular equilibrium radius sequence; a donor with a different <stellar radius response exponent> follows a different mass-period relation.

= Pre-giant Roche-lobe-filling period limit
{parent=Roche-lobe-filling period-density relation}
{title2=$P_i<2\pi\sqrt{R_{\rm BGB}^3/(C^3GM_d)}$}

For $R_L=Ca(M_d/M)^{1/3}$, the period when a donor reaches radius $R$ is $P=2\pi\sqrt{R^3/(C^3GM_d)}$. Requiring overflow before a specified evolutionary radius therefore bounds the initial period independently of companion mass in this lobe approximation. If that radius scales as $M_d^{5/3}$, the bound scales as $M_d^2$.

= Roche-lobe overflow
{c}
{parent=Roche lobe}
{wiki}

Roche-lobe overflow begins when a star fills its Roche lobe and matter flows through the inner Lagrange point toward its companion.

= Nonconservative binary mass transfer
{parent=Roche-lobe overflow}

= Nonconservative mass transfer
{synonym}

In nonconservative binary evolution, matter can escape the system and/or external processes change its orbital <angular momentum>. Escaping overflow, a <stellar wind> and a <common envelope> can invalidate the fixed-total-mass and period-product invariants of <conservative mass transfer>.

= Donor-wind angular-momentum loss
{parent=Nonconservative binary mass transfer}
{title2=$\dot J=(1-f)\dot M_d a_d^2\Omega$}

An isotropic escaping <stellar wind> has the <donor star>'s mean specific orbital <angular momentum>. With $\dot M_d<0$, retained fraction $f$, negligible spin and no additional wind torque, $\dot J=(1-f)\dot M_da_d^2\Omega$. The retained matter redistributes <angular momentum> internally. Unlike <isotropic re-emission from a binary star>, the escaping material originates at the donor rather than the accretor. With no retention this reduces to <Jeans-mode mass loss>.

= Donor-wind Roche-lobe response
{parent=Donor-wind angular-momentum loss}
{title2=$\zeta_L=(1-f)\zeta_0+f\zeta_1$}

Under <donor-wind angular-momentum loss> and the <Roche lobe> approximation $R_L=0.46a(M_d/M)^{1/3}$, the <Roche-lobe radius response exponent> is affine in retained fraction: $\zeta_L=(1-f)\zeta_0+f\zeta_1$, where $q=M_d/M_a$, $\zeta_0=1/3-4q/[3(1+q)]$ and $\zeta_1=2q-5/3$. Differentiate $\log R_L=\log a+(\log M_d-\log M)/3+\mathrm{constant}$ and the <circular-binary orbital angular momentum> to obtain this expression.

= Feasibility of donor-wind binary contact
{parent=Donor-wind Roche-lobe response}
{title2=$\min(\zeta_0,\zeta_1)\le\zeta_*\le\max(\zeta_0,\zeta_1)$}

A <donor star> with <stellar radius response exponent> $\zeta_*$ can maintain exact contact in the <donor-wind Roche-lobe response> model for some retained fraction $0\le f\le1$ precisely when $\zeta_*$ lies between $\zeta_0$ and $\zeta_1$. This follows because the attainable <Roche-lobe radius response exponents> form that closed interval. If its endpoints coincide, all fractions work when $\zeta_*$ equals the common value and none work otherwise. With negative donor mass-loss rate, $d\log(R_d/R_L)/dt=(\zeta_*-\zeta_L)\dot M_d/M_d$ distinguishes detachment from increasing overfill. <Dynamical stability of binary mass transfer> requires the adiabatic donor response.

= Period invariant for constant-fraction donor-wind mass loss
{parent=Donor-wind angular-momentum loss}
{title2=$PM_d^{3f}M_a^3(M_d+M_a)^2=\mathrm{constant}$}

For a slowly evolving <circular orbit> with constant retained fraction $f$ and <donor-wind angular-momentum loss>, logarithmic differentiation of the <circular-binary orbital angular momentum> gives $d\log P=-3f\,d\log M_d-3\,d\log M_a-2\,d\log(M_d+M_a)$. Integration gives the displayed invariant. At $f=1$ it becomes the <period-product invariant for conservative mass transfer>, while at $f=0$ it becomes $PM^2=\mathrm{constant}$. A varying retained fraction does not give this simple power-law invariant.

= Case B mass transfer
{c}
{parent=Roche-lobe overflow}

Case B mass transfer starts after core hydrogen exhaustion and before core helium ignition. It is subdivided by donor structure; <early case B mass transfer> occurs before a deep convective giant envelope develops.

= Early case B mass transfer
{parent=Case B mass transfer}

Early case B overflow occurs in an early post-main-sequence donor, commonly crossing the Hertzsprung gap with a largely radiative envelope. A stabilizing mass-loss response can allow transfer at <binary mass ratios> that would destabilize a convective giant. This is an evolutionary-stage classification, distinct from recombination terminology.

= Case A mass transfer
{c}
{parent=Roche-lobe overflow}

Case A mass transfer starts while the <donor star> is on the <main sequence>, with core hydrogen burning. Its onset precedes giant-envelope formation; donor structure and the <binary mass ratio> still determine stability.

= Binary mass-transfer contact equation
{parent=Roche-lobe overflow}
{title2=$\dot M_2/M_2=2(\dot J/J)/(\zeta+5/3-2q)$}

For a <circular orbit>, fixed total <mass>, transfer from $M_2$ to $M_1$, negligible spin and $R_L\propto a(M_2/M)^{1/3}$, the orbital <angular momentum> $J=M_1M_2\sqrt{Ga/M}$ gives
$$
\frac{\dot R_L}{R_L}=2\frac{\dot J}{J}+\left(2q-\frac53\right)\frac{\dot M_2}{M_2},\qquad q=\frac{M_2}{M_1}.
$$
If the donor follows a radius sequence with <stellar radius response exponent> $\zeta$, has no additional radius change at fixed mass, and maintains contact, equating its radius derivative to this expression proves the displayed contact equation. Negative external $\dot J$ drives donor mass loss when $\zeta+5/3-2q>0$. The appropriate equilibrium or adiabatic response and its timescale must be specified separately; the equation alone is not a universal dynamical-stability criterion.

= Conservative binary mass transfer
{parent=Roche-lobe overflow}

= Conservative mass transfer
{synonym}

Conservative binary mass transfer moves matter between the components while preserving total binary mass and orbital angular momentum.

= Period-product invariant for conservative mass transfer
{parent=Conservative binary mass transfer}
{title2=$P(M_1M_2)^3=\mathrm{constant}$}

Negligible spin and a circular orbit give $J=M_1M_2\sqrt{Ga/M}$. Fixed $M$ and $J$ imply $a(M_1M_2)^2$ is constant. <Kepler's third law> then gives the period-product invariant $P(M_1M_2)^3$.

= Minimum period-to-donor-mass ratio for conservative evolution
{parent=Period-product invariant for conservative mass transfer}
{title2=$M_d=5M/8$}

At fixed total mass $M$ and period-product invariant, $P/M_d^2$ is proportional to $[M_d^5(M-M_d)^3]^{-1}$. Its unique global minimum occurs at donor mass $5M/8$ and accretor mass $3M/8$. It is useful for testing all possible conservative progenitors against a pre-overflow period bound.

= Dynamical stability of binary mass transfer
{parent=Conservative binary mass transfer}

Binary mass transfer is dynamically stable when the donor's radius decreases relative to its Roche-lobe radius after a small mass loss, reducing overflow instead of amplifying it.

= Conservative mass-transfer critical mass ratio
{parent=Dynamical stability of binary mass transfer}
{title2=$q_{\rm crit}=(\zeta_*+5/3)/2$}

With fixed total mass and orbital <angular momentum> and $R_L\propto a(M_d/M)^{1/3}$, the <Roche-lobe radius response exponent> is $2q-5/3$. A donor with <stellar radius response exponent> $\zeta_*$ is stable to infinitesimal mass loss when $\zeta_*>2q-5/3$, giving $q<(\zeta_*+5/3)/2$. The threshold depends on the donor response.

= Stellar radius response exponent
{title2=$\zeta=d\log R/d\log M$}
{parent=Dynamical stability of binary mass transfer}

The stellar radius response exponent measures how a star's radius changes as it loses mass. The adiabatic exponent $\zeta_{\rm ad}$ describes loss too rapid for thermal adjustment; the equilibrium exponent $\zeta_{\rm eq}$ follows a sequence of thermally relaxed stars. Compare $\zeta_{\rm ad}$ with the <Roche-lobe radius response exponent> to test dynamical stability.

= Roche-lobe radius response exponent
{c}
{title2=$\zeta_L=d\log R_L/d\log M_d$}
{parent=Dynamical stability of binary mass transfer}

For <conservative binary mass transfer> and $R_L\propto a(M_d/M)^{1/3}$,
$$
\zeta_L=2q-\frac53,\qquad q=\frac{M_d}{M_a}.
$$
The <conservation of angular momentum> gives $d\log a/d\log M_d=-2(1-q)$. Since a mass loss has $d\log M_d<0$, the donor moves inside its lobe when $\zeta_{\rm ad}>\zeta_L$. An ideal fully convective donor with $\zeta_{\rm ad}=-1/3$ is therefore stable for $q<2/3$ and unstable for $q>2/3$ under these assumptions.

= White-dwarf mass--orbital-period relation
{parent=Roche-lobe overflow}

Stable envelope stripping of a low-mass red giant leaves a helium white dwarf. Because the giant radius is determined mainly by its core mass and fixes its Roche-lobe radius at detachment, the final binary orbital period is tightly related to the white-dwarf mass.

= Common envelope
{parent=Roche-lobe overflow}
{wiki}

A common-envelope phase occurs when unstable binary interaction engulfs both stellar cores in one envelope. Orbital energy and angular momentum may eject the envelope, leaving a compact binary, or the cores may merge.

= Common-envelope energy formalism
{parent=Common envelope}

The common-envelope energy formalism equates a fraction $\alpha_{\rm CE}$ of the released orbital energy to the envelope binding energy, often written $E_{\rm bind}=GM_dM_{\rm env}/(\lambda R_d)$. It estimates the post-envelope separation but compresses uncertain hydrodynamics into $\alpha_{\rm CE}$ and $\lambda$.

= Double-degenerate Type Ia supernova scenario
{c}
{parent=Common envelope}

The double-degenerate scenario forms a close pair of carbon--oxygen white dwarfs, usually through mass transfer and one or more common-envelope phases. Gravitational radiation then shrinks the orbit until merger or unstable transfer triggers thermonuclear disruption.

= Algol binary
{c}
{parent=Roche-lobe overflow}

An Algol binary is a <semidetached binary> in which an evolved, less massive <donor star> fills its <Roche lobe> while its more massive <mass gainer> remains near the <main sequence>. <Binary mass-ratio reversal> resolves the <Algol paradox>. Eclipsing members commonly have <Algol variable> light curves, but that observational class also includes detached systems.

= Algol paradox
{c}
{parent=Algol binary}
{wiki}

The Algol paradox is the apparently inconsistent evolutionary ordering in an <Algol binary>: the less massive star is more evolved, whereas higher-mass isolated <main-sequence> stars normally exhaust core fuel sooner. <Binary mass-ratio reversal> resolves it by identifying the present donor as the initially more massive star.

= Binary mass-ratio reversal
{parent=Algol binary}

During binary mass-ratio reversal, an initially more massive <donor star> loses enough material that its companion becomes the more massive member. The stars retain their different prior nuclear evolution, resolving the <Algol paradox>. <Conservative binary mass transfer> shrinks the orbit before equal masses and widens it after reversal, because $a\propto(M_dM_a)^{-2}$ at fixed total mass and orbital <angular momentum>.

= Algol mass-ratio stability limit
{c}
{title2=$q_{\rm crit}\simeq2/3$}
{parent=Algol binary}

A simplified conservative model with a fully convective donor gives long-lived stable <Roche-lobe overflow> only for donor-to-accretor mass ratio $q<2/3$. This follows from the <Roche-lobe radius response exponent> and the donor's adiabatic <stellar radius response exponent> $-1/3$. The boundary is model-dependent; radiative donors, evolved core structure and mass or angular-momentum loss change it. https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.2620[Van Rensbergen and collaborators' Algol population study] includes reported Algol mass ratios above $0.7$ and discusses observational uncertainties.

= Astrophysical disk
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

= Astrophysical disc
{synonym}

An astrophysical disk is a flattened rotating distribution of gas, dust, stars, or other matter whose orbital motion strongly influences its structure and evolution.

= Hankel representation of thin-disk gravity
{c}
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{title2=$\Phi=\int_0^\infty S(k)J_0(kR)e^{-k|z|}\,dk$}

For an axisymmetric reflection-symmetric <thin disk>, the <Laplace equation in cylindrical coordinates> separates into regular radial <Bessel functions> and vertical decaying exponentials. The <divergence theorem> imposes $\Phi_z^+-\Phi_z^-=4\pi G\Sigma$. <Hankel transform> inversion gives $S(k)=-2\pi G\int_0^\infty\Sigma(R)J_0(kR)R\,dR$. Its radial derivative gives the <galaxy rotation curve>. Infinite scale-free models may require subtracting an additive divergent constant.

= Angular-momentum-conserving self-gravitating disc radius
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{title2=$R_h/R_d=C_df_d/q^2$}

Let a disc contain fraction $f_d$ of total halo mass, take its edge gravity to be $v_d^2=C_dGM_d/R_d$, and equate its edge specific angular momentum to $qR_hv_{\rm vir}$. With $v_{\rm vir}^2=GM_h/R_h$, these conditions give $R_h/R_d=C_df_d/q^2$. The structural coefficient $C_d$ is essential: disc mass alone does not uniquely determine its edge rotation curve.

= Protoplanetary disk
{parent=Astrophysical disk}

A protoplanetary disk is a gas-and-solid <astrophysical disk> around a young star in which <planet formation> occurs. Its evolution combines <accretion>, radiation, gas dynamics and the growth of solid material. A <minimum-mass solar nebula> is one reconstruction of the material needed to form the Solar System.

= Mestel disk
{c}
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{title2=$\Sigma(R)=v_M^2/(2\pi GR)$}
{wiki}

= Mestel disc
{c}
{synonym}

A Mestel disk is a thin axisymmetric <astrophysical disk> with <surface density> inversely proportional to radius. Its <galaxy rotation curve> is exactly flat, $v_c=v_M$, and $v_c^2=GM(R)/R$ despite its nonspherical geometry. In the <Legendre expansion of thin-disk gravity>, all positive-degree interior and exterior terms cancel. Its total mass is infinite, so only potential differences, rather than a potential zero at infinity, are defined.

= Velocity moments of an isotropic Mestel disk
{parent=Mestel disk}
{title2=$\sigma_R^2=\sigma_\phi^2=v_0^2$}

The planar <Mestel disk> has $F(E)\propto e^{-E/v_0^2}$, giving independent centered <Gaussian distributions> for $v_R,v_\phi$ with <velocity dispersions> $\sigma_R=\sigma_\phi=v_0$. Its <streaming velocity> is zero. A <maximally prograde stellar distribution> instead has mean $\sqrt{2/\pi}v_0$ and azimuthal <variance> $v_0^2(1-2/\pi)$, while all second raw moments remain unchanged.

= Mestel disk potential-density pair
{c}
{parent=Mestel disk}
{title2=$\Phi=v_0^2\log[(r+|z|)/R_*]$}

The reflection-symmetric <Newtonian gravitational potential> $\Phi=v_0^2\log[(\sqrt{R^2+z^2}+|z|)/R_*]$ is harmonic off the plane. Its normal-derivative jump is $2v_0^2/R$, so the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> gives <surface density of a disk> $S=v_0^2/(2\pi GR)$ and volume <mass density> $\rho=S\delta(z)$. The midplane <circular speed> is constant. The scale-free model has infinite total <mass> and does not use a finite-mass boundary condition at infinity.

= Boundary ambiguity of a flat disk rotation curve
{parent=Mestel disk potential-density pair}

The midplane <galaxy rotation curve> fixes the radial derivative of the <Newtonian gravitational potential>, not every exterior boundary condition. Adding $a|z|$ leaves that radial derivative unchanged but adds a uniform sheet of <surface density of a disk> $a/(2\pi G)$. The pure scale-free <Mestel disk> excludes such extra sources.

= Legendre expansion of thin-disk gravity
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{title2=$\Phi=-2G\sum_{k\geq0}\alpha_{2k}(R^{-2k-1}I_{2k}+R^{2k}J_{2k})$}

For a thin axisymmetric <astrophysical disk>, angular integration of the Newtonian kernel eliminates odd <Legendre polynomials>. Define $I_n=\int_0^R\Sigma(s)s^{n+1}ds$ and $J_n=\int_R^\infty\Sigma(s)s^{-n}ds$. The even coefficient is $\alpha_n=\pi[n!/(2^n((n/2)!)^2)]^2$. In differentiating the paired inner and outer contributions, their moving-boundary terms cancel. The resulting <galaxy rotation curve> includes an inward inner-ring contribution and an outward exterior-ring contribution. For smooth <surface density>, the force at the disk is interpreted with a symmetric <Cauchy principal value> or a vanishing-thickness regularization.

= Radiation-supported vertical disk
{parent=Astrophysical disk}

A <radiation-supported vertical disk> has vertical <hydrostatic equilibrium> supplied mainly by <radiation pressure>. In a constant-<opacity>, optically thick layer in gravity $-\Omega^2z$, <radiative diffusion> gives $F=c\Omega^2z/\kappa$. This statement applies at a fixed radius and does not resolve the optically thin surface.

= Constant viscosity in radiation-supported vertical balance
{parent=Radiation-supported vertical disk}
{title2=$\mu=4c/(9\kappa)$}

Combining $F=c\Omega^2z/\kappa$ with viscous heating $dF/dz=(9/4)\mu\Omega^2$ gives a height-independent <dynamic viscosity> $\mu=4c/(9\kappa)$ under constant <opacity>. This exact limiting relation differs from an approximate vertically averaged <alpha disk> closure.

= Keplerian disk
{c}
{parent=Astrophysical disk}

= Keplerian disc
{c}
{synonym}

= Keplerian rotation
{c}
{synonym}

A Keplerian disk rotates with angular frequency $\Omega_K=\sqrt{GM/r^3}$ set mainly by a central mass, so $\Omega_K\propto r^{-3/2}$.

= Corotation radius
{parent=Keplerian disk}
{title2=$r_{\rm co}=(GM/\Omega_*^2)^{1/3}$}

The radius where <Keplerian rotation> matches the spin of the central star.

= Thin disk
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{wiki}

= Thin disc
{synonym}

A thin disk has vertical semi-thickness $H$ much smaller than cylindrical radius $r$. Vertical gravity is then approximately linear in height and orbital dynamics is nearly planar.

= Passive disk
{parent=Thin disk}

= Passive disc
{synonym}

A passive <astrophysical disk> receives most of its heat from external irradiation rather than internal <viscous dissipation>. For a stellar disk, a rough criterion is $GM\dot M/R_*\ll L_*$. The actual local requirement compares absorbed stellar flux with viscous flux; incidence angle, shadowing and absorption can invalidate a luminosity comparison alone.

= Irradiation grazing angle of a disk
{parent=Passive disk}
{title2=$\alpha\simeq z_s'-z_s/R=R\,d(z_s/R)/dR$}

For a thin illuminated surface $z=z_s(R)$ and a central point source, the radial light ray has slope $z_s/R$ whereas the surface tangent has slope $z_s'$. Their angle difference is the grazing angle. The incident flux is $L_*\alpha/(4\pi R^2)$ to leading order. This requires a positive grazing angle and an unobstructed ray; a negative formal flux instead signals failure of direct illumination, not negative heating.

= Mean projected area of a randomly oriented thin disc
{parent=Thin disk}
{title2=$\langle A_{\rm proj}\rangle=\pi R^2/2$}

A thin circular disc presents projected area $\pi R^2|\cos i|$ to a sightline. Isotropic normals have a uniform distribution of $\cos i$ on $[-1,1]$, giving mean absolute cosine one half. The resulting interception cross-section is therefore half the face-on area; no extra orientation weighting is needed after this average is used in an incidence calculation.

= Thin-disk expansion of the stellar gravitational potential
{parent=Thin disk}
{title2=$\Phi\simeq-GM/R+\Omega_K^2z^2/2$}

At fixed <cylindrical radius> $R$ with $|z|\ll R$, the central <gravitational potential> is harmonic in height. The <vertical epicyclic frequency> equals $\Omega_K$ in this spherical potential.

= Razor-thin disk approximation
{parent=Thin disk}

= Razor-thin disc
{synonym}

= Razor-thin disk
{synonym}

The razor-thin disk approximation replaces a disk's vertical density profile by $\rho(x,y,z)=\Sigma(x,y)\delta(z)$. It is useful for horizontal dynamics on scales much longer than the disk thickness.

= Sharp-edge force singularity of a thin disk
{parent=Razor-thin disk approximation}
{title2=$g_R\sim G\Sigma_{\rm edge}\log(\ell/\epsilon)$}

At an abruptly truncated <razor-thin disk> with nonzero edge <surface density of a disk>, the midplane radial force diverges logarithmically. Locally, mass occupies a half-plane and the inward force contains a nonzero angular factor multiplying $G\Sigma_{\rm edge}\int_\epsilon^\ell ds/s$. The opposite exterior half-plane is absent, so its cancellation does not occur. Finite thickness or a taper taking the edge density to zero regularizes the force. Therefore a finite-edge <galaxy rotation curve> requires more than the ideal scale-free <Mestel disk> normalization.

= Barotropic closure of a razor-thin disk
{parent=Razor-thin disk approximation}
{title2=$c_s^2=dP/d\Sigma$}

A two-dimensional disk equation with <pressure> acceleration $-\Sigma^{-1}\nabla P$ requires an equation of state for vertically integrated <pressure> $P$ in terms of <surface density of a disk> $\Sigma$. A <barotropic fluid> closure is $P=P(\Sigma)$, with linear enthalpy perturbation $h'=c_s^2\Sigma'/\Sigma_0$. Merely giving $P=P(\rho)$ for a volume density does not close the two-dimensional equations unless a vertical conversion $\rho(\Sigma)$ is specified. Positive $c_s^2$ gives ordinary <pressure> restoration of an <inertial-acoustic wave>.

= Razor-thin disk Poisson kernel
{parent=Razor-thin disk approximation}

For a horizontal Fourier mode of <wavenumber> $k>0$, the midplane gravitational potential of a razor-thin disk is
$$
\widehat\Phi_m(\mathbf k)=-\frac{2\pi G}{k}\widehat\Sigma(\mathbf k).
$$

= Off-plane razor-thin Poisson kernel
{parent=Razor-thin disk Poisson kernel}
{title2=$\widetilde\Phi(\mathbf k,z)=-(2\pi G/k)\widetilde\Sigma(\mathbf k)e^{-k|z|}$}

For $k>0$, horizontal <Fourier transform> gives $(\partial_z^2-k^2)\widetilde\Phi=4\pi G\widetilde\Sigma\delta(z)$. Decay and <continuity> give an exponential on each side; its derivative jump fixes the coefficient. The uniform mode needs a separate linear vertical solution and a potential reference.

= Positive-kernel comparison of thin-disk fields
{parent=Razor-thin disk Poisson kernel}
{title2=$|\Sigma_M|<\Sigma\ \Longrightarrow\ |\partial_z\Phi_M|<\partial_z\Phi\quad(z>0)$}

The vertical field of a disk has kernel $Gz/(|\mathbf R-\mathbf R'|^2+z^2)^{3/2}>0$. The <triangle inequality> therefore bounds a signed-<surface density> field by the field of its absolute <surface density>. If that <surface density> is everywhere smaller than a nonnegative comparison <surface density>, their vertical fields satisfy the displayed strict comparison, provided the integrals exist and the strict bound holds on positive measure. This is useful when comparing <gravity-equivalent magnetic surface density> with actual disk mass.

= Surface density of a disk
{title2=$\Sigma$}
{parent=Thin disk}

= Surface density
{synonym}

The surface density of a disk is its volume density integrated vertically, $\Sigma=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\rho\,dz$.

= Vertical structure of an astrophysical disk
{parent=Thin disk}

The vertical structure of a thin disk is set by the balance among vertical gravity, pressure, self-gravity, magnetic forces, and motions of gas and solids.

= Isothermal vertical structure in stellar gravity
{parent=Vertical structure of an astrophysical disk}
{title2=$\rho(z)=\rho_0e^{-z^2/(2H^2)},\quad H=c_s/\Omega_K$}

For a <thin disk> in the harmonic vertical <gravitational potential> $\Omega_K^2z^2/2$, <hydrostatic equilibrium> and $p=c_s^2\rho$ with height-independent <isothermal sound speed> give the displayed Gaussian density. For perturbations with <adiabatic index> $\gamma$, the <Brunt–Väisälä frequency> is $N^2=(1-1/\gamma)\Omega_K^2z^2/H^2$. An isothermal background can therefore support buoyancy for adiabatic perturbations; isothermal perturbations instead have $\gamma=1$ and zero buoyancy frequency.

= Hermite vertical mode of an isothermal disk
{c}
{parent=Isothermal vertical structure in stellar gravity}
{title2=$\xi_{xx}-x\xi_x+n\xi=0$}

= Hermite vertical modes of an isothermal disk
{c}
{synonym}

In an inviscid locally isothermal <Keplerian disk>, a radial <normal mode> of <wavenumber> $k$ has pressure variable $\xi$ proportional to a <probabilists' Hermite polynomial> in $x=z/H$. Gaussian-weighted finite energy excludes the exponentially growing vertical branch. Polynomial termination gives $n=0,1,\ldots$ and the local dispersion relation $(\widehat\omega^2-n\Omega_K^2)(\widehat\omega^2-\Omega_K^2)=k^2c_s^2\widehat\omega^2$, where $\widehat\omega=\omega-m\Omega_K$. At singular values introduced by eliminating velocity components, the original equations must also be checked. The $n=0$ propagating branch is an <inertial-acoustic wave>; $n=1$ has vertically uniform vertical velocity and odd pressure perturbation, permitting bending disturbances.

= Bending wave of a Keplerian disk
{parent=Hermite vertical mode of an isothermal disk}
{title2=$\omega\simeq\pm kc_s/2\quad(m=1,\ |k|H\ll1)$}

= Bending waves of a Keplerian disk
{synonym}

For the $n=1$, $m=1$ <Hermite vertical mode of an isothermal disk>, the local dispersion relation is $[(\omega-\Omega_K)^2-\Omega_K^2]^2=k^2c_s^2(\omega-\Omega_K)^2$. Its low inertial-frame frequency branches satisfy $\omega\simeq\pm kc_s/2$. These <waves> transport a changing tilt of successive annuli, or a warp, with <group velocity> $\pm c_s/2$. The vertical velocity is approximately independent of height. A rigid tilt is the zero-wavenumber, zero-pattern-frequency limit; the gas still executes vertical orbital motion at frequency $\Omega_K$ in its rotating frame.

= Constant gas-radiation ratio vertical disc structure
{parent=Vertical structure of an astrophysical disk}
{title2=$T\propto1-z^2/H^2,\quad\rho\propto T^3,\quad p\propto T^4$}

For constant positive $\lambda=p_r/p_g$ and molecular gas constant $\mathcal R=k/(\mu_m m_p)$, the gas–radiation <equation of state> gives $\rho=4\sigma T^3/(3c\lambda\mathcal R)$. Vertical <hydrostatic equilibrium> in harmonic central gravity therefore gives $T=T_0(1-z^2/H^2)$ with $T_0=\Omega^2H^2/[8(1+\lambda)\mathcal R]$. The <mass density> and <pressure> powers are three and four. Integrating both faces yields $\Sigma=(32/35)\rho_0H$ and $\int p\,dz=\Sigma\Omega^2H^2/9$. With constant <opacity>, <radiative diffusion> produces a linear flux and height-independent <dynamic viscosity> $\mu=4c\lambda/[9\kappa(1+\lambda)]$. This is an interior formal profile; its low-density surface requires a photospheric treatment beyond diffusion.

= Vertical dynamical timescale of a disk
{parent=Vertical structure of an astrophysical disk}
{title2=$t_{\rm dyn}\sim\Omega_z^{-1}$}

The time for a vertical <pressure> or gravity response. A <sound-crossing time> and a small vertical oscillation period have this scale in a <thin disk>. It need not equal a damping time.

= Polytropic vertical structure in stellar gravity
{parent=Vertical structure of an astrophysical disk}
{title2=$T=T_0(1-z^2/H^2),\quad\rho\propto T^n$}

In harmonic stellar gravity, an <ideal gas> with a <polytropic equation of state> of index $n>0$ has a quadratic <temperature> profile and finite semi-thickness $H^2=2(n+1)\mathcal R T_0/\Omega^2$. This differs from the <polytropic vertical structure of a self-gravitating disk>.

= Zero-radiative-flux obstruction for a polytropic ideal gas
{parent=Polytropic vertical structure in stellar gravity}

For positive finite <opacity> and nonvacuum <ideal gas> satisfying <radiative diffusion>, zero flux implies $dT/dz=0$. Write $P=\mathcal R\rho T$, where $\mathcal R=k_B/(\mu_m m_p)$ for constant <mean molecular weight> $\mu_m$ in proton-mass units. If a <polytropic equation of state> $P=K\rho^\gamma$ with constant $K>0$ is also imposed, then $T=(K/\mathcal R)\rho^{\gamma-1}$. For $\gamma\ne1$, constant <temperature> would force constant density, contradicting vertical <hydrostatic equilibrium> $P'=-\rho\Omega^2z$ on an open interval away from the midplane. The familiar finite polytropic slab for $\gamma>1$ is therefore a solution of the hydrostatic/adiabatic closure, not of this finite-opacity zero-radiative-flux system. For $\gamma=1$, the consistent isothermal profile is $\rho=\rho_m\exp[-\Omega^2z^2/(2\mathcal R T_m)]$, with no finite surface.

= Disk scale height
{title2=$H$}
{parent=Vertical structure of an astrophysical disk}

The disk scale height is the characteristic vertical thickness over which density or pressure decreases. Vertical hydrostatic balance in a thin Keplerian gas disk gives $H\simeq c_s/\Omega_K$.

= Disk aspect ratio
{parent=Disk scale height}
{title2=$H/r$}

The aspect ratio of an <astrophysical disk> is its vertical thickness scale divided by cylindrical radius. A <thin disk> has $H/r\ll1$. In gas-supported vertical <hydrostatic equilibrium> with harmonic external gravity $-\Omega_z^2z$, $H/r\sim c_s/(r\Omega_z)$, where $\Omega_z$ is the <vertical epicyclic frequency> and $c_s$ is the <sound speed>. In a centrally dominated <Keplerian disk>, $\Omega_z=\Omega$, so this compares <sound speed> with orbital speed. If disk self-gravity supplies the vertical restoring force, the relation $H\sim c_s/\Omega$ requires replacement. A polytropic free-surface height and an isothermal scale height differ by model-dependent constants even though their aspect ratios have the same basic role.

= Disk flaring
{parent=Disk aspect ratio}
{title2=$d(H/r)/dr>0$}

A disk flares when its <disk aspect ratio> increases outward. This is stronger than the statement that its height increases: a disk with $H\propto r$ has constant aspect ratio and is not flaring by this definition. The radial <temperature> and <surface density of a disk> profiles determine flaring in a specified vertical equilibrium model.

= Flaring passive disk
{parent=Disk flaring}
{title2=$z_s/R\propto R^{2/7},\quad T\propto R^{-3/7}$}

Assume a <passive disk> has local <isothermal sound speed> squared $c_s^2=aT$, <disk scale height> $H=c_s/\Omega_K$, and irradiated height $z_s=fH$ with constant $f$. Its <irradiation grazing angle of a disk> and <Stefan–Boltzmann law> give $q'=Cq^8/R^3$ for $q=z_s/R$, where $C=4\pi\sigma(GM/a)^4/(L_*f^8)$. Integrating gives $q^{-7}=R_0^2/R^2+B$, with $R_0^2=7C/2$. Where the first term dominates and the <thin disk> assumptions hold, $q\simeq(R/R_0)^{2/7}$, $H\propto R^{9/7}$ and $T\propto R^{-3/7}$. The law cannot be extrapolated into a geometrically thick region using the same grazing-angle approximation.

= Vertical epicyclic frequency
{title2=$\Omega_z$}
{parent=Vertical structure of an astrophysical disk}

The vertical epicyclic frequency is the frequency of a small vertical oscillation about a circular orbit. In an axisymmetric potential, $\Omega_z^2=\partial_z^2\Phi$ at the midplane; in a spherical potential it equals the circular orbital frequency.

= Radiation-to-gas pressure ratio
{title2=$\beta_{\rm rg}=p_{\rm rad}/p_{\rm gas}$}
{parent=Vertical structure of an astrophysical disk}

The radiation-to-gas pressure ratio compares <radiation pressure> with <gas pressure>. It is distinct from <plasma beta>, which compares gas pressure with <magnetic pressure>. Both are sometimes denoted by $\beta$, so the convention must be stated.

= Constant-pressure-ratio vertical disk model
{parent=Radiation-to-gas pressure ratio}

Assume a <thin disk> has height-independent <opacity> $\kappa_{\rm op}$ and <radiation-to-gas pressure ratio> $\beta$, with vertical gravity $-\Omega_z^2z$. <Hydrostatic equilibrium> and <radiative diffusion> imply
$$
F_z=\frac\beta{1+\beta}\frac{c\Omega_z^2}{\kappa_{\rm op}}z.
$$
Balancing its derivative against viscous heating $\rho\nu q^2\Omega^2$ fixes the <dynamic viscosity>
$$
\rho\nu=\frac\beta{1+\beta}\frac{c}{q^2\kappa_{\rm op}}\frac{\Omega_z^2}{\Omega^2}.
$$
It is independent of height under these assumptions. For a <spherically symmetric potential>, $\Omega_z=\Omega$. Integrating $\rho\nu$ through the full surface-to-surface thickness $2H$ gives $\bar\nu\Sigma=2H\rho\nu$, which determines the thickness from the radial <accretion rate> solution.

= Dust settling in an astrophysical disk
{parent=Vertical structure of an astrophysical disk}

Dust settling is the vertical drift of solid particles toward a disk's midplane under gravity and <drag force>. In the strong-drag limit, a particle with <aerodynamic stopping time> $\tau$ settles through a linear vertical gravitational field at rate $\Omega_z^2\tau$.

= Homologous vertical motion of an astrophysical disk
{parent=Vertical structure of an astrophysical disk}

In homologous vertical motion, every height scales by one time-dependent thickness $H(t)$, so $z=H(t)\zeta$ and the vertical velocity is $u_z=\dot H\zeta$. A fixed dimensionless density profile then expands and contracts without changing shape.

= Slow cooling of an astrophysical disk
{parent=Homologous vertical motion of an astrophysical disk}
{title2=$\eta=(1+Ct)^{-1/(1+2\beta)}$}

A laminar column can contract through nearly <hydrostatic equilibrium> when its cooling time greatly exceeds its <vertical dynamical timescale of a disk>. For cooling $AT^{1+\beta}$ and fixed column mass, a <similarity solution> has $\rho\propto\eta^{-1}$, $T\propto\eta^2$, $P\propto\eta$, and vertical velocity $u_z=\dot\eta z/\eta$. The coefficient $C$ also depends on the initial <mass density> normalization.

= Vertical breathing mode of an astrophysical disk
{title2=$\omega_H$}
{parent=Homologous vertical motion of an astrophysical disk}

The vertical breathing mode is a coherent oscillation of a disk's thickness. For an adiabatic gas with pressure proportional to $H^{1-\gamma}$ in a harmonic vertical potential, $\omega_H=\sqrt{\gamma+1}\,\Omega_z$.

= Accretion disk
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{wiki}

= Accretion disc
{synonym}

An accretion disk transports mass inward and angular momentum outward through stresses, while dissipating orbital energy as heat.

= Wind-powered mass loss from a steady accretion disk
{parent=Accretion disk}
{title2=$\dot M'=2\pi RS,\quad\nu\Sigma=R\dot M'/(9\pi f)$}

For a <Keplerian disk>, let $S$ be the total loss through both faces per unit projected area and $\dot M>0$ the inward <accretion rate>. Annular <mass conservation> gives $\dot M'=2\pi RS$. A circular orbit has <specific orbital energy> $-GM/(2R)$, so a marginally escaping wind requires additional energy $GM/(2R)$ per unit lost mass. Equating this sink to a fraction $f$ of the two-face <viscous dissipation> $(9/4)\nu\Sigma\Omega^2$ gives the displayed relation. If the wind carries the local <specific angular momentum> $h=\sqrt{GMR}$, <conservation of angular momentum> gives $(\dot Mh-\mathcal G)'=h\dot M'$, with <viscous torque in an accretion disk> $\mathcal G=3\pi\nu\Sigma h$. Eliminating the torque yields $R^2\dot M''+\tfrac32R\dot M'=\tfrac32f\dot M$.

= Accretion-disk boundary layer
{parent=Accretion disk}
{title2=$T_{\rm BL},\ L_{\rm BL}$}

An accretion-disk boundary layer connects nearly <Keplerian rotation> to a stellar surface rotating at a different angular speed. For a slowly rotating star, dissipation of the incoming orbital <kinetic energy> gives $L_{\rm BL}\simeq GM\dot M/(2r_*)$, comparable to the <standard thin-disk luminosity>. If the emitting area scales as $r_*H$, its <blackbody> <effective temperature> scales as $T_{\rm BL}\sim(r_*/H)^{1/4}T_{\rm in}$. The coefficient depends on geometry, and stellar rotation reduces the available energy.

= Accretion flow
{parent=Accretion disk}

An accretion flow carries matter inward through a gravitationally bound system. In a disk, angular-momentum transport permits the inward drift while stresses carry angular momentum outward.

= Magnetically channelled accretion
{parent=Accretion flow}

In magnetically channelled accretion, a strong <magnetic field> guides an <accretion flow> along <flux tubes>. The component of the <Lorentz force density> parallel to the <magnetic field> vanishes, so the longitudinal flow can be treated hydrodynamically while the field controls the transverse geometry. A <magnetic dipole field> near a <neutron star> channels matter toward small polar caps.

= Accretion column
{parent=Magnetically channelled accretion}

An <accretion column> carries an inward <accretion flow> onto a localized region of a star. In the strong-field slender approximation, the <magnetic field> controls the <flux-tube area> while longitudinal pressure and gravity determine acceleration. The signed outward-coordinate velocity is negative; the inward speed and <mass accretion rate> are usually defined positive.

= Magnetic spin equilibrium
{parent=Magnetically channelled accretion}
{title2=$\Omega_*\sim\Omega_K(r_m)$}

The spin at which the net material and <magnetic torque> on an accreting star vanishes. The <corotation radius> is typically comparable to the <magnetospheric truncation radius>, but the precise torque balance depends on the coupling and any outflow.

= Propeller effect
{parent=Magnetically channelled accretion}

= Propeller flow
{synonym}

A rapidly spinning magnetosphere adds <angular momentum> to inflowing matter outside the <corotation radius>, inhibiting accretion and potentially ejecting gas. The centrifugal onset condition does not by itself ensure that every parcel escapes to infinity.

= Magnetospheric truncation radius
{parent=Magnetically channelled accretion}
{title2=$r_m$}

The inner radius at which a stellar <magnetic dipole field> disrupts a surrounding <accretion disk>. In a <free fall> <pressure>-balance estimate with fixed aspect ratio $\epsilon=H/r$, $r_m\sim(\epsilon^2\mu^4/(GM\dot M^2))^{1/7}$. Geometry and inflow structure change its numerical coefficient.

= Transonic accretion in a dipolar flux tube
{parent=Magnetically channelled accretion}

The <dipolar flux-tube area> has $A(r)=A_\star(r/R_\star)^3$. Applying <transonic accretion in a power-law tube> with $n=3$ and a <polytropic equation of state> gives
$$
c_s^2=\frac{2c_0^2}{7-5\gamma},
\qquad
r_s=\frac{GM(7-5\gamma)}{6c_0^2},
\qquad
\rho_s=\rho_0\left(\frac2{7-5\gamma}\right)^{1/(\gamma-1)}.
$$
Here $c_0$ and $\rho_0$ are the reservoir <sound speed> and <mass density>, and $c_s$ is the <sound speed> at the <sonic point>. The <mass accretion rate> is
$$
\dot M=A_\star\left(\frac{r_s}{R_\star}\right)^3\rho_s c_s.
$$
For two equal polar caps of surface angular radius $\theta_\star\ll1$, $A_\star\simeq2\pi R_\star^2\theta_\star^2$. One loaded cap gives half the total rate. The finite-radius <transonic branch> exists for $1<\gamma<7/5$, must cross outside the star ($r_s>R_\star$), and requires $\theta_\star^2r_s/R_\star\ll1$ for the sonic region to remain a narrow <flux tube>.

= Isothermal dipolar accretion equation
{parent=Transonic accretion in a dipolar flux tube}
{title2=$(v-c_s^2/v)v\prime=3c_s^2/z-GM/z^2$}

A narrow <dipolar flux-tube area> $A(z)\propto z^3$ gives $A\rho v=\dot M$ for inward speed $v>0$. The <isothermal magnetic Bernoulli integral> in $\Phi=-GM/z$ then gives $(v-c_s^2/v)v\prime=3c_s^2/z-GM/z^2$. A smooth <transonic branch> crosses at $z_s=GM/(3c_s^2)$ with $v_s=c_s$, $\rho_s=e^{5/2}\rho_\infty$ and $\dot M=\rho_sc_sA(z_s)$. The crossing must lie in the domain; subsonic branches need not attain it.

= Critical adiabatic index for dipolar accretion
{title2=$\gamma_{\rm crit}=7/5$}
{parent=Transonic accretion in a dipolar flux tube}

For <transonic accretion in a dipolar flux tube>, the <Bernoulli equation> at the <sonic point> contains $c_s^2[1/(\gamma-1)-5/2]$. A positive reservoir energy requires $\gamma<7/5$. Compression in the $r^3$ <dipolar flux-tube area> raises the <specific enthalpy> more rapidly than in the $r^2$ geometry of spherical <Bondi accretion>, whose corresponding upper index is $5/3$.

= Accretion rate
{title2=$\dot M$}
{parent=Accretion disk}

The accretion rate is the mass crossing an inward-oriented cylindrical surface per unit time. A common convention takes $\dot M>0$ for inward flow, so the outward radial mass flux is $-\dot M$.

= Hyperaccreting black-hole disk
{parent=Accretion disk}

A hyperaccreting black-hole disk has an accretion rate so large that photon cooling can be trapped and neutrino emission can dominate the cooling of its hot dense inner region. Such disks are models for the central engines of some gamma-ray bursts.

= Neutrino-dominated accretion flow
{title2=NDAF}
{parent=Hyperaccreting black-hole disk}

A neutrino-dominated accretion flow is a hyperaccreting disk whose inner plasma cools primarily through neutrino-producing weak interactions.

= Viscous evolution of an accretion disk
{parent=Accretion disk}

Viscous disk evolution combines mass conservation with radial transport of angular momentum by stress. In a steady Keplerian disk, the sum of advective and stress-carried angular-momentum fluxes is independent of radius.

= Viscous stability of an accretion disk
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}
{title2=$\partial_\Sigma(\bar\nu\Sigma)>0$}

Within an instantaneous vertically averaged transport closure, the local <Keplerian viscous diffusion equation> has effective diffusion coefficient $3\partial_\Sigma(\bar\nu\Sigma)$. A positive coefficient smooths short radial <Fourier modes> in the allowed thin-disc wavelength range; a negative coefficient gives the <negative-diffusion criterion for viscous disk instability>. A zero coefficient is linearly degenerate and does not establish strict decay. This local response criterion alone does not settle thermal, self-gravitating or boundary-driven stability.

= Vertically averaged viscous disk equations
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}
{title2=$\partial_t\Sigma+r^{-1}\partial_r(r\Sigma v_r)=0$}

In an axisymmetric <thin disc> with no vertical mass or <torque> flux, integrate mass and <conservation of angular momentum> over height. With fixed height-independent $l=r^2\Omega$, the equations are $\partial_t\Sigma+r^{-1}\partial_r(r\Sigma v_r)=0$ and $\partial_t(\Sigma l)+r^{-1}\partial_r(r\Sigma v_rl-r^3\bar\nu\Sigma\Omega')=0$. Their difference gives $r\Sigma v_rl'=(r^3\bar\nu\Sigma\Omega')'$. The <density-weighted viscosity of a disk> supplies the integrated stress.

= Keplerian viscous diffusion equation
{c}
{parent=Vertically averaged viscous disk equations}
{title2=$\Sigma_t=(3/r)\partial_r[r^{1/2}\partial_r(r^{1/2}\bar\nu\Sigma)]$}

Use $l=\sqrt{GM_*r}$ and $\Omega'=-(3/2)\Omega/r$ in the <vertically averaged viscous disk equations>. The radial drift is $v_r=-3(\Sigma\sqrt r)^{-1}\partial_r(\sqrt r\bar\nu\Sigma)$. <Conservation of mass> gives this nonlinear <diffusion equation>. It assumes the central rotation law remains fixed and excludes wind/surface <torque> terms.

= Square-root-radius diffusion transform
{parent=Keplerian viscous diffusion equation}
{title2=$x=\sqrt r,\quad g=\sqrt r\bar\nu_0\Sigma_1,\quad g_t=3q\bar\nu_0g_{xx}/(4r)$}

For a steady zero-mass-flux background and constant <viscous transport response exponent>, this change removes the first derivative in the radial diffusion operator. If $\Sigma_0\propto r^{-p}$, the <kinematic viscosity> scales as $r^{p-1/2}$, so the transformed diffusivity scales as $x^{2p-3}$. It is constant at $p=3/2$.

= General steady viscous flux in a Keplerian disk
{parent=Keplerian viscous diffusion equation}
{title2=$\bar\nu\Sigma=A+B r^{-1/2},\quad\dot M=3\pi A$}

Two integrations of the steady <Keplerian viscous diffusion equation> give this general transported quantity. $A$ fixes the <mass accretion rate>, while $B$ fixes the additive <angular-momentum flux> or inner <torque>. A <zero-torque inner boundary condition> gives $B=-A\sqrt{r_{\rm in}}$; a <nonaccreting constant-torque disk> has $A=0$.

= Angular-momentum integrability of a Keplerian disk
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}

A <Keplerian disk> with <surface density> $\Sigma\sim r^{-p}$ at infinity has finite outer <mass> for $p>2$ and finite outer <angular momentum> for $p>5/2$, since the relevant integrals are $\int\Sigma r\,dr$ and $\int\Sigma r^{3/2}dr$. The borderline $p=5/2$ has logarithmically divergent angular momentum even though its mass converges.

= Inverse-radius viscous disk similarity
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}
{title2=$g=C e^{-1/(\tau\sqrt x)}$}

For a <Keplerian accretion disk> with $\bar\nu\propto r^{5/2}$, dimensionless $g=\bar\nu\Sigma/(\bar\nu_0\Sigma_0)$ satisfies $g_\tau=4x^{5/2}g_{xx}+6x^{3/2}g_x$. In $y=x^{-1/2}$ this is $g_\tau=y g_{yy}$. The ansatz $g=G(y/\tau)$ gives $G=A+B e^{-y/\tau}$; finite disk <mass> selects $A=0$. Its nonzero profile has logarithmically infinite disk <angular momentum> on an infinite radial domain.

= Steady viscous disk supplied by a radial source
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}
{title2=$\mathcal F(r)=2\pi\int_r^{r_2}r'S(r')\,dr'$}

For a <Keplerian disk> with evolution $\partial_t\Sigma=(2\pi r)^{-1}\partial_r\mathcal F+S$ and $\mathcal F=6\pi r^{1/2}\partial_r(r^{1/2}\bar\nu\Sigma)$, take zero viscous <torque> at $r_1>0$ and zero flux outside the source region at $r_2>r_1$. The <steady state> gives $\mathcal F'=-2\pi rS$, and its outer condition gives the displayed flux. Integrating once more from the <zero-torque inner boundary condition> yields
$$
\bar\nu(r)\Sigma(r)=\frac{1}{3\sqrt r}\int_{r_1}^{r}\frac{du}{\sqrt u}\int_u^{r_2}vS(v)\,dv.
$$
The equation determines the viscosity-density product, so a thermodynamic viscosity closure can be imposed afterward. For nonnegative integrable $S$, it is nonnegative and the inner <accretion rate> is exactly the total supplied mass per unit time.

= Outer-edge source of a viscous disk
{parent=Steady viscous disk supplied by a radial source}
{title2=$\bar\nu\Sigma=\frac{\dot M}{3\pi}(1-\sqrt{r_1/r})$}

A narrow <surface density of a disk> source approaching the outer edge from inside, with $S=S_0\delta(r-r_2)$ normalized to have full weight, gives $\dot M=2\pi r_2S_0$ and the displayed steady interior profile. This follows by replacing the inner source integral by $r_2S_0$ and integrating $u^{-1/2}$. The flux jumps from $\dot M$ just inside to zero outside the injecting edge. Thus a zero exterior flux is consistent, whereas a continuous zero interior boundary trace is not. A half-weight convention for a symmetric endpoint <Dirac delta function> represents a different total injected mass; it must not be silently mixed with the full-weight boundary source.

= Steady accretion disk with arbitrary rotation law
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}

For inward <accretion rate> $\dot M>0$, the steady <conservation of angular momentum> equation is $\mathcal G=\dot M(h-h_{\rm in})$ when the inner torque vanishes. The <viscous torque in an accretion disk> is $\mathcal G=2\pi qh\bar\nu\Sigma$, where $h=r^2\Omega$ and $q$ is the <orbital shear parameter>. Thus
$$
\bar\nu\Sigma=\frac{\dot M}{2\pi q}\left(1-\frac{h_{\rm in}}h\right).
$$
The familiar <Keplerian accretion disk> follows when $q=3/2$ and $h\propto r^{1/2}$. The general form also applies to the non-Keplerian <Paczyński-Wiita circular orbit> rotation law.

= Viscous timescale
{title2=$t_\nu$}
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}

The viscous timescale is the radial diffusion time $t_\nu\sim R^2/\nu$ associated with a kinematic viscosity $\nu$ on length scale $R$.

= Viscous torque in an accretion disk
{title2=$\mathcal G$}
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}

For vertically integrated kinematic viscosity $\bar\nu$, the viscous torque across a cylinder of radius $r$ is
$$
\mathcal G=-2\pi\bar\nu\Sigma r^3\frac{d\Omega}{dr}.
$$

= Nonaccreting constant-torque disk
{parent=Viscous torque in an accretion disk}
{title2=$\bar\nu\Sigma=B r^{-1/2},\quad\mathcal G=3\pi\sqrt{GM_*}B$}

A steady <Keplerian disk> can transport <angular momentum> by viscous stress with zero mass flux. The radial drift vanishes, but the <torque> is constant and nonzero. An inner source and outer sink must supply its boundary angular-momentum exchange. This is distinct from a decretion flow, which transports mass outward.

= Outer angular-momentum flux of a viscous disk
{parent=Viscous torque in an accretion disk}

Integrating the <Keplerian accretion disk> diffusion equation gives $dJ/dt=6\pi\sqrt{GM_\star}[r^{3/2}\partial_r(\bar\nu\Sigma)]_{\rm inner}^{\rm outer}$ when the angular-momentum integral and boundary limits are legitimate. An inner <zero-torque inner boundary condition> alone does not eliminate the outer <angular-momentum flux>. For divergent total $J$, use finite-radius cutoffs before making a conservation claim.

= Zero-torque inner boundary condition
{parent=Viscous torque in an accretion disk}

The zero-torque inner boundary condition sets the viscous torque to zero where nearly circular disk flow joins a plunging or stress-free region.

= Plunging region of a black-hole accretion disk
{parent=Zero-torque inner boundary condition}

The plunging region begins near the <innermost stable circular orbit>, where nearly circular motion becomes unstable and radial inflow becomes rapid. If the inflow time is shorter than the stress-transport time, the gas approximately conserves its <specific angular momentum> there.

= Surface magnetic torque on an accretion disk
{title2=$\mathcal T$}
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}

= Surface magnetic torque
{synonym}

= Magnetic torque
{synonym}

A magnetic field crossing the two faces of an accretion disk exerts a surface torque through its azimuthal and vertical Maxwell stress. This torque can remove angular momentum without relying on radial internal stress.

= Keplerian accretion disk
{c}
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}

In a steady Keplerian accretion disk with zero inner torque,
$$
\bar\nu\Sigma=\frac{\dot M}{3\pi}\left[1-\left(\frac{r_{\rm in}}r\right)^{1/2}\right].
$$

= Steady Keplerian accretion disk with an inner torque
{parent=Keplerian accretion disk}
{title2=$\bar\nu\Sigma=\frac{\dot M}{3\pi}[1+(\lambda-1)\sqrt{r_{\rm in}/r}]$}

For a positive inner <magnetic torque> $T_m$, define $\lambda=T_m/(\dot M h_{\rm in})$. Integrating steady <conservation of angular momentum> gives $\mathcal G=T_m+\dot M(h-h_{\rm in})$. With <Keplerian rotation>, this determines the <surface density of a disk> in the displayed formula.

= Torque-dominated accretion disk
{parent=Steady Keplerian accretion disk with an inner torque}
{title2=$\Sigma\propto r^{-1/2}\quad(\bar\nu=\text{constant})$}

When the applied inner torque dominates the advective <angular-momentum flux>, the constant-<kinematic viscosity> inner profile resembles a <decretion disk>. This resemblance does not alone imply outward mass transport.

= Viscous instability of an accretion disk
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}

The <negative-diffusion criterion for viscous disk instability> is $\partial_\Sigma(\bar\nu\Sigma)<0$. A <surface density> enhancement then transports angular momentum less effectively and grows. Freezing the <Keplerian viscous diffusion equation> locally gives growth proportional to the square of radial <wavenumber>, within the thin-disc transport range; wavelengths must still be sufficiently long compared with the <disk scale height> for that closure. The formal short-wave limit is a <backward heat equation>.

= Negative-diffusion criterion for viscous disk instability
{parent=Viscous instability of an accretion disk}
{title2=$\partial_\Sigma(\bar\nu\Sigma)<0$}

Freezing coefficients locally in the <Keplerian viscous diffusion equation> gives $\Sigma_{1,t}\simeq3\partial_\Sigma(\bar\nu\Sigma)\Sigma_{1,rr}$. A radial <Fourier mode> grows at rate $-3\partial_\Sigma(\bar\nu\Sigma)k_r^2$. Negative response reverses ordinary smoothing and gives a <backward heat equation>, within the <wavelength> range where the <thin disc> transport closure applies.

= Viscous transport response exponent
{parent=Viscous instability of an accretion disk}
{title2=$q=\partial\log(\bar\nu\Sigma)/\partial\log\Sigma$}

The response to a small <surface density> perturbation is $\delta(\bar\nu\Sigma)=q\bar\nu\delta\Sigma$. For an instantaneous law $\bar\nu\propto r^a\Sigma^b$, $q=b+1$. This exponent is not the orbital shear exponent or a satellite-to-star mass ratio, which also often use the letter $q$.

= Radiation-pressure viscous instability
{parent=Viscous instability of an accretion disk}

In a characteristic vertical model of a radiation-pressure-dominated <alpha disk> with constant <opacity>, $\mu\sim\alpha p/\Omega\sim c/\kappa$ and $p\sim\Sigma\Omega^2H$ imply $H\propto\Sigma^{-1}$ and $\bar\nu\Sigma\propto\Sigma^{-1}$. The resulting $d(\bar\nu\Sigma)/d\Sigma<0$ gives a <viscous instability of an accretion disk>, distinct from a <thermal instability of a radiation-pressure-dominated alpha disk>.

= Viscous spreading of a narrow planetary ring
{parent=Viscous instability of an accretion disk}

In a local Keplerian sheet, slow viscous drift gives $\partial_t\Sigma=3\partial_x^2(\nu\Sigma)$. For $\nu=A\Sigma^2$, the compact profile $\Sigma=\sigma\sqrt{1-x^2/w^2}$ preserves its shape, conserves $\sigma w$, and spreads with $w\propto t^{1/4}$ at late times.

= Decretion disk
{parent=Viscous evolution of an accretion disk}
{wiki}

A decretion disk is fed near its inner edge and transports mass outward. Rapidly rotating Be stars can sustain gaseous decretion disks whose outer material is removed by a companion, radiation, or a wind.

= Be star
{c}
{parent=Decretion disk}
{wiki}

A Be star is a rapidly rotating B-type star showing Balmer emission from a circumstellar gaseous decretion disk.

= Alpha disk
{title2=$\alpha$-disk}
{parent=Accretion disk}
{wiki}

The alpha-disk prescription writes turbulent kinematic viscosity as $\nu=\alpha c_sH\simeq\alpha c_s^2/\Omega$, with dimensionless $\alpha\lesssim1$. It reflects eddies no larger than the disk thickness and no faster than the sound speed.

= Vertically averaged alpha closure for a mixed-pressure layer
{parent=Alpha disk}
{title2=$\bar\nu=\alpha\Omega H^2/9$}

For the <constant gas-radiation ratio vertical disc structure>, the integrated alpha prescription $\bar\nu\Sigma=\int\mu\,dz=(\alpha/\Omega)\int p\,dz$ gives the displayed mean <kinematic viscosity> and $\lambda/(1+\lambda)=\alpha H\Omega\kappa\Sigma/(8c)$. Uniform <dynamic viscosity> and nonuniform <pressure> are compatible because this prescription is an integrated equality. Imposing the corresponding pointwise equality would not preserve the exact vertical solution.

= Gas-pressure branch of a mixed-pressure alpha disk
{parent=Vertically averaged alpha closure for a mixed-pressure layer}
{title2=$\bar\nu\propto r\Sigma^{2/3}$}

For small positive radiation-to-gas ratio, constant <opacity>, fixed alpha and molecular constants, eliminate $\lambda$ between $\lambda\propto H\Omega\Sigma$ and $\lambda\Sigma\propto H^7\Omega^6$. This gives $H^6\propto\Sigma^2\Omega^{-5}$ and $\bar\nu\propto\Sigma^{2/3}\Omega^{-2/3}$. <Keplerian rotation> supplies the displayed radial power. Since $\bar\nu\Sigma\propto\Sigma^{5/3}$, its derivative with respect to <surface density> is positive and local viscous perturbations diffuse rather than amplify.

= Pointwise alpha-prescription obstruction in a radiation-supported layer
{parent=Alpha disk}

In the exact constant-<opacity>, pure-<radiation pressure> diffusion model, <dynamic viscosity> is height-independent. Imposing an additional exact pointwise relation $\mu\propto p(z)$ forces constant pressure, incompatible with nonzero vertical <hydrostatic equilibrium> away from the midplane. The characteristic-pressure <alpha disk> prescription can still be used for order-of-magnitude scalings; it is not an exact simultaneous vertical solution.

= Accretion-disk self-gravity
{parent=Alpha disk}

Accretion-disk self-gravity becomes important when the disk's own gravitational field is comparable with the central object's restoring field. The outer disk can then become gravitationally unstable, fragment, and form stars.

= Shakura--Sunyaev thin disk
{c}
{parent=Alpha disk}
{wiki}

The Shakura--Sunyaev thin disk is a geometrically thin, optically thick, radiatively efficient accretion-disk model whose turbulent stress is parametrized by the alpha prescription.

= Multitemperature blackbody disk
{parent=Shakura--Sunyaev thin disk}

A multitemperature blackbody disk is an optically thick disk approximated as concentric blackbody annuli of different effective temperatures. If $T\propto R^{-3/4}$, its intermediate-frequency spectrum satisfies $S_\nu\propto\nu^{1/3}$.

= One-third spectrum of a multitemperature disk
{parent=Multitemperature blackbody disk}
{title2=$F_\nu\propto\nu^{1/3}$}

For a <blackbody> disk with $T\propto r^{-3/4}$, the substitution $x=h\nu/(k_BT)$ in $F_\nu\propto\nu^3\int r(e^x-1)^{-1}dr$ produces $F_\nu\propto\nu^{1/3}\int x^{5/3}(e^x-1)^{-1}dx$. When the inner and outer temperatures make the limits approximately zero and infinity, that convergent integral is independent of <frequency>. The law applies between the outer-edge turnover and the hot inner-disk cutoff.

= Rayleigh-Jeans spectrum of a finite blackbody disk
{parent=Multitemperature blackbody disk}
{c}
{title2=$F_\nu\propto\nu^2$}

For a finite <multitemperature blackbody disk>, the low-<frequency> <Rayleigh-Jeans law> gives $F_\nu\propto\nu^2\int rT(r)\,dr$. If $T\propto r^{-3/4}$ away from the inner edge, outer annuli dominate because the weight per logarithmic radial interval grows as $r^{5/4}$. A measure-zero zero-temperature inner edge does not invalidate the leading law when $rT(r)$ is integrable.

= Pressure-opacity zones of a Shakura--Sunyaev thin disk
{c}
{parent=Shakura--Sunyaev thin disk}

The standard thin-disk solution separates approximately into an inner radiation-pressure and electron-scattering zone, a middle gas-pressure and electron-scattering zone, and an outer gas-pressure and free-free-opacity zone.

= Radiation-supported inner-disk height
{title2=$H(R)$}
{parent=Pressure-opacity zones of a Shakura--Sunyaev thin disk}

The inner <Shakura--Sunyaev thin disk> is supported mainly by <radiation pressure> and has nearly constant <electron-scattering opacity>. Equating <radiative diffusion> with local dissipation and imposing vertical <hydrostatic equilibrium> gives the characteristic half-thickness
$$
H(R)\simeq\frac{3\kappa_{\rm es}\dot M}{8\pi c}\left(1-\sqrt{R_{\rm in}/R}\right).
$$
The radiation-supported height approaches a plateau away from the inner boundary. The formal zero at that boundary is outside the reliable radiation-dominated approximation. At an <Eddington accretion rate> defined using efficiency $0.1$, this height can make the inner flow only marginally thin.

= Subsonic radial drift in a thin disk
{parent=Shakura--Sunyaev thin disk}

In a steady thin alpha disk, $|u_R|/c_s$ is of order $\alpha H/R\ll1$, whereas $u_\phi/c_s$ is of order $R/H\gg1$. Matter therefore completes many nearly circular orbits during its slow viscous drift inward.

= Standard thin-disk dissipation flux
{parent=Shakura--Sunyaev thin disk}

For a steady Keplerian disk with a zero-torque inner edge $R_{\rm in}$, the flux summed over both faces is
$$
F_{\rm diss}(R)=\frac{3GM\dot M}{4\pi R^3}
\left[1-\left(\frac{R_{\rm in}}R\right)^{1/2}\right].
$$

= Effective-temperature profile of a zero-torque disk
{parent=Standard thin-disk dissipation flux}
{title2=$T_{\rm eff}(r)$}

A <Keplerian accretion disk> with a <zero-torque inner boundary condition> and local <blackbody> cooling has $T_{\rm eff}^4=3GM\dot M(1-\sqrt{r_{\rm in}/r})/(8\pi\sigma r^3)$. The <Stefan–Boltzmann law> uses the flux through one face; the <standard thin-disk dissipation flux> includes both faces. The scale $T_{\rm in}=[3GM\dot M/(8\pi\sigma r_{\rm in}^3)]^{1/4}$ is a normalization, not the actual temperature at the stress-free inner edge.

= Maximum effective temperature of a zero-torque disk
{parent=Effective-temperature profile of a zero-torque disk}
{title2=$T_{\max}\simeq0.488T_{\rm in}$}

For the <effective-temperature profile of a zero-torque disk>, differentiation of $y^{-3}(1-y^{-1/2})$ with $y=r/r_{\rm in}$ gives $y_{\max}=49/36$. Thus $T_{\max}=(6/7)^{3/2}7^{-1/4}T_{\rm in}\simeq0.488T_{\rm in}$. This distinguishes a genuine maximum <effective temperature> from the normalization used in a power-law approximation.

= Standard thin-disk luminosity
{parent=Standard thin-disk dissipation flux}

Integrating the <standard thin-disk dissipation flux> gives
$$
L_{\rm thin}=\frac{GM\dot M}{2R_{\rm in}}
=\frac12\dot M R_{\rm in}^2\Omega_{K,\rm in}^2.
$$
Half of the Newtonian potential-energy decrease remains as orbital kinetic energy until the inner edge.

= Magnetorotational instability
{title2=MRI}
{parent=Alpha disk}
{wiki}

The magnetorotational instability destabilizes a magnetized differentially rotating flow when angular velocity decreases outward. Magnetic tension couples neighboring fluid elements, allowing the inner one to lose angular momentum and fall inward while the outer one gains angular momentum and moves outward.

= Horizontally invariant magnetized shearing-sheet equations
{parent=Magnetorotational instability}

For <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> at constant <mass density> $\rho$, a <shearing sheet> with velocity $-q\Omega x\mathbf e_y+\mathbf v_h(z,t)$ and <magnetic field> $\mathbf B_h(z,t)+B_z\mathbf e_z$ obeys
$$
\partial_tv_x-2\Omega v_y=\frac{B_z}{\mu_0\rho}\partial_zB_x,\qquad
\partial_tv_y+(2-q)\Omega v_x=\frac{B_z}{\mu_0\rho}\partial_zB_y,
$$
$$
\partial_tB_x=B_z\partial_zv_x,\qquad
\partial_tB_y+q\Omega B_x=B_z\partial_zv_y.
$$
Horizontal invariance and vanishing vertical velocity eliminate nonlinear <advection>. These equations retain <Coriolis acceleration>, orbital shear and <magnetic tension>. The vertical pressure adjusts to balance gravity and <magnetic pressure>.

= Magnetic bending in an incompressible disk
{parent=Horizontally invariant magnetized shearing-sheet equations}

In the <steady state> of the <horizontally invariant magnetized shearing-sheet equations>, let $B_x(\pm H)=\pm B_+$ and $B_y(\pm H)=0$. For $q>0$ and $B_z\ne0$, the symmetric equilibrium has
$$
B_x=B_+\frac{\sin(Kz)}{\sin(KH)},\quad B_y=v_x=0,\quad
v_y=-\frac{B_z}{2\mu_0\rho\Omega}\frac{dB_x}{dz},\qquad
K^2=\frac{2q\mu_0\rho\Omega^2}{B_z^2}.
$$
This follows by combining azimuthal induction with radial <magnetic tension> balance to obtain $B_x''+K^2B_x=0$. Nonzero $B_+$ requires $\sin(KH)\ne0$; even homogeneous additions at $\cos(KH)=0$ are excluded by imposing midplane symmetry.

= Nonmonotonic magnetic bending
{parent=Magnetic bending in an incompressible disk}

For nonzero imposed surface inclination, <magnetic bending in an incompressible disk> is nonmonotonic when $KH>\pi/2$: the first maximum of $|B_x|$ occurs at $z=\pi/(2K)<H$, followed by a decrease. This is exactly the <finite-thickness magnetorotational instability criterion> for the same slab. The zero-inclination equilibrium can still be unstable, so nonmonotonic bending is a property of the forced profile, rather than a condition on every possible unstable equilibrium.

= Ideal magnetorotational dispersion relation
{parent=Magnetorotational instability}

For a vertical <magnetic field> in a homogeneous <shearing sheet>, a horizontal <normal mode> with growth rate $s$ and vertical <Alfvén frequency> $\omega_a=kv_{Az}$ satisfies
$$
(s^2+\omega_a^2)(s^2+\omega_a^2-2q\Omega^2)+4\Omega^2s^2=0.
$$
The two roots for $s^2$ are real. For $q>0$, a growing magnetic mode exists precisely when $0<\omega_a^2<2q\Omega^2$. <Magnetic tension> is strong enough to couple radially displaced elements, yet weak enough to allow their separation to increase. https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/gio10/dad14.pdf[Ogilvie's lecture on magnetorotational instability] derives this relation and its finite-thickness boundary conditions.

= Maximum growth rate of ideal magnetorotational instability
{parent=Ideal magnetorotational dispersion relation}
{title2=$s_{\max}=q\Omega/2,\quad\omega_{A,\max}^2=q(4-q)\Omega^2/4$}

For local cylindrical shear $q=-d\ln\Omega/d\ln r$ with $0<q<2$, maximizing the positive squared-growth root of the <ideal magnetorotational dispersion relation> gives the displayed formulas. For a Keplerian disk these are $s_{\max}=3\Omega/4$ and $\omega_A^2=15\Omega^2/16$. Field strength selects the fastest wavelength, while the maximum ideal rate is set by shear. Finite domains and magnetic diffusion can exclude that wavelength.

= Oblique axisymmetric magnetorotational dispersion relation
{parent=Ideal magnetorotational dispersion relation}
{title2=$\frac{k^2}{k_z^2}(s^2+\omega_A^2)^2+\kappa^2(s^2+\omega_A^2)-4\Omega^2\omega_A^2=0$}

In the local incompressible weak-field limit, take an axisymmetric <wavevector> $(k_r,0,k_z)$ and $\omega_A=\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf B/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}$. Eliminating the pressure projects the radial force by $k_z^2/k^2$. This yields the displayed dispersion relation, where $\kappa$ is the <radial epicyclic frequency>. For a hydrodynamically stable rotation profile with outward-decreasing angular velocity, instability requires $0<\omega_A^2<-(k_z^2/k^2)d\Omega^2/d\ln r$. A purely toroidal field has no axisymmetric local field-line-bending frequency and therefore no ordinary weak-field MRI in this approximation.

= Radially stratified magnetorotational dispersion relation
{parent=Ideal magnetorotational dispersion relation}
{title2=$\sigma^4+(2A+N_r^2+\Omega^2)\sigma^2+A(A+N_r^2-3\Omega^2)=0$}

For an <axisymmetric vertical mode of a shearing sheet> with a vertical <magnetic field>, <radial buoyancy frequency> $N_r$, and $A=k^2v_A^2>0$, the nonstationary amplitudes obey $\sigma^4+(2A+N_r^2+\Omega^2)\sigma^2+A(A+N_r^2-3\Omega^2)=0$. The two roots for $\sigma^2$ are real. A growing mode exists exactly when $A+N_r^2<3\Omega^2$. If the advected <buoyancy displacement variable> is retained as an independent amplitude, the full characteristic polynomial also has a stationary factor $\sigma$.

= Maximum growth rate of radially stratified magnetorotational instability
{parent=Radially stratified magnetorotational dispersion relation}
{title2=$\sigma_{\max}=(3\Omega^2-N_r^2)/(4\Omega)$}

For $-5\Omega^2<N_r^2<3\Omega^2$ and a continuum of vertical <wavenumbers>, the growing branch of the <radially stratified magnetorotational dispersion relation> is maximized at $k^2v_A^2=\Omega^2-(N_r^2+\Omega^2)^2/(16\Omega^2)$, with $\sigma_{\max}=(3\Omega^2-N_r^2)/(4\Omega)$. For $N_r^2\leq-5\Omega^2$ the optimum moves to the $k\to0$ boundary and the limiting rate is $\sqrt{-N_r^2-\Omega^2}$. For $N_r^2\geq3\Omega^2$ there is no exponential instability. Boundaries that discretize the allowed <wavenumbers> can alter the actual maximum.

= Finite-thickness magnetorotational instability criterion
{parent=Ideal magnetorotational dispersion relation}

If the horizontal <magnetic field> perturbations vanish at $z=\pm H$, their allowed <vertical wavenumbers> are $k_m=m\pi/(2H)$, $m=1,2,\ldots$. The lowest <wavenumber> is unstable exactly when
$$
0<\frac{\pi^2B_z^2}{8q\mu_0\rho H^2\Omega^2}<1.
$$
If that magnetic mode is stable, all higher magnetic modes are stable as well. A weak field admits longer unstable wavelengths that fit inside the slab; sufficiently strong <magnetic tension> stabilizes every allowed wavelength.

= Bounded vertical modes of a sech-squared magnetized disk
{parent=Finite-thickness magnetorotational instability criterion}
{title2=$F_n=P_n(\tanh(z/H)),\quad k_n^2H^2=n(n+1)$}

For density proportional to $\operatorname{sech}^2(z/H)$, the magnetic vertical equation $F''+k^2\operatorname{sech}^2(z/H)F=0$ becomes the <Legendre differential equation> under $\xi=\tanh(z/H)$. Bounded solutions at both surfaces are <Legendre polynomials>, with $k_n=\sqrt{n(n+1)}/H$. The $n=0$ constant function has no magnetically coupled finite wavenumber. The smallest MRI-relevant eigenvalue is $k_1^2=2/H^2$, which makes sufficiently strong <magnetic tension> stabilize all allowed modes.

= Fastest discrete mode of a stratified magnetorotational instability
{parent=Bounded vertical modes of a sech-squared magnetized disk}
{title2=$s^2/\Omega^2=[-(1+2a)+\sqrt{1+16a}]/2$}

The growing root of the <ideal magnetorotational dispersion relation> is maximized continuously at $a=v_A^2k^2/\Omega^2=15/16$, with growth $3\Omega/4$. Discrete vertical modes instead have $a_n=2n(n+1)/\beta$. The fastest one is found by comparing admissible integers on either side of the continuous maximum, rather than rounding a vertical wavenumber blindly. At $\beta=24$, $n=3$ gives $s=\Omega\sqrt{(\sqrt{17}-3)/2}$.

= Critical wavelength of the magnetorotational instability
{title2=$\lambda_{\rm crit}$}
{parent=Magnetorotational instability}

For a vertical magnetic field in a Keplerian disk, axisymmetric vertical MRI modes are unstable when $k^2v_A^2<3\Omega^2$. The shortest unstable wavelength is $\lambda_{\rm crit}=2\pi v_A/(\sqrt3\Omega)$.

= Thermal stability of an accretion disk
{parent=Alpha disk}

A local thermal equilibrium is stable when a small temperature increase raises cooling faster than heating at fixed surface density. The standard total-pressure alpha prescription makes a radiation-pressure-dominated, Thomson-opacity disk thermally unstable.

= Hydrogen-ionization disk instability
{parent=Thermal stability of an accretion disk}

Partial <hydrogen> <ionization> changes disk cooling and permits cold and hot stable thermal branches separated by unstable equilibria. A <dwarf nova> can cycle between a cold matter-storing disk and a hot, rapidly accreting disk. The cycle is described by the evolution of local <surface density> and <temperature>, coupled to <viscous evolution of an accretion disk>.

= Accretion-disk thermal S-curve
{parent=Hydrogen-ionization disk instability}
{title2=$T_{\rm eff}(\Sigma)$}

At fixed disk radius, balancing local heating and cooling gives thermal equilibria parameterized by <surface density> and <temperature>. In the <hydrogen-ionization disk instability>, this locus has cold and hot stable branches joined by unstable equilibria. Crossing the turning points drives the state changes of a <dwarf nova>. <Thermal stability of an accretion disk> tests the local heating and cooling response, while <viscous evolution of an accretion disk> changes the surface density.

= Thermal instability from negative hydrogen ion opacity
{parent=Thermal stability of an accretion disk}
{title2=$Q^+\propto T,\quad Q^-\propto T^{-35/6}$}

For a gas-supported <alpha disk> with $\kappa\propto\rho^{1/3}T^{10}$, compare rates at fixed <surface density of a disk>, radius, $\alpha$ and composition on the <thermal timescale of an accretion disk>. Assume that the <vertical dynamical timescale of a disk> is shorter than that thermal time, and that the <viscous timescale> is longer; the separation is controlled for $\alpha\ll1$ and small <disk aspect ratio>. Vertical <hydrostatic equilibrium> gives $H\propto T^{1/2}$, $\rho\propto T^{-1/2}$, so $\kappa\propto T^{59/6}$. Viscous heating satisfies $Q^+\propto T$ and <radiative cooling> satisfies $Q^-\propto T^4/(\kappa\Sigma)\propto T^{-35/6}$. At equilibrium a positive <temperature> perturbation changes the net heating by $(41/6)(Q_0/T)\delta T>0$. With positive effective <heat capacity>, it grows rather than relaxes. This is a local thermal statement and uses the fixed-column, rather than fixed-volume-density, comparison.

= Thermal timescale of an accretion disk
{title2=$t_{\rm th}$}
{parent=Thermal stability of an accretion disk}

The thermal timescale compares the internal energy stored in a disc annulus with its local heating or cooling. In a gas-pressure-supported <alpha disk>, $t_{\rm th}\sim(\alpha\Omega_K)^{-1}$, whereas the <viscous timescale> is longer by approximately $(R/H)^2$. A local thermal perturbation is therefore studied at fixed <surface density of a disk>, rather than fixed steady-state <mass accretion rate>.

= Thermal instability of a radiation-pressure-dominated alpha disk
{parent=Thermal stability of an accretion disk}

At fixed surface density, a total-pressure alpha disk supported by radiation pressure has viscous heating $Q^+\propto T_c^8$ but radiative cooling $Q^-\propto T_c^4$ when electron scattering supplies a nearly constant opacity. Heating therefore wins after a positive temperature perturbation.

= Gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{wiki}

Disk self-gravity can overcome pressure and rotational support, producing growing density disturbances, spiral structure, or fragmentation.

= Uniformly rotating gas-sheet dispersion relation
{parent=Gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk}
{title2=$\omega^2=c^2k^2-2\pi G\Sigma_0|k|+4\Omega^2$}

For a homogeneous inviscid <razor-thin disk approximation> with <solid-body rotation> and barotropic <sound speed> $c$, the compressive wave branch obeys $\omega^2=c^2k^2-2\pi G\Sigma_0|k|+4\Omega^2$. The <razor-thin disk Poisson kernel> supplies the <self-gravity> term, and the <radial epicyclic frequency> is $2|\Omega|$. Growing modes have $\omega^2<0$; a separate zero-frequency balanced mode can also exist.

= Marginal fragmentation wavelength of a rotating sheet
{parent=Uniformly rotating gas-sheet dispersion relation}
{title2=$\ell_{\mathrm{crit}}=\pi^2G\Sigma_0/(2\Omega^2)$}

At fixed $\Sigma_0,\Omega\ne0$, slowly decreasing $c$ first reaches a zero of the wave-frequency minimum at $c=\pi G\Sigma_0/(2|\Omega|)$. The corresponding <wavelength> is $\ell=\pi^2G\Sigma_0/(2\Omega^2)$. It estimates the spacing and scale of growing density concentrations just below marginality; linear theory does not determine the final nonlinear clump radius.

= Unstable wavenumber band of a rotating gas sheet
{parent=Uniformly rotating gas-sheet dispersion relation}
{title2=$q_-<|k|<q_+$}

Put $Q=2|\Omega|c/(\pi G\Sigma_0)$. The <uniformly rotating gas-sheet dispersion relation> has growing waves precisely for $Q<1$, at $q_-<|k|<q_+$ with $q_\pm=(\pi G\Sigma_0/c^2)(1\pm\sqrt{1-Q^2})$. <Pressure> stabilizes short waves and rotation stabilizes long waves. Equality $Q=1$ is marginal; $Q\geq1$ is the gas <Toomre stability criterion>.

= Magnetic subcriticality of a razor-thin disk
{parent=Gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk}
{title2=$B_{z0}^2<\pi G\mu_0\Sigma^2$}

For an isolated cold initially resting razor-thin disk, the displayed pointwise bound makes the absolute <gravity-equivalent magnetic surface density> smaller than its actual <surface density>. The <positive-kernel comparison of thin-disk fields> then makes the integrand in the <horizontal virial balance of a cold magnetized fluid> negative above and below the disk. Consequently its radial second moment begins to decrease. This establishes initial contraction in the global virial sense, not inward acceleration of every individual fluid element or unlimited subsequent collapse.

= Secular gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk
{parent=Gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk}

= Secular gravitational instability
{synonym}

Secular gravitational instability uses dissipation to weaken the epicyclic support that stabilizes a disk dynamically. It can therefore grow more slowly than an orbital time in parameter ranges where the inviscid disk is stable.

= Dust gravitational dispersion relation with gas drag
{parent=Secular gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk}
{title2=$s[(s+\epsilon\Omega)^2+\Omega^2]+(c^2k^2-2\pi G\sigma_0|k|)(s+\epsilon\Omega)=0$}

The local axisymmetric density/velocity system of a pressured dust layer with linear <gas drag> and a fixed <Keplerian shearing sheet> gas flow has this cubic <dispersion relation>. The $\Omega/2$ azimuthal velocity coefficient includes advection of the <Keplerian shear>. The <razor-thin disk Poisson kernel> supplies the $|k|$ self-gravity term. Keeping the full amplitude <determinant> retains the zero-frequency branch and avoids dividing out the secular mode.

= Positive-root criterion for dust self-gravity with drag
{parent=Dust gravitational dispersion relation with gas drag}
{title2=$P(0)=\epsilon\Omega(\omega^2-\Omega^2)<0$}

For any nonzero positive drag, the cubic <dust gravitational dispersion relation with gas drag> is negative at $s=0$ if $\omega^2<\Omega^2$ and positive at sufficiently large positive $s$. The <intermediate value theorem> therefore guarantees a growing real root. This existence argument is independent of the weak-drag expansion and includes the dynamically unstable $\omega^2<0$ regime.

= Weak-drag secular gravitational growth rate
{parent=Dust gravitational dispersion relation with gas drag}
{title2=$s_{\mathrm{sec}}=\epsilon\Omega(\Omega^2-\omega^2)/\omega^2+O(\epsilon^2)$}

The mode originating at the neutral root grows for $0<\omega^2<\Omega^2$. At small nonzero $|k|$ this gives $s_{\mathrm{sec}}\sim2\pi\epsilon G\sigma_0|k|/\Omega$. <Gas drag> transfers angular momentum to the fixed gas flow and permits <secular gravitational instability> even for a dust <Toomre parameter> above unity. If $\omega^2<0$, the small-root continuation is damped, while a separate dynamical root grows. The formula is singular at $\omega^2=0$.

= Turbulent mixing of a secularly unstable dust layer
{parent=Weak-drag secular gravitational growth rate}
{title2=$s\sim2\pi\epsilon G\sigma_0|k|/\Omega-Dk^2$}

<Turbulence> can raise dust random speeds, increase layer thickness and mix density enhancements by <eddy diffusion>, delaying <secular gravitational instability>. On sufficiently long wavelengths, weak-drag growth is proportional to $|k|$, whereas simple diffusive damping is proportional to $k^2$. Thus diffusion alone need not remove all long-wave growth in an infinite idealization, although finite size and lifetime can make it ineffective. Coherent turbulent concentration can also increase local <surface density>, so the net effect requires a stochastic transport model.

= Finite-size secular gravitational criterion
{parent=Weak-drag secular gravitational growth rate}
{title2=$Q\lesssim L/H$}

For a dust layer of extent $L$, its smallest admissible wavenumber is of order $1/L$. The <secular gravitational instability> band ends at $k_{\mathrm{crit}}=2\pi G\sigma_0/c^2=2/(QH)$, with <Toomre parameter> $Q=c\Omega/(\pi G\sigma_0)$ and $H=c/\Omega$. Requiring an admissible mode in the band gives $Q\lesssim L/H$. Boundary conditions fix the order-unity prefactor; a finite disk lifetime also limits practical growth.

= Weak-drag dust density waves
{parent=Dust gravitational dispersion relation with gas drag}
{title2=$s_\pm=\pm i\omega-\epsilon\Omega(\omega^2+\Omega^2)/(2\omega^2)+O(\epsilon^2)$}

For fixed $\omega^2=\Omega^2-2\pi G\sigma_0|k|+c^2k^2>0$, the two oscillatory roots of the <dust gravitational dispersion relation with gas drag> acquire negative real parts at first order in $\epsilon$. Their frequencies have no first-order shift. The expansion is not uniform near $\omega^2=0$, where the oscillation and damping scales can become comparable.

= Toomre stability criterion
{title2=$Q$}
{c}
{parent=Gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk}
{wiki=Toomre's_stability_criterion}

For a razor-thin isothermal gas disk,
$$
Q=\frac{c_s\kappa}{\pi G\Sigma}.
$$
Axisymmetric disturbances are stable when $Q\geq1$: pressure stabilizes short wavelengths, epicyclic motion stabilizes long wavelengths, and self-gravity drives intermediate wavelengths.

= Toomre marginal algebraic growth
{c}
{parent=Toomre stability criterion}
{title2=$Q=1,\quad |k|=\kappa/c_s$}

At the marginal radial <wavenumber>, the <forced axisymmetric density mode with vortensity> has zero squared frequency. Its <mass density> amplitude is $C_0+C_1t-\Omega\Sigma_0^2q't^2$. Thus there is no exponential instability but nonzero conserved <vortensity> can force quadratic growth, and a homogeneous generalized mode can grow linearly. The spectral boundary $Q=1$ of the <Toomre stability criterion> must not be mistaken for boundedness of every initial-value solution.

= Softened Toomre dispersion relation
{parent=Toomre stability criterion}
{title2=$\omega^2=\kappa^2-2\pi G\Sigma_0k e^{-k\epsilon}+c_s^2k^2$}

Linear axisymmetric compressive modes of a <barotropic closure of a razor-thin disk> combine radial epicyclic restoration, softened <self-gravity> and <pressure>. The <off-plane razor-thin Poisson kernel> supplies the exponential gravity reduction. Here $k$ is the nonnegative radial <wavenumber> magnitude. The full linear system also has an <axisymmetric geostrophic mode>.

= Critical Toomre parameter with exponential softening
{parent=Softened Toomre dispersion relation}
{title2=$Q_c^2=2s_*e^{-\delta s_*}-s_*^2=s_*^2(1+\delta s_*)/(1-\delta s_*)$}

The <most unstable softened disk wavenumber> minimizes the squared mode frequency. Instability is $Q<Q_c$, while $Q\ge Q_c$ is spectrally stable for this compressive pair. Since $s_*<(1+\delta)^{-1}$, $Q_c^2<(1+2\delta)/(1+\delta)^2\le1$. At zero <gravitational softening> both bounds become equality. The physical path with fixed $\kappa\epsilon/c_s$ obeys $\delta=(\kappa\epsilon/c_s)/Q$.

= Most unstable softened disk wavenumber
{parent=Softened Toomre dispersion relation}
{title2=$s_*=e^{-\delta s_*}(1-\delta s_*),\quad s_*<(1+\delta)^{-1}$}

With $s=Q c_sk/\kappa$ and $\delta=\kappa\epsilon/(Q c_s)$, maximize $F(s)=2se^{-\delta s}-s^2$. Its unique positive critical point satisfies the displayed equation. For positive <gravitational softening>, $e^{-\delta s_*}<1$ and $1-\delta s_*>0$, giving the strict bound. Unsoftened gravity has $s_*=1$.

= Toomre parameter
{c}
{parent=Toomre stability criterion}
{title2=$Q=c\kappa/(\pi G\Sigma)$}

For a razor-thin fluid disk with <sound speed> or effective random speed $c$, <surface density> $\Sigma$ and radial epicyclic frequency $\kappa$, $Q=c\kappa/(\pi G\Sigma)$ compares pressure and rotational support with <self-gravity>. The local axisymmetric fluid <Toomre stability criterion> is $Q\geq1$. For a <Keplerian shearing sheet>, $\kappa=\Omega$. Collisionless stellar disks use a different numerical normalization; gas drag can permit secular growth even when this fluid parameter exceeds unity.

= Disk mass form of the Toomre criterion
{parent=Toomre stability criterion}
{title2=$Q\sim(H/r)(M_*/M_D)$}

In a star-dominated <Keplerian disk> with $H\simeq c_s/\Omega$ and characteristic disk mass $M_D\sim\pi r^2\Sigma$, the <Toomre stability criterion> becomes $Q\sim(H/r)(M_*/M_D)$. Thus instability can occur at a disk-to-star mass ratio comparable to the small <disk aspect ratio>. The numerical coefficient depends on whether $M_D$ means a local annular estimate or integrated enclosed mass.

= Gravito-turbulent self-regulation
{parent=Toomre stability criterion}

In a cooling self-gravitating disk, $Q<1$ excites gravitational turbulence whose dissipation raises the sound speed, while $Q>1$ suppresses that heating and lets cooling lower the sound speed. A sustained state can therefore regulate itself near $Q\simeq1$.

= Gravitoturbulent viscosity
{parent=Gravito-turbulent self-regulation}

A mixing-length estimate for gravitational turbulence takes the largest unstable wavelength $\ell$ and orbital growth rate $\Omega$, giving turbulent velocity $u_{\rm turb}\sim\Omega\ell$ and kinematic viscosity $\nu\sim u_{\rm turb}\ell\sim\Omega\ell^2$.

= Polytropic vertical structure of a self-gravitating disk
{parent=Toomre stability criterion}

For $P=K\rho^{1+1/n}$, dimensionless pseudo-enthalpy $w=n(\rho/\rho_0)^{1/n}$, and $\zeta=\Omega z/c_0$, vertical hydrostatic balance and the <Poisson equation> reduce to
$$
w''+\frac4{n^nQ_0}w^n=-1.
$$
The midplane conditions are $w(0)=n$ and $w'(0)=0$, and the first zero gives the disk surface.

= Self-gravitating radius of an accretion disk
{title2=$R_{\rm sg}$}
{parent=Toomre stability criterion}

The self-gravitating radius of an accretion disk is the radius at which its <Toomre stability criterion> reaches $Q=1$. Beyond it, a smooth thin disk is vulnerable to spiral structure, gravitational transport, or fragmentation.

= Shearing sheet
{parent=Gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk}
{wiki}

The shearing sheet is a local Cartesian approximation to a differentially rotating disk. It retains Coriolis force, linearized tidal gravity, and a background linear shear.

= Axisymmetric geostrophic mode
{parent=Shearing sheet}
{title2=$\omega=0,\quad u=0,\quad v=ik_x(\Phi_1+c_s^2\Sigma_1/\Sigma_0)/(2\Omega)$}

A stationary <mass density> disturbance can be balanced by a changed azimuthal <velocity> in a <shearing sheet>. The <continuity> and azimuthal equations give zero <radial velocity>, while the radial equation balances <Coriolis force> against <pressure> and gravity. This mode is separate from the density-wave pair in the <softened Toomre dispersion relation> and is lost if one divides every equation by frequency.

= Infinite gravitating particle lattice
{parent=Shearing sheet}

An infinite gravitating particle lattice with spacing $h$ in the azimuthal direction is a shearing-sheet equilibrium when all particles sit at $x_n=0,y_n=nh$. Opposite-neighbor accelerations cancel absolutely. The linear gravitational response to pair displacement differences has transverse and longitudinal coefficients $1$ and $-2$, divided by $h^3|j-n|^3$.

= Instability threshold of a gravitating particle lattice
{parent=Infinite gravitating particle lattice}

For $q=GmF(hk)/(h^3\Omega^2)$, the squared dimensionless growth rates are $s^2/\Omega^2=(q-1\pm\sqrt{9q^2-26q+1})/2$. Exponential growth begins for $q>(13-4\sqrt{10})/9$. Since the <gravitating lattice Fourier kernel> is largest at $hk=\pi$, the full infinite lattice is exponentially unstable when $Gm/(h^3\Omega^2)>(13-4\sqrt{10})/[9F(\pi)]$. Marginal repeated imaginary roots and zero-frequency secular modes require separate treatment.

= Gravitating lattice Fourier kernel
{parent=Infinite gravitating particle lattice}
{title2=$F(\xi)=2\sum_{l\ge1}(1-\cos(l\xi))/l^3$}

The discrete normal modes of an <infinite gravitating particle lattice> involve $F(\xi)=2\sum_{l=1}^\infty(1-\cos(l\xi))/l^3$. It is even, nonnegative and $2\pi$-periodic. Its derivative is a positive reciprocal-square sine series for $0<\xi<\pi$, so its maxima occur exactly at odd multiples of $\pi$, with $F(\pi)=(7/2)\zeta(3)$.

= Polytropic elliptical patch in a shearing sheet
{parent=Shearing sheet}

The steady planar velocity $\mathbf u=\alpha y\mathbf e_x-\beta x\mathbf e_y$ supports a bounded polytropic patch when $\alpha=(\beta^2-3\Omega^2)/\beta$ and $\sqrt3\Omega<\beta<2\Omega$. Its <polytropic enthalpy> is $Q=Q_0-(2\Omega-\beta)(\beta x^2+\alpha y^2)/2$ with $Q_0>0$. Its <fluid free boundary> is the material ellipse $\beta x^2+\alpha y^2=2Q_0/(2\Omega-\beta)$. No additional self-gravity potential is part of this model.

= Axisymmetric compressible shearing-sheet waves
{parent=Shearing sheet}
{title2=$\omega^4-(\Omega^2+c^2k^2)\omega^2+\Omega^2c^2k_z^2=0$}

In an unstratified <Keplerian shearing sheet> with <sound speed> $c$, the four linear velocity-density equations yield $\omega^4-(\Omega^2+c^2k^2)\omega^2+\Omega^2c^2k_z^2=0$. The lower-frequency branch approaches the <inertial wave> relation $\omega^2=\Omega^2k_z^2/k^2$ for $ck\gg\Omega$; the upper branch is predominantly acoustic.

= Pressureless dust fluid in a shearing sheet
{parent=Shearing sheet}

A dilute dust component can be approximated as a pressureless fluid with velocity $\mathbf v$ and <surface density of a disk> $\Sigma_d$. In a gas velocity $\mathbf u$, linear <drag force> gives acceleration $-(\mathbf v-\mathbf u)/t_s$ for positive <aerodynamic stopping time> $t_s$. Its equations include <Coriolis acceleration>, the <shearing-sheet tidal potential>, and $\partial_t\Sigma_d+\nabla\cdot(\Sigma_d\mathbf v)=0$. Neglecting gas backreaction is justified only when the prescribed-gas approximation is appropriate. Strong orbit crossing or appreciable dust velocity dispersion can invalidate the single-valued pressureless-fluid description.

= Forced dust epicycle with aerodynamic drag
{parent=Pressureless dust fluid in a shearing sheet}
{title2=$D_\epsilon=\partial_t+\epsilon\Omega$}

Let $v_x,v_y$ and $u_x,u_y$ be the dust and gas velocity perturbations after subtracting the common background shear, suppressing primes. Put $t_s>0$ for the <aerodynamic stopping time>. Linear axisymmetric dust perturbations about a <Keplerian shearing sheet> obey $D_\epsilon v_x-2\Omega v_y=\epsilon\Omega u_x$ and $D_\epsilon v_y+\Omega v_x/2=\epsilon\Omega u_y$, with $\epsilon\Omega=t_s^{-1}>0$. Eliminating $v_y$ gives
$$
[\partial_t^2+2\epsilon\Omega\partial_t+(1+\epsilon^2)\Omega^2]v_x=\epsilon\Omega\partial_tu_x+\epsilon^2\Omega^2u_x+2\epsilon\Omega^2u_y.
$$
Thus a gas epicycle $u_x=u\cos(kx-\Omega t)$, $u_y=u\sin(kx-\Omega t)/2$ supplies both $2\epsilon\Omega^2u\sin(kx-\Omega t)$ and $\epsilon^2\Omega^2u\cos(kx-\Omega t)$. Keeping the latter is necessary for an exact linear equation beyond first order in drag. The homogeneous roots are $-\epsilon\Omega\pm i\Omega$, separating damping from epicyclic frequency.

= Resonant dust entrainment by an epicyclic gas wave
{parent=Forced dust epicycle with aerodynamic drag}
{title2=$\mathbf v'-\mathbf u'\propto e^{-\epsilon\Omega t}$}

If the prescribed gas velocity is itself a free <epicyclic motion> solution, subtract its two momentum equations from the <forced dust epicycle with aerodynamic drag> equations. The velocity difference obeys a damped epicycle and decays as $e^{-\epsilon\Omega t}$. Therefore dust approaches the gas velocity with identical phase and amplitude for every positive drag rate. At zero drag the difference does not decay, so the late-time limit is nonuniform as drag vanishes. A finite-wavelength <inertial-acoustic wave> is detuned from the free epicycle; matching its phase and amplitude in the weak-drag limit additionally requires $|\omega-\Omega|\ll\epsilon\Omega$. For a gas density wave of frequency $\omega$, the exact radial transfer amplitude is
$$
\frac{V_x}{U_x}=\frac{\epsilon\Omega[\epsilon\Omega-i\omega-i\Omega^2/\omega]}{(\epsilon\Omega-i\omega)^2+\Omega^2},
$$
obtained by solving the two dust equations and using $U_y=-i\Omega U_x/(2\omega)$. At $\omega=\Omega$ it is exactly one.

= Density contrast of an entrained dust wave
{parent=Resonant dust entrainment by an epicyclic gas wave}
{title2=$\delta_d=(ku/\Omega)\cos(kx-\Omega t)+C(x)$}

Let $\Sigma_{d0}$ be the uniform background dust <surface density of a disk> and $\delta_d=(\Sigma_d-\Sigma_{d0})/\Sigma_{d0}$ its fractional perturbation. Here $k$ is the radial <wavenumber>, $\Omega$ is the epicyclic frequency and $u$ is a real velocity amplitude. For that uniform background and late velocity $v_x=u\cos(kx-\Omega t)$, linear dust <continuity equation> gives $\partial_t\delta_d=-\partial_xv_x=ku\sin(kx-\Omega t)$. Integrating yields the displayed formula. The coherent travelling-wave fractional excess is $|ku|/\Omega$, with validity requiring this number much smaller than one. The static term $C(x)$ records initial density and transient displacement: drag damps velocity, but supplies no density diffusion. The long-wavelength gas wave has the same fractional density amplitude, so entrainment alone does not produce an additional dust-to-gas ratio enhancement at this order.

= Orbital shear parameter
{title2=$q=-d\log\Omega/d\log r$}
{parent=Shearing sheet}

The orbital shear parameter measures the fractional radial decrease in orbital angular frequency. A <Kepler orbit> has $q=3/2$, while rigid rotation has $q=0$. The <radial epicyclic frequency> obeys $\kappa_r^2=2(2-q)\Omega^2$; this follows by differentiating the squared <specific angular momentum> $h^2=r^4\Omega^2$.

= Particle Lagrangian in a shearing sheet
{title2=$L_2$}
{parent=Shearing sheet}

Expand a unit-mass particle's <Lagrangian> about an axisymmetric <circular orbit> of radius $r_0$ and frequency $\Omega_0$, using $r=r_0+x$ and $\varphi=\Omega_0t+y/r_0$. Circular-orbit balance removes the linear radial term; <total-time-derivative invariance of a Lagrangian> removes $r_0\Omega_0\dot y$. To second order,
$$
L_2=\frac12(\dot x^2+\dot y^2+\dot z^2)+2\Omega_0x\dot y-\Phi_t,
\qquad
\Phi_t=-q\Omega_0^2x^2+\frac12\Omega_z^2z^2.
$$
Here $q$ is the <orbital shear parameter> and $\Omega_z$ is the <vertical epicyclic frequency>. The <Euler-Lagrange equations> retain the local <Coriolis acceleration> and tidal gravity.

= Jacobi energy in a shearing sheet
{title2=$\varepsilon_J$}
{c}
{parent=Particle Lagrangian in a shearing sheet}

The time-independent <particle Lagrangian in a shearing sheet> has conserved rotating-frame energy
$$
\varepsilon_J=\sum_i\dot q_i\frac{\partial L_2}{\partial\dot q_i}-L_2
=\frac12(\dot x^2+\dot y^2+\dot z^2)-q\Omega_0^2x^2+\frac12\Omega_z^2z^2.
$$
The velocity-linear <Coriolis acceleration> term cancels from this expression. Up to the reference-orbit constant, it is the second-order expansion of inertial <specific orbital energy> minus $\Omega_0$ times inertial <specific angular momentum>. Its negative radial tidal term allows <inelastic collisions> to lower the total energy while increasing the radial extent of a ring.

= Dissipative spreading of a planetary ring
{parent=Shearing sheet}

In a <Keplerian shearing sheet>, an <epicyclic guiding center> $x_0$ carries rotating-frame energy $-3\Omega^2x_0^2/8$, plus nonnegative radial and vertical oscillation energies. <Inelastic collisions> dissipate the total <Jacobi energy in a shearing sheet>, while <momentum conservation> preserves $\sum x_0$. Thus $\sum x_0^2$ must grow: the ring spreads about its fixed mean radius. The effect is an <angular momentum transport> process, with some particles moving inward and others outward.

= Kida vortex
{c}
{parent=Shearing sheet}

A Kida vortex is an exact elliptical patch of uniform vorticity embedded in a linear shear flow. In a Keplerian shearing sheet its aspect ratio controls both the circulation period and the stability of three-dimensional perturbations.

= Particle attraction in an elliptical shearing-sheet vortex
{parent=Kida vortex}
{title2=$r\geq3\quad\text{for every finite positive drag rate in Keplerian shear}$}

The material-boundary and <vorticity> conditions give $\alpha=S/[r(r-1)]$ and $\beta=Sr/(r-1)$. A pressureless particle subject to <linear drag>, <Coriolis acceleration> and tidal gravity has the <drag-polynomial stability for all positive stopping rates> with $K=2\Omega(2\Omega-S)$, $L=\Omega(\alpha+\beta-S)$ and $P=\alpha\beta$. In <Keplerian shear>, $K/\Omega^2=1$, $L/\Omega^2=3(r+1)/[2r(r-1)]$ and $P/\Omega^2=9/[4(r-1)^2]$. The exact criterion is $r\geq3$, including the boundary $K=L>P$ at $r=3$. Attraction is local to the centre under the prescribed interior velocity field; a trajectory leaving the patch cannot be followed using that field alone.

= Pressure Hessian of an elliptical shearing-sheet vortex
{parent=Kida vortex}
{title2=$A=\alpha\beta+2\Omega(S-\beta),\quad B=\alpha(\beta-2\Omega)$}

For the incompressible velocity $u_x=\alpha y$, $u_y=-\beta x$ in a <shearing sheet>, advection produces $-\alpha\beta(x,y)$ and the <Coriolis acceleration> produces $(2\Omega\beta x,2\Omega\alpha y)$. Including the radial tide $2\Omega Sx$ gives $p/\rho=(Ax^2+By^2)/2+C$. A material ellipse has $\beta=r^2\alpha$. A vortex patch is immersed in surrounding fluid, so this <pressure> need not vanish on its boundary; imposing that condition would describe a different free-boundary problem.

= Stratified incompressible shearing sheet
{parent=Shearing sheet}

The stratified incompressible shearing sheet adds a buoyancy displacement $\theta$ with $D\theta/Dt=u_z$ and acceleration $-N^2\theta\mathbf e_z$. Axisymmetric plane waves obey
$$
\omega^2=\frac{k_z^2\Omega^2+k_x^2N^2}{k_x^2+k_z^2},
$$
interpolating between inertial and buoyancy oscillations.

= Keplerian shearing sheet
{c}
{parent=Shearing sheet}

A Keplerian shearing sheet has background velocity $\mathbf u_0=-(3/2)\Omega x\mathbf e_y$, radial shear rate $S=3\Omega/2$, and radial epicyclic frequency $\kappa_r=\Omega$.

= Vertical shear instability
{parent=Keplerian shearing sheet}
{title2=VSI}

Vertical shear instability destabilizes differential rotation that varies with height, when buoyancy restoration is absent or sufficiently weakened. In the homogeneous <incompressible flow> model with background $U_y=-3\Omega x/2+q\Omega z$, an axisymmetric perturbation with <wavevector> $(k_x,0,k_z)\ne0$ has
$$
s^2=\Omega^2\frac{2qk_xk_z-k_z^2}{k_x^2+k_z^2}.
$$
The pressure-free radial/vertical momentum equation and the azimuthal equation give this result after using <incompressibility>. Growth requires $2qk_xk_z>k_z^2$, so a small vertical shear favors nearly radial wavevectors. Here $q$ is the vertical shear amplitude, distinct from the <orbital shear parameter> $3/2$. Stratification, cooling and boundaries can change this simplified criterion.

= Maximum growth rate of the vertical shear instability
{parent=Vertical shear instability}
{title2=$s_{\max}=\Omega\sqrt{(\sqrt{1+4q^2}-1)/2}$}

For the unstratified local <vertical shear instability>, maximize $s^2/\Omega^2=(2qR-1)/(1+R^2)$ over $R=k_x/k_z$. Its derivative vanishes when $qR^2-R-q=0$. For $q>0$ the maximum uses $R=(1+\sqrt{1+4q^2})/(2q)$, and substitution gives the displayed expression. For small $q$, $R\sim q^{-1}$ and $s_{\max}=\Omega q(1-q^2/2+O(q^4))$. Changing the sign of the shear changes the preferred sign of $R$ but gives the same maximum magnitude; there is no exponential growth at $q=0$.

= Exact single-phase incompressible perturbation of a shear flow
{parent=Vertical shear instability}

For a perturbation $\mathbf u'=\tilde{\mathbf u}f(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x)e^{st}$ with constant $\tilde{\mathbf u},\mathbf k$ and nonconstant differentiable $f$, <incompressibility> forces $\mathbf k\cdot\tilde{\mathbf u}=0$. Consequently $\mathbf u'\cdot\nabla\mathbf u'=ff'e^{2st}(\mathbf k\cdot\tilde{\mathbf u})\tilde{\mathbf u}=0$. About a shear depending only on $x,z$ and directed along $y$, an axisymmetric such perturbation therefore obeys its linear amplitude equations exactly, including at finite amplitude in this homogeneous model. For <pressure> $P'=\tilde P g(\xi)e^{st}$, nonzero <pressure> requires $g'=Cf$, and $C$ can be absorbed into $\tilde P$; choose $g'=f$. This relation does not imply $g=f$ for an arbitrary profile.

= Energy balance of the incompressible vertical-shear model
{parent=Vertical shear instability}
{title2=$\partial_t(K+\rho_0\Phi_t)+\nabla\cdot[(K+P+\rho_0\Phi_t)\mathbf u]=-2\rho_0\Omega^2qzu_x$}

For constant density $\rho_0$ and body force $\Omega^2(3x-2qz)\mathbf e_x$, dotting the <incompressible flow> momentum equation with $\rho_0\mathbf u$ gives the <kinetic energy> flux $(K+P)\mathbf u$ and source $\rho_0\Omega^2(3x-2qz)u_x$, where $K=\rho_0|\mathbf u|^2/2$. <Coriolis acceleration> does no work. Absorbing $\Phi_t=-3\Omega^2x^2/2$ gives the displayed balance. For nonzero $q$, the remaining force has <curl> $-2\Omega^2q\mathbf e_y$, so it cannot be incorporated into a scalar mechanical potential. The model can exchange energy with its maintained vertical-shear background; at $q=0$ ordinary <Jacobi energy in a shearing sheet> conservation is recovered under zero boundary flux.

= Convective overstability
{parent=Keplerian shearing sheet}

An adverse radial <specific entropy> gradient can drive an oscillatory instability through the finite lag supplied by <thermal conduction> or relaxation. This differs from a <viscous-convective instability>, in which viscosity changes rotational stabilization.

= Convective overstability growth rate
{parent=Convective overstability}
{title2=$\sigma=-\frac{N^2\beta}{2(\beta^2+\Omega^2)}$}

For $|N^2|\ll\Omega^2$, this leading amplitude-growth rate is largest at <thermal diffusion rate of a Fourier mode> $\beta=\Omega$, giving $\sigma_{\rm max}=-N^2/(4\Omega)$ when $N^2<0$.

= Axisymmetric vertical mode of a shearing sheet
{parent=Keplerian shearing sheet}
{title2=$e^{st+ikz}$}

= Axisymmetric vertical modes of a shearing sheet
{synonym}

A horizontal-velocity perturbation depending only on height has zero vertical velocity for nonzero <wavenumber> under <incompressibility>. Its <pressure> amplitude then vanishes in the unstratified vertical momentum equation.

= Thermal energy mode of a shearing sheet
{parent=Axisymmetric vertical mode of a shearing sheet}
{title2=$s_0=-\xi k^2$}

The nonoscillatory thermal branch of the radially stratified <shearing sheet> spectrum. Its decoupled small-buoyancy limit is a diffusing scalar perturbation.

= Vortensity
{title2=$\zeta$}
{parent=Shearing sheet}
{wiki}

Vortensity is <absolute vorticity> divided by surface density. In a two-dimensional inviscid barotropic flow, it is conserved along fluid trajectories.

= Linearized vortensity
{parent=Vortensity}

Linearized vortensity is the first-order perturbation of vortensity about a background flow. For a shearing wave in a uniform Keplerian sheet, its amplitude is constant.

= Linearized vortensity conservation
{parent=Linearized vortensity}

Linearized vortensity conservation is the perturbative form of material vortensity conservation. In an inviscid barotropic shearing sheet, the linearized vortensity obeys $Df'/Dt=0$.

= Forced axisymmetric density mode with vortensity
{parent=Linearized vortensity conservation}
{title2=$\ddot\Sigma'+\omega_k^2\Sigma'=-2\Omega\Sigma_0^2q'$}

For a radial mode with constant nonzero <wavenumber> in an inviscid <razor-thin disc>, eliminate velocity using continuity and the conserved <linearized vortensity>. The resulting oscillator has $\omega_k^2=\kappa^2+c_s^2k^2-2\pi G\Sigma_0|k|$. The <vortensity> gives a constant forcing and hence the stationary balanced component when $\omega_k^2>0$. It is lost by an ansatz that divides every amplitude equation by a nonzero frequency. Negative squared frequency gives exponential instability; zero frequency allows secular growth.

= Shearing wave
{parent=Shearing sheet}

A shearing wave is a local disturbance whose wavevector evolves under background shear. In a Keplerian sheet, $k_x(t)=k_x(0)+(3/2)\Omega k_yt$.

= Barotropic shearing-wave gravitational amplitude system
{parent=Shearing wave}
{title2=$\dot k_x=Sk_y,\quad\widetilde\Phi_{d,m}=-2\pi G\widetilde\Sigma'/k$}

A <shearing-wave ansatz> in a uniform inviscid isothermal <shearing sheet> removes background advection when the radial <wavevector> grows as $k_x(t)=k_x(0)+Sk_yt$. The <razor-thin disk Poisson kernel> reduces the pressure-plus-gravity amplitude to $[c_s^2-2\pi G\Sigma_0/k]\widetilde\Sigma'/\Sigma_0$. Continuity couples it to $ik_xv_x+ik_yv_y$, while the two momentum equations have rotational couplings $-2\Omega v_y$ and $(2\Omega-S)v_x$. The <linearized vortensity> amplitude remains constant, because the barotropic <pressure> and gravitational forces have zero <curl> and the background <vortensity> is uniform.

= Shearing-wave ansatz
{parent=Shearing wave}

The shearing-wave ansatz writes a perturbation as a time-dependent amplitude times $\exp[i k_x(t)x+i k_yy]$, choosing $k_x(t)$ so that background advection does not leave an explicit factor of position in the amplitude equations.

= Shearing-wave oscillator
{parent=Shearing wave}

For an unforced inertial-acoustic disturbance, the shearing-wave amplitude obeys a time-dependent oscillator equation whose frequency depends on $k_x(t)=k_x(0)+Sk_yt$. Fourier transformation of the corresponding stationary forced equation turns radial wavenumber into the oscillator's time-like coordinate.

= Swing of a shearing wave
{parent=Shearing wave}

During the swing of a shearing wave, the radial wavenumber passes through zero and the disturbance changes from leading to trailing before winding ever more tightly.

= Inertial-acoustic wave
{parent=Shearing sheet}

An inertial-acoustic wave in a rotating compressible disk is restored jointly by pressure and epicyclic motion. For an axisymmetric radial wave in a Keplerian sheet, $\omega^2=\Omega^2+c_s^2k_x^2$.

= Density-wave dispersion relation in a non-self-gravitating disk
{parent=Inertial-acoustic wave}
{title2=$\omega^2=\kappa_r^2+c_s^2k^2$}

Let $c_s^2=(dP/d\Sigma)_0$ be the <barotropic closure of a razor-thin disk> sound-speed squared and $\kappa_r$ the <radial epicyclic frequency>, with $\kappa_r^2=2(2-q_r)\Omega^2$ for <orbital shear parameter> $q_r$. For a radial axisymmetric <normal mode> in a uniform barotropic <shearing sheet>, let $U_x,U_y$ be velocity amplitudes and $\sigma$ the surface-density amplitude. Linear momentum gives $-i\omega U_x=2\Omega U_y-ikc_s^2\sigma/\Sigma_0$ and $-i\omega U_y=-\kappa_r^2U_x/(2\Omega)$; mass conservation gives $\omega\sigma=k\Sigma_0U_x$. Eliminating the amplitudes for $\omega\ne0$ proves the displayed dispersion relation. The rotational term is epicyclic restoration; the <pressure> term is acoustic restoration. Without a perturbed gravitational potential, no self-gravity term belongs in the formula.

= Zero-frequency balanced mode of an axisymmetric disk
{parent=Density-wave dispersion relation in a non-self-gravitating disk}
{title2=$\omega=0,\quad U_x=0,\quad2\Omega U_y=ikc_s^2\sigma/\Sigma_0$}

Besides the two propagating <inertial-acoustic wave> branches, a uniform barotropic <Keplerian shearing sheet> has a stationary branch balancing <pressure> against <Coriolis acceleration>. For radial wavenumber $k\ne0$, setting $\omega=0$ in continuity gives $U_x=0$, and radial momentum gives the displayed azimuthal balance. The full determinant is proportional to $\omega(\omega^2-\Omega^2-c_s^2k^2)$. At $k=0$ the stationary branch is a uniform column-density change; it is not a travelling density wave.

= Satellite-forced density wave in an astrophysical disk
{parent=Shearing sheet}

A satellite-forced density wave is a compressive disk response to the satellite's periodic tidal potential. In a local corotating sheet, propagating zones lie where the relative orbital motion is supersonic.

= Thermal relaxation in a self-gravitating disk
{parent=Shearing sheet}

Thermal relaxation drives the local sound speed toward an equilibrium value on a cooling time $\tau$. Fast relaxation gives an isothermal response, while slow relaxation gives an adiabatic response.

= Shearing-sheet tidal potential
{title2=$\Phi_t$}
{parent=Shearing sheet}

For a frame rotating about a Keplerian circular orbit, radial gravity and centrifugal acceleration combine to give $\Phi_t=-3\Omega^2x^2/2$.

= Isothermal shearing-sheet energy conservation
{parent=Shearing-sheet tidal potential}
{title2=$\partial_tE+\nabla\cdot[(E+P)\mathbf u]=0$}

For constant <isothermal sound speed>, time-independent <shearing-sheet tidal potential> $\Phi_t$, and inviscid flow, define $E=\Sigma u^2/2+c_s^2\Sigma\ln(\Sigma/\Sigma_{\rm ref})+\Sigma\Phi_t$. The <barotropic energy density> and <kinetic energy> balances combine to give $\partial_tE+\nabla\cdot[(E+P)\mathbf u]=0$. <Coriolis acceleration> does no work. Integral conservation requires vanishing net boundary <energy flux>.

= Viscous-convective instability
{parent=Shearing sheet}

Viscous-convective instability is slow growth in a rotating fluid with an adverse buoyancy gradient when viscosity weakens epicyclic stabilization.

= Radial epicyclic frequency
{title2=$\kappa_r$}
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{wiki}

The radial epicyclic frequency describes small radial oscillations about a circular orbit. For specific angular momentum $h(r)$, $\kappa_r^2=r^{-3}d(h^2)/dr$.

= Epicyclic frequency bounds for monotone spherical density
{parent=Radial epicyclic frequency}
{title2=$\Omega\leq\kappa\leq2\Omega$}

For a nonnegative, radially nonincreasing spherical <mass density>, $\Omega^2=GM(r)/r^3$ and $\kappa^2=\Omega^2+4\pi G\rho(r)$. Since $\rho(r)$ does not exceed the mean enclosed density, the <radial epicyclic frequency> lies between $\Omega$ and $2\Omega$. General axisymmetric disks need not satisfy this bound.

= Kepler-harmonic epicyclic precession
{parent=Radial epicyclic frequency}
{c}
{title2=$\Omega^2/\kappa^2=\frac{GM/r^3+\alpha\omega^2}{GM/r^3+4\alpha\omega^2}$}

For $\Phi=-GM/r+\alpha\omega^2r^2/2$, nearly circular motion has $\Omega^2=GM/r^3+\alpha\omega^2$ and $\kappa^2=GM/r^3+4\alpha\omega^2$. Their generally nonrational ratio makes small-eccentricity orbits precess even if $\alpha$ was added slowly. This illustrates <adiabatic actions do not preserve orbital closure>.

= Epicyclic motion
{parent=Radial epicyclic frequency}

Epicyclic motion is the small oscillation of a displaced fluid element or particle about a circular orbit. Its natural radial frequency is the <radial epicyclic frequency>.

= Velocity-kick epicycle
{parent=Epicyclic motion}
{title2=$x/(\gamma a)=2\cos\theta(1-\cos nt)+\sin\theta\sin nt$}

A small planar <velocity> kick on a stellar <circular orbit> produces a local <epicyclic motion> in the source's <rotating reference frame>. With outward $x$, forward $y$, and initial displacements zero, <Hill equations> give $x/(\gamma a)=2\cos\theta(1-\cos nt)+\sin\theta\sin nt$ and $y/(\gamma a)=\cos\theta(4\sin nt-3nt)+2\sin\theta(\cos nt-1)$. Tangential kicks combine an epicycle with <Keplerian shear>; a radial kick gives a closed leading-order <epicyclic ellipse>.

= Epicyclic azimuthal advance
{parent=Epicyclic motion}
{title2=$\Delta\psi=2\pi\Omega/\kappa$}

The azimuthal advance over one complete small radial oscillation is $\Delta\psi=2\pi\Omega/\kappa$. The oscillatory azimuthal correction averages to zero at first order. This angle compares successive radial turning points of the same type and describes <apsidal precession> when it differs from a full revolution.

= Epicyclic ellipse
{parent=Epicyclic motion}
{title2=$X/Y=\kappa/(2\Omega)$}

In a Cartesian frame rotating with an <epicyclic guiding center>, a small planar oscillation obeys $x=X\cos(\kappa t+\chi)$ and $y=-(2\Omega X/\kappa)\sin(\kappa t+\chi)$. The ellipse is elongated azimuthally when the <radial epicyclic frequency> satisfies $\kappa<2\Omega$. <Conservation of angular momentum> makes the motion around the guiding centre retrograde.

= Epicyclic guiding center
{title2=$x_0$}
{parent=Epicyclic motion}

The epicyclic guiding center is the circular-orbit location about which a particle performs <epicyclic motion>. In a <Keplerian shearing sheet>, the conserved <canonical momentum> is $p_y=\dot y+2\Omega x=\Omega x_0/2$. The guiding center therefore labels the particle's first-order <specific angular momentum> offset, even while its instantaneous radial coordinate oscillates.

= Epicyclic energy
{title2=$E_{\rm epi}$}
{parent=Epicyclic motion}

For <epicyclic motion> in a <Keplerian shearing sheet>, write $x-x_0=\operatorname{Re}(Ae^{-i\Omega t})$. The energy of the radial <harmonic oscillator> per unit mass is $E_{\rm epi}=\Omega^2|A|^2/2$. The full horizontal <Jacobi energy in a shearing sheet> also includes the negative guiding-center term $-3\Omega^2x_0^2/8$.

= Plasma beta
{title2=$\beta$}
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{wiki}

Plasma beta is the ratio of gas pressure to magnetic pressure, $\beta=P_g/P_m$.

= Sub-Keplerian rotation
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{c}

= Sub-Keplerian
{c}
{synonym}

Sub-Keplerian rotation has angular speed below that of a circular <Keplerian orbit> at the same radius. In a gaseous disk, an outwardly decreasing pressure supplies some radial support and makes the gas sub-Keplerian.

= Streaming instability
{parent=Astrophysical disk}
{wiki}

The streaming instability is an instability of mutually coupled gas and solid particles in a disk. Relative drift and drag can concentrate the particles into dense structures.

= Streaming instability resonance
{parent=Streaming instability}

A streaming-instability resonance occurs when a gas wave has frequency $\omega=\mathbf k\mathbin\cdot\Delta\mathbf u$, matching the wave phase to the particle drift. The resonant forcing then produces algebraic particle-density growth in the test-particle limit.

= Galaxy
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

A galaxy is a gravitationally bound system of stars, interstellar gas and dust, stellar remnants, and dark matter.

= Galactic bulge
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki=Bulge_(astronomy)}

A <galactic bulge> is a centrally concentrated <stellar population> in a <galaxy>, usually thicker than its disk. Its origin, rotation and random stellar motions can differ from those of the disk; bulge light is often described by a <Sérsic profile>.

= Stellar halo
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

A <stellar halo> is the extended, approximately spheroidal <stellar population> surrounding a <galaxy>. It differs from the total <dark-matter halo>. If its outer luminosity density is $r^{-p}$, its projected light follows the <projection of a spherical power-law emissivity> and scales as $R^{1-p}$ away from breaks and outer truncation.

= Star-forming galaxy
{parent=Galaxy}

A star-forming galaxy is a <galaxy> with ongoing <star formation>. Its hot young stars can supply hydrogen-ionizing radiation to the <intergalactic medium>, with the contribution controlled by the <ionizing photon escape fraction>.

= Irregular galaxy
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

An <irregular galaxy> lacks a regular spiral or elliptical outline. Many contain substantial gas and recent <star formation>; irregular appearance alone does not determine a <stellar population>'s age.

= Spiral galaxy
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

A <spiral galaxy> has a <galactic disk> with spiral structure, commonly cold interstellar gas and ongoing <star formation> as well as an older <stellar population>.

= Galactic disk
{parent=Galaxy}

= Galactic disc
{synonym}

A <galactic disk> is a flattened stellar or gaseous component of a <galaxy>, commonly supported mainly by ordered rotation. Its radial mass profile determines its <galaxy rotation curve>; thickness, truncation and external forces also matter. A <galactic disk> need not be an <exponential galactic disk>, and its neutral-gas absorption radius need not equal a stellar scale length.

= Galactic disk heating
{parent=Galactic disk}

= Galactic disc heating
{synonym}

<Galactic disk heating> increases the random velocities of <stars> through encounters with <molecular clouds>, nonaxisymmetric gravitational disturbances, and other perturbations. Older <stellar populations> usually have larger <velocity dispersions> and greater vertical thickness, although birth conditions and migration also affect the relation.

= Galactic disk flaring
{parent=Galactic disk}

= Galactic disc flaring
{synonym}

<Galactic disk flaring> is an outward increase of vertical thickness. A constant vertical <velocity dispersion> in an approximately thin-sheet stellar <Newtonian gravitational field> gives $h\propto1/\Sigma_*$. An <exponential galactic disk> then predicts $h\propto e^{R/R_d}$ for a gas tracer. Inside a thick stellar layer the potential is instead approximately harmonic, and the tracer width is $\sigma_z/\nu_z$ with <vertical epicyclic frequency> $\nu_z$.

= Self-gravitating isothermal slab
{parent=Galactic disk}
{title2=$\rho(z)=\rho_0\operatorname{sech}^2(z/z_0)$}

A plane-symmetric, self-gravitating <stellar population> with constant vertical <velocity dispersion> satisfies the <vertical Jeans equation> and the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity>. With $\Phi'(0)=0$, their solution is
$$
\rho=\rho_0\operatorname{sech}^2(z/z_0),\quad \Phi-\Phi(0)=2\sigma_z^2\log\cosh(z/z_0),\quad z_0^2=\frac{\sigma_z^2}{2\pi G\rho_0}.
$$
Its <surface density> is $\Sigma=2\rho_0z_0$, so $z_0=\sigma_z^2/(\pi G\Sigma)$. Its outer density tail is exponential with <scale height> $z_0/2$; its central profile is not exponential.

= Galaxy stellar mass
{title2=$M_*$}
{parent=Galaxy}

= Galactic stellar mass
{synonym}

Galaxy stellar mass is the total mass in a galaxy's stars and stellar remnants. It is usually inferred from luminosity and colour or a spectral-energy-distribution fit, so it depends on stellar-population models, dust treatment, and the adopted <initial mass function>.

= Mass-to-light ratio
{parent=Galaxy stellar mass}
{title2=$\Upsilon=M/L$}

The <mass-to-light ratio> compares the mass assigned to an astronomical system with its luminosity in a specified band, often in solar units. A stellar value uses only stellar mass and depends on the stellar population, age, composition and band. A dynamical value can include gas and <dark matter>, so it need not equal the stellar value. In a halo-to-galaxy mapping, $L=M_*/\Upsilon_*$ requires the stellar ratio and not the total <dark-matter halo> ratio.

= Mass-to-light ratio of a power-law stellar population
{parent=Mass-to-light ratio}

Write $x=m/M_\odot$, the <initial mass function> as $dN=A x^{-\alpha}dx$, and a main-sequence <mass-luminosity relation> as $L/L_\odot=x^\beta$. On $a<x<b$, the <mass-to-light ratio> in solar units is
$$
\frac{\Upsilon}{M_\odot/L_\odot}=\frac{\int_a^b x^{1-\alpha}dx}{\int_a^b x^{\beta-\alpha}dx}.
$$
For $\alpha=\beta=3$ this is exactly $1/(ab)$. The result concerns the specified unevolved <stellar population>; <stellar remnants>, evolved luminous stars and cooling substellar objects require different accounting.

= Galaxy stellar mass function
{parent=Galaxy stellar mass}
{title2=$dn_{\rm gal}/dM_*$}

= Galactic stellar mass function
{synonym}

The <galaxy stellar mass function> counts galaxies per stellar-mass interval and volume, often using <comoving volume>. Its stellar-mass moment gives the <cosmic stellar mass density>, while its number moment counts galaxies only if the low-mass population is integrable or truncated. It differs from the initial mass function of individual stars.

= Cosmic stellar mass density
{parent=Galaxy stellar mass function}
{title2=$\rho_*^{\rm com}=\int_0^\infty M_*n_*(M_*)\,dM_*$}

The <cosmic stellar mass density> is the mass moment of a <galaxy stellar mass function> in a stated volume convention. With a comoving mass function it is mass per <comoving volume>, while proper density is larger by $(1+z)^3$. Comparing it to a critical or matter density requires keeping the epoch and volume convention explicit.

= Stellar mass function with a square-root exponential cutoff
{parent=Galaxy stellar mass function}
{title2=$n_*(M_*)\propto M_*^{-7/4}e^{-\sqrt{M_*/M_{*,\rm ch}}}$}

A generalized mass function with low-mass slope $-7/4$ and cutoff $e^{-\sqrt{M_*/M_{*,\rm ch}}}$ differs from the ordinary exponential Schechter cutoff. Its total stellar-mass density is finite, even though its untruncated total galaxy count diverges. This form arises from a scale-free Press-Schechter mass function with a constant stellar-to-halo mass mapping.

= Stellar mass density from a square-root exponential cutoff
{parent=Stellar mass function with a square-root exponential cutoff}
{title2=$\rho_*=2\sqrt\pi\,\phi_{\rm ch}M_{*,\rm ch}$}

Integrating <galactic stellar mass> against a $x^{-7/4}e^{-\sqrt x}$ mass function requires $\int_0^\infty x^{-3/4}e^{-\sqrt x}dx$. Substitution $u=\sqrt x$ turns it into $2\Gamma(1/2)=2\sqrt\pi$. Thus the stellar density is $2\sqrt\pi$ times characteristic number density times characteristic <galactic stellar mass>, in the same volume convention as the mass function.

= Schechter function
{title2=$\Phi(M)$}
{c}
{parent=Galaxy stellar mass}
{wiki}

= Stellar mass of a galaxy
{synonym}

The Schechter function models a galaxy luminosity or mass distribution as a low-value power law with an exponential high-value cutoff. In stellar-mass form,
$$
\Phi(M)\,dM=\frac{\Phi_*}{M_*}
\left(\frac M{M_*}\right)^\alpha e^{-M/M_*}\,dM.
$$

= Gas-to-stellar mass ratio
{title2=$r_g=M_g/M_*$}
{parent=Galaxy}

The gas-to-stellar mass ratio compares a galaxy's gas reservoir with its stellar mass. Different observational definitions may include only molecular gas, only atomic gas, or both with a helium correction.

= Galaxy evolution
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

Galaxy evolution studies how galaxies acquire mass, form stars, enrich their gas, exchange material with their surroundings, change morphology, and merge over cosmic time.

= Starburst galaxy
{parent=Galaxy evolution}
{wiki}

A starburst galaxy undergoes an episode of rapid <star formation>. An instantaneous-burst idealization places all newly formed <stars> at one <stellar age>, so their present evolutionary-state fractions follow from the <initial mass function> at that fixed age rather than from an average over formation times.

= Galaxy merger
{parent=Galaxy evolution}
{wiki}

A galaxy merger combines two or more <galaxies> into a remnant. Their orbital <angular momentum> and energy are redistributed into internal stellar and halo motions, escaping matter, and, when present, dissipative gas. A bound pair produced by <tidal capture of galaxies> need not coalesce immediately.

= Gas-rich galaxy merger
{parent=Galaxy merger}

= Wet galaxy merger
{synonym}

A <gas-rich galaxy merger> involves a substantial reservoir of gas capable of <radiative cooling>. Dissipation and gravitational torques permit gas inflow and enhanced <star formation>, unlike the nearly collisionless stellar evolution in a <dry galaxy merger>. A surviving or replenished gas reservoir can subsequently form a <galactic disk>.

= Major galaxy merger
{parent=Galaxy merger}

A <major galaxy merger> joins galaxies with comparable masses. Rapid changes in the gravitational potential can redistribute stellar orbital energies through <violent relaxation>, substantially disturbing preexisting <galactic disks>. The outcome depends on gas content, orbit and subsequent accretion; a major merger does not universally prohibit disk rebuilding.

= Geometric galaxy-encounter rate
{parent=Galaxy merger}
{title2=$\Gamma=\pi R^2N\langle v_{\mathrm{rel}}\rangle$}

A target <galaxy> with geometric encounter cross section $\pi R^2$ in a population of number density $N$ has encounter rate $\Gamma=\pi R^2N\langle v_{\mathrm{rel}}\rangle$. A <Poisson process> gives merger probability $1-e^{-\Gamma T}$, approximated by $\Gamma T$ only for rare encounters. Counts of distinct pairs across the whole population additionally require a factor of one-half.

= Tidal capture of galaxies
{parent=Galaxy merger}

Tidal capture converts sufficient relative orbital energy into internal <tidal heating> to leave two initially unbound <galaxies> on a bound orbit. For identical spherical galaxies of mass $m_g$, a distant rapid flyby of <impact parameter> $p$ and relative speed $v$ gives the approximate capture condition $pv<[(32/3)G^2m_g^2\langle r^2\rangle]^{1/4}$. This requires the <impulse approximation> and weak <gravitational focusing>; it is not a general criterion for subsequent coalescence.

= Equal-mass tidal-capture threshold
{parent=Tidal capture of galaxies}
{title2=$pv<[32G^2m_g^2\langle r^2\rangle/3]^{1/4}$}

Two equal galaxies each receive the <tidal impulse heating of a spherical galaxy>, while their relative orbital energy at infinity is $m_gv^2/4$. Transfer of more internal energy than that orbital energy gives $pv<[32G^2m_g^2\langle r^2\rangle/3]^{1/4}$. Slow encounters require <adiabatic shielding of tidal encounters>, so the impulsive inequality does not apply there.

= Dry galaxy merger
{parent=Galaxy merger}

A dry galaxy merger joins galaxies with little cold gas, so dissipation and merger-triggered star formation are weak. Conservation of energy followed by <virial theorem>[re-virialization] can make repeated dry minor mergers increase a galaxy's radius faster than its mass.

= Parabolic dry-merger energy accounting
{parent=Dry galaxy merger}
{title2=$E_F=E_I+E_A$}

In a <dry galaxy merger>, zero orbital energy at infinite separation and retention of all mass and energy give $E_F=E_I+E_A$. If the progenitors and remnant satisfy the <virial theorem>, their <gravitational radii> obey $R_F/R_I=(1+\eta)^2/(1+\epsilon\eta)$, where $\eta=M_A/M_I$ and $\epsilon=\langle v_A^2\rangle/\langle v_I^2\rangle$. The latter average is weighted by satellite mass. Nonzero orbital energy, escaping stars, or dissipative losses change the energy accounting.

= Minor galaxy merger
{parent=Dry galaxy merger}

A minor galaxy merger has a small satellite-to-host mass ratio. Its stars are commonly deposited at large host radii after <tidal stripping> and <Chandrasekhar dynamical friction>.

= Cold minor-merger size growth
{parent=Minor galaxy merger}
{title2=$R_g\propto M^2$}

For repeated <minor galaxy mergers> with negligible internal satellite binding energy and zero orbital energy at infinity, <parabolic dry-merger energy accounting> leaves total energy nearly constant. The <virial theorem> then gives <gravitational radius> $R_g\propto M^2$ and mean-square speed proportional to $M^{-1}$. Small satellite mass does not by itself imply negligible internal binding energy.

= Stellar feedback
{parent=Galaxy evolution}
{wiki}

Stellar feedback is the transfer of energy, momentum, mass, and newly formed elements from stars to surrounding matter through radiation, winds, supernovae, and cosmic rays.

= Radiation-pressure limit of a dusty starburst
{parent=Stellar feedback}
{title2=$\kappa F/c\lesssim g$}

For <flux> $F$ and effective dust <opacity> $\kappa$, radiative acceleration is $\kappa F/c$. Comparing it with the gravitational acceleration gives an <Eddington luminosity> condition appropriate to dusty gas. In an optically thick <starburst galaxy>, feedback can limit the <star formation rate> per area and drive a <galactic outflow>, while the gas supply limits the duration. A surface-density limit is not a universal ceiling on total <star formation rate>: the star-forming area matters. https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0503027[Radiation-supported starburst disk calculations] give characteristic optically thick infrared fluxes near $10^{13}L_\odot\,\mathrm{kpc}^{-2}$.

= Impulsive mass-loss expansion law
{parent=Stellar feedback}

If a virialized, well-mixed self-gravitating system instantaneously retains mass fraction $\epsilon>1/2$, homologous revirialization gives $R'/R=\epsilon/(2\epsilon-1)$. At or below one half, the idealized remnant is unbound.

= Impulsive disruption by stellar mass loss
{parent=Impulsive mass-loss expansion law}
{title2=$E'=|W|f(1/2-f)$}

Take an initially isolated virialized self-gravitating <stellar population> with <kinetic energy> $T=|W|/2$ and <Newtonian gravitational potential energy> $W<0$. Instantaneous spatially uniform removal of mass, without kicks to surviving material, leaves fraction $f$ with $T'=fT$ and $W'=f^2W$. Hence $E'=|W|f(1/2-f)$: the homologous whole-system energy is negative only for $f>1/2$. Positive whole-system energy does not forbid a bound core after selective escape, and slow mass loss follows the <adiabatic mass-loss expansion law> instead.

= Critical initial mass function slope for impulsive disruption
{parent=Impulsive disruption by stellar mass loss}
{title2=$\alpha_{\rm crit}\simeq1.791$}

For an <initial mass function> $dN\propto m^{-\alpha}dm$ on $0.1<m/M_\odot<100$, complete loss of material initially above $10M_\odot$ removes fraction
$$
F(\alpha)=\frac{100^{2-\alpha}-10^{2-\alpha}}{100^{2-\alpha}-0.1^{2-\alpha}},\qquad F(2)=\frac13.
$$
The <impulsive disruption by stellar mass loss> threshold is $F=1/2$, with unique finite root $\alpha\simeq1.79101236$. Decreasing $\alpha$ shifts mass to larger stars and increases $F$, so the idealized system is globally unbound for $\alpha<\alpha_{\rm crit}$. The derivation requires rapid, well-mixed loss and absence of a confining external potential.

= Adiabatic mass-loss expansion law
{parent=Stellar feedback}

If a self-gravitating system loses mass slowly compared with its orbital times, adiabatic invariance gives $MR=\text{constant}$ in the homologous approximation, so retaining fraction $\epsilon$ expands the system by $R'/R=1/\epsilon$.

= Galactic outflow
{parent=Galaxy evolution}

A galactic outflow carries gas, metals, energy, and momentum away from a galaxy under stellar or active-galactic-nucleus feedback. Whether the material escapes or later returns depends on its speed and the galactic potential.

= Escape-speed scaling of wind mass loading
{parent=Galactic outflow}
{title2=$\nu\propto v_{\rm esc}^{-2}\text{ or }v_{\rm esc}^{-1}$}

If <stellar feedback> supplies energy $e_*$ per unit formed stellar mass, an <energy-driven outflow> requires $\nu v_{\rm esc}^2/2\lesssim e_*$. Fixed energy coupling therefore suggests <mass-loading factor> $\nu\propto v_{\rm esc}^{-2}$. A fixed momentum supply $p_*$ instead gives $\nu v_{\rm esc}\lesssim p_*$ and <momentum-driven outflow> scaling $\nu\propto v_{\rm esc}^{-1}$. These are launch and escape estimates, not universal wind laws; subsequent entrainment, cooling and fallback change the escaping mass.

= Mass-loading factor
{title2=$\lambda$}
{parent=Galactic outflow}

The mass-loading factor is the ratio of outflow rate to star-formation rate, $\lambda=\dot M_{\rm out}/\dot M_*$. Its value can depend on galaxy mass, radius, gas phase, and the surface through which the flow is measured.

= Galactic gas inflow
{parent=Galaxy evolution}

Galactic gas inflow supplies a galaxy from its circumgalactic medium, cooling halo, or intergalactic filaments. It can sustain <star formation> and dilute the metallicity of the existing interstellar gas.

= Galactic chemical evolution
{parent=Galaxy evolution}
{wiki}

Galactic chemical evolution follows the production, transport, dilution, locking, and loss of chemical elements as stars form and die and gas enters or leaves a galaxy.

= Alpha element
{parent=Galactic chemical evolution}

= Alpha-capture element
{synonym}

An alpha element is an element such as oxygen, neon or magnesium associated with nucleosynthesis involving helium nuclei and massive-star burning. <Core-collapse supernovae> promptly return substantial abundances of these elements. Their abundance relative to iron can distinguish a rapid enrichment history from one with substantial delayed <Type Ia supernova> enrichment.

= Delayed iron enrichment
{parent=Alpha element}

Massive stars produce prompt enrichment through <core-collapse supernovae>, whereas a <Type Ia supernova> follows binary stellar evolution and contributes substantial iron after a distribution of delays. A <stellar population> formed rapidly before much delayed enrichment can have a larger alpha-element to iron ratio than a <stellar population> formed over a prolonged interval. Relative abundance also depends on the <initial mass function>, <stellar yields>, and gas exchange; this is a chemical clock with model assumptions.

= Proportional-infall model of galactic chemical evolution
{parent=Galactic chemical evolution}
{title2=$\dot M_{\rm in}=\nu\psi$}

With well-mixed <gas-phase metallicity> $Z$, zero mass return, <star formation rate> $\psi$, pristine <galactic gas inflow> $\nu\psi$, and constant <stellar yield> $y$, <mass conservation> and metal accounting give $\dot M=-(1-\nu)\psi$ and $M\dot Z=(y-\nu Z)\psi$. Thus, for $0<\nu<1$,
$$
Z=\frac y\nu+\left(Z_0-\frac y\nu\right)(M/M_0)^{\nu/(1-\nu)}.
$$
The initial <gas-phase metallicity> $Z_0=0$ gives the usual pristine-infall expression. Neglecting returned bulk mass while retaining a nonzero metal yield is a trace-metal idealization.

= Metallicity distribution in the half-rate infall model
{parent=Proportional-infall model of galactic chemical evolution}
{title2=$dN/dZ=\kappa M_0/y$}

For $\nu=1/2$ and initially pristine gas, $M=M_0(1-Z/(2y))$ and the formed stellar mass is $S=2(M_0-M)=M_0Z/y$. If a fixed <initial mass function> supplies $\kappa$ surviving tracers per unit formed mass, then $N(<Z)=\kappa M_0Z/y$ and $dN/dZ=\kappa M_0/y$ until the current gas abundance. Complete exhaustion gives a uniform normalized <metallicity distribution function> on $0<Z<2y$. This is uniform in linear metallicity, not in its logarithm.

= Gas fraction of a galaxy
{parent=Galactic chemical evolution}
{title2=$\mu$}

The gas fraction of a <galaxy> is commonly $\mu=M_g/(M_g+M_s)$, using the gas and stellar masses remaining in the system. In a <leaky-box model of galactic chemical evolution>, it differs from the fraction $g=M_g/M_{g,0}$ of the initial gas reservoir. For an initially star-free box with outflow rate $\alpha\dot M_s$, $\mu=(1+\alpha)g/(1+\alpha g)$. These different fractions give different-looking metallicity formulae and <effective yields>.

= Galactic metallicity
{parent=Galactic chemical evolution}

Galactic metallicity measures the abundance of elements heavier than helium in a galaxy. Gas and stars retain different time-weighted records of enrichment.

= Gas-phase metallicity
{title2=$Z_g$}
{parent=Galactic metallicity}

Gas-phase metallicity is the heavy-element abundance of interstellar gas. In star-forming galaxies it is commonly inferred from nebular emission lines in <H II regions>, often through oxygen abundance $12+\log_{10}(\mathrm O/\mathrm H)$.

= Stellar metallicity
{title2=$Z_*$}
{parent=Galactic metallicity}

Stellar metallicity is the heavy-element abundance locked in stars. Integrated-light measurements are luminosity weighted unless a stellar-population reconstruction converts them to a mass-weighted quantity.

= Galaxy mass--metallicity relation
{parent=Galactic metallicity}

The galaxy mass--metallicity relation is the empirical tendency for galaxies with greater <galaxy stellar mass> to have higher <gas-phase metallicity> and <stellar metallicity>. Mass-dependent metal loss, gas inflow, star-formation efficiency, and recycling all contribute.

= Instantaneous recycling approximation
{title2=IRA}
{parent=Galactic chemical evolution}

The instantaneous recycling approximation treats short-lived stars as returning their ejecta immediately after formation while long-lived stars retain their mass indefinitely. It turns delayed chemical enrichment into local differential equations.

= Stellar yield
{title2=$y_z$}
{parent=Instantaneous recycling approximation}

The stellar yield is the newly synthesized metal mass returned to gas per unit mass locked into long-lived stars, under a specified recycling convention and <initial mass function>.

= Closed-box model of galactic chemical evolution
{parent=Galactic chemical evolution}

The closed-box model evolves a fixed total baryonic mass with no inflow or outflow. Under instantaneous recycling and constant yield it predicts $Z=y\log(1/\mu)$ for gas fraction $\mu$.

= Leaky-box model of galactic chemical evolution
{parent=Closed-box model of galactic chemical evolution}

The leaky-box model adds a <galactic outflow> to the closed box. If the outflow has the ambient gas metallicity and constant <mass-loading factor> $\lambda$, its effective yield is reduced to $y/(1+\lambda)$ when returned fractions are absorbed into the definitions.

= Metallicity distribution in a leaky box
{parent=Leaky-box model of galactic chemical evolution}
{title2=$p(Z)=y_e^{-1}e^{-Z/y_e}$}

For initially pristine gas, zero bulk mass return, a constant <stellar yield> $y$ and a well-mixed <galactic outflow> of <mass-loading factor> $\nu$, gas conservation gives $dM_g=-(1+\nu)dM_s$ and metal accounting gives $M_g\,dZ=y\,dM_s$. Consequently $M_g=M_{g,0}e^{-Z/y_e}$ with $y_e=y/(1+\nu)$. Since $dM_s/dZ=M_g/y$, complete depletion gives a normalized mass-weighted <metallicity distribution function> $p(Z)=e^{-Z/y_e}/y_e$ and mean <stellar metallicity> $y_e$. A time-independent <initial mass function> makes the distribution of surviving tracers proportional to this distribution; a sample selected by light need not have the same weights.

= Effective yield
{title2=$y_{\rm eff}$}
{parent=Leaky-box model of galactic chemical evolution}

The effective yield is the yield that a simple closed-box relation would need to reproduce an observed gas fraction and metallicity. Outflow and pristine inflow generally lower it below the nucleosynthetic yield.

= G-dwarf problem
{c}
{parent=Closed-box model of galactic chemical evolution}
{wiki}

The G-dwarf problem is the deficit of low-metallicity long-lived G dwarfs near the Sun relative to the prediction of a constant-yield closed-box model. Prolonged accretion of metal-poor gas is a standard ingredient in its resolution.

= Metallicity distribution function
{title2=MDF}
{parent=G-dwarf problem}

A metallicity distribution function gives the number or mass of stars per metallicity interval. Its interpretation depends on sample selection, stellar lifetimes, spatial migration, and whether metallicity means total heavy-element abundance or a particular abundance ratio.

= Logarithmic metallicity distribution
{parent=Metallicity distribution function}
{title2=$dN/d\log_{10}Z=(\ln10)Z\,dN/dZ$}

Changing from linear <stellar metallicity> to its logarithm multiplies the <metallicity distribution function> by its <Jacobian determinant>. A flat distribution in linear metallicity rises exponentially in logarithmic metallicity. The commonly used iron abundance coordinate is equivalent to total-metallicity scaling only if relative element abundances are fixed.

= Dwarf galaxy
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

A dwarf galaxy has relatively low stellar mass and luminosity and is often strongly dark-matter dominated. Its shallow potential makes gas loss and stellar feedback dynamically important.

= Dark-matter core
{parent=Dwarf galaxy}

A dark-matter core is an inner halo region whose density approaches a finite value rather than continuing as a steep cusp. Repeated rapid potential fluctuations driven by bursty stellar feedback can transfer orbital energy to dark matter and create such a core.

= Elliptical galaxy
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

An elliptical galaxy has a smooth spheroidal stellar distribution and little cold gas. Low-mass systems are commonly flattened fast rotators, while the most massive systems are commonly slowly rotating, anisotropic, and triaxial.

= Core and power-law elliptical galaxies
{parent=Elliptical galaxy}

Bright <elliptical galaxies> commonly have a shallow central surface-brightness cusp inside a break radius, whereas many less luminous <elliptical galaxies> have a steep cusp continuing inward. The latter can have many more luminous tracers at a fixed small radius, helping central <black-hole> dynamical measurements. This empirical distinction is not a universal classification by mass alone. https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9610055[Measurements of central galaxy profiles] quantify the contrast.

= Oblate isotropic rotator
{parent=Elliptical galaxy}

An oblate isotropic rotator is flattened primarily by ordered rotation while its random velocity dispersion is isotropic. Its location in a $v/\sigma$--ellipticity diagram provides a reference against which anisotropy is measured.

= Fast and slow rotator galaxy
{parent=Elliptical galaxy}

Fast rotators have substantial ordered stellar rotation and are often disky and nearly axisymmetric. Slow rotators have low specific angular momentum and are often massive, anisotropic, triaxial, and core-profiled.

= Local Group
{c}
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

The Local Group is the gravitationally bound galaxy group containing the Milky Way, M31, and their satellite galaxies.

= Local Group timing argument
{c}
{parent=Local Group}
{wiki=Timing_argument}

The Local Group timing argument treats the Milky Way and M31 as a nearly radial two-body orbit whose present separation, approach speed, and cosmic age constrain their total mass.

= Milky Way
{c}
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

The Milky Way is the barred spiral galaxy containing the Solar System.

= Interstellar medium
{title2=ISM}
{parent=Milky Way}
{wiki}

The interstellar medium is the gas, dust, magnetic field, and energetic-particle population between stars in a galaxy.

= Superbubble
{parent=Interstellar medium}
{wiki}

A superbubble is an expanding cavity of hot gas driven by the combined energy input of stellar winds and multiple <supernova> explosions. Constant mechanical power and negligible radiative losses lead to a radius proportional to $t^{3/5}$ in a uniform ambient medium.

= Adiabatic superbubble similarity solution
{parent=Superbubble}
{title2=$R=\alpha(\dot E/\rho_1)^{1/5}t^{3/5}$}

For constant injected power $\dot E$ and ambient <mass density> $\rho_1$, <dimensional analysis> gives $R=\alpha(\dot E/\rho_1)^{1/5}t^{3/5}$. In a <similarity solution>, set $\eta=r/R$, $\rho=\rho_1f(\eta)$, $p=\rho_1\dot R^2g(\eta)$ and $u=\dot Rh(\eta)$. The strong-shock values on the inner side of $\eta=1$ are $f=(\gamma+1)/(\gamma-1)$ and $g=h=2/(\gamma+1)$.

= Central energy input in a similarity solution
{parent=Adiabatic superbubble similarity solution}

For point injection of constant power, the outward <energy flux> through a sphere tending to the source must tend to $\dot E$. In the <adiabatic superbubble similarity solution>, this requires
$$
\lim_{\eta\downarrow0}\eta^2h\left(\frac12fh^2+\frac{\gamma g}{\gamma-1}\right)=\frac{125}{108\pi\alpha^5}.
$$
Imposing finite regular central pressure and density with $h=O(\eta)$ would instead give zero flux and fail to supply the prescribed power. A resolved source region or an inner contact-boundary model can replace the point-source condition.

= Energy normalization of a continuously driven spherical shock
{parent=Adiabatic superbubble similarity solution}

If the similarity profiles account for all kinetic and internal energy inside an adiabatic strong shock, let
$$
I=\int_0^1\left(\frac12fh^2+\frac{g}{\gamma-1}\right)\eta^2\,d\eta.
$$
The injected energy $\dot Et$ equals $4\pi\rho_1\dot R^2R^3I$. Consequently <conservation of energy> fixes
$$
\alpha=\left(\frac{25}{36\pi I}\right)^{1/5}.
$$
An interior region not included in the profiles must have its energy added separately; a radiatively cooled shell changes this energy budget.

= Continuity equation for a superbubble similarity solution
{parent=Adiabatic superbubble similarity solution}

Substitution of the <adiabatic superbubble similarity solution> into spherical <mass conservation> gives
$$
-\eta f'+\eta^{-2}(\eta^2fh)'=0,
\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad(h-\eta)f'+fh'+2fh/\eta=0.
$$

= Supernova remnant
{title2=SNR}
{parent=Interstellar medium}
{wiki}

A supernova remnant is the expanding ejecta and shocked ambient medium left by a supernova explosion.

= Adiabatic phase of a supernova remnant
{parent=Supernova remnant}

During its adiabatic phase, a supernova remnant loses little energy radiatively and expands as a hot, broad shocked flow rather than a thin cooled shell.

= Molecular cloud
{parent=Interstellar medium}
{wiki}

A molecular cloud is a cold dense region of the interstellar medium in which hydrogen is predominantly molecular and stars can form.

= Carbon-monoxide tracer of molecular hydrogen
{title2=CO tracer of $\mathrm H_2$}
{parent=Molecular cloud}

Rotational carbon-monoxide line emission traces cold molecular clouds because abundant molecular hydrogen is difficult to observe directly. A conversion factor $X_{\rm CO}$ relates integrated CO intensity to molecular-hydrogen column density.

= Interstellar dust
{parent=Interstellar medium}
{wiki}

Interstellar dust absorbs and scatters short-wavelength starlight and reradiates the absorbed energy thermally in the infrared.

= Interstellar extinction
{parent=Interstellar dust}
{wiki}

Interstellar extinction is the attenuation of light by absorption and scattering from dust between a source and observer. Its wavelength dependence commonly reddens a source as well as dimming it.

= H II region
{c}
{parent=Interstellar medium}
{wiki}

An H II region is a volume of ionized hydrogen surrounding one or more sources of ultraviolet photons. Photoionization heating, recombination, gas dynamics, and dust determine its structure and emission spectrum.

= Ionization front
{parent=H II region}
{wiki}

An ionization front is the transition between predominantly ionized and predominantly neutral gas. Its motion and thickness depend on photon supply, recombination, gas density, and hydrodynamic response.

= Ionization-front growth from an exponentially brightening source
{parent=Ionization front}
{title2=$\frac{4\pi}3n_{H,0}X^3=Q_0\tau(e^{t/\tau}-1)$}

In a homogeneous initially neutral medium, an isotropic source with <hydrogen-ionizing photon production rate> $Q(t)=Q_0e^{t/\tau}$ ionizes the displayed comoving inventory when every escaping photon produces one primary ionization and recombinations are absent. The physical front radius is $a(t)X$ in an expanding background. Retarded photon propagation prevents any instantaneous front from travelling faster than light. This photon-budget front is not the equilibrium <Strömgren radius>, which balances emission against recombinations.

= Photoionization equilibrium
{parent=H II region}

Photoionization equilibrium holds when the local or integrated photoionization rate equals the radiative recombination rate, so the ionized fraction is stationary.

= Recombination integral for a power-law nebula
{parent=Photoionization equilibrium}

For fully ionized pure hydrogen of density $n(r)=n_0(r/r_0)^{-\beta}$ and constant <Case B recombination> coefficient $\alpha$, the total recombination rate is
$$
\mathcal R(R;r_{\rm in})=4\pi\alpha n_0^2r_0^{2\beta}\int_{r_{\rm in}}^R r^{2-2\beta}\,dr.
$$
The central integral converges only for $\beta<3/2$; an envelope extending to infinity has finite recombination capacity only for $\beta>3/2$. At $\beta=3/2$, each logarithmic radius interval contributes equally. A <cored power-law density profile> removes the central singularity.

= Critical ionizing photon rate of a cored power-law nebula
{parent=Recombination integral for a power-law nebula}
{title2=$Q_{\rm crit}$}

For the <cored power-law density profile> that is constant inside $r_c$ and falls as $r^{-\beta}$ outside, with $\beta>3/2$, the maximum integrated <Case B recombination> rate is
$$
Q_{\rm crit}=4\pi\alpha n_0^2r_c^3\left(\frac13+\frac1{2\beta-3}\right).
$$
A <hydrogen-ionizing photon production rate> greater than this has no finite radius at <photoionization equilibrium>. Equality requires an infinite ionized radius. Other core shapes change the coefficient but retain the convergence condition $\beta>3/2$.

= Recombination time
{title2=$t_{\rm rec}$}
{parent=Photoionization equilibrium}

The recombination time is the characteristic time for an ionized plasma to recombine after its ionizing source is removed. Uniform hydrogen with electron density $n_e$ and recombination coefficient $\alpha$ has $t_{\rm rec}=1/(\alpha n_e)$.

= Case B recombination
{c}
{title2=$\alpha_B$}
{parent=Photoionization equilibrium}

Case B recombination excludes direct recombinations to the ground state because their ionizing photons are assumed to be absorbed locally. The coefficient $\alpha_B$ therefore counts recombinations to excited states.

= Hydrogen-ionizing photon production rate
{title2=$Q_{\rm H}$}
{parent=H II region}

The hydrogen-ionizing photon production rate is the number of photons emitted per unit time above the hydrogen ionization threshold:
$$
Q_{\rm H}=\int_{\nu_0}^{\infty}\frac{L_\nu}{h\nu}\,d\nu.
$$

= Ionizing photon escape fraction
{parent=Hydrogen-ionizing photon production rate}
{title2=$f_{\rm esc}$}

The ionizing photon escape fraction is the fraction of photons capable of <photoionization> that leave their source environment. Only these escaping photons contribute to ionizing distant <intergalactic medium>. Its value can depend on frequency, source geometry, and time.

= Mean ionizing photon energy of a power-law spectrum
{parent=Hydrogen-ionizing photon production rate}
{title2=$\langle E\rangle=\frac{s}{s-1}h\nu_0\quad(s>1)$}

For $L_\nu=A\nu^{-s}$ above an ionization threshold $\nu_0$, the energy luminosity is $A\nu_0^{1-s}/(s-1)$ and the photon rate is $A\nu_0^{-s}/(sh)$. Their ratio is the displayed emitted mean energy. For slope two it is twice the threshold energy. Absorption weighting, a high-energy cutoff and secondary ionizations can change the energy deposited per ionization; emitted and locally absorbed spectra must not be conflated.

= Strömgren sphere
{c}
{parent=H II region}
{wiki}

A Strömgren sphere is the idealized ionized region around a steady source embedded in uniform hydrogen gas, bounded where integrated recombinations consume the source's ionizing-photon supply.

= Strömgren radius
{title2=$R_S$}
{c}
{parent=Strömgren sphere}

For ionizing-photon rate $Q_{\rm H}$, uniform pure-hydrogen density $n$, and <Case B recombination> coefficient $\alpha_B$, the Strömgren radius is
$$
R_S=\left(\frac{3Q_{\rm H}}{4\pi\alpha_Bn^2}\right)^{1/3}.
$$

= Ionization-front growth of a Strömgren sphere
{c}
{parent=Strömgren sphere}

Before hydrodynamic expansion matters, photon counting in a uniform initially neutral medium gives
$$
R(t)=R_S\left(1-e^{-t/t_{\rm rec}}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
The ionization front approaches the Strömgren radius on the recombination time.

= Dusty Strömgren sphere
{c}
{parent=Strömgren sphere}

A dusty Strömgren sphere includes ionizing photons absorbed by dust before they can ionize hydrogen. Dust therefore decreases the ionized radius below the dust-free <Strömgren radius> at fixed source luminosity and gas density.

= Neutral-hydrogen 21-centimeter line
{title2=H I 21-cm line}
{parent=Interstellar medium}
{wiki}

The neutral-hydrogen 21-centimeter line is the ground-state hyperfine transition of atomic hydrogen. Its brightness and Doppler shift map atomic-gas column density and line-of-sight velocity.

= Cosmic ray
{parent=Interstellar medium}
{wiki}

A cosmic ray is a high-energy charged particle propagating through space. Galactic cosmic-ray electrons emit synchrotron and inverse-Compton radiation, while cosmic-ray nuclei produce gamma rays through collisions with gas.

= Galactic multiwavelength astronomy
{parent=Milky Way}

Galactic multiwavelength astronomy combines spectral bands that respond differently to stars, gas, dust, magnetic fields, temperature, and energetic particles.

= Galactic synchrotron emission
{parent=Galactic multiwavelength astronomy}

Galactic synchrotron emission is nonthermal radio radiation from relativistic electrons spiralling in the Galactic magnetic field. Its broad latitude distribution reflects cosmic-ray transport and the magnetic halo.

= Galactic gamma-ray emission
{parent=Galactic multiwavelength astronomy}

Diffuse Galactic gamma rays arise mainly from neutral-pion decay after cosmic-ray nuclear collisions, electron bremsstrahlung in gas, and inverse-Compton scattering from radiation fields.

= Galactic scale height
{title2=$H$}
{parent=Galactic multiwavelength astronomy}

The Galactic scale height measures the vertical thickness of a tracer. Gravity, velocity dispersion, thermal pressure, magnetic and cosmic-ray support, particle transport, and source lifetime determine its value.

= Galactic bar
{parent=Milky Way}
{wiki}

The Galactic bar is the elongated central stellar structure of the Milky Way. Extinction-corrected near-infrared standard candles and gas position--velocity maps constrain its distance, orientation, and three-dimensional shape.

= Rotating-frame relative effective potential
{parent=Galactic bar}
{title2=$\psi_{\mathrm{eff}}=\psi+\tfrac12\Omega_b^2R^2$}

For a bar rotating with constant pattern speed $\Omega_b$, use $\psi_{\mathrm{eff}}=\psi+\Omega_b^2R^2/2$ in the force convention $+\nabla\psi$. The centrifugal acceleration is its added gradient. This is the negative of the usual physical effective potential $\Phi_{\mathrm{eff}}$; Coriolis acceleration is separate.

= Star formation rate
{title2=SFR}
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

The star formation rate is the mass converted into stars per unit time. Infrared luminosity traces dust-reprocessed light from young stars, while molecular-gas tracers locate their cold fuel.

= Linear gas-consumption law
{parent=Star formation rate}
{title2=$\psi=\epsilon M_g$}

A linear gas-consumption law assumes that the net rate of locking mass into long-lived stars is proportional to the gas mass, $\psi=\epsilon M_g$, with constant $\epsilon$. In a <leaky-box model of galactic chemical evolution> with ambient-metallicity outflow $\alpha\psi$, gas mass decays as $M_g=M_{g,0}e^{-(1+\alpha)\epsilon t}$, while <gas-phase metallicity> grows as $Z=Z_0+p\epsilon t$. The wind changes the time to consume gas but, under these assumptions, not the enrichment rate at a given time.

= Galaxy rotation curve
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

= Rotation curve
{synonym}

A galaxy rotation curve gives circular speed as a function of radius. Its decomposition into stellar, gaseous, and dark-matter contributions is generally nonunique.

= Angular-momentum estimate of a self-gravitating galactic disk
{parent=Galaxy rotation curve}
{title2=$R_d/R_h=f_j^2/(\kappa f_d),\quad v_d/v_h=\kappa f_d/f_j$}

Let a disk contain fraction $f_d$ of its host mass and let its edge <specific angular momentum> be $f_jR_hv_h$. With halo relation $v_h^2=GM_h/R_h$ and disk geometry coefficient $\kappa=v_d^2R_d/(GM_d)$, combining centrifugal balance and the edge <angular momentum> gives the displayed estimates. The coefficient records the radial mass profile and thickness: <enclosed mass does not determine a disc rotation curve>. Treating $\kappa=1$ as a monopole estimate must not be mistaken for an exact thin-disk theorem.

= Enclosed mass does not determine a disc rotation curve
{parent=Galaxy rotation curve}

The <spherical shell theorem> yields $v_c^2=GM(<r)/r$ only in spherical geometry. A razor-thin axisymmetric disc instead requires $v_c^2=R\partial_R\Phi(R,0)$, including forces from exterior annuli. The <interior gravitational force of an exterior thin ring> gives a concrete counterexample with identical enclosed mass but different circular speeds.

= Interior gravitational force of an exterior thin ring
{parent=Galaxy rotation curve}
{title2=$\partial_R\psi_{\mathrm{ring}}=GmR/(2a^3)+\cdots$}

A thin ring of mass $m$ and radius $a$ has interior relative potential $Gm[1+R^2/(4a^2)+\cdots]/a$. Its leading radial acceleration is outward, $GmR/(2a^3)$, unlike the zero interior force of a spherical shell. Exterior disc annuli therefore affect a <galaxy rotation curve>.

= Circular speed
{title2=$v_c$}
{parent=Galaxy rotation curve}

In a spherical potential, the circular speed satisfies
$$
v_c^2(r)=r\frac{d\Phi}{dr}=\frac{GM(<r)}r.
$$
It measures the gravitational field even when the system itself is not supported by circular motion.

= Flat galaxy rotation curve
{parent=Galaxy rotation curve}

= Flat rotation curve
{synonym}

A flat galaxy rotation curve has approximately constant circular speed $v_c$ over a radial interval. Under spherical symmetry it implies $M(\lt r)=v_c^2r/G$ and mass density $\rho(r)=v_c^2/(4\pi Gr^2)$.

= Exponential galactic disk
{parent=Galaxy rotation curve}

A thin exponential galactic disk has surface density $\Sigma(R)=\Sigma_0e^{-R/R_d}$ and total mass $2\pi\Sigma_0R_d^2$.

= Exponential-disk Keplerian asymptotic
{parent=Exponential galactic disk}
{title2=$v_c^2=GM/R\,[1+9R_d^2/(2R^2)+O(R^{-4})]$}

For an <exponential galactic disk>, the first nonspherical term in the <Legendre expansion of thin-disk gravity> is positive and proportional to $R^{-3}$ in $v_c^2$. The missing enclosed mass and exterior-ring terms are exponentially small at large radius. Hence the <galaxy rotation curve> approaches the point-mass Keplerian curve from above.

= Maximum disk
{parent=Exponential galactic disk}
{wiki}

The maximum disk hypothesis assigns the largest stellar mass-to-light ratio compatible with a measured rotation curve, conventionally making the disk supply about $85\%$ of the circular speed near $2.2R_d$.

= Disk--halo degeneracy
{parent=Galaxy rotation curve}

The disk--halo degeneracy is the ability of different stellar mass-to-light ratios and dark-halo profiles to reproduce nearly the same total galaxy rotation curve.

= Galaxy dynamics
{parent=Galaxy}
{wiki}

Galaxy dynamics studies the gravitational motions and evolution of stars, gas, dark matter, and satellite systems in galaxies.

= Stellar asymmetric drift
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}
{title2=$v_a=v_c-\overline v_\phi$}

= Asymmetric drift
{synonym}
{wiki}

<Stellar asymmetric drift> is the lag of a pressure-supported <stellar population>'s mean rotation behind the local <circular speed>. Neglecting the tilted <velocity ellipsoid>, radial <Jeans equation> balance gives
$$
v_c^2-\overline v_\phi^2=\sigma_R^2\left[-\frac{d\log(n\sigma_R^2)}{d\log R}-1+\frac{\sigma_\phi^2}{\sigma_R^2}\right].
$$
A declining density and larger random velocities generally increase the lag; gravitational balance is supplied partly by random-motion stresses.

= Galactic dynamical time
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}
{title2=$t_{\rm dyn}\sim GM/\sigma^3$}

A <galaxy> of characteristic radius $R$ and <velocity dispersion> $\sigma$ has crossing time $R/\sigma$. Gravitational balance gives $GM/R\sim\sigma^2$, hence $t_{\rm dyn}\sim GM/\sigma^3$. A numerical coefficient depends on the radius definition, density profile, and whether one uses a crossing, orbital, or free-fall time; these are not identical clocks.

= Violent relaxation
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}

<Violent relaxation> is rapid redistribution of the orbital energies of collisionless matter in a time-dependent collective gravitational potential. It can occur during collapse or a <major galaxy merger>. It differs from collisional two-body relaxation and from <phase mixing> in a stationary potential; it does not imply perfect final thermal equilibrium.

= Oort constants
{c}
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}
{title2=$A,B$}
{wiki}

= Oort's constants
{c}
{synonym}

The Oort constants describe local differential galactic rotation: $A=-R\Omega'/2$ and $B=-\Omega-R\Omega'/2$. Thus $\Omega=A-B$, the <radial epicyclic frequency> obeys $\kappa^2=-4\Omega B$, and the <epicyclic ellipse> has axis ratio $\sqrt{-B/(A-B)}$.

= Cusped logarithmic spherical galaxy model
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}
{title2=$\Phi=v_0^2\log(1+r/a)$}

This spherical <gravitational potential>, with $a,v_0>0$, has density $\rho=v_0^2(2a+r)/(4\pi Gr(a+r)^2)$ and <circular speed> $v_c=v_0\sqrt{r/(a+r)}$. It combines a central $1/r$ density cusp with an outer <flat galaxy rotation curve>, but its total mass is infinite. It differs from a logarithmic potential with a constant-density core.

= Galactic tide
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}

The differential <Newtonian gravitational field> of a <galaxy> across a smaller bound system. Its <tidal tensor> can slowly change the <angular momentum> of distant <comets>, changing their <periapses> and their access to planetary encounters.

= Impulse approximation
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}

In a rapid encounter, the impulse approximation holds stellar positions fixed while integrating an external acceleration over time. A stellar velocity kick is $\Delta\mathbf v=\int\mathbf a_{\rm tidal}(t)\,dt$. If the encounter time $p/v$ is much shorter than the orbital time $\Omega^{-1}$, this gives reliable leading <tidal heating> for a weak, distant encounter. Slow perturbations require <adiabatic shielding of tidal encounters> instead.

= Complete-flyby gravitational impulse
{parent=Impulse approximation}
{title2=$|\Delta v_\perp|=2GM/(Vb),\quad\Delta v_{\parallel,\rm reduction}=2G^2M^2/(V^3b^2)$}

Integrate the transverse force along a complete straight path: $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}(1+s^2)^{-3/2}ds=2$. This gives the transverse kick. Conservation of relative speed gives longitudinal speed reduction $\Delta v_\perp^2/(2V)$ at second order. As an independent check, exact weak hyperbolic scattering has angle $2\arctan\eta$, where $\eta=GM/(bV^2)$, and reduction $V(1-\cos(2\arctan\eta))=2V\eta^2/(1+\eta^2)$. The small-$\eta$ coefficient agrees. Keeping only one half of the encounter in the transverse kick makes this quadratic reduction four times too small.

= Satellite impulse normalization
{parent=Complete-flyby gravitational impulse}
{title2=$\Delta v_y=\chi(GM_s)^2/(S^3x^5)$}

For a straight shearing-sheet encounter, $V=S|x|$, and the outer and inner longitudinal changes have opposite signs. The <complete-flyby gravitational impulse> gives $\chi=2$. Some simplified prescribed kick models use a different coefficient; carrying it explicitly makes the corresponding <one-sided impulse torque on a disk> and migration rates unambiguous. This coefficient does not assert an exact solution of the full rotating Hill encounter.

= One-sided impulse torque on a disk
{parent=Satellite impulse normalization}
{title2=$\Gamma_+=\chi r_0(GM_s)^2S^{-2}\int_H^\infty\Sigma(x)x^{-4}dx$}

Multiply encounter mass flux $\Sigma(x)Sx\,dx$ by specific angular-momentum gain $r_0\Delta v_y$. The net disk <torque> replaces $\Sigma(x)$ by $\Sigma(x)-\Sigma(-x)$, so a symmetric <mass density> gives zero net <torque>. For constant <surface density>, <Keplerian shear> and the full-flyby value $\chi=2$, the one-sided coefficient is $8/27$ in $q^2\Sigma r_0^4\Omega^2(r_0/H)^3$, matching https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/468/4/4610/3098191[the primary coplanar impulse normalization].

= Density-slope satellite torque
{parent=One-sided impulse torque on a disk}
{title2=$\Gamma=(4\chi/9)\beta q^2\Sigma_0r_0^4\Omega^2(H/r_0)^{-2}$}

A local odd <mass density> slope $\Sigma(x)-\Sigma(-x)=2\Sigma_0\beta x/r_0$ changes the cancelling one-sided <torques> into this net <torque>. A local outer cutoff $L$ adds the factor $1-(H/L)^2$. Positive slope transfers net <angular momentum> from satellite to disk. An infinite linear <mass density> law is only a formal local extension, since it cannot remain nonnegative on both sides.

= Integrated tidal tensor of a straight-line flyby
{parent=Impulse approximation}
{title2=$\int T\,dt=\frac{2Gm_p}{vp^2}\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,0)$}

For a perturber following $(p,0,vt)$, the <impulse approximation> integrates its <tidal tensor> to $2Gm_p\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,0)/(vp^2)$. A fixed target position therefore receives $\Delta\mathbf v=2Gm_p(x,-y,0)/(vp^2)$. The longitudinal kick vanishes after integrating the symmetric trajectory.

= Tidal impulse heating of a spherical galaxy
{parent=Integrated tidal tensor of a straight-line flyby}
{title2=$\Delta U_g=\frac{4G^2m_p^2m_g}{3v^2p^4}\langle r^2\rangle$}

If the initial stellar velocities are uncorrelated with the <impulse approximation> kicks, mean specific heating is $|\Delta\mathbf v|^2/2$. Spherical position averaging gives total heating $4G^2m_p^2m_g\langle r^2\rangle/(3v^2p^4)$, where the mass-weighted mean square radius must be finite.

= Adiabatic shielding of tidal encounters
{parent=Impulse approximation}

Adiabatic shielding suppresses the heating of an orbit when an external <tidal tensor> varies slowly compared with its orbital frequency. In a nonresonant slow encounter, <adiabatic invariance of an orbital action> lets the orbit adjust approximately reversibly, so a frozen-position <impulse approximation> overestimates the energy transfer. An <orbital resonance> can invalidate the simple slow-variation argument.

= Chandrasekhar dynamical friction
{c}
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}
{wiki}

Chandrasekhar dynamical friction is the drag on a massive body produced by its gravitational wake in a background of lighter particles. Its magnitude is proportional to the body's mass and the background density.

= Tidal radius
{title2=$r_t$}
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}
{wiki}

The tidal radius bounds material that remains gravitationally attached to a satellite in the differential gravitational field of its host.

= Jacobi tidal radius
{c}
{parent=Tidal radius}

For a low-mass satellite of mass $M_s$ on a circular orbit of radius $r_o$ in a spherical host,
$$
r_t=r_o\left[
\frac{M_s}{(3-d\log M_h/d\log r_o)M_h(r_o)}
\right]^{1/3}.
$$
This local approximation requires $r_t\ll r_o$.

= Tidal stripping
{parent=Tidal radius}
{wiki}

Tidal stripping removes satellite material outside the tidal radius. The resulting mass loss weakens dynamical friction and can greatly delay or prevent a merger.

= Tidal tail
{parent=Tidal stripping}
{wiki}

A tidal tail is an elongated stream of material removed by a differential gravitational field. A satellite generally produces one leading and one trailing tail as stripped material enters nearby orbits with slightly different energy and angular momentum.

= Dynamical-friction stalling by tidal stripping
{parent=Tidal stripping}

Dynamical-friction stalling by tidal stripping occurs when inward motion shrinks a satellite's tidal radius so rapidly that its remaining mass and frictional drag tend to zero before it reaches the host center.

= Stellar dynamics
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}
{wiki}

Stellar dynamics studies the collective gravitational motion of stars in galaxies and star clusters.

= Spherical density projection
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{title2=$\Sigma(R)=2\int_R^\infty\rho(r)r(r^2-R^2)^{-1/2}\,dr$}

A spherical <mass density> $\rho$ gives projected <surface density> $\Sigma(R)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\rho(\sqrt{R^2+z^2})\,dz$. On the positive half of the <line of sight>, substitute $r=\sqrt{R^2+z^2}$ to obtain the displayed <Abel transform>. Spherical symmetry is essential: a general three-dimensional density cannot be reconstructed uniquely from one projection.

= Strip density of a spherical system
{parent=Spherical density projection}
{title2=$S(x)=2\pi\int_x^\infty r\rho(r)\,dr$}

The strip density is the mass per unit projected Cartesian coordinate $x$: $S(x)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\Sigma(\sqrt{x^2+y^2})\,dy$. It is even in $x$. For $x\geq0$, changing variables to projected radius gives $S(x)=2\int_x^\infty\Sigma(R)R/\sqrt{R^2-x^2}\,dR$. Alternatively integrate the three-dimensional <mass density> over the plane of coordinates $y,z$ to obtain the displayed expression. Consequently $S'(x)=-2\pi x\rho(x)$ for $x>0$.

= Gravitational binding energy from strip density
{parent=Strip density of a spherical system}
{title2=$W=-2G\int_0^\infty S(x)^2\,dx$}

For an isolated spherical distribution, shell assembly gives <gravitational potential energy> $W=-\int_0^\infty GM(r)r^{-1}\,dM(r)$. Use $S'=-2\pi r\rho$ and $M'=-2rS'$ to write $W=2G\int MS'\,dr=-2G\int SM'\,dr=4G\int rSS'\,dr$. A second <integration by parts> gives the displayed negative quadratic functional. The boundary terms $MS$ and $rS^2$ must vanish; finite-mass regular profiles with finite binding energy satisfy the usual sufficient decay conditions.

= Spherical Abel deprojection
{parent=Spherical density projection}
{title2=$\rho(r)=-\pi^{-1}\int_r^\infty\Sigma'(R)(R^2-r^2)^{-1/2}\,dR$}

Put $F(u)=\Sigma(\sqrt u)$ and $g(v)=\rho(\sqrt v)$. Then $F(u)=\int_u^\infty g(v)/\sqrt{v-u}\,dv$. Composing this <Abel transform> with itself and exchanging convergent <integrals> gives $\int_t^\infty F(u)/\sqrt{u-t}\,du=\pi\int_t^\infty g(v)\,dv$, because the inner integral from $t$ to $v$ is $\pi$. Differentiate after writing $u=t+s$ to obtain $g(t)=-\pi^{-1}\int_t^\infty F'(u)/\sqrt{u-t}\,du$. Substituting $t=r^2,u=R^2$ yields the displayed reconstruction. Decay and regularity sufficient for the exchanges and derivatives are required.

= Plummer model
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{c}
{title2=$\psi=GM/\sqrt{a^2+r^2}$}

The spherical <Plummer model> has $\psi=GM/\sqrt{a^2+r^2}$ and $\rho=3Ma^2/[4\pi(a^2+r^2)^{5/2}]$. It is the $p=2$ <hypervirial model>. In unit scales its isotropic distribution is $24\sqrt2E^{7/2}/(7\pi^3)$ for positive <relative energy>.

= Hernquist model
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{c}
{title2=$\psi=GM/(r+a)$}

The <Hernquist model> has <relative potential> $\psi=GM/(r+a)$ and density $\rho=Ma/[2\pi r(r+a)^3]$. It has enclosed <mass> $Mr^2/(r+a)^2$, a central $r^{-1}$ cusp and an outer $r^{-4}$ tail. Since $r\rho=a\psi^3/(2\pi G^3M^2)$, the <half-anisotropic distribution function> is $f(E,L)=3aE^2/(4\pi^3G^3M^2L)$ for $0<E<GM/a$. Its moments are $\langle v_r^2\rangle=\psi/4$ and each tangential moment $\psi/8$. Isotropic and other admissible anisotropic distributions can also support this same density; the <gravitational potential> does not select a unique <velocity> anisotropy.

= N-body simulation
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{c}
{title2=$N$}

An <N-body simulation> approximates a dynamical system by evolving $N$ interacting particles. In a collisionless <galaxy> calculation these particles sample a smooth <mass> distribution rather than represent individual physical <stars>. Their larger individual <masses> enhance artificial <two-body relaxation>; softening, <force> accuracy and convergence with particle number must be checked over the intended duration.

= Gravitational softening
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{title2=$\epsilon$}

<Gravitational softening> regularizes a simulation particle's short-distance <force>, for example by replacing $-Gm/r$ by $-Gm/\sqrt{r^2+\epsilon^2}$. It suppresses artificial close encounters and effectively raises the diffusion cutoff to order $\max(\epsilon,Gm/u^2)$, but alters the resolved <force> below $\epsilon$. Particle number, softening and duration must be chosen together; a point-particle relaxation estimate alone does not specify a simulation's accuracy.

= Height-evaluation gravitational softening
{parent=Gravitational softening}
{title2=$\Phi_\epsilon(\mathbf R)=-G\int\Sigma(\mathbf R')(|\mathbf R-\mathbf R'|^2+\epsilon^2)^{-1/2}d^2R'$}

Evaluating the potential of a <razor-thin disk> at height $\epsilon$ gives a softened horizontal force. Its Fourier multiplier is $e^{-k\epsilon}$ times the <razor-thin disk Poisson kernel>. This suppresses short <wavelengths> while retaining the long-wave potential. A real finite-thickness profile generally has a different full reduction factor.

= Vertical-profile softening match
{parent=Height-evaluation gravitational softening}
{title2=$\epsilon_{\rm match}=\langle|z|\rangle_\rho$}

For a vertically separable perturbation with normalized height profile $h(z)$, the midplane gravity reduction factor is $\int h(z)e^{-k|z|}dz$. Its small-$k$ expansion is $1-k\langle|z|\rangle+o(k)$ when this first moment is finite. Match it to $e^{-k\epsilon}$ to get the displayed choice. For $h=e^{-|z|/H}/(2H)$ the exact factor is $(1+kH)^{-1}$, demonstrating why no single exponential matches every <wavelength>.

= Stellar crossing time
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{title2=$t_{\rm dyn}\sim R/v$}

The <stellar crossing time> is a characteristic <time> to traverse a system, $t_{\rm dyn}\sim R/v$. In a virialized self-gravitating system, $v^2\sim GM/R$, so $t_{\rm dyn}\sim\sqrt{R^3/(GM)}$. It is distinct from the much longer spherical <stellar relaxation time>. Radius versus diameter and characteristic-speed conventions change order-one factors.

= Star cluster
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{wiki}

A star cluster is a gravitationally associated population of stars formed in a compact region.

= Open cluster
{parent=Star cluster}
{wiki}

An <open cluster> is a relatively low-mass group of <stars> formed together, typically in a <galactic disk>. <Two-body relaxation>, <stellar evaporation>, and external tidal disturbances disperse many <open clusters> on much shorter times than the lifetimes of their low-mass member <stars>.

= Stellar evaporation
{parent=Star cluster}

<Two-body relaxation> redistributes stellar energies and feeds stars above the escape threshold. The escaping population removes mass and generally nonnegative energy at infinity. Radius evolution and external tidal limits must be considered before inferring the change in cluster density.

= Virial contraction under stellar evaporation
{parent=Stellar evaporation}
{title2=$R_g\propto M^2,\quad\bar\rho\propto M^{-5}$}

For an isolated quasistatic cluster with near-zero escape energies, the bound negative energy is approximately fixed. The <virial theorem> and <gravitational radius> then imply $R_g\propto M^2$. A homologous mean density scales as $M^{-5}$ and increases as mass evaporates. Tidal limitation or finite positive escape energies modifies this simple model.

= Globular cluster
{parent=Star cluster}
{wiki}

A globular cluster is an old, dense, approximately spheroidal star cluster whose long-term evolution is shaped by two-body relaxation and the tidal field of its host galaxy.

= Oblate spheroid
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{wiki=Spheroid#Oblate_spheroids}

An oblate spheroid is obtained by rotating an ellipse about its shorter axis. If its equatorial semiaxis is one and its polar semiaxis is $q\leq1$, then $q$ is its intrinsic axis ratio.

= Projected axis ratio of an oblate spheroid
{title2=$Q$}
{parent=Oblate spheroid}

If the symmetry axis of an <oblate spheroid> makes angle $i$ with the line of sight, its projected axis ratio satisfies
$$
Q^2=\cos^2i+q^2\sin^2i.
$$
Thus a face-on spheroid appears round and an edge-on spheroid reveals its intrinsic axis ratio.

= Random-orientation distribution of an apparent oblate axis ratio
{parent=Projected axis ratio of an oblate spheroid}

For an unoriented symmetry axis chosen uniformly on the sphere, $\cos i$ is uniform on $[0,1]$. At fixed intrinsic ratio $q$, the <change-of-variables formula for a probability density> gives
$$
p(Q\mid q)=\frac{Q}{\sqrt{1-q^2}\sqrt{Q^2-q^2}},
\qquad q\leq Q\leq1.
$$

= Spherical isochrone model
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{wiki=Isochrone_model}

The spherical isochrone model has potential
$$
\Phi(r)=-\frac{GM}{b+\sqrt{b^2+r^2}}.
$$
Its radial period depends only on orbital energy, while its <radial action> and frequencies are elementary functions of the integrals of motion.

= Radial action of the spherical isochrone model
{title2=$J_r$}
{parent=Spherical isochrone model}

For specific energy $E<0$ and specific angular momentum $L$,
$$
J_r=\frac{GM}{\sqrt{-2E}}
-\frac12\left(L+\sqrt{L^2+4GMb}\right).
$$
Consequently the Hamiltonian in actions is
$$
H(J_r,L)=-\frac{2(GM)^2}
{\left(2J_r+L+\sqrt{L^2+4GMb}\right)^2}.
$$

= Frequency ratio of the spherical isochrone model
{parent=Spherical isochrone model}

The radial and azimuthal frequencies obey
$$
\frac{\Omega_\phi}{\Omega_r}
=\frac12\left(1+\frac{L}{\sqrt{L^2+4GMb}}\right).
$$
The ratio tends to one half in the harmonic core and to one in the Keplerian exterior.

= Hypervirial model
{parent=Stellar dynamics}

The hypervirial family has relative potential
$$
\Psi(r)=\frac{GM}{(r^p+a^p)^{1/p}},
\qquad 0<p\leq2,
$$
and density
$$
\rho(r)=\frac{(p+1)Ma^p}{4\pi}
\frac{r^{p-2}}{(r^p+a^p)^{2+1/p}}.
$$
Its name comes from satisfying the scalar virial relation pointwise.

= Global binding integral of the hypervirial model
{parent=Hypervirial model}
{title2=$I=\frac{p+1}{p}\frac{\Gamma(1+1/p)^2}{\Gamma(2+2/p)}$}

In unit scales $I=\int\rho\psi d^3x=(p+1)\Gamma(1+1/p)^2/[p\Gamma(2+2/p)]$. The <local virial relation of the hypervirial model> gives global energies $T=I/4$ and $W=-I/2$. Restoring physical scales multiplies each integral by $GM^2/a$.

= Hypervirial density-potential family
{parent=Hypervirial model}
{title2=$\psi=GM/(a^p+r^p)^{1/p}$}

For $p>0$, the relative potential $GM/(a^p+r^p)^{1/p}$ generates density $(p+1)Ma^pr^{p-2}/[4\pi(a^p+r^p)^{2+1/p}]$. In unit scales the separable <augmented density> is $(p+1)r^{p-2}\psi^{2p+1}/(4\pi)$. Its <Jeans moments from an augmented density> give constant anisotropy $\beta=1-p/2$.

= Hypervirial distribution function
{parent=Hypervirial model}

The <hypervirial model> is generated self-consistently by a <constant-anisotropy distribution function> proportional to
$$
L^{p-2}\mathcal E^{(3p+1)/2}.
$$
Its <velocity-anisotropy parameter> is $\beta=1-p/2$.

= Normalization of the hypervirial distribution function
{parent=Hypervirial distribution function}
{title2=$C=\frac{(p+1)\Gamma(2p+2)}{2^{(p+5)/2}\pi^{5/2}\Gamma(p/2)\Gamma((3p+3)/2)}$}

In units $G=M=a=1$, velocity integration of $F=CL^{p-2}E^{(3p+1)/2}$ gives $C=(p+1)\Gamma(2p+2)/[2^{(p+5)/2}\pi^{5/2}\Gamma(p/2)\Gamma((3p+3)/2)]$. The <beta function> separates the radial-speed and angular integrals. This constant is positive for every $p>0$.

= Local virial relation of the hypervirial model
{parent=Hypervirial model}

For the <hypervirial model>, $\langle v^2\rangle=\Psi/2=-\Phi/2$. The local kinetic- and potential-energy densities
$$
K=\frac12\rho\langle v^2\rangle,
\qquad
W=\frac12\rho\Phi
$$
therefore obey $2K+W=0$ at every radius, a stronger statement than the integrated <virial theorem>.

= Galactic distribution function
{title2=$f(\mathbf x,\mathbf v,t)$}
{parent=Stellar dynamics}

A galactic distribution function gives stellar mass or number per <phase space> volume. Integrating over velocity gives spatial density, while velocity moments and projection along the line of sight predict kinematic observables.

= Two-integral galactic distribution function
{parent=Galactic distribution function}
{title2=$F(E,L_z)$}

= Two-integral distribution
{synonym}

A stationary axisymmetric <galactic distribution function> depending on the <specific orbital energy> and axial <specific angular momentum> is a two-integral distribution. These quantities are conserved in a time-independent axisymmetric <Newtonian gravitational potential>, so the <collisionless Boltzmann equation> holds by the chain rule. Even dependence on $L_z$ removes net azimuthal <streaming velocity>; an odd part can supply rotation without changing the <mass density>.

= Logarithmic-potential two-integral distribution
{parent=Two-integral galactic distribution function}
{title2=$F=AL_z^2e^{-4E/V^2}+Be^{-2E/V^2}$}

The singular <axisymmetric logarithmic gravitational potential> admits this stationary <galactic distribution function> with
$$
A=\frac{4\sqrt2(1-q^2)}{\pi^{5/2}Gq^2V^3},\qquad B=\frac{2q^2-1}{4\pi^{5/2}Gq^2V}.
$$
The zero of <specific orbital energy> is the printed logarithmic convention. Products of <Gaussian integrals> normalize its two spatial density terms. For $1/2\le q^2\le1$ the two terms are separately nonnegative; mildly prolate models require a bound on their sum.

= Nonnegativity bound for a prolate logarithmic-potential distribution
{parent=Logarithmic-potential two-integral distribution}
{title2=$q^2\le(16\sqrt2-e)/(16\sqrt2-2e)$}

For the <logarithmic-potential two-integral distribution>, $q^2>1$ makes $A<0$. Accessible phase space satisfies $L_z^2e^{-2E/V^2}\le V^2/e$, attained by equatorial circular motion. Hence the <galactic distribution function> is nonnegative precisely when $B+AV^2/e\ge0$, giving the displayed upper bound. A larger prolate flattening parameter produces negative phase-space density even though the spatial <mass density> remains positive.

= Meridional velocity isotropy of a two-integral distribution
{parent=Two-integral galactic distribution function}
{title2=$\langle v_R^2\rangle=\langle v_z^2\rangle$}

At fixed position a <two-integral distribution> depends on $v_R,v_z$ only through $v_R^2+v_z^2$. Swapping these integration variables makes their second moments equal. Their odd moments and mixed second moments vanish by reflection symmetry. For an even function of $L_z$ the azimuthal <velocity> is also centered, so all mixed entries of the <velocity ellipsoid> vanish.

= Streaming velocity
{parent=Galactic distribution function}
{title2=$\langle v_\phi\rangle$}

The streaming <velocity> is the local mean stellar <velocity> obtained by averaging with the <galactic distribution function>. In an axisymmetric disk it usually denotes $\langle v_\phi\rangle$. It differs from the <circular speed>, which is set by the <force> field, and from the <velocity dispersion>, which measures fluctuations about the mean.

= Maximally prograde stellar distribution
{parent=Streaming velocity}
{title2=$F_+(E,L_z)=2F(E,L_z)\Theta(L_z)$}

For a nonrotating axisymmetric <galactic distribution function> even in $L_z$, reverse every retrograde azimuthal <velocity>. The resulting distribution is $F_+(E,L_z)=2F(E,L_z)\Theta(L_z)$: it preserves density and even <velocity> moments but makes the <streaming velocity> $\langle|v_\phi|\rangle$. Dependence on conserved $E,L_z$ preserves stationarity. A centered <Gaussian distribution> of width $\sigma$ becomes a <half-normal distribution>, with mean $\sqrt{2/\pi}\sigma$.

= Planar isotropic stellar distribution function
{parent=Galactic distribution function}
{title2=$F(E),\quad E=v^2/2+\Phi$}

= Planar isotropic distribution
{synonym}

For a stationary <collisionless stellar system> confined to a plane, <velocity> isotropy makes the <galactic distribution function> depend on <speed> through $u=v^2/2$. The <collisionless Boltzmann equation> implies $\nabla_x f=f_u\nabla_x\Phi$, hence $f=F(E)$ with <specific orbital energy> $E=u+\Phi$. This conclusion requires stationarity as well as isotropy.

= Planar isotropic distribution inversion
{parent=Planar isotropic stellar distribution function}
{title2=$F(E)=-\frac1{2\pi}\frac{dS}{d\Phi}\big|_{\Phi=E}$}

The <surface density of a disk> associated with a <planar isotropic distribution> is $S(\Phi)=2\pi\int_\Phi^\infty F(E)dE$. Differentiation gives $F(E)=-(2\pi)^{-1}dS/d\Phi|_{\Phi=E}$. A vanishing upper-limit boundary value recovers the original integral; monotonic decrease of $S(\Phi)$ ensures a nonnegative distribution.

= Osipkov-Merritt distribution function
{parent=Galactic distribution function}
{c}
{title2=$Q=E-L^2/(2r_a^2)$}

An Osipkov-Merritt spherical distribution has $F=f(Q)$ for $Q=E-L^2/(2r_a^2)>0$ and vanishes otherwise. Rescaling transverse velocities by $\sqrt{1+r^2/r_a^2}$ makes its velocity integral isotropic in the rescaled variables. Its <velocity-anisotropy parameter> is $\beta=r^2/(r^2+r_a^2)$.

= Anisotropic Plummer distribution with unit anisotropy radius
{parent=Osipkov-Merritt distribution function}
{title2=$F=3Q^{3/2}/(\sqrt2\pi^3)$}

In unit <Plummer model> scales, $F=3Q^{3/2}/(\sqrt2\pi^3)$ for $Q=E-L^2/2>0$ gives $\rho=3\psi^3/[4\pi(1+r^2)]=3\psi^5/(4\pi)$. It reproduces the isotropic Plummer density but has anisotropy $r^2/(1+r^2)$ and different second velocity moments.

= Nonuniqueness of spherical galactic distribution functions
{parent=Galactic distribution function}

The density and self-consistent potential of a spherical galaxy need not determine its <galactic distribution function>. Different angular-momentum dependence can reproduce the same density with different <velocity-anisotropy parameters>. An isotropic <Plummer model> and its positive <anisotropic Plummer distribution with unit anisotropy radius> provide an explicit example. A chosen full <augmented density> is additional information.

= Augmented stellar density
{parent=Galactic distribution function}
{title2=$\rho(r,\psi)$}

= Augmented density
{synonym}

An augmented density $\rho(r,\psi)$ treats radius and <relative potential> as independent variables and reduces to the physical density on $\psi=\psi(r)$. Its extension away from that curve contains additional orbital-anisotropy information. Different extensions can give the same physical density; physical realizability additionally requires a nonnegative <galactic distribution function>.

= Jeans moments from an augmented density
{parent=Augmented stellar density}
{c}
{title2=$A=\int_0^\psi\rho\,dq,\quad B=A+\tfrac r2\partial_rA$}

With radial pressure zero at $\psi=0$, an <augmented density> supplies $\rho\langle v_r^2\rangle=\int_0^\psi\rho(r,q)dq$ and $\rho\langle v_\theta^2\rangle=(2r)^{-1}\int_0^\psi\partial_r[r^2\rho(r,q)]dq$. The derivative holds $q$ fixed. Their chain-rule combination satisfies the <spherical Jeans equation>, but the physical density alone does not choose an augmented extension.

= Velocity-ellipsoid alignment in a Stäckel potential
{parent=Galactic distribution function}
{title2=$\langle v_i v_j\rangle=0\quad(i\ne j)$}

A regular phase-mixed distribution $F(H,p_\phi,I)$ in an axisymmetric <Stäckel potential> is even separately in $v_\lambda$ and $v_\mu$, because these <integrals> depend on those components only through squares. Odd <integration> then gives all three mixed <second moments> zero, even if the $p_\phi$ dependence produces azimuthal streaming. Bare stationarity alone is insufficient in degenerate resonant potentials with additional <integrals>. For example the isotropic harmonic <Hamiltonian> has the extra conserved $K=v_xv_y+\omega^2xy$; a positive Gaussian $\exp(-aH+\epsilon K)$ with $a>|\epsilon|>0$ is a steady tracer and has nonzero $\langle v_xv_y\rangle$. Its harmonic <gravitational potential> is a <Stäckel potential> in these spheroidal coordinates. This tracer need not itself source that harmonic <gravitational potential>. The alignment claim thus needs the phase-mixed three-integral hypothesis, not merely the weak form of <Jeans theorem>.

= Eddington inversion
{parent=Galactic distribution function}
{c}
{title2=$f(E)=\frac1{\sqrt8\pi^2}\frac d{dE}\int_0^E\frac{\rho'(\psi)}{\sqrt{E-\psi}}\,d\psi$}

= Eddington's formula
{synonym}
{c}

For a spherical isotropic bound distribution $f(E)$ with $E=\psi-v^2/2$, no unbound population and $\rho(0)=0$, <velocity> <integration> gives $\rho(\psi)=4\pi\sqrt2\int_0^\psi f(E)\sqrt{\psi-E}\,dE$. Differentiation followed by <Abel transform> inversion yields $f(E)=(\sqrt8\pi^2)^{-1}(d/dE)\int_0^E\rho'(\psi)/\sqrt{E-\psi}\,d\psi$. The factor is $1/(\sqrt8\,\pi^2)$, with $\pi^2$ outside the square root. Regularity sufficient for these operations and nonnegativity of the resulting distribution are separate requirements; an arbitrary density-potential pair need not give a physical distribution.

= Untruncated Eddington inversion
{parent=Eddington inversion}
{title2=$f(E)=\frac1{\sqrt8\pi^2}\frac d{dE}\int_{-\infty}^E\frac{\rho'(\psi)}{\sqrt{E-\psi}}\,d\psi$}

If the <galactic distribution function> is not restricted to positive <relative energy>, isotropic <velocity> integration gives $\rho(\psi)=4\pi\sqrt2\int_{-\infty}^{\psi}f(E)\sqrt{\psi-E}\,dE$. The same <Abel transform> argument as <Eddington inversion> yields the displayed formula, with lower limit $-\infty$. For $\rho=\rho_0e^{\psi/\sigma_0^2}$, substitution $\psi=E-\sigma_0^2s^2$ gives $f(E)=\rho_0(2\pi\sigma_0^2)^{-3/2}e^{E/\sigma_0^2}$. An escape-energy truncation changes both the density law and the velocity tails.

= Action-based galactic distribution function
{title2=$f(\mathbf J)$}
{parent=Galactic distribution function}

An action-based galactic distribution function is a nonnegative function of orbital actions. Actions are integrals of motion in an integrable potential, so $f(\mathbf J)$ automatically gives a steady collisionless model and can represent flattened or rotating systems.

= Schwarzschild orbit-superposition model
{c}
{parent=Galactic distribution function}

A Schwarzschild orbit-superposition model integrates a library of orbits in a trial gravitational potential and chooses nonnegative orbit weights to reproduce observed density and kinematics.

= Made-to-measure stellar-dynamical model
{parent=Galactic distribution function}

A made-to-measure stellar-dynamical model evolves particles in a trial potential while adjusting their weights so that projected observables approach measured constraints.

= Velocity dispersion
{title2=$\sigma$}
{parent=Galactic distribution function}
{wiki}

Velocity dispersion is the standard deviation of velocities about their mean. Its tensor form records anisotropy, while a line-of-sight measurement combines intrinsic kinematics with projection and observational weighting.

= Velocity ellipsoid
{parent=Velocity dispersion}
{title2=$\sigma_{ij}^2=\langle(v_i-\bar v_i)(v_j-\bar v_j)\rangle$}

The <velocity ellipsoid> represents the positive <velocity> <covariance> tensor by its orthogonal principal axes and eigenvalue square roots. The uncentered second-moment tensor differs from <covariance> when a population streams. Vanishing mixed <second moments> align the second-moment <ellipsoid> with the chosen frame; for pure azimuthal streaming in a phase-mixed axisymmetric model, the off-diagonal <covariance> also vanishes.

= Heliocentric projection of a spherical velocity dispersion
{parent=Velocity dispersion}

For a non-streaming spherical tracer population, the <line of sight> makes angle $\chi$ with the Galactocentric radial direction. The projected <velocity dispersion> is
$$
\sigma_{\rm los}^2=\sigma_r^2\cos^2\chi+\sigma_t^2\sin^2\chi
=\sigma_r^2(1-\beta\sin^2\chi),
$$
where $\sigma_t^2$ is one tangential variance and $\beta=1-\sigma_t^2/\sigma_r^2$ is the <velocity-anisotropy parameter>. At a fixed radius, averaging with the observational angular weights gives $\sigma_\odot=\sigma_r\sqrt{1-\beta A}$, where $A=\langle\sin^2\chi\rangle$.

= Geometric correction for heliocentric velocity dispersion
{parent=Heliocentric projection of a spherical velocity dispersion}
{title2=$A(r,R_\odot)$}

For tracers sampled uniformly on a Galactocentric shell of radius $r$, with the Sun at radius $R_\odot$, the correction in the <heliocentric projection of a spherical velocity dispersion> is
$$
A=\frac{r^2+R_\odot^2}{4r^2}
-\frac{(r^2-R_\odot^2)^2}{8r^3R_\odot}
\ln\left|\frac{r+R_\odot}{r-R_\odot}\right|.
$$
The limiting value at $r=R_\odot$ is $1/2$; it tends to $2/3$ as $r/R_\odot\to0$ and to $2R_\odot^2/(3r^2)$ at large radius. Uniform heliocentric <solid angle> at fixed $r>R_\odot$ instead gives $A=2R_\odot^2/(3r^2)$ exactly. Specify the selection measure when applying the correction.

= Velocity-anisotropy parameter
{title2=$\beta$}
{parent=Velocity dispersion}

For spherical radial dispersion $\sigma_r^2$ and total two-component tangential dispersion $\sigma_t^2$, the velocity-anisotropy parameter is
$$
\beta=1-\frac{\sigma_t^2}{2\sigma_r^2}.
$$
Positive values are radially biased, zero is isotropic, and negative values are tangentially biased.

= Relative potential
{title2=$\Psi$}
{parent=Galactic distribution function}

In a bounded stellar system, a relative potential shifts the gravitational potential so that $\Psi$ vanishes at a chosen escape boundary. A common sign convention is $\Psi=\Phi_0-\Phi$.

= Relative energy
{title2=$\mathcal E$}
{parent=Relative potential}

Relative energy is the binding energy per unit mass measured from the chosen escape level, commonly $\mathcal E=\Psi-v^2/2$. Bound phase-space points have $\mathcal E>0$ in this convention.

= Constant-anisotropy distribution function
{parent=Galactic distribution function}

A spherical constant-anisotropy distribution function has
$$
f(\mathcal E,L)=L^{-2\beta}f_E(\mathcal E).
$$
Its velocity moments have the radius-independent <velocity-anisotropy parameter> $\beta$.

= Inverse-angular-momentum velocity integration
{parent=Constant-anisotropy distribution function}
{title2=$F(E,L)=L^{-1}f_E(E)$}

In a spherical <galactic distribution function> with $F=L^{-1}f_E(E)$, write $L=rv_T$ and $d^3v=dv_r\,v_Tdv_T\,d\psi$. The velocity Jacobian cancels the apparent singularity at $v_T=0$. Integration of Gaussian energy components then reduces to one full-line and one half-line <Gaussian integral>. The angular average makes each tangential <velocity dispersion> half the radial one, so the <velocity-anisotropy parameter> is $1/2$.

= Half-anisotropic distribution function
{parent=Constant-anisotropy distribution function}
{title2=$f(E,L)=f_E(E)/L,\quad\beta=1/2$}

For $f(E,L)=f_E(E)/L$, with $L=rv\sin\eta$ and $E=\psi-v^2/2$, <velocity> <integration> gives $r\rho=2\pi^2\int_0^\psi f_E(E)\,dE$. Thus $f_E(E)=(2\pi^2)^{-1}d(r\rho)/d\psi$ at $\psi=E$. The factor $1/L$ is locally integrable under the velocity-volume measure. Its angular measure is uniform in $\eta$; averages of $\cos^2\eta$ and $\sin^2\eta$ are both $1/2$, so the total tangential <second moment> equals the radial <second moment> and the <velocity-anisotropy parameter> is $\beta=1/2$. At the largest allowed radius $\psi(r_E)=E$, the <derivative> becomes $[\rho+r(d\rho/dr)]/(d\psi/dr)$, not $[\rho+d\rho/d\psi]/(d\psi/dr)$. Positivity requires $d(r\rho)/d\psi\geq0$.

= Scale-free density-potential component
{parent=Constant-anisotropy distribution function}

For $f=f_0L^{-2\beta}\mathcal E^n$, velocity integration gives
$$
\rho=A r^{-2\beta}\Psi^{n+3/2-\beta}.
$$
Thus a scale-free monomial in radius and relative potential corresponds directly to a power-law constant-anisotropy distribution function.

= Jeans theorem
{c}
{parent=Galactic distribution function}
{wiki}

Jeans theorem states that a steady <collisionless Boltzmann equation> solution depends on phase-space coordinates only through <integrals of motion>, and that a nonnegative function of isolating integrals gives a steady collisionless distribution function on its domain.

= Two-body relaxation
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{wiki}

Two-body relaxation is the cumulative randomization of stellar velocities by many weak gravitational encounters. Its timescale in a system of $N$ comparable stars is of order $N/\log N$ crossing times.

= Finite-thickness disk relaxation
{parent=Two-body relaxation}
{title2=$t_{\rm disk}/t_{\rm sphere}\sim\lambda^3(z_0/R)\log(R/b_0)/\log(z_0/b_0)$}

For a disk with thickness $z_0$, random relative speeds $u=\lambda v$ and small-impact cutoff $b_0\ll z_0\ll R$, the leading three-dimensional logarithmic encounter term has upper cutoff of order $z_0$. At fixed <mass> and radius, its number <volume> density exceeds the spherical density by order $R/z_0$. Hence $t_{\rm disk}/t_{\rm sphere}\sim\lambda^3(z_0/R)\log(R/b_0)/\log(z_0/b_0)$ when the same cutoff is used for comparison. Different speed-dependent cutoffs must be distinguished in numerical applications. Distant planar encounters contribute a convergent, non-logarithmic correction. This scaling ceases to apply if thickness is comparable to the cutoff.

= Razor-thin disk relaxation
{parent=Two-body relaxation}
{title2=$t_{\rm rel}\sim\lambda t_{\rm dyn}$}

In an unsoftened <plane> with number surface density $n_2$ and relative <speed> $u$, an encounter strip has area $2u\,dt\,db$. Thus the <velocity> diffusion rate is $8G^2m^2n_2/u\int db/b^2$, dominated by the small-impact cutoff. Setting this cutoff to $Gm/u^2$ and requiring a change of order $u^2$ gives $t_{\rm rel}\sim u/(Gmn_2)$. With $u=\lambda v$, $n_2\sim N/R^2$ and $v^2\sim GNm/R$, this is of order $\lambda R/v$, independent of $N$ at fixed total <mass>. The point-particle, zero-thickness limit is singular; fixed softening or nonzero thickness changes it. Numerical coefficients are not controlled by extending weak kicks down to order-one deflections.

= Small-angle gravitational encounter
{parent=Two-body relaxation}
{title2=$\Delta v_\perp\simeq2Gm/(bu)$}

For a <mass> $m$ passing at relative <speed> $u$ and <impact parameter> $b$, <integration> of $Gmb/(b^2+u^2t^2)^{3/2}$ gives the transverse kick $2Gm/(bu)$. Weak deflection requires $b\gg Gm/u^2$, up to the equal-mass order-one factor. This scale supplies a small-impact cutoff for the weak-scattering diffusion approximation, not a physical exclusion of stronger encounters.

= Collisionless stellar system
{parent=Two-body relaxation}
{title2=$T\ll t_{\rm rel}$}

A <collisionless stellar system> is one for which discrete gravitational encounters change its distribution negligibly over the <time> under study, usually $T\ll t_{\rm rel}$. <Stars> can <orbit> and respond collectively to a varying smooth <gravitational potential> even in this regime; collisionless does not mean force-free or stable against collective gravitational instabilities. Its smooth distribution follows the <collisionless Boltzmann equation>.

= Stellar relaxation time
{parent=Two-body relaxation}
{title2=$t_{\rm rel}$}

The <stellar relaxation time> is the <time> on which random gravitational encounters change a <star>'s <velocity> by an amount comparable to its characteristic random <velocity>. A weak straight-line encounter at relative <speed> $u$ and <impact parameter> $b$ gives a kick $2Gm/(bu)$. Three-dimensional encounter counting gives $d\langle|\Delta\mathbf v|^2\rangle/dt=8\pi G^2m^2n\log(b_{\max}/b_{\min})/u$. Definitions of relaxation, <velocity> averaging and system structure alter order-one coefficients; the robust spherical scaling is $t_{\rm rel}\sim v^3/(G^2m^2n\log\Lambda)\sim(N/\log N)t_{\rm dyn}$. Collisionless simulation requirements depend on the duration and permitted fractional relaxation, not a universal minimum particle number.

= Random-walk derivation of stellar relaxation
{parent=Stellar relaxation time}
{title2=$t_{\mathrm{rel}}\sim0.1(N/\ln N)t_{\mathrm{cross}}$}

<Small-angle gravitational encounters> occur at rate $2\pi b\,db\,nu$ and supply kicks $2Gm/(bu)$. Independent kick variances add to $d\langle\Delta v^2\rangle/dt=8\pi G^2m^2n\ln\Lambda/u$. Setting the accumulated variance to $u^2$ and using virial scaling gives a <stellar relaxation time> of order $0.1N/\ln N$ <stellar crossing times>, with the coefficient dependent on profile and crossing convention.

= Coulomb logarithm in stellar dynamics
{title2=$\log\Lambda$}
{parent=Two-body relaxation}

The Coulomb logarithm $\log\Lambda=\log(b_{\max}/b_{\min})$ collects the logarithmically equal contributions of gravitational encounters over a wide range of impact parameters. For a self-gravitating system of $N$ comparable stars, $\Lambda$ is of order $N$.

= Collisional stellar system
{parent=Two-body relaxation}

A stellar system is collisional on a timescale of interest when its two-body relaxation time is shorter than that timescale, so discrete encounters materially alter its distribution function.

= Collisionless Boltzmann equation
{c}
{parent=Stellar dynamics}
{wiki}

The collisionless Boltzmann equation states that the phase-space distribution function is constant along trajectories in a smooth gravitational potential: $dF/dt=0$.

= Rotating-frame collisionless Boltzmann equation
{parent=Collisionless Boltzmann equation}
{title2=$dF/dt=0$}

= Collisionless Boltzmann equation in a rotating frame
{synonym}

In physical polar velocity components, a planar rotating system has accelerations $\dot v_R=2\Omega_bv_\phi+v_\phi^2/R+\psi_{\mathrm{eff},R}$ and $\dot v_\phi=-2\Omega_bv_R-v_Rv_\phi/R+\psi_{\mathrm{eff},\phi}/R$. The <collisionless Boltzmann equation> is constant distribution along these characteristics. Its physical phase-space measure includes the spatial Jacobian $R$.

= Cylindrical continuity equation for a rotating stellar system
{parent=Rotating-frame collisionless Boltzmann equation}
{title2=$\rho_t+R^{-1}(R\rho u_R)_R+R^{-1}(\rho u_\phi)_\phi=0$}

The zeroth velocity moment gives $\partial_t\rho+R^{-1}\partial_R(R\rho\langle v_R\rangle)+R^{-1}\partial_\phi(\rho\langle v_\phi\rangle)=0$. In the <rotating-frame collisionless Boltzmann equation>, velocity integration by parts supplies the geometric $\rho\langle v_R\rangle/R$ term. This equation expresses conservation of planar mass.

= Collisionless Jeans dispersion relation
{parent=Collisionless Boltzmann equation}
{title2=$1+\frac{4\pi G}{k^2}\int\frac{\mathbf k\cdot\nabla_v f_0}{\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf v-\omega}\,d^3v=0$}

Linearize the <collisionless Boltzmann equation> and the perturbation Poisson equation about a homogeneous <velocity> distribution $f_0$. A <plane wave> gives $f_1=\phi_1\mathbf k\cdot\nabla_vf_0/(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf v-\omega)$. Integrating this density and using $-k^2\phi_1=4\pi G\int f_1d^3v$ yields $1+(4\pi G/k^2)\int[\mathbf k\cdot\nabla_vf_0/(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf v-\omega)]d^3v=0$. Growing modes have $\operatorname{Im}\omega>0$ and no real-velocity pole; real and damped frequencies require the causal continuation prescription. The Newtonian homogeneous background requires the <Jeans swindle>.

= Cauchy velocity distribution
{parent=Collisionless Jeans dispersion relation}
{c}
{title2=$k_J=\sqrt{4\pi G\rho_0}/b$}

The isotropic three-dimensional <Cauchy velocity distribution> $f_0=\rho_0b/[\pi^2(v^2+b^2)^2]$, $b>0$, has density $\rho_0$ and one-dimensional marginal $F(u)=\rho_0b/[\pi(u^2+b^2)]$. Its second <velocity> moment diverges, so $b$ is a scale rather than a finite <velocity dispersion>. At zero phase <speed>, $\int F'(u)/u\,du=-\rho_0/b^2$, giving $k_J=\sqrt{4\pi G\rho_0}/b$. In the upper-half frequency <plane>, the marginal response is $\rho_0/(\omega/k+ib)^2$; the growing branch has $\omega=i(\sqrt{4\pi G\rho_0}-kb)$ for $0<k<k_J$.

= Jeans equation
{c}
{parent=Collisionless Boltzmann equation}
{wiki}

The Jeans equations are velocity moments of the <collisionless Boltzmann equation>. They relate density, mean motion, velocity dispersion, and gravitational acceleration without requiring a closed fluid equation of state.

= Vertical Jeans equation
{parent=Jeans equation}
{title2=$\partial_z(n\sigma_z^2)+R^{-1}\partial_R(Rn\overline{v_Rv_z})=-n\partial_z\Phi$}

For a steady axisymmetric <stellar population> with no mean vertical motion, the vertical <Jeans equation> balances the gradient of the vertical random-motion stress and the radial gradient of the tilted <velocity ellipsoid> against the vertical <Newtonian gravitational field>. Neglecting the mixed moment gives $\partial_z(n\sigma_z^2)=-n\partial_z\Phi$. A tracer density $n$ need not equal the density sourcing the <gravitational potential>.

= Dynamical surface density from vertical stellar motions
{parent=Vertical Jeans equation}
{title2=$\Sigma_{\rm dyn}(<|z|)$}

The <vertical Jeans equation> determines $K_z=\partial_z\Phi$ from a tracer's density and <velocity dispersion>, with a correction for the tilted <velocity ellipsoid>. Integrating the axisymmetric <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> gives
$$
2\pi G\Sigma_{\rm dyn}(<|z|)=K_z(z)+\int_0^z\frac1R\partial_R(R\partial_R\Phi)\,dz'
$$
for $z>0$ and reflection symmetry. Subtracting independently estimated stellar and gas <surface densities> constrains any additional gravitating component; a <dark-matter halo> also contributes, so an excess is not automatically a dark disk.

= Cylindrical Jeans equations in a rotating frame
{parent=Jeans equation}

The first moments of the <rotating-frame collisionless Boltzmann equation> express radial and azimuthal momentum balance. Coriolis terms enter as $-2\Omega_b\rho\langle v_\phi\rangle$ and $+2\Omega_b\rho\langle v_R\rangle$ on the left. Geometric stress terms are $\rho(\langle v_R^2\rangle-\langle v_\phi^2\rangle)/R$ and $2\rho\langle v_Rv_\phi\rangle/R$. Second moments include random stress and streaming flux.

= Spherical Jeans equation
{c}
{parent=Jeans equation}

For a steady nonrotating spherical system,
$$
\frac{d(\rho\sigma_r^2)}{dr}
+\frac{2\beta}{r}\rho\sigma_r^2
=-\rho\frac{d\Phi}{dr}.
$$
It relates radial pressure, velocity anisotropy, and the gravitational field.

= Isothermal collisionless tracer
{parent=Spherical Jeans equation}
{title2=$\rho=\rho_0e^{\psi/\sigma_0^2}$}

A steady <galactic distribution function> depending only on <relative energy> $E=\psi-v^2/2$ is isotropic and has zero mean <velocity>. If its one-dimensional <velocity dispersion> is constant, the <spherical Jeans equation> reduces to $\sigma_0^2\rho'=\rho\psi'$, yielding the displayed density. This is a tracer relation in a specified <gravitational potential>, not a requirement that the tracer generate that potential. With no escape-energy cutoff its local <velocity> distribution is <Maxwell-Boltzmann velocity distribution>.

= Gaussian line-of-sight velocity distribution
{c}
{parent=Isothermal collisionless tracer}
{title2=$p(v_{\rm los})=(\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma_0)^{-1}e^{-v_{\rm los}^2/(2\sigma_0^2)}$}

For an <isothermal collisionless tracer> without streaming or an escape cutoff, integrate its three-dimensional <Maxwell-Boltzmann velocity distribution> over the two transverse <velocities>. The remaining marginal is the displayed <normal distribution>. Its width and mean do not depend on position. Integrating along the <line of sight> therefore only multiplies the marginal by the projected <surface density>; the normalized observed velocity distribution remains the same Gaussian at every projected radius.

= Scale-free spherical Jeans solution
{parent=Spherical Jeans equation}

For tracer density $\nu\propto r^{-\alpha}$, circular speed $V_c\propto r^\gamma$, and constant anisotropy $\beta$, the boundary-free scale-free solution is
$$
\sigma_r^2=\frac{V_c^2}{\alpha-2\beta-2\gamma},
$$
which requires a positive denominator.

= Mass--anisotropy--density degeneracy
{parent=Spherical Jeans equation}

The mass--anisotropy--density degeneracy is the ability of different gravitational mass profiles, tracer-density profiles, and orbital-anisotropy profiles to predict similar projected tracer kinematics through the Jeans equation.

= Tensor virial theorem
{parent=Jeans equation}
{wiki}

For an isolated collisionless system, the tensor virial theorem is $\frac12\ddot I_{jk}=2T_{jk}+\Pi_{jk}+W_{jk}$, separating ordered kinetic, random kinetic, and gravitational potential-energy tensors.

= Flattening–anisotropy virial relation
{parent=Tensor virial theorem}
{title2=$\Pi_\perp/(2\Pi_{zz})=1+\gamma(q^{-2}-1)/5+O((q^{-2}-1)^2)$}

For a pressure-supported scale-free tracer $\rho\propto(R^2+z^2/q^2)^{-\gamma/2}$ in a spherical <Newtonian gravitational potential>, the <tensor virial theorem> constrains integrated directional <velocity dispersions>. Modest oblate flattening and a decreasing radial <mass density> require greater in-plane random support per direction. The displayed expansion assumes finite global virial integrals; the corresponding ratio of force moments also has a cutoff-independent angular interpretation.

= Scale-free tracer virial ratio
{parent=Flattening–anisotropy virial relation}
{title2=$\Pi_\perp/\Pi_{zz}=\int\sin^3\theta\,h\,d\theta\,/\int\sin\theta\cos^2\theta\,h\,d\theta$}

For $h=(\sin^2\theta+q^{-2}\cos^2\theta)^{-\gamma/2}$, separation in <spherical coordinates> makes both <stellar potential-energy tensor> integrals contain $\int r^{3-\gamma}\Phi'(r)dr$. Their ratio is the displayed positive finite angular expression. If the radial integrals diverge, identical spherical cutoffs give this same ratio of force moments, but there is no finite global pressure-energy ratio without further hypotheses. A finite-aperture <tensor virial theorem> retains its surface tensor.

= Planar virial trace
{parent=Tensor virial theorem}
{title2=$\Pi_\perp=\Pi_{xx}+\Pi_{yy}$}

The planar trace of the <stellar pressure-energy tensor> is the sum of its two Cartesian in-plane components. For an axisymmetric nonstreaming population it equals $\int\rho(\langle v_R^2\rangle+\langle v_\phi^2\rangle)d^3x$, not merely the cylindrical radial second moment. A spherical isotropic population has $\Pi_\perp=2\Pi_{zz}$.

= Stellar potential-energy tensor
{parent=Tensor virial theorem}
{title2=$W_{ij}=-\int\rho x_i\partial_j\Phi\,d^3x$}

This force-moment tensor enters the <tensor virial theorem>. For an isolated self-gravitating population it is symmetric by pairwise interchange of the Newtonian interaction; for a tracer in an external field use the force-moment definition directly, with the appropriate symmetric combination in a time-dependent inertia identity.

= Stellar pressure-energy tensor
{parent=Tensor virial theorem}
{title2=$\Pi_{ij}=\int\rho\sigma_{ij}\,d^3x$}

This tensor integrates the local <pressure tensor>, where $\sigma_{ij}=\langle(v_i-u_i)(v_j-u_j)\rangle$ is the <velocity> <covariance>. Half its trace is the random <kinetic energy>. It enters the <tensor virial theorem> with coefficient one.

= Stellar kinetic-energy tensor
{parent=Tensor virial theorem}
{title2=$T_{ij}=\tfrac12\int\rho u_iu_j\,d^3x$}

This tensor records the ordered <kinetic energy> of the <streaming velocity> $u_i=\langle v_i\rangle$. It excludes random motion; its vanishing is compatible with a nonzero <stellar pressure-energy tensor>.

= Magnetized-fluid tensor virial theorem
{parent=Tensor virial theorem}
{title2=$\ddot I_{ij}/2=2K_{ij}-\int T_{ij}\,dV$}

For an isolated self-gravitating fluid obeying <ideal magnetohydrodynamics>, let $I_{ij}=\int\rho x_ix_jdV$ and $K_{ij}=\frac12\int\rho u_iu_jdV$. Use the force-stress convention $\rho D_tu_i=\partial_jT_{ij}$, with <pressure>, magnetic and negative <Newtonian gravitational stress tensor> contributions. Two time derivatives of $I$ give $4K_{ij}$ plus $\int(x_i\partial_kT_{jk}+x_j\partial_kT_{ik})dV$. Integration by parts and symmetry yield the theorem when surface terms vanish. This fluid version complements the collisionless formulation of the <tensor virial theorem>.

= Horizontal virial balance of a cold magnetized fluid
{parent=Magnetized-fluid tensor virial theorem}
{title2=$\ddot I_\perp/2=\int[B_z^2/\mu_0-(\partial_z\Phi)^2/(4\pi G)]\,dV$}

At an initially resting instant with negligible <pressure>, sum the horizontal components of the <magnetized-fluid tensor virial theorem>. The horizontal magnetic trace is $-B_z^2/\mu_0$ and the gravitational force-stress trace is $(\partial_z\Phi)^2/(4\pi G)$. Their negatives give the displayed radial second-moment balance. Vacuum field stresses contribute too; being initially at rest does not imply zero acceleration.

= Singular isothermal sphere
{title2=SIS}
{parent=Galaxy dynamics}
{wiki}

A singular isothermal sphere with one-dimensional velocity dispersion $\sigma$ has $\rho(r)=\sigma^2/(2\pi Gr^2)$, enclosed mass $M(<r)=2\sigma^2r/G$, and constant circular speed $\sqrt2\sigma$.

= Circular and isotropic speed comparison in a singular isothermal sphere
{parent=Singular isothermal sphere}
{title2=$\langle v^2\rangle_{\mathrm{iso}}=3v_0^2/2$}

For the $r^{-2}$ <singular isothermal sphere>, the isotropic <spherical Jeans equation> gives $\sigma_r^2=\sigma_\theta^2=\sigma_\phi^2=v_0^2/2$, so $\langle v^2\rangle=3v_0^2/2$. Randomly oriented circular orbits instead have $\sigma_r^2=0$, each transverse second moment $v_0^2/2$, and total mean square speed $v_0^2$.

= Boundary virial term of a singular isothermal sphere
{parent=Singular isothermal sphere}
{title2=$4\pi R^3\rho(R)\sigma_r^2$}

A finite spherical subvolume has radial-pressure virial surface term $4\pi R^3\rho(R)\sigma_r^2$. The infinite <singular isothermal sphere> has divergent total mass and energies, so a finite isolated <virial theorem> cannot omit this term. Different orbital distributions with the same density can have different radial boundary pressures.

= Astrophysical black hole
{parent=Astrophysics}

An astrophysical black hole is a black hole studied through its formation, accretion, mergers, dynamical environment, and electromagnetic or gravitational-wave signatures.

= Black-hole sphere of influence
{parent=Astrophysical black hole}
{title2=$r_{\rm inf}\simeq GM_\bullet/\sigma^2$}

The <black-hole sphere of influence> is a characteristic radius where the central <black hole> appreciably changes surrounding stellar dynamics. Equating its <Newtonian gravitational potential> scale $GM_\bullet/r$ to the ambient squared <velocity dispersion> gives $r_{\rm inf}\simeq GM_\bullet/\sigma^2$. Dynamical mass measurements depend on angular resolution $r_{\rm inf}/D$, tracer brightness, and degeneracies in the orbital distribution, not solely on <black-hole> mass.

= Black-hole spin
{title2=$a_*$}
{parent=Astrophysical black hole}

Black-hole spin is the angular momentum of a black hole, commonly expressed by the dimensionless Kerr parameter $a_*=cJ/(GM^2)$. It changes the <innermost stable circular orbit>, accretion efficiency, and relativistic frame dragging.

= Black-hole spin-up by coherent accretion
{parent=Black-hole spin}

Coherent prograde gas accretion increases the <angular momentum> of a <black hole>. For a zero-torque thin disc, the rest mass $dm_0$ entering from the <innermost stable circular orbit> changes the hole by $dM=E_{\rm ISCO}dm_0$ and $dJ=\ell_{\rm ISCO}dm_0$. Its dimensionless spin obeys $da_*/d\log M=c\ell_{\rm ISCO}/(GME_{\rm ISCO})-2a_*$. Starting from zero spin and neglecting captured radiation, the formal extremal endpoint has $M_f=\sqrt6M_i$ and requires rest-mass supply approximately $1.8464M_i$. Photon capture leads instead to the <Thorne spin limit>.

= Thorne spin limit
{c}
{title2=$a_*\simeq0.998$}
{parent=Black-hole spin-up by coherent accretion}

The Thorne spin limit is the equilibrium spin produced by competition between coherent thin-disc matter accretion and capture of disc photons by a <Kerr black hole>. Preferential capture of counterrotating photon angular momentum prevents the standard radiatively efficient model from reaching $a_*=1$. The commonly quoted limit is approximately $0.998$, with some dependence on emission and accretion assumptions. https://articles.adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1974ApJ...191..507T[Thorne's original calculation] derives this result.

= Black-hole accretion
{parent=Astrophysical black hole}
{wiki}

Black-hole accretion converts gravitational binding energy of inflowing matter into heat, radiation, outflows, and growth of the black hole.

= Collisionless black-hole capture
{parent=Black-hole accretion}

Collisionless matter approaching a <black hole> generally cannot dissipate enough orbital energy to remain bound, so direct capture requires sufficiently small <angular momentum>. For nonrelativistic incident speed $v$, a <Schwarzschild black hole> has direct test-particle capture cross-section approximately $16\pi G^2M^2/(c^2v^2)$. A Newtonian pericentre estimate using the <Schwarzschild radius> instead gives $4\pi G^2M^2/(c^2v^2)$. Both show that capture at a given mass density is suppressed by order $(v/c)^2$ relative to supersonic <Bondi--Hoyle--Lyttleton accretion>. <Tidal disruption events> provide a distinct way to accrete some stellar debris.

= Dynamical upper bound on black-hole fuelling
{parent=Black-hole accretion}

For a <singular isothermal sphere> of dispersion $\sigma$ and gas fraction $f_g$, placing all gas inside radius $R$ onto a hole in time $R/\sigma$ gives $\dot M_{\rm dyn}\sim2f_g\sigma^3/G$. This is an optimistic supply bound that omits <angular momentum> transport, <star formation>, and <active-galactic-nucleus feedback>. A numerical comparison with the <Eddington accretion rate> requires a gas fraction, reference radiative efficiency, opacity, and crossing-time convention.

= Inflow time
{title2=$t_{\rm in}$}
{parent=Black-hole accretion}

The inflow time is the characteristic time for accreting matter to move radially by its current radius, $t_{\rm in}\sim R/|u_R|$.

= Paczyński-Wiita potential
{c}
{title2=$\Phi=-GM/(r-r_S)$}
{parent=Black-hole accretion}
{wiki}

The Paczyński-Wiita potential is a Newtonian pseudo-potential that reproduces the Schwarzschild innermost stable circular orbit at $r=3r_S$.

= Isothermal transonic accretion in the Paczyński-Wiita potential
{parent=Paczyński-Wiita potential}
{title2=$r_c=\frac{GM}{4c_s^2}(1+\beta/2+\sqrt{1+\beta})$}

For $\beta=16c_s^2/c^2$, the exterior <sonic point> of the <barotropic spherical accretion equation> lies outside $r_G=2GM/c^2$. Its <mass density> is $\rho_c=\rho_\infty\exp[4/(1+\sqrt{1+\beta})-1/2]$. The selected smooth transonic accretion rate is $4\pi r_c^2\rho_cc_s$. Subsonic formal solutions are not assigned this rate without transonic selection.

= Paczyński-Wiita circular orbit
{c}
{parent=Paczyński-Wiita potential}

Let $r_S=2GM/c^2$. In the <Paczyński-Wiita potential>, <circular orbit> balance and the <radial epicyclic frequency> formula give
$$
\Omega^2=\frac{GM}{r(r-r_S)^2},\quad
h=\frac{\sqrt{GM}\,r^{3/2}}{r-r_S},\quad
\kappa_r^2=\frac{GM(r-3r_S)}{r(r-r_S)^3}.
$$
Thus the <innermost stable circular orbit> lies at $r=3r_S$. Its <specific orbital energy> is $-c^2/16$, giving a zero-torque <radiative efficiency of black-hole accretion> of $1/16$. The model reproduces the Schwarzschild inner orbit exactly but approximates its relativistic energy and frequency. https://arxiv.org/abs/0904.0913[Abramowicz's derivation] explains the potential's relation to the Schwarzschild effective potential.

= Radiative efficiency of black-hole accretion
{title2=$\eta$}
{parent=Black-hole accretion}

The radiative efficiency is $\eta=L/(\dot M c^2)$. For a zero-torque thin disk it is $1-E_{\rm ISCO}$, the binding energy per unit rest mass released before matter crosses the innermost stable circular orbit.

= Maximal radiative efficiency of black-hole accretion
{title2=$\eta_{\max}$}
{parent=Radiative efficiency of black-hole accretion}

The maximal radiative efficiency is the available binding energy per unit accreted rest-mass energy when all energy released outside the capture boundary escapes as radiation. A relativistic zero-torque disk has $\eta_{\max}=1-E_{\rm ISCO}$.

= Innermost stable circular orbit
{title2=ISCO}
{parent=Radiative efficiency of black-hole accretion}
{wiki}

The innermost stable circular orbit is the smallest stable circular timelike orbit around a black hole. Its radius and orbital energy depend on black-hole spin and on whether the orbit is prograde or retrograde.

= Bondi accretion
{c}
{parent=Black-hole accretion}
{wiki}

Bondi accretion is steady spherical accretion from a uniform gas reservoir onto a compact gravitating object. In its adiabatic form, compression raises internal energy without providing the irreversible shear heating of a radiatively efficient disk.

= Relativistic spherical accretion
{parent=Bondi accretion}

= Michel accretion
{c}
{synonym}

Relativistic spherical accretion is steady radial gas inflow in <Schwarzschild spacetime>, with conserved rest-mass flux and relativistic <Bernoulli function>. A regular solution passes a finite <sonic point> outside the <event horizon>. For an isentropic gas with $\gamma=5/3$ and cold asymptotic <sound speed> $c_\infty\ll c$, the critical radius is approximately $3GM/(4cc_\infty)$ and the leading <mass accretion rate> is $\pi G^2M^2\rho_\infty/c_\infty^3$. Relativistic corrections resolve the zero-radius critical point of the Newtonian $\gamma=5/3$ model without eliminating accretion.

= Bondi–Hoyle accretion
{c}
{parent=Bondi accretion}
{wiki}

= Bondi--Hoyle--Lyttleton accretion
{c}
{synonym}

Bondi--Hoyle--Lyttleton accretion is gravitational capture by a compact object moving through a gas. In the pressureless supersonic limit, ballistic streamlines focus into a downstream wake.

= Ballistic streamline focusing by a moving star
{parent=Bondi–Hoyle accretion}
{title2=$b=d^2v^2/(2GM)$}

In a cold pressureless stream of asymptotic speed $v$, conservation of <specific angular momentum> and the <eccentricity vector> makes a streamline at impact parameter $d$ cross the downstream axis at $b=d^2v^2/(2GM)$. Its axial velocity there remains $v$, while opposing transverse velocities collide in a shock.

= Post-shock capture criterion in ballistic accretion
{parent=Ballistic streamline focusing by a moving star}
{title2=$d_{\rm cap}=2GM/v^2$}

If transverse <kinetic energy> is dissipated at the downstream axis, the remaining <specific orbital energy> is $v^2/2-GM/b$. Binding requires $b<2GM/v^2$, corresponding to incident impact parameter $d<2GM/v^2$. Integrating the captured <mass flux> gives the <Bondi--Hoyle--Lyttleton accretion rate>.

= Bondi--Hoyle--Lyttleton accretion rate
{title2=$\dot M_{\rm BHL}$}
{c}
{parent=Bondi–Hoyle accretion}

In the ballistic supersonic limit, streamline collisions bind gas with impact parameter $b<2GM/v_\infty^2$, giving
$$
\dot M_{\rm BHL}=\frac{4\pi G^2M^2\rho_\infty}{v_\infty^3}.
$$

= Isothermal Bondi accretion
{c}
{parent=Bondi accretion}

Isothermal Bondi accretion has $p=c_s^2\rho$ with constant sound speed. Its regular transonic solution crosses the <Bondi sonic point> and approaches free fall near the accretor.

= Isothermal Bondi accretion rate
{parent=Isothermal Bondi accretion}
{title2=$\dot M=\pi e^{3/2}G^2M^2\rho_\infty/c_s^3$}

The regular spherical isothermal inflow from <mass density> $\rho_\infty$ at rest at infinity has sonic <mass density> $e^{3/2}\rho_\infty$. Combining it with the <Bondi sonic point> and <mass conservation> gives $\dot M=\pi e^{3/2}G^2M^2\rho_\infty/c_s^3$.

= Isothermal Bondi equation
{c}
{parent=Isothermal Bondi accretion}

For inward speed $u>0$, steady spherical mass conservation and radial momentum balance give
$$
\left(u-\frac{c_s^2}{u}\right)\frac{du}{dr}
=\frac{2c_s^2}{r}-\frac{GM}{r^2}.
$$

= Integrated isothermal Bondi flow relation
{parent=Isothermal Bondi equation}
{title2=$\mathcal M^2/2-\ln\mathcal M=2/x+2\ln x-3/2$}

With $x=r/r_s$ and $\mathcal M=|u_r|/c_s$, the radial isothermal equation integrates to $\mathcal M^2/2-\ln\mathcal M=2/x+2\ln x+C$. A regular <transonic branch> has $C=-3/2$, and the reservoir-fed accretion branch crosses $(x,\mathcal M)=(1,1)$ with slope $-1$.

= Bondi sonic point
{title2=$r_s$}
{c}
{parent=Isothermal Bondi equation}

The isothermal Bondi sonic point is $r_s=GM/(2c_s^2)$, where the regular transonic solution has $u=c_s$.

= Critical point of the isothermal Bondi equation
{parent=Bondi sonic point}

At the critical point, both the coefficient of $du/dr$ and the remaining right-hand side of the <isothermal Bondi equation> vanish, allowing a finite derivative through the sonic transition.

= Mass accretion rate
{title2=$\dot M$}
{parent=Bondi accretion}

The mass accretion rate is the mass entering an accretor per unit time. In a steady spherical flow, $\dot M=4\pi r^2\rho u$ is independent of radius.

= Transonic accretion in a steep dark-matter cusp
{title2=Transonic accretion in an $r^{-5/2}$ density cusp}
{parent=Mass accretion rate}

For a background gravitating density $\rho_{\rm dm}=\Psi_0r^{-5/2}$, the potential is $\Phi=-16\pi G\Psi_0r^{-1/2}$. A steady polytropic inflow has a regular sonic point only for $1<\gamma<9/7$, and its accretion rate scales as $\dot M\propto\rho_0G^4\Psi_0^4c_0^{-7}$.

= Free-fall speed
{title2=$v_{\rm ff}$}
{parent=Bondi accretion}

The Newtonian free-fall speed from rest at infinity toward a point mass is $v_{\rm ff}=\sqrt{2GM/r}$.

= Critical Bondi accretion rate for gamma equals five thirds
{parent=Bondi accretion}

For an adiabatic gas with $\gamma=5/3$, asymptotic density $\rho_0$, and sound speed $v_{s0}$ accreting onto a Newtonian point mass $M$, the largest globally subsonic steady rate is
$$
\dot M_{\rm crit}=\frac{\pi\rho_0(GM)^2}{v_{s0}^3}.
$$

= Radiatively inefficient accretion flow
{title2=RIAF}
{parent=Black-hole accretion}

A radiatively inefficient accretion flow carries a substantial fraction of locally generated energy with the gas instead of radiating it before accretion. It can occur at low density because emission is weak or at high optical depth because photons are trapped.

= Advection-dominated accretion flow
{title2=ADAF}
{parent=Radiatively inefficient accretion flow}
{wiki}

An advection-dominated accretion flow is a hot, geometrically thick, radiatively inefficient flow in which most dissipated energy remains in the gas and is carried inward rather than radiated locally.

= Two-temperature accretion flow
{parent=Advection-dominated accretion flow}

A two-temperature accretion flow has ions and electrons at substantially different temperatures because their Coulomb equilibration time exceeds the inflow time. Ions can retain most viscous heat while electrons, which radiate more effectively, remain cooler.

= Electron--proton thermal equilibration time
{title2=$t_{e-p}$}
{parent=Two-temperature accretion flow}

The electron--proton thermal equilibration time is the timescale on which Coulomb collisions exchange energy between electron and proton populations. Its inverse dependence on number density helps make dilute accretion flows two-temperature and radiatively inefficient.

= Slim accretion disk
{parent=Radiatively inefficient accretion flow}
{wiki=Slim_disc}

= Slim accretion disc
{synonym}

A slim accretion disk is an optically thick, moderately geometrically thick flow at a high accretion rate. Its inflow time can be shorter than its photon-diffusion time, so radial advection carries trapped radiation inward.

= Photon trapping in an accretion flow
{parent=Slim accretion disk}

Photon trapping occurs when the radiative diffusion time across an optically thick flow exceeds its inward advection time. The trapped radiation is carried inward, reducing the emergent radiative efficiency.

= Photon-trapping radius
{title2=$r_t$}
{parent=Photon trapping in an accretion flow}

The photon-trapping radius is where the outward <radiative diffusion> time equals the inward <inflow time>. Radiation generated inside it is predominantly advected toward the accretor.

= Luminous hot accretion flow
{title2=LHAF}
{parent=Radiatively inefficient accretion flow}

A luminous hot accretion flow has radiative cooling greater than local viscous heating, so compressive inward advection supplies the difference. In the convention $q_{\rm adv}=q^+-q^-$, it has $q_{\rm adv}<0$.

= Accretion-flow advection balance
{title2=$q_{\rm adv}=q^+-q^-$}
{parent=Radiatively inefficient accretion flow}

The accretion-flow advection balance compares local volumetric heating $q^+$, radiative cooling $q^-$, and energy transport with the gas. Positive $q_{\rm adv}$ means advection removes locally generated heat; zero gives local radiative balance; negative means advection supplies heat that is radiated locally.

= Eddington ratio
{title2=$f_{\rm Edd}$}
{parent=Black-hole accretion}
{wiki}

The Eddington ratio is the luminosity divided by the Eddington luminosity. At fixed radiative efficiency it also measures the accretion rate relative to the Eddington rate.

= Eddington accretion rate
{title2=$\dot M_{\rm Edd}$}
{c}
{parent=Eddington ratio}

An Eddington accretion rate converts the <Eddington luminosity> into a mass supply rate using an adopted reference efficiency $\eta_0$: $\dot M_{\rm Edd}=L_{\rm Edd}/(\eta_0c^2)$. The common choice $\eta_0=0.1$ gives $\dot M_{\rm Edd}=10L_{\rm Edd}/c^2$.

= Salpeter time
{title2=$t_{\rm Sal}$}
{c}
{parent=Eddington ratio}
{wiki}

The Salpeter time is the e-folding time for Eddington-limited black-hole growth. If $t_E=\sigma_Tc/(4\pi Gm_p)$, then $t_{\rm Sal}=\eta t_E/[(1-\eta)f_{\rm Edd}]$.

= Intermediate-mass black hole
{title2=IMBH}
{parent=Astrophysical black hole}
{wiki}

An intermediate-mass black hole has a mass between stellar remnants and supermassive black holes, approximately $10^2$--$10^5M_\odot$.

= Supermassive black hole
{title2=SMBH}
{parent=Astrophysical black hole}
{wiki}

A supermassive black hole has a mass of roughly $10^6M_\odot$ or more and commonly occupies the nucleus of a massive galaxy.

= M-sigma relation
{c}
{title2=$M_{\rm BH}\propto\sigma^\alpha$}
{parent=Supermassive black hole}

The M-sigma relation is an empirical correlation between <supermassive black hole> mass and the stellar <velocity dispersion> of its host's spheroidal component. A commonly used rough parametrization has exponent near four, but the normalization, exponent, scatter, and selection effects depend on the observed sample. It is distinct from the <black-hole--bulge mass relation>.

= Black-hole--bulge mass relation
{parent=Supermassive black hole}

The black-hole--bulge mass relation is the empirical correlation between a galaxy's central black-hole mass and the stellar mass of its spheroidal component. Locally, the black hole is typically of order $10^{-3}$--$10^{-2}$ of the bulge mass, with substantial scatter and possible redshift evolution.

= Active galactic nucleus
{title2=AGN}
{parent=Supermassive black hole}
{wiki}

= Active galactic nuclei
{synonym}

An active galactic nucleus is a compact luminous galactic nucleus powered primarily by accretion onto a supermassive black hole.

= Quasar
{parent=Active galactic nucleus}
{wiki}

= QSO
{synonym}
{c}

A quasar is an extremely luminous active galactic nucleus whose compact central emission can be observed across cosmological distances.

= Quasar light curve
{parent=Quasar}

A quasar light curve records its measured brightness as a function of time. Intrinsic accretion variability, measurement noise, and gravitational lensing can all contribute structure.

= Active-galactic-nucleus feedback
{title2=AGN feedback}
{parent=Active galactic nucleus}
{wiki}

Active-galactic-nucleus feedback transfers radiative or mechanical energy and momentum from black-hole accretion to surrounding galactic gas.

= Thermal suppression of black-hole accretion
{parent=Active-galactic-nucleus feedback}

Heating a gas reservoir reduces its <Bondi accretion> rate because $\dot M_B\propto\rho_\infty c_s^{-3}$. At fixed density, increasing the <sound speed> by ten reduces the rate by about a thousand and the gravitational capture radius by a hundred. Expansion and density loss can strengthen this negative feedback. Neither the density nor the cold pre-heating accretion rate is determined by the temperature change alone.

= Active-galactic-nucleus wind bubble
{title2=AGN wind bubble}
{parent=Active-galactic-nucleus feedback}

An active-galactic-nucleus wind bubble forms when a fast nuclear wind shocks against galactic gas. From inside outward it contains free wind, a reverse shock, shocked wind, a contact discontinuity, a swept-up shocked shell, a forward shock, and undisturbed interstellar gas.

= Momentum-driven outflow
{parent=Active-galactic-nucleus wind bubble}

An outflow is momentum driven when shocked driving material cools rapidly, so it cannot retain enough thermal energy to inflate a pressure-supported bubble. The swept shell then receives approximately the direct wind momentum flux, often of order $L/c$.

= Energy-driven outflow
{parent=Active-galactic-nucleus wind bubble}

An outflow is energy driven when cooling of the shocked wind is slow. Its hot bubble stores mechanical energy and expands by pressure work on the swept-up shell.

= Momentum boost
{parent=Energy-driven outflow}

A momentum boost is a shell momentum flux exceeding the direct input momentum flux. An energy-conserving wind of speed $v_w$ driving a shell of speed $v_s\ll v_w$ can give $\dot p_s/(L/c)$ of order $v_w/(2v_s)$.

= Dusty radiation-pressure-driven shell
{parent=Active-galactic-nucleus feedback}

A dusty radiation-pressure-driven shell is swept up when ultraviolet radiation from an active nucleus is absorbed by dust and reradiated in the infrared. Single scattering supplies up to roughly $L/c$ of momentum flux, while trapped infrared photons can enhance it by the infrared optical depth.

= Terminal speed of a constant-mass dusty shell
{title2=$v_\infty$}
{parent=Dusty radiation-pressure-driven shell}

A fixed-mass shell with ultraviolet opacity $\kappa_{\rm UV}$ has <dust transparency radius> $R_{\rm UV}=\sqrt{\kappa_{\rm UV}M_{\rm sh}/(4\pi)}$. For an initially expanding shell driven by constant ultraviolet luminosity, a sharp opaque-to-transparent approximation gives
$$
v_\infty^2=v_0^2+\frac{2GM}{R_0}\left[\Gamma_{\rm ss}(2X-1)+\Gamma_{\rm IR}(1-X^{-1})-1\right],
\qquad X=R_{\rm UV}/R_0.
$$
Here $\Gamma_{\rm ss}=LR_0^2/(GM M_{\rm sh}c)$ is the initial single-scattering force ratio and $\Gamma_{\rm IR}=\kappa_{\rm IR}L/(4\pi GMc)$. The trajectory must actually reach $R_{\rm UV}$. Large initial ultraviolet <optical depth> enhances the radiative work at fixed initial single-scattering ratio; the same conclusion does not follow unconditionally at fixed electron-scattering luminosity.

= Critical luminosity of a dusty shell
{title2=$L_{\rm crit}$}
{parent=Dusty radiation-pressure-driven shell}

The critical luminosity of a dusty shell is the luminosity at which radiation pressure balances gravity. For shell mass $M_s$, enclosed gravitating mass $M$, and ultraviolet and infrared optical depths,
$$
L_{\rm crit}=\frac{GcMM_s}{r^2(1+\tau_{\rm IR}-e^{-\tau_{\rm UV}})}.
$$

= Thin-shell momentum equation
{parent=Dusty radiation-pressure-driven shell}

For a spherical swept shell of mass $M_s(R)$, radial speed $\dot R$, driving force $F_d$, and enclosed gravitating mass $M(<R)$,
$$
\frac d{dt}[M_s(R)\dot R]=F_d-\frac{GM_s(R)M(<R)}{R^2}.
$$
The derivative includes momentum needed to accelerate newly swept ambient mass.

= Dust transparency radius
{title2=$R_\tau$}
{parent=Dusty radiation-pressure-driven shell}

The dust transparency radius is where a swept shell's ultraviolet optical depth equals one. For a gas fraction $f_g$ in a singular isothermal sphere, $R_\tau=\kappa_{\rm UV}f_g\sigma^2/(2\pi G)$.

= Soltan argument
{c}
{parent=Supermassive black hole}
{wiki}

The Soltan argument compares the cosmological energy density radiated by active galactic nuclei with the present mass density in supermassive black holes. Their ratio constrains the mass-weighted mean radiative efficiency of black-hole growth.

= Tidal disruption event
{title2=TDE}
{parent=Astrophysical black hole}
{wiki}

A tidal disruption event occurs when a star passes close enough to a black hole that differential gravity tears it apart outside the capture boundary, producing returning debris and often a luminous flare.

= Tidal disruption radius
{title2=$r_t$}
{parent=Tidal disruption event}

For a star of mass $M_*$ and radius $R_*$ near a black hole of mass $M_{\rm BH}$, the Newtonian tidal disruption radius is $r_t\simeq R_*(2M_{\rm BH}/M_*)^{1/3}$ under a peak-to-center tidal-acceleration convention.

= Hills mass
{c}
{parent=Tidal disruption event}
{wiki}

The Hills mass is the black-hole mass above which a given star crosses the capture boundary before being tidally disrupted. For a Schwarzschild capture radius $2GM_{\rm BH}/c^2$, it is $c^3R_*^{3/2}/(2G^{3/2}M_*^{1/2})$ with the corresponding tidal-radius convention.

= Black-hole seed
{parent=Astrophysical black hole}
{wiki}

A black-hole seed is an early black hole from which a later massive black hole can grow by accretion and mergers.

= Population III remnant black-hole seed
{c}
{parent=Black-hole seed}

A Population III remnant seed forms when a massive metal-free star collapses, typically leaving a light seed of order $10$--$10^2M_\odot$ depending on stellar mass loss and pair-instability effects.

= Runaway stellar-collision black-hole seed
{parent=Black-hole seed}

A runaway stellar-collision seed forms when mass segregation and repeated mergers in a dense young cluster create a very massive star that collapses to a black hole of roughly $10^2$--$10^4M_\odot$.

= Direct-collapse black-hole seed
{title2=DCBH seed}
{parent=Black-hole seed}

A direct-collapse black-hole seed forms when rapidly inflowing primordial gas avoids fragmentation, builds a supermassive star or quasistar, and collapses to a seed of roughly $10^4$--$10^6M_\odot$.

= Observational astronomy
{parent=Astrophysics}
{wiki}

= Flux-limited galaxy selection
{parent=Observational astronomy}
{title2=$V_{\max}\propto L^{3/2}$}

For a nearby Euclidean survey with limiting <flux> $f_{\min}$, a <galaxy> of <luminosity> $L$ is detectable out to $d_{\max}=\sqrt{L/(4\pi f_{\min})}$. Its accessible volume is therefore proportional to $L^{3/2}$. With a <Schechter function> $\phi(L)\propto L^\alpha e^{-L/L_*}$, counts per logarithmic interval are proportional to $L^{\alpha+5/2}e^{-L/L_*}$ and peak at $(\alpha+5/2)L_*$ when $\alpha>-5/2$. This selection suppresses faint objects relative to a volume-limited census; finite survey boundaries and cosmological evolution modify the simple law.

= Angular resolution
{parent=Observational astronomy}
{title2=$\theta_{\rm res}$}
{wiki}

<Angular resolution> describes the smallest angular separation or structure an observation can distinguish. A physical scale $r$ at distance $D$ subtends approximately $r/D$ radians when $r\ll D$. Resolving a <black-hole sphere of influence> therefore involves distance as well as its physical radius, and useful kinematics also require adequate <surface brightness>.

= Surface brightness
{parent=Observational astronomy}
{title2=$I$}
{wiki}

<Surface brightness> is received flux per unit solid angle on the sky. It describes the brightness of an extended source independently of a particular aperture's area. An <isophote> is a contour of constant <surface brightness>; central <surface brightness> sets the available photons for resolved galaxy spectroscopy.

= Sérsic profile
{c}
{parent=Surface brightness}
{wiki}
{title2=$I(R)=I_e e^{-b_n[(R/R_e)^{1/n}-1]}$}

A <Sérsic profile> models projected galactic light, with index $n$ controlling curvature and $b_n$ chosen so that $R_e$ encloses half the <luminosity>. Its logarithmic slope is $d\ln I/d\ln R=-(b_n/n)(R/R_e)^{1/n}$. The $n=1$ case is exponential; $n=4$ gives the traditional de Vaucouleurs form. Unlike a fixed power law, its slope steepens outward.

= Projection of a spherical power-law emissivity
{parent=Surface brightness}
{title2=$j\propto r^{-p}\Longrightarrow I\propto R^{1-p}$}

An optically thin spherical source with volume emissivity $j(r)=j_0r^{-p}$ has <surface brightness>
$$
I(R)=\frac{j_0}{4\pi}R^{1-p}\sqrt\pi\,\frac{\Gamma((p-1)/2)}{\Gamma(p/2)},\qquad p>1.
$$
Substitute $z=Ru$ in the line-of-sight <integral> and evaluate $\int_{-\infty}^\infty(1+u^2)^{-p/2}du$ using the <beta function>. The condition $p>1$ is convergence at large distance. A core or finite outer boundary changes the projection near that boundary; the stated scaling applies between those scales.

= Isophote
{parent=Observational astronomy}
{wiki}

An <isophote> is a contour of equal surface brightness in an astronomical image. Deviations from fitted ellipses encode additional information about a <galaxy>'s projected stellar structure.

= Boxy and disky isophotes
{parent=Isophote}
{title2=$a_4\cos(4\theta)$}

Writing an <isophote>'s radial residual about its fitted ellipse as $\delta r=a_4\cos4\theta+\cdots$, positive $a_4$ extends the major and minor axes and gives a pointed, disky shape; negative $a_4$ extends diagonal directions and gives a boxy shape. The sign stated here is for radial residuals, not an unconverted brightness residual. Massive slowly rotating <elliptical galaxies> commonly have boxy <isophotes>, while embedded rotating disks commonly make lower-mass <elliptical galaxies> disky.

= Sky brightness
{parent=Observational astronomy}
{wiki}

= Sky background
{synonym}

Sky brightness is the diffuse radiance received away from an astronomical target. Ground-based observations include atmospheric airglow, scattered light, and <thermal radiation>; extraterrestrial diffuse light also contributes. Its photon fluctuations contribute to the <signal-to-noise ratio in photon counting>.
